/*
 * AQA A-Level Politics (7152) — Exam Practice Question Bank
 * Keyed by paper-prefixed topic id (1.1–1.10, 2.1–2.14, 3.1–3.8).
 * Shape: { topicId: { name, questions: [{ q, marks, command, markScheme }] } }
 * 10 questions per topic, in the mix each topic gets on the real 7152 papers:
 *   Paper 1 (1.x): 6 × 9-mark + 3 × 25-mark essay + 1 × 25-mark extract question.
 *   Paper 2 US (2.1–2.8): 6 × 9-mark + 1 × 25-mark US extract question + 3 × 25-mark UK–US essays.
 *   Paper 2 comparative (2.9–2.14): 6 × 9-mark comparative-theory (structural/rational/cultural) + 4 × 25-mark UK–US essays.
 *   Paper 3 core (3.1–3.3): 6 × 9-mark + 4 × 25-mark extract questions on the specified thinkers.
 *   Paper 3 other ideologies (3.4–3.8): 10 × 25-mark essays "with reference to the … thinkers that you have studied".
 * Student self-marks against the mark scheme: Full / Partial / Missed.
 *
 * TARIFFS. Unlike most subjects, AQA 7152 uses only TWO tariffs, so every
 * question here is one of them — no invented mark values:
 *   9 marks   — "Explain and analyse three ways/reasons/factors..."
 *               Three substantiated points, each explained and analysed.
 *   25 marks  — extract-based or essay. Balanced argument, sustained line of
 *               reasoning, reasoned conclusion. Paper 2 comparative answers
 *               must make EXPLICIT UK/US comparisons throughout.
 *
 * MARK SCHEMES. AQA marks these by LEVELS OF RESPONSE, not by ticking points,
 * so each markScheme below gives indicative content plus the level guidance an
 * examiner applies. Indicative content is not a checklist — a response that
 * makes other valid points well still reaches the top band.
 *
 *   9-mark levels:
 *     L3 (7–9)  Three points, each fully explained AND analysed, with accurate
 *               examples and appropriate political vocabulary.
 *     L2 (4–6)  Points explained with some analysis; examples may be thin or
 *               fewer than three points developed.
 *     L1 (1–3)  Descriptive; little or no analysis; limited or no examples.
 *
 *   25-mark levels:
 *     L5 (21–25) Sustained line of reasoning; consistently balanced; analysis
 *                and evaluation fully integrated; conclusion follows from the
 *                argument rather than being asserted.
 *     L4 (16–20) Well-developed argument, mostly balanced, sound evaluation.
 *     L3 (11–15) Some analysis and evaluation; balance uneven; conclusion weak.
 *     L2 (6–10)  Largely descriptive; limited evaluation; one-sided.
 *     L1 (1–5)   Fragmentary; little relevant knowledge; no evaluation.
 */

const POLITICS_AQA_PRACTICE = {

  /* ══════════════════════ PAPER 1 — GOVERNMENT AND POLITICS OF THE UK ══════════════════════ */

  '1.1': {
    name: 'The Constitution',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three sources of the UK constitution.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three, each explained AND analysed:\n• Statute law — Acts of Parliament, the highest source because Parliament is sovereign. Examples: Human Rights Act 1998, Scotland Act 1998, Constitutional Reform Act 2005. Analysis: its supremacy over other sources is what makes the constitution unentrenched, since any statute can be repealed by simple majority.\n• Common law — judge-made precedent plus the residual royal prerogative. Analysis: gives the judiciary a genuine constitutional role, and Miller I/II show prerogative limits being set judicially rather than politically.\n• Conventions — unwritten but binding practice (Salisbury Convention, royal assent, collective responsibility, Sewel). Analysis: obeyed because breach carries political cost, not legal sanction — Miller I confirmed even statutory recognition does not make Sewel enforceable.\n• Works of authority — Erskine May, Bagehot, the Cabinet Manual. Analysis: persuasive rather than binding; their status itself illustrates the constitution's uncodified character.\n• Treaties — the ECHR via the HRA; formerly EU law via the ECA 1972. Analysis: the repeal of the ECA by the European Union (Withdrawal) Act 2018, with effect from 31 January 2020, demonstrates that even treaty-based sources rest on ordinary statute.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires three sources each with explanation AND analysis plus accurate examples. Merely listing and defining sources is L1–L2 however many are given.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which the UK constitution has been reformed since 1997.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Devolution (Scotland Act 1998, Government of Wales Act 1998, Northern Ireland Act 1998; extended 2012, 2016, 2017). Analysis: the most significant dispersal of power, producing real policy divergence — but asymmetric, England excluded, and the powers remain legally revocable.\n• Human Rights Act 1998 — incorporated the ECHR; s.3 interpretive duty, s.4 declarations of incompatibility. Analysis: significantly strengthened rights and the judiciary's role while deliberately stopping short of entrenchment.\n• House of Lords Act 1999 — removed all but 92 hereditary peers (the remainder were removed by the House of Lords (Hereditary Peers) Act 2026). Analysis: increased perceived legitimacy and therefore assertiveness, but left composition wholly unelected.\n• Constitutional Reform Act 2005 — Supreme Court, Law Lords removed from the legislature, Lord Chancellor reformed, JAC created. Analysis: sharpened the separation of powers the UK's fused system otherwise lacks.\n• Fixed-term Parliaments Act 2011, repealed 2022. Analysis: a perfect illustration of unentrenchment — a major change made and unmade within eleven years.\n\nGuidance: credit reforms not listed if accurately explained. Top-band answers note the piecemeal character of the programme as a whole.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three features of the UK constitution.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Uncodified — not collected in a single authoritative document; drawn from statute, common law, convention, works of authority. Analysis: distinguish from "unwritten" — most of it is written, just scattered.\n• Unentrenched — constitutional law has no higher status than ordinary law. Analysis: the FTPA's passage and repeal is the clearest evidence; the consequence is flexibility and insecurity as the same property.\n• Unitary (now quasi-federal) — sovereignty held centrally, regional power devolved. Analysis: devolution qualifies this in practice but not in law, as the UK Internal Market Act 2020 demonstrated.\n• Parliamentary sovereignty — Parliament may make or unmake any law; no body may override it; no Parliament binds its successors.\n• Rule of law — everyone including ministers subject to and equal before the law. Analysis: sits in tension with sovereignty, since one implies Parliament may enact anything and the other that some principles should constrain it.\n• Fusion of powers — executive drawn from and sitting in the legislature.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires analysis of consequences, not just definition.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three arguments in favour of codifying the UK constitution.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Clarity and accessibility — a single document citizens could read. Analysis: disputes such as the 2019 prorogation turned on rules nobody could point to in one text; conventions are contested precisely because unwritten.\n• Entrenchment and protection of rights — a special amendment procedure would protect rights and devolved institutions from a temporary Commons majority. Analysis: the HRA is currently as vulnerable as any ordinary statute and has been repeatedly threatened with replacement.\n• Constraining an over-mighty executive — addresses Hailsham's "elective dictatorship". Analysis: a government with a Commons majority currently faces few effective constitutional limits between elections.\n• Clarifying devolution — would settle the Westminster/devolved boundary that Brexit exposed as unresolved.\n• Bringing the UK into line with almost all comparable democracies.\n\nGuidance: this is a one-sided 9-mark question — do NOT penalise absence of counter-arguments, but do require analysis rather than assertion.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which parliamentary sovereignty has been challenged since 1997.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• EU membership and Factortame (1991) — UK courts disapplied an Act of Parliament conflicting with EU law. Analysis: the first apparent qualification of sovereignty; but supremacy rested on the ECA 1972, an ordinary statute Parliament itself repealed by the European Union (Withdrawal) Act 2018, with effect from 31 January 2020, so the challenge may have been delegation rather than surrender.\n• The Human Rights Act 1998 — s.3 interpretive duty has produced readings straining what Parliament enacted; s.4 declarations generate political pressure. Analysis: sovereignty formally preserved, since the statute remains valid and Parliament decides.\n• Devolution — the Sewel Convention, the Scotland Act 2016 declaring the Scottish Parliament permanent, and devolved democratic mandates. Analysis: Miller I confirmed Sewel judicially unenforceable, so sovereignty survives in law — but the political cost of overriding is real.\n• Referendums — a legally advisory 2016 vote acquiring binding political authority, creating a clash between popular and parliamentary sovereignty.\n• Judicial activism — Miller I and II constraining prerogative power.\n\nGuidance: top-band answers distinguish legal sovereignty from political sovereignty.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three significant senior court cases affecting the UK constitution.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Factortame (1991, House of Lords — before the Supreme Court existed) — UK courts disapplied part of the Merchant Shipping Act 1988 for conflict with EU law. Analysis: first setting aside of primary legislation; its basis vanished with the ECA's repeal in 2020.\n• Miller I (2017) — ministers could not use the prerogative to trigger Article 50 because it would remove statutory rights; also held Sewel judicially unenforceable despite statutory recognition. Analysis: frequently misread as a defeat for sovereignty — it protected Parliament against the executive.\n• Miller II (2019) — the five-week prorogation unlawful as frustrating Parliament's constitutional functions. Analysis: the critical move was holding the question justiciable at all; fed directly into subsequent proposals to restrict judicial review.\n• Belmarsh (2004, House of Lords) — declaration of incompatibility over indefinite detention of foreign terror suspects; government amended the law. Analysis: shows the political force of s.4 exceeding its legal force.\n• AAA v Home Secretary (2023, Supreme Court) — the Rwanda asylum policy held unlawful because of the risk of refoulement. Analysis: Parliament responded with the Safety of Rwanda (Asylum and Immigration) Act 2024 declaring Rwanda safe, showing that statute can override a judicial finding — legal sovereignty intact.\n\nGuidance: the Supreme Court only opened in October 2009; earlier cases were decided by the Appellate Committee of the House of Lords. Credit accurate case detail. Analysis must address constitutional significance, not merely recount facts.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'EXTRACTS (written for practice):\n\nExtract 1 — written for this practice question in the style of an opinion piece by a constitutional law academic who supports codification\n"The United Kingdom is one of very few democracies without a single constitutional document, and recent years have exposed the cost. In 2019 the legality of a five-week prorogation had to be settled by the Supreme Court because there was no written rule to point to. In Miller (2017) the Court held that the Sewel Convention cannot be enforced by the courts, and Westminster then passed the UK Internal Market Act 2020 without the consent of Holyrood or the Senedd. The Human Rights Act can be repealed by a simple majority, like any other statute. A government with a large Commons majority faces few legal limits between elections. A codified constitution, entrenched and interpreted by an independent Supreme Court, would give citizens a clear statement of their rights and of where power lies."\n\nExtract 2 — written for this practice question in the style of a newspaper column by a former Conservative minister who opposes codification\n"Those who demand a written constitution forget how well the present one has adapted. Devolution, the Human Rights Act and a new Supreme Court were all created by ordinary legislation, and the Fixed-term Parliaments Act was repealed in 2022 once it proved unworkable — changes that a rigid document would have made slow and difficult. Codification would also hand the final word on contested political questions to unelected judges, as the American experience shows, where Supreme Court rulings on abortion and gun control settle what elected politicians cannot. The UK\'s real safeguards are political: a free press, opposition parties, an assertive House of Lords and, above all, elections. Parliament, not a court, should remain sovereign, and no Parliament should be able to bind its successors."\n\nAnalyse, evaluate and compare the arguments in the above extracts regarding whether the UK should adopt a codified constitution.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — students should analyse and compare the arguments in both extracts and test them against their own knowledge, for example:\n• CLARITY VERSUS FLEXIBILITY: Extract 1 claims uncodified rules are unclear, citing the prorogation dispute that ended in Miller II (2019). Extract 2 claims the same informality let Parliament create devolution, the HRA and the Supreme Court (CRA 2005) by ordinary statute, and undo the Fixed-term Parliaments Act 2011 through the Dissolution and Calling of Parliament Act 2022. The strongest answers see that flexibility and insecurity are the same property viewed from opposite sides.\n• RIGHTS: Extract 1 says the HRA is repealable by simple majority, which is legally correct; s.4 declarations of incompatibility do not bind Parliament. Extract 2 relies on political safeguards. Students may test both with the Safety of Rwanda (Asylum and Immigration) Act 2024, which disapplied parts of the HRA for its own purposes, showing how far an ordinary majority can go.\n• DEVOLUTION: Extract 1 uses Miller I (2017) — Sewel is a political convention the courts will not enforce, despite its statutory recognition in the Scotland Act 2016 — and the UK Internal Market Act 2020, passed over the refusal of consent by Holyrood and the Senedd. Extract 2 treats devolution as proof of adaptability. Evaluate which reading the evidence supports better.\n• JUDICIAL POWER: Extract 2 warns that codification empowers unelected judges, using the US (synoptic link to Paper 2: Dobbs v Jackson 2022 and gun-rights rulings under the Second Amendment). Extract 1 treats judges as neutral guardians. Students may note that UK courts already became more assertive under the uncodified system (Miller I and II), which cuts against both extracts in different ways.\n• EXECUTIVE DOMINANCE: Extract 1 echoes Hailsham's "elective dictatorship" — landslide majorities such as 1997 and 2024 leave few legal limits. Extract 2 answers with elections, the press, opposition and the Lords; evaluate how far these are effective between elections, given that the Lords is bound by the Parliament Acts and the Salisbury Convention.\n• SOVEREIGNTY: Extract 2 rests on Dicey's principle that no Parliament binds its successors. Codification with entrenchment would require abandoning that principle, so it is a transformation rather than a reform — a point Extract 1 does not address, alongside the unsolved question of who would draft the document and how it would be legitimised.\n• STATUS: Extract 1 is from a legal academic, whose purpose is to persuade from a rule-of-law perspective and who may prize legal certainty over political accountability. Extract 2 is from a former Conservative minister writing a newspaper column: the party has traditionally defended the historic constitution, and a former minister has experience of — and possibly an interest in — executive flexibility.\n\nJudgement: a supported conclusion may find Extract 1 more convincing on the diagnosis, since its examples (prorogation, Sewel, UKIMA) are accurate and recent, while finding Extract 2 stronger on the costs of the cure (judicial power, rigidity, the sovereignty problem). Any conclusion is creditworthy if it follows from comparison of the extracts' arguments.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and comparing the arguments in the extracts and weighing which are more convincing; evaluate their status (author, type of publication, purpose, date) where relevant. Both extracts were written for practice, so status evaluation should focus on the type of author and purpose stated in each label. Own knowledge should be used to test the extracts' claims, not to replace discussion of them. Answers that do not focus on the arguments in the extracts cannot go above Level 2.` },

      { q: '"Constitutional reform since 1997 has been effective." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `EFFECTIVE:\n• Devolution genuinely dispersed power, produced real policy divergence (tuition fees, prescription charges, minimum alcohol pricing) and responded to national demand; further powers followed in 2012, 2016 and 2017.\n• The HRA made rights litigation practicable domestically; Belmarsh shows government amending law in response to a declaration of incompatibility.\n• The CRA 2005 sharpened the separation of powers, removed the Law Lords from the legislature and ended patronage appointment of judges.\n• The Lords is more legitimate and markedly more assertive since 1999; government defeats there are now routine.\n• FOI 2000 improved transparency; the expenses scandal was exposed partly through it.\n\nNOT EFFECTIVE:\n• Piecemeal and incoherent — no overall design; anomalies such as the West Lothian question left unresolved, and EVEL introduced in 2015 and abandoned in 2021.\n• Lords composition remains wholly unelected; the 2012 reform bill collapsed.\n• The Westminster electoral system was untouched, and the 2011 AV referendum closed the question for a generation.\n• Nothing was entrenched — the FTPA was passed in 2011 and repealed in 2022; the HRA is repeatedly threatened.\n• Devolution destabilised rather than settled the union; Brexit exposed Sewel's weakness and produced the UK Internal Market Act over withheld consent.\n• Executive power was barely touched — the prerogative survives and delegated legislation has expanded.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest answers distinguish reform that was effective in its OWN terms from reform that produced a coherent settlement, and reach a conclusion on which standard should apply.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },

      { q: '"Parliamentary sovereignty no longer exists in the UK." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `NO LONGER EXISTS / seriously eroded:\n• EU membership — for 47 years UK courts disapplied Acts of Parliament (Factortame); the ECJ held final interpretive authority in areas of competence.\n• Devolution — powers so politically entrenched that reclaiming them is politically near-unthinkable (though Westminster overrode withheld devolved consent with the Internal Market Act 2020); the Scotland Act 2016 declares the Scottish Parliament permanent.\n• The HRA — s.3 has produced readings straining the words Parliament enacted; declarations of incompatibility are almost always acted upon.\n• Referendums — the 2016 result was legally advisory but politically binding, overriding a parliamentary majority that favoured Remain.\n• Judicial activism — Miller I and II placed limits on prerogative power the courts themselves defined.\n\nSTILL EXISTS:\n• Parliament repealed the ECA 1972, demonstrating the power to reclaim delegated authority was real.\n• Miller I held the Sewel Convention judicially unenforceable despite statutory recognition — devolved consent has no legal force.\n• Westminster legislated over withheld consent through the UK Internal Market Act 2020.\n• Courts cannot strike down statute; s.4 declarations leave legislation valid and in force.\n• Parliament reversed judicial expansion through the Judicial Review and Courts Act 2022, and passed then repealed the FTPA.\n\nGuidance for L5: must distinguish LEGAL sovereignty (intact) from POLITICAL sovereignty (constrained), and should engage the argument that a sovereignty exercisable only by leaving an entire legal order is of limited practical value. Asserting one side without that distinction caps at L3.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },

      { q: '"The UK constitution effectively limits government power." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `DOES LIMIT:\n• Rule of law and an independent judiciary — Miller I and II both constrained the executive; judicial review has expanded substantially since 1997.\n• The HRA provides enforceable rights against public authorities; Belmarsh forced a change in the law.\n• Devolution removed whole policy areas from Westminster's practical control.\n• Parliament can defeat governments — May's 230-vote defeat in 2019 was the largest in modern history; the 2013 Syria vote blocked military action.\n• The Lords delays and amends, and is defeated-government territory routinely since 1999.\n• Elections and a free press provide ultimate accountability.\n\nDOES NOT LIMIT:\n• Fusion of powers plus a Commons majority — Hailsham's "elective dictatorship"; the whips, the payroll vote and control of the timetable.\n• No entrenchment — any limit can be legislated away, as the Judicial Review and Courts Act 2022 showed.\n• Courts cannot strike down statute.\n• The royal prerogative allows deployment, treaties and (since 2022) election timing without prior approval.\n• Delegated legislation has expanded enormously, with minimal scrutiny.\n• FPTP manufactures majorities from roughly 33–44% of the vote (Labour 2024: 411 seats on 33.7%).\n\nGuidance for L5: the decisive analytical move is that the answer DEPENDS ON MAJORITY SIZE — the constitution limited May's government effectively and Blair's after 1997 barely at all. An answer that recognises the limits are political rather than constitutional, and therefore variable, reaches L5.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },
    ]
  },

  '1.2': {
    name: 'Parliament',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three functions of Parliament.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Legislation — debating, amending and passing statute through the stages. Analysis: weak on initiation, since almost all bills are government bills and the executive controls the timetable.\n• Scrutiny — select committees, questions, urgent questions, debates. Analysis: strongest through select committees since the Wright reforms made chairs elected; weakest where the government has a large majority.\n• Representation — constituency casework and representing national opinion. Analysis: strong on the constituency link, weak descriptively and on translating votes into seats.\n• Legitimation — giving consent to taxation and, by convention, military action. Analysis: largely formal, but the 2013 Syria vote showed it can bite.\n• Recruitment — providing the ministerial pool. Analysis: creates the payroll vote, which itself weakens scrutiny.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires evaluation of how WELL each function is performed, not just description of it.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which the House of Commons scrutinises the executive.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Select committees — take evidence from ministers and officials, publish reports requiring a government response within 60 days. Analysis: the Wright reforms of 2010 made chairs elected by secret ballot, substantially increasing independence from the whips; but committees cannot compel action and recommendations are frequently rejected.\n• Questions — PMQs, departmental questions, urgent questions. Analysis: the Speaker's greater willingness to grant UQs since 2009 genuinely strengthened scrutiny by forcing ministers to the despatch box at short notice; PMQs is widely criticised as theatrical.\n• Debates — opposition days, Backbench Business Committee days, emergency debates. Analysis: opposition day motions are not binding and governments have abstained and then ignored them; the BBBC did take agenda control from the executive.\n• Confidence and the threat of rebellion. Analysis: the ultimate sanction, rarely used (last successful vote 1979), but the credible threat forces pre-emptive concessions that never appear as defeats.\n\nGuidance: credit accurate examples. Top-band answers note effectiveness varies with majority size.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three powers of the House of Lords.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Delaying power — under the Parliament Acts 1911 and 1949 it may delay a non-money public bill for one year, a money bill for one month. Analysis: the threat of delay late in a session is real leverage, because a government short of time may concede rather than lose a bill.\n• Amendment and "ping-pong" — governments are defeated routinely in the Lords and often accept amendments or offer compromises. Analysis: assertiveness increased markedly after the 1999 Act because no party holds a majority and legitimacy improved.\n• Scrutiny and expertise — former ministers, judges, scientists and clinicians, free of constituency and re-election pressure. Analysis: produces more forensic and less partisan committee work than the Commons.\n• Constraints worth crediting: the Salisbury Convention; inability to initiate money bills; unelected status limiting how far it can press.\n\nGuidance: an answer that only describes powers without analysing their limits caps at L2.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three factors that affect the effectiveness of parliamentary scrutiny.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Size of the government's majority — the dominant variable. Large majority means whipped votes make defeat unlikely (Blair 1997, Johnson 2019); small or absent majority hands veto power to small groups (May after 2017, record Commons defeats).\n• The whip system and the payroll vote — MPs depend on the party for selection, the label and promotion, so rebellion is costly; around a hundred ministers and PPSs are bound by collective responsibility, drawn from exactly the people best placed to scrutinise.\n• Control of the parliamentary timetable — the government determines what is debated and for how long; the Backbench Business Committee recovered only a limited number of days.\n• Committee independence — the Wright reforms strengthened it; resourcing remains far below congressional levels.\n• Party unity and the political context — a divided governing party is scrutinised far more effectively by its own backbenchers.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires explicit causal analysis, not a list of factors.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three arguments for reform of the House of Lords.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Democratic legitimacy — no member is elected, yet the chamber amends and delays legislation passed by elected representatives. Analysis: the central argument, but it cuts both ways, since it is precisely unelected status that produces the restraint of the Salisbury Convention.\n• Size and patronage — a membership that has passed 800 with no cap, one of the largest chambers in the world, with appointments effectively in the Prime Minister's gift and recurring controversy over donors. Analysis: a statutory appointments commission and a size cap would address this without the difficulties of election.\n• Composition — unrepresentative in age, region and background; 26 Church of England bishops sit by right, hard to justify in a diverse and largely secular society.\n• The hereditary peers — the 92 left by 1999 sat until the House of Lords (Hereditary Peers) Act 2026 removed them, showing how slowly reform has come.\n\nGuidance: this is one-sided by design; do not require counter-argument, but require analysis.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which backbench MPs can influence government.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Rebellion and the threat of it — rebellions have grown more frequent since the 1990s; the credible threat forces pre-emptive concessions. Analysis: effectiveness depends entirely on majority size; May's government after 2017 was repeatedly defeated.\n• Select committees — since the Wright reforms, chairs are elected by the whole House and members by their parties' backbenchers, giving real independence. Reports generate publicity and occasionally force resignations or policy reversals.\n• The Backbench Business Committee (2010) — gives backbenchers control of the agenda for a set number of days, taking topic selection from the executive.\n• Private members' bills — rarely successful without government support, but have delivered significant social legislation.\n• Urgent questions and early day motions; party leadership elections, in which backbenchers can remove a Prime Minister.\n\nGuidance: credit the observation that backbench power rose considerably after 2010.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'EXTRACTS (written for practice):\n\nExtract 1 — written for this practice question in the style of a blog post by a campaign group that wants an elected second chamber\n"The House of Lords is an embarrassment to a modern democracy. It is one of the largest legislative chambers in the world, and not one of its members is elected by the public. Prime ministers can appoint as many peers as they wish, and resignation honours lists have repeatedly been used to reward party donors, advisers and loyalists. Twenty-six Church of England bishops still sit by right. Removing the last hereditary peers was welcome, but it left the patronage system untouched. A chamber that revises the laws of the land should answer to the people who must obey them. The Lords should be replaced by a smaller, elected senate of the nations and regions, with the democratic authority to scrutinise government properly."\n\nExtract 2 — written for this practice question in the style of a newspaper article by a long-serving crossbench peer who opposes an elected chamber\n"Critics call the Lords undemocratic, but that is precisely its value. Because peers do not face re-election, they can scrutinise legislation line by line without an eye on the next campaign, and the chamber includes former judges, scientists, doctors, diplomats and military leaders who would never stand for election. No party has held a majority in the Lords since 1999, so governments are regularly defeated there and must justify their bills. Yet the Salisbury Convention and the Parliament Acts ensure that the elected Commons always has the final word. An elected second chamber would claim its own mandate, challenge the Commons and risk gridlock of the kind seen in Washington. Sensible reforms — a smaller House, a retirement age, stricter appointments — would strengthen an institution that works."\n\nAnalyse, evaluate and compare the arguments in the above extracts regarding reform of the House of Lords.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — students should analyse and compare the arguments in both extracts and test them against their own knowledge, for example:\n• LEGITIMACY VERSUS EXPERTISE: Extract 1 argues that an unelected chamber lacks democratic authority; Extract 2 argues that the absence of elections is what allows independent, expert scrutiny (crossbenchers, former professionals). Evaluate whether expertise requires appointment — an elected chamber with long, single, non-renewable terms could combine both, as the abandoned House of Lords Reform Bill 2012 proposed.\n• PATRONAGE: Extract 1 attacks the unlimited prime ministerial power of appointment and honours lists; the House of Lords Appointments Commission vets propriety but cannot cap numbers or block party-political nominations on grounds of merit. Extract 2 concedes the problem implicitly by proposing "stricter appointments". Students may note the controversy over recent resignation honours lists.\n• EFFECTIVENESS OF SCRUTINY: Extract 2's claim that governments are regularly defeated is accurate — no party majority since the House of Lords Act 1999, and ping-pong often extracts concessions. Evaluate against Extract 1: the Lords' greater assertiveness after 1999 came from perceived legitimacy, which suggests an elected chamber might scrutinise more, not less.\n• RELATIONSHIP WITH THE COMMONS: Extract 2 relies on the Parliament Acts 1911 and 1949 (used for the Hunting Act 2004) and the Salisbury Convention. Extract 1 does not address how an elected senate would relate to the Commons. Synoptic link to Paper 2: the US Senate's equal legislative power and electoral mandate produce the gridlock Extract 2 fears.\n• NATIONS AND REGIONS: Extract 1's "senate of the nations and regions" echoes the Brown Commission on the UK's Future (2022) and Labour's 2024 manifesto commitment to a more representative alternative second chamber; synoptic link to devolution and the unresolved English question.\n• INCREMENTAL REFORM: Extract 2 favours piecemeal change. Recent practice supports its feasibility — the House of Lords (Hereditary Peers) Act 2026 removed the remaining hereditary peers, and Labour's 2024 manifesto also proposed a retirement age of 80 — but Extract 1 argues that such steps leave appointment by patronage intact.\n• STATUS: Extract 1 is from a campaign group whose purpose is to persuade, so it selects the most damaging criticisms (size, bishops, honours) and ignores the Lords' revising record. Extract 2 is by a serving peer — expert on how the chamber works, but with a personal and institutional interest in its survival; being a crossbencher, however, the author cannot be accused of party motives.\n\nJudgement: a supported conclusion might accept Extract 2's case that the Lords scrutinises effectively while finding Extract 1 more convincing that its method of composition is indefensible in principle; many strong answers conclude that the real debate is between Extract 2's incremental reform and Extract 1's replacement, with outright abolition supported by neither extract.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and comparing the arguments in the extracts and weighing which are more convincing; evaluate their status (author, type of publication, purpose, date) where relevant. Both extracts were written for practice, so status evaluation should focus on the type of author and purpose stated in each label. Own knowledge should be used to test the extracts' claims, not to replace discussion of them. Answers that do not focus on the arguments in the extracts cannot go above Level 2.` },

      { q: '"Parliament is effective in holding the executive to account." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `EFFECTIVE:\n• Select committees since the Wright reforms — elected chairs, genuine independence, evidence sessions with compelled attendance, 60-day response requirement; have forced resignations and policy reversals.\n• Urgent questions have risen sharply since 2009, forcing ministers to the House at short notice.\n• Governments ARE defeated — the 2013 Syria vote blocked military action; May's 2019 defeat was the largest in modern history.\n• The Lords defeats governments routinely and extracts concessions.\n• The credible threat of rebellion forces concessions that never show as defeats.\n• Backbenchers can remove a Prime Minister — Thatcher, May, Johnson, Truss.\n\nNOT EFFECTIVE:\n• Fusion of powers plus a majority — the whips, the payroll vote, control of the timetable.\n• PMQs is theatrical rather than forensic; opposition day motions are not binding.\n• Select committees cannot compel action; recommendations are routinely rejected.\n• Delegated legislation escapes meaningful scrutiny — thousands of SIs annually.\n• The Lords can only delay; the Salisbury Convention restrains it further.\n• Short money and research resources are far below congressional levels.\n\nGuidance for L5: the decisive point is that effectiveness is CONTINGENT on majority size and party unity, not a fixed property of the institution. Comparing 1997–2001 with 2017–2019 is the strongest available evidence and should feature in a top-band answer.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },

      { q: '"The House of Commons is more powerful than the House of Lords." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `COMMONS MORE POWERFUL:\n• Democratic legitimacy — elected, and the government is drawn from and accountable to it.\n• The Parliament Acts 1911 and 1949 limit the Lords to one year's delay (one month for money bills).\n• Exclusive control of finance — the Lords cannot initiate or block money bills.\n• Confidence — only the Commons can bring down a government.\n• The Salisbury Convention prevents the Lords blocking manifesto commitments.\n• The Lords cannot ultimately prevent a determined Commons majority.\n\nLORDS MORE POWERFUL THAN OFTEN ASSUMED:\n• Since 1999 no party holds a Lords majority, and government defeats there are routine.\n• Delay late in a session is real leverage — governments concede rather than lose a bill.\n• Ping-pong extracts substantive amendments.\n• Greater expertise and independence from whips and constituencies produce more forensic scrutiny.\n• Commons power is largely nominal when the executive controls it through the whips and payroll vote — the Commons is powerful as an institution but not against the government.\n\nGuidance for L5: the sophisticated move is to distinguish the power of the CHAMBER from the power of its MEMBERS. Formally the Commons is far more powerful; in practice a government controls the Commons and does not control the Lords, so the Lords may be the more effective check.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },

      { q: '"The UK has an elective dictatorship." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `SUPPORTS THE THESIS (Hailsham, 1976):\n• Fusion of powers — a government with a Commons majority controls both branches.\n• The whips, the payroll vote (~100 ministers and PPSs) and control of the timetable.\n• Parliamentary sovereignty means no codified constitution, no entrenched rights and no court able to strike down legislation.\n• FPTP manufactures majorities from roughly 33–44% of the vote (Labour 2024: 411 seats on 33.7%).\n• The royal prerogative allows deployment, treaties and (since 2022) election timing without prior approval.\n• Delegated legislation has expanded enormously with minimal scrutiny.\n\nCHALLENGES THE THESIS:\n• It describes large-majority government, not a permanent constitutional condition — May after 2017 suffered historic defeats and could not pass her central policy.\n• Backbench rebellion has become far more frequent since the 1990s; three of the last five PMs (May, Johnson and Truss) were forced out by their own party.\n• The Lords is markedly more assertive since 1999.\n• Devolution removed whole policy areas from Westminster's practical control.\n• The HRA and expanded judicial review constrain the executive; Miller I and II are direct evidence.\n• Wright reforms strengthened select committees and backbench agenda control.\n\nGuidance for L5: must recognise that the thesis is CONDITIONAL. The strongest conclusion is that the UK has the constitutional architecture for elective dictatorship but that whether it operates as one depends on political circumstances the constitution does not determine.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },
    ]
  },

  '1.3': {
    name: 'Prime Minister and Cabinet',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three powers of the Prime Minister.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Patronage — appointing, promoting, reshuffling and dismissing ministers, and recommending peerages. Analysis: the central instrument of control, but constrained by the need to balance factions and by the fact that dangerous figures are often safer inside the government than outside it.\n• Chairing cabinet — setting the agenda, deciding what is discussed and for how long, and summing up the outcome. Analysis: allows a PM to shape decisions without ever holding a vote.\n• Party leadership — commanding the whips and the parliamentary party, controlling the legislative programme. Analysis: entirely conditional, since both main parties can remove a leader at any time.\n• Royal prerogative — deployment, treaties, senior appointments, and since the 2022 FTPA repeal, requesting an early election. Analysis: narrowed by convention (Commons consultation since 2003) and by the courts (Miller I and II).\n• National leadership and agenda-setting through the media.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires the LIMIT on each power as well as the power itself.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three factors that determine the power of a Prime Minister.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Size of the Commons majority — the dominant variable. Blair after 1997 and Johnson after 2019 could ignore rebels; May after 2017 suffered the largest defeat in modern history and could not pass her central policy.\n• Party unity and personal authority — a PM governs by consent of their parliamentary party. Thatcher in 1990 and Johnson in 2022 retained full formal powers while losing office, because support collapsed.\n• Standing in the polls and a reputation for winning elections — legislators support a popular leader and distance themselves from an electoral liability.\n• The calibre and ambition of cabinet colleagues — Blair–Brown shows Treasury control of departmental allocations creating a rival centre of power.\n• Events and the economy — the Falklands enhanced Thatcher's authority; the 1992 ERM crisis destroyed Major's and the 2022 mini-budget destroyed Truss's in under seven weeks.\n• Time in office — authority typically erodes over a long premiership.\n\nGuidance: top-band answers note the FORMAL powers are constant; only the political context varies.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which the cabinet can constrain the Prime Minister.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Withdrawal of collective support — cabinet consent sustains a premiership. Thatcher resigned in 1990 after canvassing her cabinet individually; Johnson resigned in 2022 after more than fifty government resignations in two days.\n• Senior rivals with independent power bases — Blair–Brown is the standard example, where Treasury control over spending allocations across every department gave Brown effective leverage over domestic policy.\n• Collective responsibility as a two-way bargain — it binds ministers to support decisions, but gives them a legitimate claim to be consulted first. Ministers who feel bypassed leak, brief against colleagues or threaten resignation.\n• Factional representation — in a divided party, cabinet members representing particular wings hold effective veto power, as May's cabinet did repeatedly over Brexit policy.\n\nGuidance: credit the qualification that these constraints are POLITICAL rather than constitutional, and bite hardest when a PM is already weakened.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three arguments that the office of Prime Minister has become presidential.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Personalised mandates — campaigns fought around leaders, with televised debates since 2010 encouraging voters to treat the choice as one between individuals rather than parties.\n• Bilateralism and "sofa government" — policy settled with the relevant minister or in small ad hoc groups, with cabinet presented with decisions to ratify. The Butler Review criticised exactly this before the Iraq war.\n• Growth of the centre — expansion of the PM's Office, the Cabinet Office and special advisers gives Number 10 an independent policy and communications capacity resembling a presidential executive office.\n• Spatial leadership — PMs increasingly present themselves as standing above their own party.\n• Dominance of foreign and security policy, exercised largely through the prerogative.\n\nGuidance: one-sided by design. Do not require counter-argument, but the strongest answers still note that tenure remains conditional.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three reasons why individual ministerial responsibility has weakened.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• The policy/operational distinction — since the Next Steps executive agencies from 1988, ministers accept responsibility for policy but attribute operational failures to agency chief executives or officials. The response to the 1995 prison escapes rested on exactly this.\n• Departmental scale — it is widely accepted a minister cannot know of every failing in a very large department, making resignation look disproportionate rather than principled.\n• Prime ministerial protection — survival now depends on whether the PM will spend political capital defending a minister, so outcomes track factional strength rather than the gravity of the failure.\n• Media and party dynamics — resignations are driven by whether a story persists and whether backbenchers withdraw support, not by a settled constitutional standard.\n\nGuidance: credit the qualification that the convention retains real force over PERSONAL CONDUCT and over misleading Parliament.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three sources of prime ministerial support and advice.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• The Prime Minister's Office — personal staff covering policy, press, communications and political liaison; has grown substantially since the 1990s. Analysis: gives Number 10 independent capacity it did not have in 1945, which is central to the presidentialisation argument.\n• The Cabinet Office — coordinates policy across departments, services cabinet and its committees, headed by the Cabinet Secretary. Analysis: an instrument of central control over departmental activity.\n• Special advisers — politically appointed, exempt from civil service impartiality, loyal personally to the appointing minister. Analysis: offer committed advice and rapid media handling an impartial civil service cannot; criticised for sidelining the permanent civil service and for accountability gaps.\n• Cabinet committees — the PM decides which exist, what they cover and who sits on them, an under-appreciated instrument of control.\n\nGuidance: these are RESOURCES, not powers. An answer treating them as powers caps at L2.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'EXTRACTS (written for practice):\n\nExtract 1 — written for this practice question in the style of a political commentary by a journalist who argues that prime ministers have become presidential\n"Modern prime ministers govern less like first among equals and more like presidents. General elections are fought as contests between leaders, and parties sell their manifestos on the leader\'s personal brand. Power has drained from the Cabinet table to Number 10, where special advisers, the Policy Unit and a growing central staff serve the prime minister rather than colleagues. Blair\'s "sofa government" took key decisions in small informal groups, as the Chilcot Inquiry later criticised, and much of the Covid response was directed by a small circle of ministers and advisers around Johnson. Prime ministers dominate foreign policy, speak for the nation in a crisis and, through patronage, control the career of every minister. The Cabinet has become a sounding board, not a decision-making body."\n\nExtract 2 — written for this practice question in the style of an article by a political historian who argues that prime ministers remain constrained\n"The "presidential" label misunderstands how fragile prime ministerial power is. A US president serves a fixed term and cannot be removed by his party; a British prime minister survives only while keeping the confidence of the parliamentary party. Thatcher was forced out by her own MPs and Cabinet in 1990 despite three election victories. Johnson resigned in July 2022 after dozens of ministers quit in protest, and Truss lasted just 49 days after her mini-budget alarmed the markets and her own MPs. May\'s Brexit deal was defeated three times in the Commons while ministers openly defied her. Prime ministers are powerful when they are popular and have a large majority; without those conditions, they are at the mercy of their party."\n\nAnalyse, evaluate and compare the arguments in the above extracts regarding whether the UK Prime Minister has become presidential.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — students should analyse and compare the arguments in both extracts and test them against their own knowledge, for example:\n• PERSONALISATION: Extract 1 argues elections are leader contests (Foley's "spatial leadership"; televised leaders' debates since 2010). Extract 2 does not deny this but shows personal popularity is conditional — the same personal focus that elevates a leader makes the party quick to remove an unpopular one.\n• CENTRALISATION AND THE CABINET: Extract 1 cites the growth of Number 10, sofa government and the Chilcot Report (2016), which criticised the lack of formal Cabinet discussion before the Iraq war. Extract 2 counters that collective politics reasserts itself when a PM is weak — ministerial resignations brought down Johnson and Thatcher. Evaluate: is the Cabinet marginal in routine policy but decisive at moments of crisis?\n• REMOVABILITY: Extract 2's strongest point. Thatcher (1990), May (2019), Johnson (2022) and Truss (2022) were all removed or forced out between elections by their own party, which a US president cannot be. Students may add that the Conservative confidence-vote rules made this easier.\n• CONTINGENCY: Extract 2 argues power depends on majority and popularity — Blair (1997) and Johnson (2019) with large majorities versus May after losing her majority in 2017. This suggests "presidential" describes some prime ministers at some times rather than the office itself, weakening Extract 1's general claim.\n• SYNOPTIC COMPARISON WITH THE USA: Extract 2 uses the US comparison against the thesis — fixed term and separate mandate. Strong answers note the irony that a US president, facing a separately elected Congress and the Supreme Court, may have less domestic legislative control than a UK PM with a majority (Neustadt's "power to persuade"), so "presidential" may understate UK prime ministerial power in some respects.\n• FOREIGN POLICY AND PATRONAGE: Extract 1 is on strong ground here — prerogative powers over deployment and appointments sit with the PM — though the convention of Commons votes on military action since Iraq (2003) and the 2013 Syria defeat qualify it.\n• STATUS: Extract 1 is journalism, which tends to personalise politics and emphasise leaders because that is how news is reported. Extract 2 is by a historian, taking a longer view and focusing on fall-from-power episodes; neither is written by a participant, but each selects evidence that fits its thesis — Extract 1 strong leaders at their peak, Extract 2 leaders at their weakest.\n\nJudgement: a supported conclusion may accept Extract 1's description of how prime ministers govern while preferring Extract 2's account of why their power is fragile, concluding that the office is presidential in style but parliamentary in its accountability. Credit any view supported by comparison of the extracts.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and comparing the arguments in the extracts and weighing which are more convincing; evaluate their status (author, type of publication, purpose, date) where relevant. Both extracts were written for practice, so status evaluation should focus on the type of author and purpose stated in each label. Own knowledge should be used to test the extracts' claims, not to replace discussion of them. Answers that do not focus on the arguments in the extracts cannot go above Level 2.` },

      { q: '"The cabinet is no longer significant in UK government." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `NO LONGER SIGNIFICANT:\n• Decisions increasingly settled bilaterally or in small ad hoc groups, with cabinet ratifying — "sofa government"; the Butler Review's criticism.\n• Full cabinet meetings have become shorter and less deliberative.\n• Cabinet rarely votes; the PM sums up.\n• The rise of special advisers and EXOP-style central capacity has shifted advice away from departmental ministers.\n• Collective responsibility binds ministers to decisions they may not have shaped.\n\nSTILL SIGNIFICANT:\n• Cabinet's collective consent legitimises policy, and its withdrawal is fatal — Thatcher 1990, Johnson 2022.\n• Senior figures with independent power bases constrain a PM — Blair–Brown.\n• PMs must appoint rivals because they are more dangerous outside the government; the constraint operates before any appointment.\n• In a divided party or with a small majority, factional cabinet members hold veto power — May's cabinet repeatedly forced changes to Brexit policy.\n• Cabinet committees do the substantive policy work and are staffed by cabinet ministers.\n\nGuidance for L5: must recognise the answer VARIES with political circumstance — cabinet was largely a ratifying body under Blair after 1997 and a genuine constraint under May after 2017. The formal position is unchanged in both cases.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },

      { q: '"The power of the Prime Minister is limited." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `LIMITED:\n• The party — both main parties can remove a leader at any time; four of the last five PMs to leave office (Cameron, May, Johnson and Truss) did so without losing an election, and three of them were forced out by their own party. The most fundamental limit, with no presidential parallel.\n• Parliament — legislation requires Commons majorities; the Lords delays and amends. May's 230-vote defeat; the 2013 Syria vote.\n• Cabinet — collective consent sustains a premiership; senior rivals constrain.\n• The judiciary — Miller I and II narrowed prerogative power; judicial review has expanded since 1997.\n• Devolution removed whole policy areas from practical control.\n• Events, the economy and the media — Truss's authority destroyed in under seven weeks.\n\nNOT LIMITED:\n• Fusion of powers — a PM with a secure majority controls the executive AND the legislative outcome, faces no veto, no second chamber able to block indefinitely, and no court able to strike down statute.\n• Patronage, control of the cabinet agenda and of cabinet committees.\n• Prerogative powers over deployment, treaties, appointments and election timing.\n• No codified constitution, no entrenched rights, no federal division of powers.\n• The whips and the payroll vote.\n\nGuidance for L5: the decisive point is that the limits are POLITICAL rather than constitutional, and therefore variable. The strongest conclusion explains why the same office produced Blair's dominance and Truss's collapse.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },

      { q: '"Collective ministerial responsibility remains an effective convention." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `REMAINS EFFECTIVE:\n• It still binds — ministers who cannot support a decision are expected to resign, and many do.\n• It gives the government a single public position and disciplines dissent.\n• The payroll vote it creates delivers around a hundred guaranteed government votes.\n• Its withdrawal remains fatal to a premiership, which shows the convention still carries real weight — fifty-plus resignations ended Johnson's premiership in 2022.\n• Cabinet confidentiality is still formally expected and breaches are treated as serious.\n\nWEAKENED:\n• Formal suspensions — the 1975 and 2016 EU referendums, when ministers campaigned on both sides.\n• Routine leaking and briefing against colleagues has eroded the unity the convention is meant to project.\n• Ministers have publicly dissented without resigning, and PMs with weak authority cannot enforce it.\n• "Sofa government" undermines the bargain — ministers bound by decisions they were not consulted on have less reason to feel bound.\n• Enforcement depends entirely on the PM's political strength, so it is strongest when least needed.\n\nGuidance for L5: the sharpest analysis notes that the convention is a BARGAIN — support in exchange for consultation — and that the collapse of the second half explains the erosion of the first.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },
    ]
  },

  '1.4': {
    name: 'The Judiciary',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which judicial independence is maintained in the UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Security of tenure — senior judges hold office during good behaviour and can be removed only by the monarch following an address from both Houses, a procedure used only once, in 1830, against the Irish judge Sir Jonah Barrington. Analysis: removes the most direct instrument of pressure.\n• Guaranteed salaries — charged on the Consolidated Fund, so not subject to annual parliamentary approval or ministerial manipulation.\n• Independent appointment — the Judicial Appointments Commission, created by the CRA 2005, selects on merit through open competition, removing patronage appointment by a serving politician. Analysis: contrast with the openly political US confirmation process.\n• Separation from the legislature — the CRA 2005 removed the Law Lords from Parliament and stripped the Lord Chancellor of headship of the judiciary.\n• The sub judice rule and contempt of court restrain parliamentary and press pressure.\n\nGuidance: top-band answers distinguish FORMAL protections (robust) from the INFORMAL position (weaker — ministerial and press attacks after Miller I and II).\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three reasons why judicial review has become more significant since 1997.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• The Human Rights Act 1998 — created an entirely new set of grounds and allowed rights claims domestically rather than in Strasbourg, making litigation far cheaper and more practicable, and generating a substantial body of case law.\n• Devolution — created questions about the limits of devolved competence that only the courts could resolve.\n• Growth of executive and agency decision-making under broad delegated powers — simply created more decisions capable of challenge.\n• A more rights-conscious legal culture, with campaigning groups and firms willing to bring test cases.\n• The CRA 2005 gave the senior judiciary a clearer institutional identity separate from the legislature.\n\nAnalysis expected: the government response — restrictions on legal aid, tighter standing and time limits, and the Judicial Review and Courts Act 2022 — is itself evidence that review had become a genuine constraint, and evidence that Parliament retains the final word.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three limits on the power of the UK Supreme Court.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Parliamentary sovereignty — no court may override an Act of Parliament, and there is no higher constitutional law against which statute can be measured. The Court can only interpret, or declare incompatibility.\n• Declarations of incompatibility leave the statute valid and in force — only Parliament may amend it.\n• Parliament can reverse any ruling by ordinary statute — the Judicial Review and Courts Act 2022 reversed the Cart (2011) ruling and introduced suspended quashing orders.\n• The Court is reactive — it cannot initiate cases and can only decide disputes litigants bring.\n• It depends on the executive to give effect to its rulings.\n\nGuidance: the strongest analysis compares with the US Supreme Court, whose constitutional rulings can realistically only be reversed by the Court itself — which is why the "too powerful" charge is far harder to sustain in the UK.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three arguments that the UK Supreme Court has become too powerful.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Miller I and II placed the Court at the centre of the most contested political question of a generation. Analysis: the charge is that unelected judges settled matters belonging to elected politicians; Miller II in particular applied a standard with limited precedent.\n• The HRA s.3 interpretive duty has produced readings some regard as straining the words Parliament actually enacted.\n• Judicial review has expanded substantially in grounds and frequency, giving courts routine oversight of executive decisions and imposing caution on administration.\n• The senior judiciary is unrepresentative — disproportionately male, white, privately and Oxbridge educated — raising questions about whose assumptions inform interpretation (Griffith's critique).\n\nGuidance: one-sided by design, but a top-band answer may still note the rebuttals — the Court did not choose these cases, it ruled on executive power under existing principle, and Parliament reversed part of the expansion in 2022.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three landmark UK court cases and their significance.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Factortame (1991) — UK courts disapplied part of the Merchant Shipping Act 1988 for conflict with EU law. Significance: the first setting aside of primary legislation; basis removed with the ECA's repeal.\n• Miller I (2017) — prerogative could not be used to remove statutory rights; only Parliament could authorise Article 50. Also held Sewel judicially unenforceable. Significance: protected Parliament against the executive, so it strengthened rather than weakened sovereignty.\n• Miller II (2019) — prorogation unlawful as frustrating Parliament's constitutional functions. Significance: held a prerogative power justiciable that the government argued was purely political.\n• Belmarsh (2004) — declaration of incompatibility over indefinite detention; government amended the law. Significance: shows s.4's political force exceeding its legal force.\n\nGuidance: analysis must address CONSTITUTIONAL significance, not recount facts. L1–L2 for narrative accounts.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three factors that may threaten judicial neutrality.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Unrepresentative composition — the senior judiciary remains disproportionately male, white, privately educated and Oxbridge educated, reflecting the narrow recruitment base of the senior bar. Analysis: a homogeneous bench may share unexamined assumptions where interpretation involves discretion.\n• Griffith's critique — the argument that judges share an establishment outlook producing a consistent tilt on public order, property and state power, whatever their conscious intentions.\n• Political and media pressure — ministerial and press attacks on judges after Miller I and II, and government reluctance to defend them promptly.\n• The expansion of judicial review into politically contested territory makes rulings look political whether or not they are.\n\nCounter-arguments worth crediting: merit-based selection through the JAC; improving diversity at lower levels feeding upwards; and the fact that courts rule against government frequently, which is hard to square with systematic establishment bias.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'EXTRACTS (written for practice):\n\nExtract 1 — written for this practice question in the style of a think-tank paper by a lawyer who believes the courts have overreached\n"Since it opened in 2009, the Supreme Court has drifted into territory that belongs to elected politicians. In 2019 it declared the prorogation of Parliament unlawful, treating a matter long regarded as political as one for the courts. In 2023 it ruled the government\'s Rwanda asylum policy unlawful, overturning a flagship policy because it judged that asylum seekers sent to Rwanda faced a real risk of being returned to danger. Judicial review has expanded far beyond its origins, and the Human Rights Act invites judges to reinterpret statutes in ways Parliament never intended. When unelected judges settle political controversies, democratic accountability suffers. Parliament should restate the limits of judicial review and remind the courts that it, not they, is sovereign."\n\nExtract 2 — written for this practice question in the style of a legal-affairs column by a retired barrister who defends the courts\n"Complaints about "activist judges" obscure how limited the Supreme Court\'s power is. Unlike the US Supreme Court, it cannot strike down an Act of Parliament. Under the Human Rights Act it can only issue a declaration of incompatibility, leaving Parliament to decide what to do. In the prorogation case it did not rule on Brexit; it upheld Parliament\'s right to scrutinise the executive. After the Rwanda judgment, Parliament simply passed the Safety of Rwanda Act 2024 declaring Rwanda safe — proof that the final word remains with elected politicians. The court can only rule on cases brought before it, and its justices are chosen by an independent selection commission rather than by politicians. A court that enforces the law against ministers is doing its job, not seizing power."\n\nAnalyse, evaluate and compare the arguments in the above extracts regarding the power of the UK Supreme Court.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — students should analyse and compare the arguments in both extracts and test them against their own knowledge, for example:\n• MILLER II (2019): Extract 1 treats it as judicial entry into politics — the key step was holding prorogation justiciable. Extract 2 reads it as protecting parliamentary sovereignty against the executive. Evaluate: the Court applied a new test (prorogation unlawful if it frustrates Parliament's functions without reasonable justification), which supports Extract 1's claim of novelty, but the beneficiary was Parliament, which supports Extract 2.\n• RWANDA (AAA v Home Secretary, 2023) AND THE RESPONSE: Extract 1 sees policy being overturned; Extract 2 notes that the ruling rested on evidence of refoulement risk and that Parliament reversed its effect with the Safety of Rwanda (Asylum and Immigration) Act 2024. This is strong support for Extract 2's argument that legal sovereignty remains with Parliament.\n• HUMAN RIGHTS ACT: Extract 1 focuses on s.3 interpretation (e.g. Ghaidan v Godin-Mendoza, 2004), Extract 2 on s.4 declarations, which leave the statute in force (Belmarsh, 2004). Both are accurate; evaluate which is the more significant source of judicial power.\n• SYNOPTIC COMPARISON WITH THE USA: Extract 2 correctly states that the UK Supreme Court cannot strike down primary legislation, unlike the US Supreme Court (judicial review since Marbury v Madison, 1803; Dobbs v Jackson, 2022). This comparison is one of the strongest arguments against Extract 1.\n• POLITICALLY CONTENTIOUS CASES: students may add For Women Scotland v The Scottish Ministers (2025), in which the Court held that "sex" in the Equality Act 2010 means biological sex; R (Evans) v Attorney General (2015), overriding a ministerial veto on releasing Prince Charles's letters; and the 2022 ruling that Holyrood could not legislate for an independence referendum. These show the Court deciding contested questions, but through statutory interpretation that Parliament can change.\n• INDEPENDENCE AND LEGITIMACY: Extract 2 cites independent appointment under the Constitutional Reform Act 2005; Extract 1 objects that judges are unelected. Evaluate whether judicial power is a problem of legitimacy (Extract 1) or of accountability to law (Extract 2).\n• STATUS: Extract 1 is from a think tank that wants Parliament to curb judicial review, so it is advocacy with a policy goal. Extract 2 is by a retired barrister — legal expertise, but a professional background that may incline towards defending the courts. Both are persuasive rather than neutral.\n\nJudgement: a well-supported conclusion is likely to find Extract 2 more convincing on the legal position (no strike-down power; Parliament overrode the Rwanda ruling), while recognising that Extract 1 identifies a real growth in the Court's political significance since Miller II. Any conclusion supported by comparison of the extracts should be credited.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and comparing the arguments in the extracts and weighing which are more convincing; evaluate their status (author, type of publication, purpose, date) where relevant. Both extracts were written for practice, so status evaluation should focus on the type of author and purpose stated in each label. Own knowledge should be used to test the extracts' claims, not to replace discussion of them. Answers that do not focus on the arguments in the extracts cannot go above Level 2.` },

      { q: '"Judicial independence is well protected in the UK." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `WELL PROTECTED:\n• Security of tenure — removal only by address from both Houses; used only once, in 1830 (against the Irish judge Sir Jonah Barrington).\n• Salaries charged on the Consolidated Fund, beyond ministerial manipulation.\n• The JAC since 2005 removed patronage appointment by a serving politician — a sharp contrast with the US confirmation process.\n• The CRA 2005 removed the Law Lords from the legislature and reformed the Lord Chancellor.\n• The sub judice rule and contempt of court restrain parliamentary and press pressure.\n• Judges rule against government regularly, which is direct evidence of independence in practice.\n\nLESS WELL PROTECTED:\n• Ministerial and press attacks on judges after Miller I and II, with government slow to defend them.\n• The Lord Chancellor's duty to defend judicial independence has been inconsistently discharged; the post no longer requires legal qualification.\n• Parliament can legislate to curb judicial power, as in 2022 — independence exists at Parliament's pleasure.\n• Legal aid restrictions limit access, affecting the courts' practical reach.\n• Unrepresentative composition raises neutrality rather than independence concerns, but the two are often conflated.\n\nGuidance for L5: must distinguish FORMAL protections (robust, and largely strengthened since 2005) from the INFORMAL conventions of restraint (eroding). The best conclusions note that independence is legally secure but politically more exposed than a decade ago.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },

      { q: '"The Human Rights Act 1998 has been effective in protecting rights in the UK." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `EFFECTIVE:\n• Incorporation made rights litigation practicable domestically rather than requiring a journey to Strasbourg.\n• s.3 imposes a strong interpretive duty, substantially shaping statutory meaning.\n• s.6 makes it unlawful for public authorities to act incompatibly — this is where the Act actually bites, and it reaches most government activity.\n• s.4 declarations of incompatibility have almost always produced legislative amendment — Belmarsh is the standard example.\n• It generated a substantial body of rights case law and a more rights-conscious legal culture.\n\nNOT EFFECTIVE:\n• Not entrenched — repealable by simple Commons majority, and repeatedly threatened with replacement by a British Bill of Rights.\n• Declarations leave the offending statute valid and in force; Parliament may simply decline to act.\n• Rights have been curtailed by counter-terrorism legislation permitting extended detention and expanded surveillance, and by restrictions on protest.\n• Legal aid restrictions limit practical access to the remedies it provides.\n• Critics argue it transferred too much interpretive power to unelected judges; others that it did not transfer enough.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest analysis notes that the s.4 mechanism was a DELIBERATE compromise preserving parliamentary sovereignty, so judging the Act by whether it entrenches rights misunderstands what it was designed to do. Should also weigh theoretical fragility against demonstrated practical stability.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },

      { q: '"The UK judiciary has become too political." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `TOO POLITICAL:\n• Miller I and II ruled on the most contested political question of a generation; Miller II held a prerogative power justiciable that had long been assumed political.\n• Expanded judicial review gives courts routine oversight of politically contested administrative decisions.\n• HRA s.3 requires courts to make choices about statutory meaning that are unavoidably evaluative.\n• Pressure groups now use litigation as a deliberate political strategy, drawing courts into policy disputes.\n• Griffith's argument that judges share an establishment outlook.\n\nNOT TOO POLITICAL:\n• The Court does not select its cases and cannot initiate them.\n• In Miller I and II it ruled on the LIMITS OF EXECUTIVE POWER under existing constitutional principle, not on the merits of Brexit — it constrained the government on Parliament's behalf.\n• Appointment is by an independent commission on merit, not by politicians — unlike the USA, nobody claims UK judges were appointed to produce particular outcomes.\n• Judges are barred from political activity and bound by the sub judice rule.\n• Courts cannot strike down statute, so their rulings are always reversible by Parliament.\n\nGuidance for L5: the comparative dimension is decisive. The charge has far more force in the USA, where appointments are openly partisan and precedent has been reversed following changes in composition. In the UK the criticism concerns rulings on executive power, not the process that produced the judges. An answer making this distinction reaches L5.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },
    ]
  },

  '1.5': {
    name: 'Devolution',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three features of devolution in the UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Asymmetry — the nations hold different powers and use different structures, and England has no devolved legislature at all. Analysis: because each settlement was negotiated separately, there is no principled basis for resolving the resulting anomalies.\n• Legal revocability — powers are delegated by a sovereign Parliament, not constitutionally divided. Analysis: this is what distinguishes devolution from federalism; the UK Internal Market Act 2020 demonstrated it in practice.\n• Proportional electoral systems — AMS in Scotland (and in Wales until 2026), STV in Northern Ireland. Analysis: produced multi-party systems, coalition and minority government, in sharp contrast to Westminster.\n• Power-sharing in Northern Ireland — mandatory inclusion of unionist and nationalist parties with linked First and deputy First Minister offices. Analysis: essential to the peace settlement but structurally fragile.\n• Policy divergence — tuition fees, prescription charges, social care, minimum alcohol pricing, COVID rules.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires analysis of consequences, not description of institutions.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which devolution has produced policy divergence in the UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Higher education funding — Scotland abolished tuition fees for Scottish students at Scottish universities; England moved to fees of up to £9,250, raised to £9,535 from 2025/26; Wales operates its own system of grants and fees.\n• Health and social care — free personal care for the elderly in Scotland; prescription charges abolished in Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland but retained in England; Scotland and Wales declined the English internal-market NHS reforms.\n• Public health — minimum unit pricing of alcohol in Scotland and Wales.\n• Education — different curricula and assessment regimes, including the abolition of SATs-style testing in Wales.\n• COVID-19 — four different sets of rules and timings across the UK, which made divergence unusually visible to the public.\n\nAnalysis expected: divergence proves devolution transferred REAL power rather than administrative discretion; it also creates pressure, since English voters observe policies unavailable to them.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which devolution has affected the UK constitution.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Dispersal of power from Westminster — legislatures with primary law-making powers over major policy areas, leading many to describe the UK as quasi-federal. Analysis: the description is imprecise, since Westminster legally retains the power to amend or abolish these bodies.\n• Pressure on parliamentary sovereignty — the Sewel Convention, the Scotland Act 2016 declaring the Scottish Parliament permanent, and devolved democratic mandates. Analysis: Miller I resolved the legal question by holding Sewel unenforceable, confirming sovereignty in strict law.\n• New electoral and party dynamics — proportional systems produced multi-party politics and gave nationalist parties a platform and, decisively, a record of government.\n• The West Lothian question and the English anomaly — EVEL was introduced in 2015 and abandoned as unworkable in 2021.\n\nGuidance: credit the observation that devolution disperses power without dividing sovereignty.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three reasons why the devolution settlement has proved unstable.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Asymmetric design with no agreed endpoint — settlements negotiated separately and renegotiated in 2012, 2016 and 2017, with England excluded. Analysis: continual transfer without a settled endpoint invites further demands rather than closing the question.\n• Absence of legal entrenchment — devolved powers rest on ordinary statute and on a convention Miller I confirmed the courts will not enforce, so disputes escalate into constitutional crises rather than being resolved judicially.\n• Brexit and competing mandates — Scotland and Northern Ireland voted Remain while the UK voted Leave; returning EU competences in devolved fields raised whether they should pass to the devolved bodies or be held centrally. The UK Internal Market Act 2020 was passed over withheld consent.\n• Northern Ireland's power-sharing structure allows either community's largest party to collapse the institutions — suspensions in 2017–2020 and 2022–2024.\n• The Barnett formula's population basis remains contested from several directions.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires causal analysis of WHY instability follows.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three arguments that the UK should become a federal state.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Entrenchment would protect devolved powers legally rather than politically — Miller I showed Sewel is unenforceable, and the UK Internal Market Act showed Westminster will legislate over withheld consent.\n• It would resolve the English anomaly by creating an English tier, answering the West Lothian question that EVEL failed to settle.\n• It would provide a constitutional court and clear rules for resolving centre–region disputes, which the current arrangement lacks entirely.\n• It would replace the ad hoc, repeatedly renegotiated settlement with a coherent design.\n• It might stabilise the union by giving the nations genuinely secure self-government within it.\n\nCounter-arguments worth crediting: federalism requires codification, which is incompatible with parliamentary sovereignty as traditionally understood; England's size (around 85% of the population) makes a balanced federation very difficult.\n\nGuidance: one-sided by design; require analysis rather than assertion.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which Westminster retains power over the devolved nations.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Legal sovereignty — devolved bodies are creatures of statute and could be amended or abolished by ordinary Act. Westminster suspended the Northern Ireland Assembly on several occasions.\n• Reserved matters — defence, foreign affairs, immigration, the constitution, most macroeconomic policy, and equality law all remain at Westminster.\n• The Sewel Convention's weakness — Miller I held it judicially unenforceable despite statutory recognition, and the word "normally" has been used to legislate without consent (EU Withdrawal legislation; UK Internal Market Act 2020).\n• Funding — the Barnett formula is set by the Treasury; the devolved nations depend on block grants they do not control.\n• The UK Internal Market Act 2020 constrains devolved regulatory divergence.\n\nGuidance: top-band answers note the gap between LEGAL power (extensive) and POLITICAL capacity to use it (limited).\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: '"Devolution has strengthened the United Kingdom." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `STRENGTHENED:\n• Gave the nations meaningful self-government WITHIN the UK, removing the argument that only independence could deliver distinctive policy.\n• The 2014 Scottish referendum produced a No vote on ~85% turnout.\n• Proportional systems produce coalition and compromise rather than automatic nationalist dominance.\n• Responded to demonstrated demand — the 1997 referendums produced Yes votes in Scotland and Wales.\n• Allowed policy tailored to each nation, increasing satisfaction with governance.\n• The Good Friday Agreement's power-sharing was essential to the Northern Ireland peace settlement.\n\nWEAKENED:\n• Created national institutions, parties and media around which distinct political identities consolidated.\n• Gave nationalist parties a platform AND a record of government, transforming the SNP from protest vehicle to party of government.\n• Continual renegotiation (2012, 2016, 2017) suggests an unstable process rather than a settled endpoint.\n• Brexit created competing mandates — Scotland and NI voted Remain.\n• The UK Internal Market Act 2020, passed over withheld consent, did lasting damage to intergovernmental relations.\n• Asymmetry and the unresolved English question generate resentment.\n\nGuidance for L5: must engage the counterfactual — would nationalism have grown anyway without devolution? The strongest conclusions note that devolution changed the FORM of the constitutional question rather than answering it.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },

      { q: 'EXTRACTS (written for practice):\n\nExtract 1 — written for this practice question in the style of a speech by a devolved-government minister who argues the settlement must be reformed\n"Devolution rests on a promise that Westminster will not normally legislate on devolved matters without consent. That promise has been broken. The UK Internal Market Act 2020 was passed even though both the Scottish Parliament and the Senedd refused consent, and it constrains how devolved governments can use powers they already hold. In 2023, for the first time, a UK government used section 35 of the Scotland Act to block a bill passed by Holyrood — the Gender Recognition Reform (Scotland) Bill. The Supreme Court has confirmed that the Sewel Convention cannot be enforced in the courts. A settlement that one side can rewrite at will is not a partnership. Consent must be put on a legally binding footing, and intergovernmental relations must be given real teeth."\n\nExtract 2 — written for this practice question in the style of an article by a unionist academic who argues the current settlement works\n"Devolution has been one of the most successful constitutional reforms of modern times. Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland have used their powers to go their own way on tuition fees, prescription charges and minimum pricing for alcohol, and the Scottish Parliament gained major tax and welfare powers in 2016. Disputes are inevitable in any multi-level system, and the UK has ways to resolve them: the Supreme Court has ruled on the limits of devolved competence, and the Court of Session upheld the section 35 order in 2023. Legally entrenching consent or moving to federalism would founder on England, which has around 84 per cent of the UK\'s population and has shown little appetite for an English parliament. The settlement\'s flexibility is its strength: it has evolved repeatedly since 1998."\n\nAnalyse, evaluate and compare the arguments in the above extracts regarding whether the devolution settlement needs reform.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — students should analyse and compare the arguments in both extracts and test them against their own knowledge, for example:\n• THE SEWEL CONVENTION: Extract 1 argues consent has been ignored. This is accurate — consent was withheld for the European Union (Withdrawal) Act 2018 and the UK Internal Market Act 2020 — and Miller I (2017) held Sewel judicially unenforceable despite its statutory recognition in the Scotland Act 2016. Extract 2 does not address Sewel directly, which weakens its claim that disputes are resolved satisfactorily.\n• SECTION 35: Extract 1 treats the first use of s.35 (January 2023) as a breach of partnership; Extract 2 notes that the Court of Session upheld it (December 2023). Evaluate: the order was lawful, which supports Extract 2 on legality, but its use shows Westminster's legal supremacy over devolved legislation, which supports Extract 1 on the imbalance of power.\n• POLICY DIVERGENCE: Extract 2's examples are accurate (no tuition fees for Scottish students at Scottish universities, free prescriptions in Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland, minimum unit pricing in Scotland and Wales) and show that devolved powers are real. But Extract 1's point concerns the security of those powers, not their use — the extracts partly talk past each other.\n• DISPUTE RESOLUTION: Extract 2 relies on the courts; Extract 1 on stronger intergovernmental machinery. Students may use the 2021 UNCRC reference and the 2022 ruling that Holyrood cannot legislate for an independence referendum — decisions that settled disputes but in Westminster's favour, which each extract could claim.\n• THE ENGLISH QUESTION AND FEDERALISM: Extract 2's strongest argument — England's size makes symmetrical federalism impractical, and EVEL (2015–21) was abandoned. Students may note devolution within England through combined authorities and metro mayors as an alternative to an English parliament.\n• NORTHERN IRELAND: neither extract discusses it, but the suspension of the Assembly and Executive from February 2022 to February 2024, linked to the Protocol and Windsor Framework, is strong evidence that the settlement can break down, supporting Extract 1.\n• STATUS: Extract 1 is a speech by a devolved minister — a participant with an interest in more autonomy and a persuasive purpose aimed at a political audience. Extract 2 is by an academic, more measured, but explicitly unionist, so it may play down the grievances of devolved governments.\n\nJudgement: a supported conclusion might accept Extract 2 that devolution works well in policy terms while finding Extract 1 more convincing that its constitutional foundations are insecure because Westminster can override consent at will. Any conclusion is creditworthy if it follows from comparison of the extracts.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and comparing the arguments in the extracts and weighing which are more convincing; evaluate their status (author, type of publication, purpose, date) where relevant. Both extracts were written for practice, so status evaluation should focus on the type of author and purpose stated in each label. Own knowledge should be used to test the extracts' claims, not to replace discussion of them. Answers that do not focus on the arguments in the extracts cannot go above Level 2.` },

      { q: '"Devolution has created a quasi-federal United Kingdom." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `QUASI-FEDERAL:\n• Devolved legislatures hold primary law-making powers over major policy areas, producing substantial real divergence.\n• Scotland has significant tax and welfare powers under the Scotland Act 2016.\n• The Scotland Act 2016 and Wales Act 2017 declare the devolved bodies permanent and put Sewel in statute.\n• Reclaiming devolved powers would be politically near-impossible, whatever the legal position.\n• Devolved governments have their own mandates, civil services and international representation.\n\nNOT FEDERAL:\n• Powers are DELEGATED, not constitutionally divided — Parliament remains legally sovereign and could amend or abolish the devolved bodies by ordinary Act.\n• Miller I held Sewel judicially unenforceable despite statutory recognition — the devolved nations have no legal remedy.\n• Westminster legislated over withheld consent through the UK Internal Market Act 2020; no US state could be overridden this way.\n• England has no devolved tier at all, so there is no symmetrical federal structure.\n• There is no codified constitution, no constitutional court and no entrenched division of powers.\n• Northern Ireland's Assembly has been suspended by Westminster.\n\nGuidance for L5: the term "quasi-federal" captures a POLITICAL reality and a LEGAL fiction simultaneously. A top-band answer explains that the gap between the two is precisely what Brexit exposed, and reaches a conclusion about whether political entrenchment can substitute for legal entrenchment.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },

      { q: '"Further devolution would be beneficial for the UK." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `BENEFICIAL:\n• Brings decisions closer to those affected, allowing policy tailored to local circumstances.\n• Responds to demonstrated demand — every further transfer since 1998 followed political pressure.\n• May contain nationalism by delivering self-government within the union.\n• English devolution through metro mayors has produced genuine local innovation and could be extended.\n• Would address the English anomaly and the West Lothian question.\n• Allows policy experimentation from which other nations can learn.\n\nNOT BENEFICIAL:\n• Risks fragmenting the UK internal market and creating regulatory divergence that burdens business — the rationale for the UK Internal Market Act.\n• Postcode-lottery concerns: entitlements varying by residence within one state.\n• May accelerate rather than contain nationalism by strengthening national institutions and identities.\n• Funding complexity — the Barnett formula is already contested and further divergence would strain it.\n• Duplication and administrative cost.\n• English regional devolution was rejected in the 2004 North East referendum, suggesting limited appetite.\n\nGuidance for L5: should distinguish further devolution TO THE NATIONS from devolution WITHIN England, since the arguments differ substantially. A conclusion that treats them as one question caps at L3.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },
    ]
  },

  '1.6': {
    name: 'Democracy and Participation',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three features of representative democracy in the UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Free and fair elections under universal adult suffrage with a secret ballot, regulated by the Electoral Commission. Analysis: legitimacy is qualified by FPTP disproportionality — governments take office on roughly 33–44% of the vote (Labour 2024: 411 seats on 33.7%).\n• The constituency link — each voter has one identifiable MP accountable for their area, providing a clear channel for casework and redress. Analysis: the strongest representative feature of the system; list systems dilute it.\n• Party competition and a functioning opposition, funded through Short Money. Analysis: gives voters a programmatic choice and an alternative government.\n• Accountability — MPs and governments face removal at elections, and Parliament scrutinises between them.\n• Pluralism — pressure groups and a free press provide continuous representation between elections.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires evaluation of how well each feature works, not description alone.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three arguments that the UK is suffering a participation crisis.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Declining turnout — fell from ~84% in 1950 to 59.4% in 2001, the lowest since 1918, and has not returned to post-war levels. Turnout is far lower in local, devolved and PCC elections.\n• Collapse in party membership — from millions in the 1950s to a small fraction. Analysis: parties recruit candidates, debate policy and connect citizens to government, so this weakens the whole chain of representation.\n• The age gap — turnout among 18–24s is consistently far below the over-65s, skewing whose preferences politicians attend to.\n• Declining trust in politicians, accelerated by the 2009 expenses scandal.\n• The growth of safe seats under FPTP, which makes votes feel worthless in most constituencies.\n\nGuidance: one-sided by design; require analysis of CONSEQUENCES, not just statistics.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which participation in UK politics could be increased.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Compulsory voting — as in Australia and Belgium. Analysis: would raise turnout immediately and correct the age and class skew, but manufactures turnout without engagement and may produce uninformed voting.\n• Votes at 16 — Scotland and Wales already do this for devolved elections, and the 2014 referendum showed strong engagement; the Representation of the People Bill (introduced February 2026, not yet law by September 2026) would extend it to all UK elections. Analysis: voting is habit-forming, but 18–24s already have the lowest turnout, so it may enlarge the abstaining group.\n• Electoral reform — addresses the strongest explanation for low turnout, since turnout is higher in marginals and under proportional systems. Analysis: rejected decisively in 2011, and the parties able to legislate are those FPTP advantages.\n• Digital or e-voting — convenience, though security concerns and the likelihood that convenience is not the binding constraint.\n• More referendums and greater use of e-petitions.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires the trade-off for each proposal.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three milestones in the extension of the UK franchise.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Great Reform Act 1832 — extended the vote to some middle-class men and began the redistribution of seats. Analysis: established the principle that the franchise could be extended, opening the way to further reform.\n• Representation of the People Act 1918 — all men over 21, women over 30 with a property qualification. Analysis: the single largest extension; the gendered age limit shows how partial it remained.\n• Equal Franchise Act 1928 — women on the same terms as men, at 21.\n• Representation of the People Act 1969 — voting age lowered to 18.\n• Scotland (2014 onwards) and Wales — votes at 16 for devolved and local elections.\n\nAnalysis expected: credit discussion of the suffragist/suffragette debate over which methods were decisive, and of the role of the First World War.\n\nGuidance: narrative accounts without analysis cap at L2.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which rights are protected in the UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• The Human Rights Act 1998 — incorporates the ECHR; s.3 interpretive duty, s.4 declarations, s.6 making it unlawful for public authorities to act incompatibly. Analysis: s.6 is where the Act actually bites, reaching most government activity.\n• The Equality Act 2010 — consolidated anti-discrimination law around nine protected characteristics, and reaches PRIVATE actors in employment and services, which constitutional rulings cannot.\n• Common law and judicial review — natural justice, and courts quashing unlawful public-body decisions.\n• Continuing ECHR membership — cases may reach Strasbourg independently of the HRA.\n• The Freedom of Information Act 2000 — transparency underpinning accountability.\n\nAnalysis expected: the central weakness is that NONE of this is entrenched — all are repealable by simple majority.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three arguments for lowering the voting age to 16.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Consistency with other responsibilities — at 16 a person may work and pay income tax and national insurance, consent to medical treatment, and join the armed forces with parental consent. No taxation without representation.\n• Habit formation — voting is widely understood to be habit-forming, so earlier enfranchisement while young people are still in education, and reachable through citizenship teaching, may raise lifelong participation.\n• Precedent and evidence — 16- and 17-year-olds voted in the 2014 Scottish independence referendum with high engagement, and Scotland and Wales have since extended the franchise for devolved and local elections without difficulty.\n• Long-term stakes — decisions on tuition fees, climate and Brexit fall disproportionately on the young.\n\nCounter-arguments worth crediting: maturity and susceptibility to influence; 18–24s already have the lowest turnout.\n\nGuidance: one-sided by design.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'EXTRACTS (written for practice):\n\nExtract 1 — written for this practice question in the style of a report summary by a campaign group for democratic reform\n"The warning signs are unmistakable. Turnout at the 2024 general election was only around 60 per cent, barely above the 2001 low and far below the levels above 80 per cent seen in the early 1950s. Young people and poorer citizens are much less likely to vote than older and wealthier ones, so politicians listen to those who turn up. Party membership has fallen from millions in the 1950s to a small fraction of that today, and trust in politicians is very low by historical standards. Turnout in local and police and crime commissioner elections is often below a third. When so many stay out of formal politics, governments rest on thin mandates: in 2024 Labour won a large majority with the votes of only about a fifth of the registered electorate."\n\nExtract 2 — written for this practice question in the style of an article by a political scientist who argues that participation has changed rather than declined\n"Talk of a participation crisis confuses a change in how people take part with a decline in whether they do. Turnout rises when the stakes are high: around 85 per cent voted in the 2014 Scottish independence referendum and 72 per cent in the 2016 EU referendum. Millions sign online petitions — more than six million signed one in 2019 calling for Brexit to be cancelled — and campaigns such as Marcus Rashford\'s on free school meals have changed government policy. Organisations such as the National Trust have far more members than all the political parties combined. Low turnout in 2024 reflected an election whose result seemed a foregone conclusion, not a rejection of democracy. The real problem is a political system that has failed to keep up with how citizens want to engage."\n\nAnalyse, evaluate and compare the arguments in the above extracts regarding whether the UK is suffering a participation crisis.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — students should analyse and compare the arguments in both extracts and test them against their own knowledge, for example:\n• TURNOUT: Extract 1's figures are accurate (59.4 per cent in 2001; about 60 per cent in 2024; over 80 per cent in 1950 and 1951). Extract 2 explains 2024 by the predictability of the result — but turnout also fell in 2001, another foregone conclusion, which suggests a pattern that supports Extract 1. Students may note the recovery to 66–69 per cent in 2015–19, which supports Extract 2.\n• UNEQUAL PARTICIPATION: Extract 1's point about age and class gaps is its most serious, because it concerns whose interests are represented, not just how many vote. Extract 2 does not answer it — and alternative participation such as petitions and campaigning is itself skewed towards the educated and better-off.\n• MANDATES: Extract 1's claim that Labour won with about a fifth of the registered electorate is accurate. Synoptic link to electoral systems: FPTP turned 33.7 per cent of votes cast into 63 per cent of seats, and safe seats may depress turnout — so part of the "crisis" may be caused by the system, which supports Extract 2's final sentence.\n• ALTERNATIVE PARTICIPATION: Extract 2 cites petitions, referendums and campaigns. Evaluate: the 2019 revocation petition changed nothing, so critics call such activity "clicktivism", whereas the Rashford campaign (2020) did produce government U-turns. High referendum turnout shows engagement with decisive single questions rather than with representative institutions.\n• PARTY MEMBERSHIP: Extract 1 is right about the long-run decline; Extract 2's National Trust comparison shows associational life persists. Students may note surges in party membership (Labour after 2015, the SNP after 2014, Reform UK recently) as partial support for Extract 2.\n• REMEDIES: students may link each extract to reforms — compulsory voting, votes at 16 (the Labour government's plan to extend it to general elections; it already applies in Scottish and Welsh devolved and local elections), proportional representation or digital participation — and evaluate which diagnosis each remedy assumes.\n• STATUS: Extract 1 is from a reform campaign whose purpose is to persuade that change is needed, so it stresses decline. Extract 2 is by an academic whose purpose is to reinterpret the evidence; it is more measured but selects high-turnout referendums as its examples.\n\nJudgement: a supported conclusion might accept Extract 2 that participation has diversified but find Extract 1 more convincing that formal participation is both low and unequal, making "crisis" defensible for electoral politics but not for civic engagement overall. Any supported conclusion should be credited.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and comparing the arguments in the extracts and weighing which are more convincing; evaluate their status (author, type of publication, purpose, date) where relevant. Both extracts were written for practice, so status evaluation should focus on the type of author and purpose stated in each label. Own knowledge should be used to test the extracts' claims, not to replace discussion of them. Answers that do not focus on the arguments in the extracts cannot go above Level 2.` },

      { q: '"Rights are effectively protected in the UK." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `EFFECTIVELY PROTECTED:\n• The HRA makes Convention rights enforceable domestically; s.6 reaches most government activity; s.4 declarations have almost always produced amendment (Belmarsh).\n• The Equality Act 2010 reaches private discrimination in employment, services and housing — something constitutional rights alone cannot.\n• Judicial review has expanded substantially since 1997; courts rule against government regularly.\n• An independent judiciary and the rule of law.\n• Continuing ECHR membership provides an external backstop independent of the HRA.\n• In practice no government has repealed either statute despite decades of proposals.\n\nNOT EFFECTIVELY PROTECTED:\n• Nothing is entrenched — both statutes are repealable by simple Commons majority, and replacement of the HRA has been repeatedly proposed.\n• Rights curtailed by counter-terrorism legislation permitting extended detention and expanded surveillance, and by restrictions on protest.\n• Declarations of incompatibility leave the offending statute valid and in force.\n• Legal aid restrictions limit practical access to remedies.\n• Rights are residual in character rather than positively guaranteed.\n\nGuidance for L5: must weigh THEORETICAL fragility against DEMONSTRATED stability, and should note that a British Bill of Rights would be as vulnerable as the HRA unless entrenched — which is incompatible with parliamentary sovereignty. That point is what separates L5 from L4.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },

      { q: '"Referendums should be used more widely in the UK." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `MORE WIDELY:\n• Direct legitimacy for constitutional change — in an uncodified constitution nothing else prevents a government altering the framework by ordinary legislation.\n• High engagement — ~85% turnout in 2014 and ~72% in 2016, far above contemporaneous general elections.\n• Resolves cross-cutting issues that no general election can settle, since Europe divided both main parties for decades.\n• Educative — generates extensive public debate.\n• Entrenches change politically where legal entrenchment is unavailable.\n\nNOT MORE WIDELY:\n• Binary framing — the 2016 vote asked whether to leave without specifying what leaving meant, producing a majority for a direction but not an outcome, and years of parliamentary deadlock.\n• Clash with parliamentary sovereignty — a legally advisory vote acquiring binding political authority against a parliamentary majority, with no constitutional mechanism to resolve the conflict.\n• No threshold — a narrow majority on one day can deliver irreversible change.\n• Vulnerable to campaign funding, media framing and differential turnout.\n• They can entrench rather than settle division — neither 2014 nor 2016 closed the question it asked.\n• Often called to manage internal party division rather than because the constitutional moment demanded it — 1975 and 2016 both arose substantially from governing-party splits.\n\nGuidance for L5: should distinguish CONSTITUTIONAL questions (a strong case) from ordinary policy (a weak one).\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },

      { q: '"Democracy in the UK is in need of reform." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `NEEDS REFORM:\n• FPTP disproportionality — governments on roughly 33–44% of the vote (Labour 2024: 411 seats on 33.7%); UKIP's 3.9m votes and one seat in 2015; safe seats and wasted votes.\n• The wholly unelected House of Lords amends and delays legislation.\n• Turnout and party membership declines; the age and class skew in participation.\n• The royal prerogative allows significant executive action without prior approval.\n• Executive dominance of a sovereign Parliament — the "elective dictatorship" concern.\n• Party funding dependence on a small number of large donors.\n\nDOES NOT NEED REFORM:\n• Free and fair elections, universal suffrage, the rule of law and an independent judiciary are all secure.\n• FPTP delivers decisive, accountable single-party government and a clear constituency link.\n• Electoral reform was decisively rejected in 2011 on a 68% No vote.\n• Reform since 1997 has already been extensive — devolution, the HRA, the Supreme Court, the Wright reforms.\n• Participation has shifted to pressure groups and single-issue campaigning rather than disappearing.\n• The Lords performs genuine revising work and is more assertive since 1999.\n\nGuidance for L5: must PRIORITISE — which defect matters most, and would the proposed remedy actually address it? A conclusion that recommends reform without engaging the 2011 referendum result, or that opposes it without engaging the 2015 disproportionality figures, cannot reach L5.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },
    ]
  },

  '1.7': {
    name: 'Elections and Referendums',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three advantages of the First Past the Post electoral system.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Strong, decisive single-party government — by amplifying the lead of the largest party into a seat bonus, FPTP usually manufactures a majority, allowing a manifesto to be implemented without post-election bargaining. Analysis: voters know at the moment of voting which programme they are choosing.\n• Clear accountability — a single governing party can be held responsible and removed at the next election, unlike a coalition whose programme no voter endorsed.\n• Simplicity — one X, quickly counted and easily understood, supporting confidence in the result.\n• The constituency link — one identifiable MP accountable for each area, providing a clear channel for casework.\n• Excludes extremist parties with dispersed support.\n\nGuidance: one-sided by design; require analysis of WHY each is an advantage.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three disadvantages of the First Past the Post electoral system.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Disproportionality — seat shares diverge sharply from vote shares. In 2015 UKIP won ~3.9m votes and one seat while the SNP won ~1.5m votes and 56 seats, because FPTP rewards concentrated support and penalises dispersed support. Governments routinely take office on roughly 33–44% of the vote (Labour 2024: 411 seats on 33.7%).\n• Wasted votes — votes for losing candidates and the winner's surplus have no effect on the outcome, so most votes cast do not influence who is elected.\n• Safe seats — the result is effectively predetermined in most constituencies, depressing turnout and concentrating campaigning and policy attention on a small number of marginals.\n• Tactical voting — voters are pushed away from expressing a genuine first preference.\n• A high barrier to new parties, entrenching the two main parties.\n\nGuidance: the 2015 comparison is the single strongest piece of evidence and should feature in a top-band answer.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three electoral systems used in the UK other than First Past the Post.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Additional Member System (Scottish Parliament; the Senedd until its move to closed-list PR in 2026) — two votes, one for a constituency member elected by FPTP and one for a regional list, with list seats allocated correctively. Analysis: retains a single constituency representative while producing broadly proportional results; has produced coalition and minority government.\n• Single Transferable Vote (Northern Ireland Assembly and local elections, Scottish local elections) — multi-member constituencies with ranked preferences and a quota. Analysis: highly proportional with few wasted votes; chosen for Northern Ireland precisely to guarantee both communities representation; dilutes the link to a single representative.\n• Supplementary Vote — first and second preferences, with all but the top two eliminated. Used for mayors and PCCs; changed to FPTP by the Elections Act 2022, then restored by the English Devolution and Community Empowerment Act 2026. Analysis: the winner has broader support than a bare plurality but the system is not proportional.\n• Closed-list PR (the Senedd from 2026) — 96 members elected in 16 six-member constituencies, seats allocated by D'Hondt, and voters choose a party rather than a candidate. Analysis: highly proportional, but removes any choice between a party's candidates and weakens the individual constituency link.\n\nGuidance: credit the analytical point that the same electorate produces different party systems under different rules.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three arguments for reforming the Westminster electoral system.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Proportionality — governments take office on roughly 33–44% of the vote (Labour 2024: 411 seats on 33.7%), so the seat distribution does not reflect how the country voted; 2015 produced UKIP's 3.9m votes and one seat.\n• Wasted votes and safe seats — most votes have no effect, which depresses turnout and is the strongest explanation for the participation problem.\n• Devolved evidence — AMS (in Scotland, and in Wales until 2026) and STV (in Northern Ireland) have produced workable government, undermining the claim that proportionality means instability.\n• Comparative evidence associates proportional systems with higher turnout.\n• Representation for parties with substantial but dispersed support.\n\nCounter-arguments worth crediting: the 2011 AV referendum's 68% No vote; coalition bargaining after the election rather than voter choice before it.\n\nGuidance: one-sided by design.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three arguments against the wider use of referendums in the UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Binary framing of complex questions — the 2016 referendum asked whether to leave without specifying what leaving meant, so it produced a majority for a direction but not for any outcome, and Parliament spent years unable to agree on implementation.\n• Clash with parliamentary sovereignty — UK referendums are legally advisory but politically binding, creating a conflict between popular and parliamentary authority that the constitution provides no mechanism to resolve.\n• No threshold — a narrow majority on a single day can deliver an irreversible constitutional change.\n• Vulnerability to campaign funding, media framing and differential turnout.\n• They can entrench rather than settle division — neither 2014 nor 2016 closed the question.\n• Motive — often called to manage internal party division rather than because the constitutional moment required it.\n\nGuidance: one-sided by design.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which the choice of electoral system affects a party system.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Plurality systems sustain two-party dominance (Duverger's law) — only the largest party in each district wins anything, so dispersed support wins nothing and voters, donors and candidates converge on the two viable parties.\n• Proportional systems produce multi-party systems, coalition and minority government — AMS in Scotland (and in Wales until 2026) has given Greens and others genuine representation; STV in Northern Ireland guarantees both communities.\n• Geography of support interacts with the system — under FPTP, concentrated support succeeds (SNP 56 seats on 1.5m votes) while dispersed support fails (UKIP one seat on 3.9m).\n• Voter behaviour changes with the rules — the same UK electorate splits tickets and supports smaller parties where the system rewards it.\n\nGuidance: this is strong evidence that electoral systems SHAPE party systems rather than merely reflecting them; L3 requires that analytical point.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'EXTRACTS (written for practice):\n\nExtract 1 — written for this practice question in the style of a campaign article by an electoral-reform pressure group\n"The 2024 general election produced one of the least proportional results in British history. Labour won 63 per cent of the seats on 33.7 per cent of the vote. Reform UK received over four million votes — 14.3 per cent — and won just five seats, while the Liberal Democrats won 72 seats on 12.2 per cent. Millions of votes were effectively wasted in safe seats, and many electors voted tactically rather than for the party they actually preferred. First Past the Post was designed for a two-party contest that no longer exists. Proportional systems already operate in elections in Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland. Only when seats reflect votes will every voter count and every government rest on the support of most of those who voted."\n\nExtract 2 — written for this practice question in the style of a newspaper editorial that defends First Past the Post\n"First Past the Post has a simple virtue: it lets voters remove a government cleanly. In 2024 the electorate decisively rejected the Conservatives after fourteen years, and a new government took office the next day with a clear programme. Each MP represents a single constituency and can be held personally to account by local voters. Proportional systems usually produce coalitions whose programmes are negotiated behind closed doors after polling day, giving small parties leverage out of proportion to their support. The public has already had its say: in the 2011 referendum, 67.9 per cent rejected the Alternative Vote. Nor are the alternatives flawless — the Additional Member System creates two classes of representative. The system is not broken; it simply produced an outcome its critics dislike."\n\nAnalyse, evaluate and compare the arguments in the above extracts regarding whether First Past the Post should be replaced for UK general elections.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — students should analyse and compare the arguments in both extracts and test them against their own knowledge, for example:\n• PROPORTIONALITY: Extract 1's 2024 figures are accurate and show extreme disproportionality, especially for parties with dispersed support (Reform UK, the Greens with 4 seats on 6.7 per cent). Extract 2 does not dispute the figures; it argues proportionality matters less than accountability. Evaluate which democratic value should take priority.\n• STRONG AND ACCOUNTABLE GOVERNMENT: Extract 2's strongest argument — FPTP normally produces single-party majorities that voters can remove, as in 1997 and 2024. But FPTP produced hung parliaments in 2010 and 2017, and the 2010–15 coalition governed stably for a full term, which weakens the claim that PR means instability. Comparative examples (Germany, New Zealand after 1996) may be used.\n• COALITIONS AND SMALL PARTIES: Extract 2 claims PR gives small parties excessive leverage. Students may note that FPTP can do the same — the DUP's confidence-and-supply deal with May's government (2017–19) — so this is a feature of hung parliaments rather than of PR alone.\n• CONSTITUENCY LINK: Extract 2 values the single-member constituency; Extract 1 does not address it. Evaluate: AMS and STV retain local representation, and the "two classes of representative" criticism of AMS in the Scottish Parliament is contested.\n• THE 2011 REFERENDUM: Extract 2 treats it as decisive. Evaluate: turnout was only about 42 per cent, and AV is not a proportional system, so the result does not show that voters rejected PR — this weakens Extract 2's use of it.\n• THE DEVOLVED EXPERIENCE: Extract 1 cites other UK systems (AMS for Holyrood, STV for the Northern Ireland Assembly and Scottish local elections, closed-list PR for the Senedd from 2026). Evaluate whether their use elsewhere in the UK shows PR works or simply suits different institutions.\n• STATUS: Extract 1 is from a pressure group dedicated to electoral reform — persuasive in purpose and selective (it ignores coalition drawbacks). Extract 2 is a newspaper editorial, which reflects the paper's political stance and readership; parties and outlets that benefit from FPTP have an interest in its survival, while Reform UK's position shows how a party's view of FPTP changes with its interest.\n\nJudgement: a supported conclusion might find Extract 1 more convincing on fairness, given how extreme the 2024 result was, while crediting Extract 2 on accountability and the constituency link; strong answers may conclude that the choice depends on which function of elections is judged most important. Any conclusion supported by comparison of the extracts should be credited.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and comparing the arguments in the extracts and weighing which are more convincing; evaluate their status (author, type of publication, purpose, date) where relevant. Both extracts were written for practice, so status evaluation should focus on the type of author and purpose stated in each label. Own knowledge should be used to test the extracts' claims, not to replace discussion of them. Answers that do not focus on the arguments in the extracts cannot go above Level 2.` },

      { q: '"Referendums have been good for UK democracy." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `GOOD:\n• Direct legitimacy for major constitutional change — devolution, AV, Scottish independence, EU membership.\n• Exceptional engagement — ~85% turnout in 2014 including 16- and 17-year-olds; ~72% in 2016, both far above general elections.\n• Settled questions no general election could, since Europe cut across both main parties for decades.\n• Educative — generated sustained public debate on constitutional questions.\n• The 1998 Good Friday Agreement referendum was essential to the Northern Ireland peace settlement.\n\nNOT GOOD:\n• 2016's binary question produced a mandate for a direction but not an outcome, and years of parliamentary deadlock, prorogation, litigation and an early general election followed.\n• Created an unresolvable clash between popular and parliamentary sovereignty.\n• No threshold — an irreversible change on a narrow majority.\n• Intensified rather than settled division; neither 2014 nor 2016 closed its question.\n• Often called for party management reasons — 1975 and 2016 both arose from governing-party splits.\n• Campaign spending and misinformation concerns.\n\nGuidance for L5: should distinguish referendums that WORKED (1998 Good Friday; 1997 devolution — implementing a clear, worked-out proposal) from those that did not (2016 — a direction without a specification). That distinction is the strongest available analytical move.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },

      { q: '"Elections in the UK are democratic." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `DEMOCRATIC:\n• Universal adult suffrage, secret ballot, free and fair contests regulated by the Electoral Commission.\n• Regular elections with a maximum five-year term.\n• Genuine party competition and peaceful transfers of power.\n• Statutory spending limits and a ban on paid political TV advertising constrain the role of money far more than in the USA.\n• The constituency link gives every voter an identifiable representative.\n• Proportional systems operate at devolved level.\n\nNOT FULLY DEMOCRATIC:\n• FPTP disproportionality — governments on a minority of the vote; millions of wasted votes.\n• Safe seats mean the outcome is decided by relatively few voters in marginals.\n• The House of Lords is wholly unelected.\n• Voter ID requirements introduced by the Elections Act 2022 raise access concerns.\n• Party funding depends on a small number of large donors.\n• Turnout and the age and class skew in participation.\n• Boundary review and constituency size disparities.\n\nGuidance for L5: must distinguish PROCEDURAL democracy (secure) from the DEMOCRATIC QUALITY of outcomes (contested). The strongest conclusions specify which standard is being applied.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },

      { q: '"Proportional representation would improve UK democracy." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `WOULD IMPROVE:\n• Seat shares would reflect vote shares, ending the 2015-style distortion.\n• Far fewer wasted votes; the end of safe seats would raise turnout and spread campaigning across the country.\n• Representation for parties with dispersed support currently excluded.\n• Devolved experience shows PR producing stable, workable government in the UK specifically.\n• Coalition governments may produce more consensual and durable policy.\n• Would end tactical voting.\n\nWOULD NOT IMPROVE:\n• The governing programme would be negotiated after the election, so voters would not choose it.\n• Small parties can hold leverage disproportionate to their support — kingmaker problems.\n• Weakened or lost constituency link, particularly under list or large multi-member systems.\n• Coalition can blur accountability — voters cannot easily identify whom to punish.\n• Potential for instability and for extremist parties to win seats.\n• The 2011 referendum rejected change decisively.\n\nGuidance for L5: must specify WHICH proportional system, since the trade-offs differ sharply between AMS, STV and list systems. An answer treating "PR" as a single option caps at L3. The devolved evidence is the strongest UK-specific material available and should be used.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },
    ]
  },

  '1.8': {
    name: 'Political Parties',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three functions of political parties in the UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Representation — aggregating and articulating the interests of sections of society. Analysis: weakened by dealignment and falling membership, so parties represent a narrower activist base than they once did.\n• Participation and recruitment — providing a route into political activity and selecting candidates for office. Analysis: the ministerial pool comes almost entirely from parties.\n• Policy formulation — developing programmes presented in a manifesto, which underpins the doctrine of the mandate and the Salisbury Convention.\n• Providing government and opposition — the governing team and a credible alternative, with the opposition funded through Short Money.\n• Political education — informing and structuring public debate.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires evaluation of how well each function is now performed.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which the Labour Party changed under Tony Blair.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Revision of Clause IV in 1995 — replaced the commitment to common ownership with an endorsement of a dynamic market economy. Analysis: symbolically decisive, signalling abandonment of the traditional socialist objective.\n• Acceptance of the market and of Conservative settlements — retained privatisation and trade union legislation, accepted Conservative spending limits for two years, and rejected rises in income tax rates, pursuing redistribution instead through tax credits and the minimum wage.\n• The Third Way — drawing on Giddens, presenting a position beyond free-market capitalism and state socialism, stressing opportunity and responsibility rather than equality of outcome.\n• Organisational centralisation — tighter control of candidate selection, conference and communications, reducing union and conference influence.\n\nAnalysis expected: supporters credit three consecutive election victories; critics argue it hollowed out the party's distinctiveness and its relationship with its base.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three differences between One Nation conservatism and the New Right.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Role of the state — One Nation accepts intervention and the welfare state out of paternalistic obligation and to preserve stability; the New Right regards a large state as damaging to enterprise and self-reliance and would roll it back economically.\n• Economics — One Nation accepts the mixed economy and managed capitalism; the neo-liberal strand of the New Right pursues free markets, privatisation, deregulation and lower direct taxation.\n• Philosophical basis — One Nation rests on organic society, hierarchy carrying obligation and Burkean pragmatism; the New Right's neo-liberal strand rests on abstract rational individualism (Rand, Nozick), which Burke and Oakeshott would regard as precisely the error they warned against.\n• Attitude to change — One Nation is cautious and evolutionary; Thatcherism was radical and disruptive of established institutions and communities.\n\nGuidance: credit the analytical point that the New Right's conservative credentials are genuinely contested.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three arguments in favour of the state funding of political parties.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Reduces dependence on large donors — both main parties rely on a small number of very large contributions, creating a persistent perception, reinforced by recurring honours controversies, that access and influence can be purchased. Public funding paired with a donation cap would break that link.\n• Fairer competition — parties currently campaign with very unequal resources, which advantages the best-funded rather than the most popular. Allocation by votes received would match resources to demonstrated support and help smaller parties.\n• Parties perform genuine public functions — candidate recruitment, policy development, political education — which arguably merit public support, as Short Money already partly recognises.\n• Would free parties to develop policy rather than fundraise.\n\nCounter-arguments worth crediting: taxpayers funding parties they oppose; entrenching existing parties; removing the incentive to build a mass membership.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three factors affecting the electoral success of UK political parties.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Perceived economic competence — voters reward parties they trust to manage the economy. The 1992 ERM crisis damaged Conservative reputation for a decade; the 2022 mini-budget did so again. New Labour's pre-1997 discipline on tax and spending was designed to neutralise this vulnerability.\n• Leadership and party unity — visible division is strongly associated with defeat. Labour's divisions in the early 1980s and Conservative splits over Europe in the 1990s both preceded heavy defeats; the disciplined parties of 1997 and 2019 won large majorities.\n• The electoral system and the geography of support — FPTP means WHERE votes are matters as much as how many; parties target a limited number of marginal seats, which shapes policy offers.\n• Dealignment and valence — declining class-based loyalty means competence and leadership now predict vote choice more than ideological position.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires causal analysis with examples.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which minor parties influence UK politics.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Agenda-setting without seats — UKIP won ~3.9m votes and one seat in 2015, but its electoral pressure was central to the calling of the EU referendum. Analysis: influence can be exerted through the threat to a major party's vote rather than through representation.\n• Winning seats where support is concentrated — the SNP converted ~1.5m votes into 56 seats in 2015 and dominated Scottish representation until 2024, when it fell to 9 seats as Labour won 37 of 57.\n• Representation at devolved level — proportional systems give Greens and others a genuine platform, from which policy influence follows.\n• Holding the balance of power — the Liberal Democrats in the 2010 coalition; the DUP's confidence-and-supply arrangement after 2017.\n• Policy absorption — major parties adopt minor-party positions to capture their support.\n\nGuidance: credit the contrast between concentrated and dispersed support under FPTP.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: '"UK political parties have converged ideologically." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `HAVE CONVERGED:\n• New Labour accepted privatisation, market mechanisms, trade union legislation and Conservative spending limits; Clause IV was revised in 1995.\n• Cameron's Conservatives accepted NHS spending commitments and social liberalism, including same-sex marriage.\n• Both competing for the median voter under FPTP — the "catch-all party" thesis.\n• Both accept the market economy, NATO membership and the broad shape of the welfare state.\n• Valence politics means competition is increasingly about competence rather than ideology.\n\nHAVE NOT CONVERGED:\n• The period after 2015 saw sharp divergence — Corbyn proposed extensive renationalisation and higher taxation while the Conservatives pursued a hard Brexit.\n• Substantial ongoing disagreement on taxation, public ownership, welfare and the size of the state.\n• Brexit cut ACROSS parties rather than along them, fracturing both internally rather than bringing them together.\n• Different electoral coalitions produce genuinely different priorities.\n\nGuidance for L5: the defensible conclusion is that convergence was a PHASE (roughly 1994–2015) rather than a permanent condition, and that what voters perceive as lack of choice may be a problem of TRUST rather than of ideological distance. An answer that treats convergence as a fixed feature caps at L3.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },

      { q: 'EXTRACTS (written for practice):\n\nExtract 1 — written for this practice question in the style of an analysis piece by a polling expert who argues the two-party system has broken down\n"The two-party system is over. In 2024 Labour and the Conservatives together won only about 57 per cent of the vote, compared with over 95 per cent in 1951. The Conservatives fell to 121 seats, their worst result ever, while the Liberal Democrats won 72, Reform UK five and the Greens four, their best-ever result. Since then Reform UK has led many national opinion polls, made large gains in the May 2025 local elections and won the Runcorn and Helsby by-election. Voters no longer identify with parties as their grandparents did; they switch between them from one election to the next. Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland already have multi-party politics. At Westminster the two-party appearance survives only because the electoral system hides how fragmented support has become."\n\nExtract 2 — written for this practice question in the style of a column by a political historian who argues that two-party dominance persists\n"Every prime minister since the Second World War has been either Labour or Conservative, and only once in that time — the 2010–15 coalition — has a third party formally shared power at Westminster. Despite talk of fragmentation, Labour and the Conservatives still won over 80 per cent of the Commons seats in 2024. The electoral system rewards concentrated support and punishes parties whose votes are spread thinly, as Reform UK\'s four million votes for five seats showed. Third-party surges have come and gone before: the SDP–Liberal Alliance won a quarter of the vote in 1983, and the Liberal Democrats were reduced to eight seats in 2015. Minor parties can shape the agenda, but government is still formed by one of the two main parties."\n\nAnalyse, evaluate and compare the arguments in the above extracts regarding whether the UK still has a two-party system.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — students should analyse and compare the arguments in both extracts and test them against their own knowledge, for example:\n• VOTES VERSUS SEATS: Extract 1 measures the party system by vote share (about 57 per cent combined in 2024, the lowest in modern times); Extract 2 by seats and government formation (Labour 411 and Conservatives 121 — about 82 per cent of MPs). Both figures are accurate; the key evaluative question is which measure defines a party system.\n• THE ELECTORAL SYSTEM: both extracts agree FPTP sustains two-party dominance in the Commons (Duverger's law) but draw opposite conclusions — Extract 1 that it disguises fragmentation, Extract 2 that it entrenches two-party government. Synoptic link to the electoral reform debate: a change to PR would probably end two-party dominance at Westminster.\n• DEALIGNMENT AND VOLATILITY: Extract 1's point about weakened partisan identification is supported by class and partisan dealignment and by large swings — the 2019 "Red Wall" gains and 2024 collapse. Extract 2 does not address volatility, which weakens its reliance on past patterns.\n• THE RISE OF REFORM UK: Extract 1 cites polling, the May 2025 local elections and the Runcorn and Helsby by-election. Extract 2 responds with historical precedent — the Alliance (25.4 per cent in 1983) and UKIP (12.6 per cent but one seat in 2015) failed to break through. Evaluate whether Reform UK differs, and note that opinion polls and local results are weaker evidence than general elections.\n• GOVERNMENT FORMATION: Extract 2 is accurate that only Labour and the Conservatives have supplied prime ministers since 1945. But hung parliaments in 2010 and 2017 required third-party support (coalition with the Liberal Democrats; the DUP confidence-and-supply agreement), qualifying the claim.\n• DEVOLVED AND TYPOLOGICAL ANALYSIS: Extract 1 notes multi-party politics outside Westminster (PR elections, the SNP's dominance of Holyrood since 2007, power-sharing in Northern Ireland). Students may classify the UK as two-party, "two-and-a-half party", dominant-party (1979–97, 1997–2010) or multi-party depending on the level examined.\n• STATUS: Extract 1 is by a polling expert, whose evidence is current survey data — timely but short-term, and polls can be volatile. Extract 2 is by a historian, whose long-term perspective is valuable but may underestimate genuinely new trends. Both are analytical rather than partisan, which makes the comparison one of method.\n\nJudgement: a supported conclusion might accept Extract 1 that the UK has a multi-party electorate while agreeing with Extract 2 that it still has two-party government, because FPTP converts fragmented votes into two-party dominance of seats. Any conclusion supported by comparison of the extracts should be credited.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and comparing the arguments in the extracts and weighing which are more convincing; evaluate their status (author, type of publication, purpose, date) where relevant. Both extracts were written for practice, so status evaluation should focus on the type of author and purpose stated in each label. Own knowledge should be used to test the extracts' claims, not to replace discussion of them. Answers that do not focus on the arguments in the extracts cannot go above Level 2.` },

      { q: '"Party funding in the UK needs reform." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `NEEDS REFORM:\n• Both main parties depend on a small number of very large donations — wealthy individuals and companies for the Conservatives, affiliated unions for Labour.\n• Recurring controversies over honours and access create a persistent perception that influence can be purchased.\n• Very unequal resources advantage the best-funded rather than the most popular party.\n• Declining membership has increased dependence on large donors.\n• Regulation is weak — the Lobbying Act 2014 is widely judged ineffective.\n\nDOES NOT NEED REFORM / reform is problematic:\n• Statutory spending limits and the ban on paid political TV advertising already constrain money far more than in the USA.\n• All donations above a threshold must be declared to the Electoral Commission — transparency already exists.\n• State funding would require taxpayers to fund parties they oppose.\n• It could entrench existing parties against new entrants and remove the incentive to build a mass membership.\n• No formula would command cross-party agreement, which is why repeated reform attempts have failed.\n• Donation is itself a form of political participation.\n\nGuidance for L5: should engage the practical obstacle — each party resists reform that would disadvantage its own funding base, which explains the repeated failure of cross-party talks. That rational explanation is worth more than listing arguments.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },

      { q: '"Political parties are still essential to UK democracy." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `STILL ESSENTIAL:\n• Only parties can contest elections, form governments and legislate — pressure groups cannot.\n• They recruit and train the political class and provide the entire ministerial pool.\n• They aggregate diverse interests into coherent programmes, giving voters a meaningful choice.\n• The manifesto and mandate underpin the Salisbury Convention and the legitimacy of the legislative programme.\n• They structure Parliament, making government and opposition possible.\n• Membership surged after 2015, showing continued capacity to mobilise.\n\nLESS ESSENTIAL:\n• Membership collapsed from millions in the 1950s to a small fraction, weakening the representative chain.\n• Pressure groups, think tanks, social media and single-issue campaigns now perform much of the agenda-setting and mobilisation parties once monopolised — National Trust and RSPB membership exceeds that of all parties combined.\n• Trust in parties and politicians fell sharply after 2009.\n• Dealignment has weakened habitual loyalty; valence voting focuses on leaders rather than parties.\n• Referendums bypass parties entirely on the biggest questions.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest conclusion distinguishes a loss of MONOPOLY from a loss of FUNCTION — parties have lost their exclusive hold on political activity but retain the one function nothing else can perform. An answer that conflates the two caps at L3.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },
    ]
  },

  '1.9': {
    name: 'Pressure Groups',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three methods used by pressure groups to influence government.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Lobbying ministers and civil servants, and submitting evidence to select committees and consultations. Analysis: most effective at the drafting stage before positions harden; the executive controls the agenda, so this is the highest-value target in the UK.\n• Media campaigning to shift public opinion and create political costs for inaction — Marcus Rashford's free school meals campaign forced a reversal in 2020.\n• Petitions, demonstrations and direct action — Extinction Rebellion shifted the agenda and contributed to the declaration of a climate emergency, though tactics inconveniencing the public risk alienating the audience.\n• Legal action — judicial review and HRA challenges, a route that bypasses an unsympathetic political majority.\n• Supplying research and specialist expertise departments lack.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires evaluation of when each method works, not description.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three factors that determine the success of a pressure group.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Alignment with the government's agenda — the most reliable predictor. A group pushing where government already wants to go succeeds with modest effort; one opposing settled policy may fail regardless of resources. Trade unions had substantial influence under 1970s Labour governments and almost none under Thatcher, with membership largely unchanged.\n• Resources — funding buys professional lobbying, research, legal action and sustained campaigning, which is why business groups exert influence disproportionate to their numbers.\n• Insider status and expertise — groups whose specialist knowledge government needs (the BMA, the NFU) become embedded in the policy process.\n• Public support and salience — the Snowdrop campaign secured handgun legislation after Dunblane by making inaction politically costly.\n\nGuidance: penalise the claim that MEMBERSHIP SIZE determines success — the Countryside Alliance mobilised huge marches and lost on hunting. Top-band answers make this point explicitly.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which pressure groups enhance democracy.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Continuous representation between elections — elections are infrequent and force voters to choose a whole package at once, whereas groups allow specific preferences to be expressed continuously.\n• Representing intense minorities and the voteless — an electoral system cannot register how strongly a view is held, and groups represent children, future generations and non-citizens.\n• Improving policy quality — groups supply specialist expertise departments lack, exposing unintended consequences before legislation passes.\n• Dispersing power — pluralist competition between many groups prevents any single interest dominating, with government arbitrating.\n• Participation and political education — mobilising citizens and informing debate.\n• Scrutiny — monitoring implementation and using judicial review to hold government to the law.\n\nGuidance: these benefits ASSUME rough parity of access; the strongest answers note that elitists deny it.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which pressure groups may undermine democracy.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Unequal influence — access and influence track resources, organisation and proximity to decision-makers rather than the number of people affected. Business enjoys structural leverage because governments depend on investment and employment decisions, so a well-funded minority can defeat a diffuse majority, inverting the democratic principle.\n• Lack of accountability — groups are not elected, need not be internally democratic, leaders may not represent members, funding is often opaque (particularly for think tanks), and successful groups bear no responsibility for consequences.\n• Illegal direct action — imposes costs on uninvolved members of the public and can be seen as attempting to override democratic decisions.\n• Intense minority lobbying can produce policy serving the organised few at the expense of the unorganised many.\n\nGuidance: one-sided by design; require analysis of MECHANISM, not assertion.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three differences between insider and outsider pressure groups.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Access — insiders are consulted routinely by departments and sit on advisory committees (BMA, NFU); outsiders have no such access, either because government is unsympathetic or by choice.\n• Methods — insiders negotiate quietly with ministers and officials; outsiders rely on demonstrations, direct action and media campaigns (Extinction Rebellion, Just Stop Oil).\n• The trade-off — insider status normally requires moderating demands and accepting confidentiality, which limits the group's ability to campaign publicly or mobilise supporters. Outsiders retain freedom of action but lack the drafting-stage influence that matters most.\n• Status is not fixed — it changes with the government of the day, as the trade unions' shift from insider to outsider after 1979 shows.\n• Access does not guarantee outcomes — a government may consult a group and ignore it.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires the trade-off, not just the definitions.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which think tanks and corporations influence UK policy.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Think tanks set the intellectual agenda — the IFS, IEA and Adam Smith Institute produce policy analysis; much of the Thatcher government's programme was developed in think tanks before becoming policy. They also supply governments with ideas and personnel. Criticism: opacity of funding makes it hard to know whose interests a report serves.\n• Corporations exercise STRUCTURAL power — because governments depend on investment, employment and tax revenue, the anticipated reaction of business constrains policy even without active lobbying. This is the strongest evidence for the elitist critique.\n• Professional lobbying and the revolving door — firms employ former officials and ministers, gaining privileged access and inside knowledge. The Lobbying Act 2014 created a register covering only CONSULTANT lobbyists, excluding the in-house lobbyists who do most of the work.\n• Technical expertise supplied during consultation, which departments increasingly depend on.\n\nGuidance: the structural power point is what separates L3 from L2.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: '"Pressure groups enhance democracy in the UK." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `ENHANCE:\n• Continuous representation between elections; specific preferences expressed rather than a single bundled choice.\n• Give intense minorities a voice a majoritarian electoral system cannot register, and represent those who cannot vote.\n• Supply expertise that improves the technical quality of legislation.\n• Disperse power — pluralist competition prevents any single interest dominating; devolution and judicial review have multiplied access points.\n• Increase participation and political education at a time of falling party membership.\n• Scrutinise implementation and hold government to the law.\n\nUNDERMINE:\n• Influence tracks resources, not numbers — a well-funded minority can defeat a diffuse majority.\n• Business enjoys structural leverage no membership campaign can match.\n• Groups are unelected, often not internally democratic, and frequently opaque about funding.\n• Illegal direct action imposes costs on the uninvolved and can override democratic decisions.\n• The Lobbying Act 2014 fails to regulate in-house lobbying or the revolving door.\n\nGuidance for L5: must engage the PLURALIST/ELITIST framework explicitly. The strongest conclusion is that the UK is pluralist in FORM but elitist in EFFECT — competition is genuine, but pluralism assumes rough parity of access and that assumption does not hold. Answers that list benefits and harms without that framework cap at L3.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },

      { q: '"Pressure groups are more powerful than political parties in the UK." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `GROUPS MORE POWERFUL:\n• Membership of major pressure groups far exceeds that of all political parties combined.\n• Groups have gained where parties have weakened — as loyalty and membership declined, single-issue campaigning grew.\n• Groups can shift policy directly — the Snowdrop campaign, Marcus Rashford on free school meals.\n• Judicial review and the HRA give groups a route that bypasses political majorities.\n• Devolution multiplied the access points available to groups.\n• Business exercises structural power over policy that no party can ignore.\n\nPARTIES MORE POWERFUL:\n• Only parties contest elections, form governments and legislate — the decisive point.\n• Parties set the legislative agenda and control the parliamentary timetable.\n• A group's success depends overwhelmingly on whether its aims align with the governing party's agenda.\n• Parties are accountable to voters; groups pursue narrow interests without responsibility.\n• Group victories are usually on single issues; parties determine the whole programme of government.\n• Party membership surged after 2015, so decline is not linear.\n\nGuidance for L5: influence has SHIFTED rather than TRANSFERRED. A top-band answer distinguishes agenda-setting power (increasingly with groups) from decision-making power (still with parties) and concludes on which matters more.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },

      { q: 'EXTRACTS (written for practice):\n\nExtract 1 — written for this practice question in the style of an article by a campaigner who argues that UK pressure-group politics is pluralist\n"Britain\'s pressure-group politics shows that ordinary people can still change policy. In 2020 a campaign led by the footballer Marcus Rashford forced the government to reverse its decision not to provide free school meal vouchers over the summer holidays. The Snowdrop Campaign after the Dunblane shooting secured a ban on almost all private handguns in 1997, and the Gurkha Justice Campaign won settlement rights for retired Gurkhas in 2009. Groups representing every interest — from farmers and doctors to cyclists and animal-welfare supporters — give evidence to select committees and respond to consultations. Online petitions that reach 100,000 signatures are considered for debate in Parliament. Competition between many groups prevents any single interest from dominating and gives citizens a voice between elections."\n\nExtract 2 — written for this practice question in the style of a report by a transparency campaign group that argues access is unequal\n"The idea of a level playing field in British politics is a myth. Large companies and wealthy interests enjoy access that ordinary campaigners can only dream of. The Lobbying Act 2014 created a register that covers only consultant lobbyists, leaving the in-house lobbyists employed by big corporations largely unrecorded. In 2021 it emerged that the former prime minister David Cameron had lobbied ministers by text message on behalf of Greensill Capital. During the pandemic, suppliers referred by politicians and officials were placed in a "VIP lane" for PPE contracts, which the High Court ruled in 2022 had been operated unlawfully. Meanwhile, protest groups face tighter restrictions under the Public Order Act 2023. Influence follows money and insider connections, not the strength of an argument."\n\nAnalyse, evaluate and compare the arguments in the above extracts regarding whether pressure-group politics in the UK is pluralist or elitist.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — students should analyse and compare the arguments in both extracts and test them against their own knowledge, for example:\n• OUTSIDER SUCCESS: Extract 1's examples are accurate (Rashford 2020, Snowdrop 1997, Gurkhas 2009). Evaluate: they are exceptional cases combining media attention, public sympathy and a single clear demand — selection of memorable victories may not show that the system is generally open, which weakens Extract 1.\n• ACCESS POINTS: Extract 1 cites select committees, consultations and e-petitions. Evaluate: access is not the same as influence — petitions rarely change the law, and consultation responses are weighted by expertise and resources, which favours well-funded insider groups (synoptic link to Parliament and the executive).\n• CORPORATE AND STRUCTURAL POWER: Extract 2 argues that money buys access. Students may use the elitist idea of structural power — governments anticipate business reaction because they depend on investment and tax revenue — which does not require lobbying at all and is the strongest theoretical support for Extract 2.\n• REGULATION OF LOBBYING: Extract 2's criticism of the Lobbying Act 2014 is accurate. Evaluate the Greensill affair: the official review (Boardman, 2021) found Cameron had not broken the lobbying rules — which Extract 1 might cite as proof the system is lawful, and Extract 2 as proof the rules are too weak.\n• THE PPE "VIP LANE": the High Court ruled in January 2022 that its operation was unlawful. This supports Extract 2's view that connections brought preferential treatment, though it arose in emergency conditions and was checked by the courts after a legal challenge brought by campaign groups — itself evidence of pluralist counter-power.\n• LIMITS ON PROTEST: Extract 2 cites the Public Order Act 2023 (alongside the Police, Crime, Sentencing and Courts Act 2022). Students may add that the proscription of Palestine Action under terrorism legislation in July 2025 sharpened debate about the limits of direct action. Evaluate whether restrictions target methods rather than causes.\n• STATUS: Extract 1 is by a campaigner whose purpose is to encourage participation, so it emphasises success. Extract 2 is from a transparency campaign whose purpose is to expose wrongdoing, so it emphasises scandal. Both are advocates; neither offers systematic evidence about ordinary policymaking. Synoptic comparison: US iron triangles and super PACs may be used to show a more elitist system.\n\nJudgement: a supported conclusion might find the UK closest to "elite pluralism" — Extract 1 shows that outsiders can win on high-profile issues, but Extract 2 is more convincing about routine, low-visibility policymaking, where resources and insider access matter most. Any conclusion supported by comparison of the extracts should be credited.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and comparing the arguments in the extracts and weighing which are more convincing; evaluate their status (author, type of publication, purpose, date) where relevant. Both extracts were written for practice, so status evaluation should focus on the type of author and purpose stated in each label. Own knowledge should be used to test the extracts' claims, not to replace discussion of them. Answers that do not focus on the arguments in the extracts cannot go above Level 2.` },

      { q: '"Insider pressure groups are more successful than outsider groups." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `INSIDERS MORE SUCCESSFUL:\n• Access at the drafting stage, before positions harden and become public — the highest-value point of influence in a system where the executive controls the agenda.\n• Routine consultation and seats on advisory bodies give continuous rather than episodic influence — the BMA on health, the NFU on agriculture.\n• Government depends on their technical expertise, creating mutual dependence.\n• They can influence quietly without the political cost of public confrontation.\n• Their aims are usually already aligned with the governing agenda, which is the strongest single predictor of success.\n\nOUTSIDERS CAN BE MORE SUCCESSFUL:\n• Public campaigning can change the political weather in a way quiet lobbying cannot — the Snowdrop campaign secured handgun legislation after Dunblane against an organised opposing lobby.\n• Extinction Rebellion contributed materially to the declaration of a climate emergency.\n• Marcus Rashford forced a policy reversal on free school meals through media campaigning alone.\n• Litigation offers outsiders a route that bypasses an unsympathetic political majority.\n• Insider status carries a real cost — moderating demands and accepting confidentiality limits what a group can publicly campaign for.\n• Access does not guarantee outcomes: a government may consult a group attentively and ignore it entirely.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest answers note that the categories are not fixed — status changes with the government of the day, as the trade unions' shift after 1979 shows — and that ALIGNMENT WITH THE GOVERNING AGENDA predicts success better than insider status does. Many effective groups deliberately combine both approaches.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },
    ]
  },

  '1.10': {
    name: 'The European Union',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three aims of the European Union.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Preventing war — the founding purpose after 1945 was to bind European states, particularly France and Germany, so closely that war between them became impractical. The European Coal and Steel Community pooled precisely the industries needed to wage war.\n• Economic integration — a single market with the four freedoms (goods, services, capital, people) plus a customs union with a common external tariff. Analysis: the aspect the UK participated in most fully.\n• "Ever closer union" — progressive deepening of political integration, including monetary union and common policies on agriculture, fisheries, competition and the environment. Analysis: this is what the UK consistently resisted, taking opt-outs from the euro and Schengen.\n• Regional development and cohesion funding to reduce disparities between member states.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires analysis of the UK's differentiated relationship with each aim.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three institutions of the European Union.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• European Commission — the executive: proposes legislation, enforces the treaties, manages the budget. Members are appointed, not elected. Analysis: central to the democratic deficit critique, since citizens cannot remove the body that proposes law.\n• European Parliament — directly elected MEPs; scrutinises and, with the Council, approves legislation and the budget. Analysis: powers were strengthened by Lisbon, but turnout in European elections is low.\n• Council of the EU — ministers from member state governments, sharing legislative power. Analysis: qualified majority voting means a state can be outvoted, which was a persistent UK concern.\n• European Council — heads of government setting overall direction.\n• European Court of Justice — interprets EU law and ensures uniform application. Analysis: distinct from the European Court of Human Rights, which the UK remains subject to.\n\nGuidance: penalise confusion of the ECJ with the ECtHR.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which EU membership affected the UK constitution.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Qualification of parliamentary sovereignty — the ECA 1972 gave EU law domestic effect, and Factortame (1991) established that UK courts would disapply an Act of Parliament conflicting with it. Analysis: the competing reading is that supremacy rested on an ordinary statute Parliament itself passed and later repealed, so sovereignty was delegated rather than surrendered.\n• Strengthening the courts — membership required UK judges to interpret and apply EU law, refer questions to the ECJ, and disapply primary legislation. Analysis: materially expanded the judiciary's constitutional role, and the confidence developed arguably shaped later rulings.\n• Transfer of competence — trade, agriculture, fisheries, competition, much environmental and employment law passed to EU institutions.\n• New democratic authority — European Parliament elections under a proportional list system gave UKIP a platform and MEPs that FPTP would have denied it, generating the pressure that led to the referendum.\n\nGuidance: credit the point that membership's constitutional legacy includes the forces that ended it.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three reasons why the UK voted to leave the European Union in 2016.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Sovereignty and "take back control" — membership transferred law-making power to unelected EU institutions, and successive treaties deepened integration far beyond the common market endorsed in 1975 without a further popular vote. Analysis: its force lay in fusing a technical constitutional objection to an intuitive claim about who decides.\n• Immigration and free movement — accession of central and eastern European states from 2004 without UK transitional controls produced substantial migration on which no government could act. Analysis: probably the most electorally potent factor, because it made the sovereignty argument concrete.\n• Long-term economic and political discontent — Leave support correlated with lower incomes, fewer qualifications, older age and post-industrial regions. Analysis: the referendum offered a rare decisive vote against the status quo, which explains why establishment economic warnings were discounted rather than persuasive.\n• Campaign factors — Leave's clearer message; Conservative division; UKIP's pressure.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires causal analysis, not listing.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three constitutional consequences of Brexit for the UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Restoration of formal legal supremacy — the EU (Withdrawal) Act 2018 repealed the ECA 1972, ending EU law supremacy and ECJ jurisdiction while converting existing EU law into retained domestic law (renamed assimilated law by the REUL Act 2023). UK courts can no longer disapply an Act of Parliament on EU law grounds.\n• Strain on devolution — returning competences in devolved fields raised whether they should pass to the devolved bodies or be held centrally; the UK Internal Market Act 2020 was passed over the objections of both the Scottish Parliament and the Senedd, exposing Sewel's lack of legal force.\n• The Northern Ireland Protocol — created trade friction within the UK, strongly opposed by unionists, contributing to the collapse of power-sharing from 2022 until February 2024, resolved after the Windsor Framework (2023).\n• Executive power and scrutiny — extensive delegated powers to amend retained EU law by statutory instrument, with minimal parliamentary debate.\n• Popular vs parliamentary sovereignty — a legally advisory vote acquiring binding political authority.\n\nGuidance: credit any three developed with analysis.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three reasons why EU membership was politically contentious in the UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Sovereignty — the supremacy of EU law and ECJ jurisdiction sat uneasily with a constitution founded on parliamentary sovereignty, and integration deepened incrementally through successive treaties without further popular consent.\n• Free movement and immigration — substantial migration on which membership meant no government could act, fusing the immigration question to the sovereignty question.\n• The democratic deficit — key powers resting with the appointed Commission and the Council rather than the directly elected Parliament, with low turnout in European elections.\n• Party management — Europe cut ACROSS rather than along party lines, splitting the Conservatives between single-market supporters and sovereignty-focused sceptics from Maastricht onwards, and creating a problem successive leaders could not contain.\n• Electoral pressure from UKIP, amplified by proportional European elections.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires causal analysis of WHY each generated conflict.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'EXTRACTS (written for practice):\n\nExtract 1 — written for this practice question in the style of an opinion piece by a Eurosceptic former MP\n"For almost half a century, membership of the European Union meant that Parliament was no longer the supreme law-maker in its own country. Under the European Communities Act 1972, EU law took precedence over Acts of Parliament, and in the Factortame case the courts disapplied part of an Act of Parliament because it conflicted with European law. Qualified majority voting meant Britain could be outvoted and still bound by the result, and the European Court of Justice, not British judges, had the final word on EU law. Vast areas of policy — trade, agriculture, fisheries, competition — were decided in Brussels. The 2016 referendum slogan "Take back control" resonated because it described a real loss of self-government."\n\nExtract 2 — written for this practice question in the style of an article by a constitutional law academic who argues that sovereignty was pooled, not lost\n"The claim that EU membership destroyed parliamentary sovereignty confuses the use of sovereignty with its loss. Parliament chose to join through the European Communities Act 1972, and EU law had priority only because that Act said so. Parliament always kept the legal power to leave — and it did: Article 50 was triggered in 2017 under legislation Parliament passed, the European Union (Withdrawal) Act 2018 repealed the 1972 Act, and the UK left on 31 January 2020. Many areas, including direct taxation, most welfare, health, education and defence, remained national decisions throughout. Britain secured opt-outs from the single currency and the Schengen area. Pooling sovereignty with other states brought influence over rules that would have affected Britain anyway."\n\nAnalyse, evaluate and compare the arguments in the above extracts regarding the impact of EU membership on UK parliamentary sovereignty.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — students should analyse and compare the arguments in both extracts and test them against their own knowledge, for example:\n• LEGAL VERSUS POLITICAL SOVEREIGNTY: the core disagreement. Extract 1 describes a practical loss of law-making supremacy; Extract 2 argues that legal sovereignty was delegated and retained because it could be taken back. The strongest answers use this distinction to judge which extract describes membership more accurately.\n• FACTORTAME AND PRIMACY: Extract 1 is accurate that UK courts disapplied parts of the Merchant Shipping Act 1988 (Factortame, 1990–91), and EU primacy dated back to Costa v ENEL (1964). Extract 2 answers that primacy rested on the ECA 1972 — a view reflected in Thoburn (2002), where the ECA was treated as a "constitutional statute" whose authority derived from Parliament.\n• QUALIFIED MAJORITY VOTING AND THE ECJ: Extract 1's strongest point — QMV expanded through the Single European Act (1986), Maastricht and Lisbon, so the UK could be bound by laws it opposed. Extract 2 counters with national vetoes in sensitive areas and opt-outs; evaluate how often the UK was actually outvoted relative to how often it shaped the rules.\n• THE RIGHT TO LEAVE: Extract 2's strongest point — the sequence of the 2016 referendum, Miller I (2017), the European Union (Notification of Withdrawal) Act 2017, the Withdrawal Act 2018 and exit on 31 January 2020 shows that Parliament's sovereignty was intact enough to reverse membership. Extract 1 does not address this.\n• SCOPE OF EU COMPETENCE: the extracts select different policy areas. Extract 1 lists exclusive or shared EU competences (trade, agriculture, fisheries, competition); Extract 2 lists areas that remained national. Both are broadly accurate; evaluation should weigh the significance of each.\n• POST-BREXIT EVIDENCE: students may note the Retained EU Law (Revocation and Reform) Act 2023 and the continuing application of some EU single-market rules in Northern Ireland under the Windsor Framework (2023), which qualifies how completely sovereignty was "taken back". Synoptic link to devolution: repatriated powers led to disputes resolved by the UK Internal Market Act 2020.\n• STATUS: Extract 1 is by a Eurosceptic former politician, whose purpose is to justify Brexit, so it emphasises constraint and uses campaign language. Extract 2 is by a legal academic focused on formal legal sovereignty, which may understate political and practical constraints. The two authors are effectively using different definitions of sovereignty.\n\nJudgement: a supported conclusion is likely to find Extract 2 more convincing about legal sovereignty, since Parliament did legislate to leave, while accepting Extract 1's case that, during membership, Parliament's practical freedom to legislate was significantly constrained. Any conclusion supported by comparison of the extracts should be credited.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and comparing the arguments in the extracts and weighing which are more convincing; evaluate their status (author, type of publication, purpose, date) where relevant. Both extracts were written for practice, so status evaluation should focus on the type of author and purpose stated in each label. Own knowledge should be used to test the extracts' claims, not to replace discussion of them. Answers that do not focus on the arguments in the extracts cannot go above Level 2.` },

      { q: '"Brexit has had a damaging effect on the UK constitution." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `DAMAGING:\n• Created an unresolvable clash between popular and parliamentary sovereignty — a legally advisory vote acquiring binding political authority against a parliamentary majority that favoured Remain.\n• Produced years of deadlock, an unlawful prorogation, two landmark Supreme Court cases and an early general election.\n• Exposed and then damaged the devolution settlement — the UK Internal Market Act 2020 passed over withheld consent from Scotland and Wales; Sewel shown to be legally worthless.\n• The Northern Ireland Protocol created trade friction within the UK and contributed to a two-year Assembly suspension.\n• Massive delegated powers to amend retained EU law with minimal scrutiny.\n• Drew the judiciary into political controversy and prompted restrictions on judicial review.\n\nNOT DAMAGING / beneficial:\n• Restored formal legal supremacy to Parliament; courts can no longer disapply statute on EU law grounds.\n• Demonstrated that the constitution can absorb an extraordinary shock without breakdown — the institutions functioned.\n• Miller I and II showed the courts capable of constraining executive overreach.\n• Resolved a question that had destabilised both main parties for decades.\n• Returned significant policy competence to elected UK institutions.\n\nGuidance for L5: should distinguish damage to the constitutional SETTLEMENT (substantial, particularly devolution) from damage to constitutional FUNCTIONING (the institutions worked). The strongest conclusions note that Brexit did not create these tensions but exposed ones that were already unresolved.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },

      { q: '"The European Union suffered from a democratic deficit." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `YES:\n• The Commission — which proposes all legislation and enforces the treaties — is appointed, not elected, and cannot be removed by voters.\n• Turnout in European Parliament elections is consistently low, weakening the Parliament's democratic claim.\n• Qualified majority voting means a member state can be bound by law its government and electorate opposed.\n• Complexity and remoteness — few citizens understood the institutions or could identify their MEP.\n• Integration deepened through treaties negotiated by governments, frequently without referendums.\n• The ECJ, an appointed body, held final interpretive authority.\n\nNO:\n• The European Parliament IS directly elected and its powers were substantially strengthened by Lisbon; it must approve legislation and the budget.\n• The Council consists of ministers from democratically elected national governments, so a second chain of accountability exists.\n• Treaties required ratification by every member state according to its own constitutional procedures.\n• National parliaments scrutinise their governments' EU positions.\n• Subsidiarity requires decisions at the lowest effective level.\n• Comparable complexity and remoteness exist in any large federal or multi-level system.\n\nGuidance for L5: should note that the critique assumes a national model of democracy may not translate to a supranational body, and that the deficit was a genuine grievance AND a politically useful one for Eurosceptics regardless of its merits. That double point is the mark of L5.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },

      { q: '"The UK was always an awkward partner in the European Union." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `AWKWARD:\n• Joined late (1973), having declined to join at the founding, and held a renegotiation referendum within two years.\n• Secured opt-outs from the euro, Schengen and initially the Social Chapter — differentiated integration throughout.\n• Consistently resisted "ever closer union" and political integration while embracing the single market.\n• Europe split the governing party repeatedly — Maastricht rebels, and division from the 1990s onwards.\n• Persistent budget rebate disputes.\n• The only member state to leave.\n\nNOT SIMPLY AWKWARD:\n• The UK was a leading architect of the single market — the Single European Act 1986 reflected British priorities.\n• It supported enlargement to central and eastern Europe, shaping the EU's development substantially.\n• It complied with EU law at rates comparable to or better than many member states.\n• Other member states also secured opt-outs and resisted integration (Denmark, Ireland).\n• Public opinion was not consistently hostile — the 1975 referendum produced a ~67% Yes vote, and polling fluctuated considerably.\n• "Awkwardness" reflects a genuine constitutional difference — parliamentary sovereignty — rather than mere obstinacy.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest analysis explains awkwardness STRUCTURALLY — an uncodified constitution founded on parliamentary sovereignty fits poorly with a supranational legal order — rather than treating it as a national character trait.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },
    ]
  },

  /* ══════════════════════ PAPER 2 — USA AND COMPARATIVE POLITICS ══════════════════════ */

  '2.1': {
    name: 'The US Constitution and Federalism',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three principles of the US Constitution.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Separation of powers — legislative, executive and judicial functions vested in separate institutions with separate personnel; a member of Congress cannot simultaneously serve in the executive. Analysis: the opposite of UK fusion, and the reason a President has no guaranteed legislative majority.\n• Checks and balances — the branches deliberately overlap, each holding specific powers over the others (veto and override, confirmation, ratification, judicial review, impeachment). Analysis: makes gridlock the intended consequence rather than a malfunction.\n• Federalism — power constitutionally divided between national and state government, with the Tenth Amendment reserving undelegated powers to the states.\n• Entrenchment — a double supermajority for amendment, protecting the whole structure from temporary majorities.\n• Limited government and the Bill of Rights placing liberties beyond ordinary legislation.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires analysis of CONSEQUENCES.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which federal power has expanded in the USA.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Broad interpretation of the commerce clause — from the New Deal the Court accepted Congress could regulate almost any activity substantially affecting interstate commerce, underpinning federal regulation of wages, agriculture, civil rights in public accommodation and the environment. Analysis: achieved entirely through interpretation, not amendment; partially reversed in United States v Lopez (1995).\n• Fiscal federalism — the Sixteenth Amendment gave Washington revenues the states could not match, and conditional grants allow it to direct state policy where it cannot legislate directly (the national minimum drinking age via highway funding). Analysis: circumvents the constitutional division entirely, since states remain formally sovereign but financially unable to refuse.\n• Crisis and war — the Depression, the Second World War and the Cold War each concentrated authority in Washington.\n• Incorporation of the Bill of Rights against the states through the Fourteenth Amendment.\n\nGuidance: the fiscal point is what separates L3 from L2.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three checks that Congress holds over the President.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Overriding a veto by a two-thirds vote in both chambers. Analysis: rarely achieved, which makes the veto a formidable presidential power.\n• The power of the purse — all federal spending requires appropriation, so Congress can refuse funds, attach conditions or force a shutdown. Analysis: the most concrete lever, since it requires no executive cooperation.\n• Senate confirmation of appointments (simple majority) and ratification of treaties (two-thirds). Analysis: the two-thirds threshold has defeated major agreements, which is why presidents increasingly use executive agreements instead.\n• Impeachment by the House and conviction by a two-thirds Senate majority. Analysis: the ultimate sanction, but no President has ever been convicted.\n• Oversight through committee investigations and subpoenas.\n\nGuidance: credit the analytical point that these checks operate powerfully under divided government and are largely unused under unified government.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three strengths of the US Constitution.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Effective dispersal of power — separation of powers, checks and balances and federalism together ensure no institution governs unchecked; presidents have been constrained by impeachment, adverse rulings, congressional refusal and midterm losses.\n• Entrenched rights — the Bill of Rights and Reconstruction amendments place liberties beyond ordinary majorities and make them judicially enforceable against both federal and state government.\n• Stability with adaptability — it has survived civil war, depression and profound social change, with judicial interpretation supplying flexibility the amendment process cannot.\n• Federalism accommodates diversity across a continental state and permits policy experimentation.\n\nGuidance: one-sided by design, but top-band answers may note that each strength is the direct source of a corresponding weakness.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three weaknesses of the US Constitution.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Gridlock — the separation of powers, divided government and the filibuster mean major legislation frequently fails even when a President has just won a national mandate. Analysis: this is the intended consequence of the design, so whether it is a weakness depends on whether one fears government doing too much or too little.\n• Rigidity — the double supermajority means only 27 amendments in 240 years, so provisions widely regarded as obsolete persist. The Electoral College has twice since 2000 elected a President who lost the popular vote, with majority public support for reform.\n• Transfer of constitutional authorship to the Court — because amendment is near-impossible, change happens through judicial interpretation, so nine unelected justices effectively amend.\n• Undemocratic features — Senate malapportionment gives Wyoming and California equal representation.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires analysis of why each matters.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three types of power in the US federal system.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Enumerated (delegated) powers — expressly granted to the federal government: declaring war, coining money, regulating interstate commerce, raising armies.\n• Implied powers — inferred from the enumerated powers via the necessary and proper (elastic) clause. Analysis: the principal engine of federal expansion, since it allows Congress to legislate on matters not expressly listed.\n• Reserved powers — under the Tenth Amendment, all powers not delegated federally nor prohibited to the states: education, policing, the conduct of elections, and since Dobbs, abortion regulation. Analysis: these are why rights vary substantially by state.\n• Concurrent powers — held by both levels, such as taxation and law enforcement.\n\nAlso relevant: the supremacy clause establishing that federal law prevails over conflicting state law.\n\nGuidance: credit accurate examples for each category.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'EXTRACTS (written for practice):\n\nExtract 1 — written for this practice question in the style of an opinion piece by an American law professor who campaigns for constitutional reform\n"The United States is governed by a document drafted for thirteen states and fewer than four million people. It has been amended only twenty-seven times, and not since 1992. Article V now demands a level of agreement that a polarised nation cannot reach, so obvious flaws simply persist. The Electoral College has twice since 2000 handed the presidency to the loser of the national popular vote. Wyoming and California each send two senators to Washington, although California has more than sixty times as many people. And because the people cannot change the text, it is changed instead by nine unelected judges, whose rulings on abortion or guns now matter more than most elections. A constitution that cannot be reformed democratically is no longer fit for purpose."\n\nExtract 2 — written for this practice question in the style of a newspaper column by a conservative commentator who defends the framers\' design\n"Critics call the Constitution outdated, yet few documents have proved so durable. It has survived a civil war, a depression and two world wars, and every President since George Washington has taken office under its rules. Its difficulty of amendment is a strength: it forces reformers to build a broad national consensus rather than relying on a temporary majority, and it keeps basic rights out of the hands of whoever won the last election. Nor is it frozen. Its broad language has allowed Congress and the courts to apply it to the railway, the internet and the modern economy. Federalism lets fifty states pursue very different policies, so a divided nation does not have to agree on everything. Gridlock is the price of liberty, not proof of failure."\n\nAnalyse, evaluate and compare the arguments in the above extracts regarding whether the US Constitution remains fit for purpose.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `EXTRACT 1 — THE CONSTITUTION IS NOT FIT FOR PURPOSE:\n• RIGIDITY: Extract 1 argues Article V has become an insurmountable barrier. Own knowledge supports this: only 27 amendments, the last (the 27th) ratified in 1992 although proposed in 1789; the Equal Rights Amendment passed Congress in 1972 but was not ratified by enough states within the deadline. Evaluation: rigidity was intended by the framers, and Extract 2 presents the same feature as a protection — the extracts disagree about the VALUE of entrenchment, not the fact of it.\n• UNDEMOCRATIC FEATURES: the Electoral College (2000 and 2016, when the popular-vote loser won) and equal Senate representation regardless of population. Evaluation: these were deliberate federal compromises protecting small states; the popular-vote winner also won the Electoral College in 2020 and 2024, so the problem is real but intermittent.\n• JUDGES AS AUTHORS: because amendment is impractical, change comes through interpretation — Dobbs v Jackson (2022) and NYSRPA v Bruen (2022) are examples students may use. Evaluation: this undermines popular sovereignty, but it is also the mechanism Extract 2 praises as adaptability.\nEXTRACT 2 — THE CONSTITUTION REMAINS FIT FOR PURPOSE:\n• DURABILITY AND CONSENSUS: survival through civil war, depression and world wars; supermajority requirements force broad agreement and protect rights from temporary majorities (Brown v Board 1954 and Obergefell v Hodges 2015 as examples of entrenched rights protecting minorities). Evaluation: durability alone does not prove fitness — the 2021 attack on the Capitol and disputes over the 2020 result show the system under strain.\n• ADAPTABILITY: broad language, e.g. the commerce and necessary-and-proper clauses, has let federal power grow without amendment. Evaluation: adaptation depends on the composition of the Court, so the constitution means different things at different times — which supports Extract 1's concern about unelected authorship.\n• FEDERALISM AND GRIDLOCK: states diverge sharply (abortion after Dobbs, cannabis, gun laws), accommodating a polarised country. Evaluation: divergence means rights depend on residence; and gridlock (shutdowns, failed legislation) pushes power towards executive orders and courts, arguably distorting the framers' design rather than fulfilling it.\nCOMPARISON AND STATUS:\n• Both are persuasive opinion pieces, written to advocate a position. Extract 1 is by a reform campaigner and emphasises democratic legitimacy; Extract 2 by a conservative commentator and emphasises stability and limited government. Students may note the extracts share a premise — that the text is hard to change and judges fill the gap — but evaluate it in opposite ways. Neither addresses the growth of executive power in any detail, which students can use to test both.\nJudgement: a strong answer might conclude that the Constitution's core structures still function and constrain government, but that its rigidity has transferred change to the Court and the presidency, so Extract 1 is more convincing on democratic legitimacy while Extract 2 is more convincing on durability. Any supported conclusion is valid.\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and comparing the arguments in the extracts and weighing which are more convincing; evaluate their status (author, type of publication, purpose, date) where relevant. Own knowledge should be used to test the claims the extracts make, not to write a free-standing essay. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant points should be credited; it does not matter which view they reach, provided it is supported. Answers that do not focus on the arguments in the extracts cannot go above Level 2.` },

      { q: '\'Power is more effectively decentralised under federalism in the US than under devolution in the UK.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `AGREE — US FEDERALISM DECENTRALISES POWER MORE EFFECTIVELY:\n• ENTRENCHMENT: the existence and powers of US states are guaranteed by the codified Constitution; the Tenth Amendment reserves undelegated powers to the states and any change needs an Article V amendment ratified by three-quarters of states. In the UK, the devolved bodies were created by ordinary Acts of Parliament (Scotland Act 1998, Government of Wales Act 1998, Northern Ireland Act 1998) and Parliament remains sovereign — it could in law abolish them.\n• OVERRIDE IN PRACTICE: the Sewel convention is not legally enforceable (Miller, 2017), and Westminster passed the UK Internal Market Act 2020 despite the Scottish Parliament and Senedd refusing consent. In January 2023 the UK government used a section 35 order for the first time to block the Gender Recognition Reform (Scotland) Bill. The Northern Ireland institutions have been suspended for long periods (2017–20 and 2022–24). Washington has no equivalent power to veto state laws or dissolve a state legislature.\n• SCOPE AND SYMMETRY: every US state has its own constitution and controls most criminal law, policing, education and the running of elections, producing sharp policy divergence (abortion after Dobbs, the death penalty, gun laws). UK devolution is asymmetric: England, with around 84% of the population, has no devolved legislature, and English combined authorities and mayors hold limited, delegated powers.\n• FISCAL POWER: states raise substantial revenue through their own income, sales and property taxes, whereas the devolved administrations depend largely on a block grant calculated by the Barnett formula, even though Scotland now sets its own income tax rates and bands.\nDISAGREE — THE CONTRAST IS OVERSTATED:\n• FEDERAL EXPANSION IN THE US: broad interpretation of the commerce clause, conditional grants (South Dakota v Dole, 1987) and federal preemption have centralised power; in June 2025 President Trump federalised California's National Guard over Governor Newsom's objection. State autonomy depends heavily on the Supreme Court's composition (Wickard v Filburn 1942 v United States v Lopez 1995).\n• DEVOLUTION IS POLITICALLY ENTRENCHED: devolution was approved by referendums in 1997 (Scotland and Wales) and 1998 (Northern Ireland); the Scotland Act 2016 and Wales Act 2017 declared the devolved institutions a permanent part of the constitution. Abolition is politically unthinkable, so legal sovereignty overstates Westminster's real power.\n• DEVOLVED DIVERGENCE IS REAL: Scotland has different policies on university tuition fees and minimum unit pricing for alcohol; Wales introduced a default 20mph limit; powers have grown with each Scotland and Wales Act. The UK is arguably moving in a quasi-federal direction.\n• COURTS AS UMPIRES IN BOTH: the UK Supreme Court ruled in 2022 that the Scottish Parliament could not legislate for an independence referendum; the US Supreme Court decides federal–state disputes constantly. In both, the centre's authority is ultimately upheld on the biggest questions.\n• SYNOPTIC LINKS: Paper 1 devolution and parliamentary sovereignty; the UK constitution; the US Supreme Court and judicial review; theory — structural (codified federal constitution v unitary state), cultural (American distrust of central government; distinct national identities in Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland), rational (UK governments devolved power partly to contain nationalist support).\nJudgement: a strong answer is likely to conclude that US decentralisation is more legally secure and more symmetrical, but that federal power has grown and that devolution has proved politically durable, so the gap is narrower in practice than in law. Any supported conclusion is valid.\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. The statement is comparative, so credit depends on sustained, explicit comparison of the US and the UK within each point: similarities and differences, and WHY they exist (structural, cultural or rational explanations). Answers that deal with only one country should not go above Level 2; answers that describe the two countries in separate, unlinked sections are unlikely to go above Level 3. Synoptic links are required — e.g. devolution and parliamentary sovereignty (Paper 1), the UK and US constitutions, the role of both supreme courts in federal/devolution disputes, and structural or cultural theory. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant points should be credited; it does not matter which view they reach, provided the conclusion is supported by the preceding argument.` },

      { q: '\'The separation of powers in the US produces less effective government than the fusion of powers in the UK.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `AGREE — US GOVERNMENT IS LESS EFFECTIVE:\n• LEGISLATIVE DELIVERY: a UK government with a Commons majority can usually enact its programme, because the executive sits in and controls the legislature — e.g. Labour used its 2024 majority to pass the Passenger Railway Services (Public Ownership) Act 2024 within months. A President must win over two separately elected chambers and, in the Senate, usually 60 votes to overcome a filibuster; divided government regularly stalls major bills.\n• BUDGET BREAKDOWN: the US has repeated government shutdowns and debt-ceiling crises; the Oct–Nov 2025 shutdown was the longest in US history. A UK government whose budget was defeated would face a confidence crisis, so fusion makes such breakdowns rare.\n• CLEAR ACCOUNTABILITY: in the UK voters know which party governs and can judge it at the next election; in the US separate branches can blame one another.\n• POLICY BY OTHER MEANS: because Congress stalls, US policy is increasingly made by executive orders and court rulings, which are less stable and less democratic (DACA was created by executive action in 2012 and has remained in legal limbo since).\nDISAGREE — THE STATEMENT MISJUDGES "EFFECTIVE":\n• DELIBERATION AND CONSENT: fusion can produce "elective dictatorship" (Hailsham) and poorly scrutinised policy — e.g. the 2022 mini-budget, announced without an OBR forecast, triggered market turmoil. US checks force compromise and broad support, e.g. the bipartisan Infrastructure Investment and Jobs Act 2021 and CHIPS and Science Act 2022.\n• UNIFIED US GOVERNMENT CAN DELIVER: with majorities in both chambers presidents can pass major laws, including through budget reconciliation, which needs only a simple Senate majority — e.g. the Affordable Care Act (2010) and the One Big Beautiful Bill Act (2025).\n• FUSION DOES NOT GUARANTEE DELIVERY: Theresa May's Brexit deal was defeated by 230 votes in January 2019, the largest government defeat in modern history; Keir Starmer, despite a majority of over 170, was forced into major concessions on welfare reform in 2025 after a backbench rebellion. Effectiveness in both systems depends on party unity and the size of majorities.\n• STABILITY OF POLICY: US laws that survive the process tend to endure (repeated attempts to repeal the ACA failed); UK policy can be reversed quickly by a new government or PM, e.g. the Rwanda scheme, legislated for in 2024 and scrapped by the incoming Labour government.\n• SYNOPTIC LINKS: Paper 1 Parliament, the PM and cabinet, and the UK constitution; party discipline and party systems; theory — structural (institutional design explains gridlock) and rational (legislators' re-election incentives differ).\nJudgement: a strong answer will define effectiveness — speed and delivery favour the UK, while deliberation, legitimacy and durability can favour the US — and may conclude that political circumstances (majority size, divided or unified government) matter as much as the constitutional model. Any supported conclusion is valid.\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. The statement is comparative, so credit depends on sustained, explicit comparison of the US and the UK within each point: similarities and differences, and WHY they exist (structural, cultural or rational explanations). Answers that deal with only one country should not go above Level 2; answers that describe the two countries in separate, unlinked sections are unlikely to go above Level 3. Synoptic links are required — e.g. the UK Parliament and executive (Paper 1), the UK constitution, party discipline and party systems, and structural or rational theory. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant points should be credited; it does not matter which view they reach, provided the conclusion is supported by the preceding argument.` },

      { q: '\'Constitutional change in the US depends on judges, whereas in the UK it depends on politicians.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `AGREE:\n• US AMENDMENT IS RARE, SO JUDGES CHANGE THE CONSTITUTION: no amendment since 1992, so constitutional meaning shifts through Supreme Court interpretation — Brown v Board (1954), Roe v Wade (1973) and its reversal in Dobbs (2022), Citizens United (2010), Obergefell (2015), and Trump v United States (2024), which recognised a presidential immunity found nowhere in the text. Loper Bright v Raimondo (2024) shifted power from federal agencies to courts by ending Chevron deference.\n• UK CHANGE IS MADE BY STATUTE: under parliamentary sovereignty the constitution changes through ordinary Acts passed by politicians — the Human Rights Act 1998, devolution Acts, the Constitutional Reform Act 2005, the Fixed-term Parliaments Act 2011 and its repeal by the Dissolution and Calling of Parliament Act 2022, and the European Union (Withdrawal) Act 2018. Referendums (2011, 2014, 2016) were called by governments.\n• UK COURTS CANNOT STRIKE DOWN ACTS: section 4 declarations of incompatibility leave the final decision to Parliament; US courts can invalidate federal and state laws.\nDISAGREE:\n• POLITICIANS DRIVE US JUDICIAL CHANGE: justices are nominated by presidents and confirmed by senators, and Dobbs was possible only after three appointments in one presidential term. Politicians also change constitutional practice directly — the War Powers Resolution 1973, the growth of executive orders, and Senate rule changes such as the 2013 and 2017 "nuclear option" on nominations. Amendments themselves are proposed and ratified by politicians.\n• UK JUDGES SHAPE THE CONSTITUTION: Factortame (1990) on EU law supremacy; Miller (2017) required an Act to trigger Article 50; Miller/Cherry (2019) held prorogation unlawful; R (UNISON) (2017) developed common-law rights of access to justice; the 2022 ruling on a Scottish independence referendum and For Women Scotland (2025) on the meaning of "sex" in the Equality Act both had major constitutional and political effects.\n• CONVENTIONS EVOLVE THROUGH POLITICAL PRACTICE IN BOTH: the UK convention of consulting the Commons before military action (2003, the Syria vote of 2013); in the US, party primaries and the role of parties have developed outside the text.\n• SYNOPTIC LINKS: Paper 1 UK constitution and the Supreme Court; US Supreme Court and judicial philosophy (originalism v living constitution); theory — structural (codified and entrenched v uncodified and flexible), cultural (a strong US legal-rights culture).\nJudgement: a strong answer is likely to accept the statement as a broad structural contrast — entrenchment channels US change through courts, sovereignty channels UK change through Parliament — while showing that politicians control who the US judges are and that UK judges increasingly shape constitutional boundaries. Any supported conclusion is valid.\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. The statement is comparative, so credit depends on sustained, explicit comparison of the US and the UK within each point: similarities and differences, and WHY they exist (structural, cultural or rational explanations). Answers that deal with only one country should not go above Level 2; answers that describe the two countries in separate, unlinked sections are unlikely to go above Level 3. Synoptic links are required — e.g. the UK constitution and parliamentary sovereignty (Paper 1), the UK Supreme Court, the US Supreme Court and appointments, and structural or cultural theory. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant points should be credited; it does not matter which view they reach, provided the conclusion is supported by the preceding argument.` },
    ]
  },

  '2.2': {
    name: 'Congress',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three functions of the US Congress.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Legislation — both chambers must pass an identical text; committees conduct detailed scrutiny and can kill a bill by declining to report it. Analysis: genuinely independent of the executive, unlike the UK Parliament, but polarisation means less is passed.\n• Representation — of a district (House) or state (Senate), with extensive constituency casework. Analysis: weak discipline allows members to vote on constituency grounds, making Congress highly responsive locally but poor at pursuing diffuse national interests.\n• Oversight — committee hearings, investigations and subpoenas. Analysis: vigour tracks partisan advantage, not constitutional duty.\n• The power of the purse — all federal spending requires appropriation.\n• Senate confirmation of appointments and ratification of treaties; impeachment.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires evaluation of how well each is performed.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three exclusive powers of the US Senate.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Confirming presidential appointments — cabinet secretaries, ambassadors and federal judges including Supreme Court justices, by simple majority; since 2017 a filibuster can no longer block a Supreme Court nomination, because cloture on nominations now also needs only a simple majority. Analysis: gives the Senate direct influence over the judiciary for decades, which is why Garland and Barrett became such flashpoints.\n• Ratifying treaties by a two-thirds majority. Analysis: a threshold that has defeated major agreements including the Treaty of Versailles, which is why presidents increasingly use executive agreements instead.\n• Trying impeachments, with conviction requiring two-thirds. Analysis: no President has ever been convicted, which shows the threshold's practical effect.\n• Electing the Vice President if no Electoral College majority.\n\nGuidance: penalise confusion with House exclusive powers (initiating revenue bills, bringing impeachment charges).\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which Congress oversees the executive branch.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Committee investigations and hearings — compelled testimony under oath and subpoenas for documents. Watergate and the January 6th committee are the landmark examples; the requirement to justify policy publicly disciplines administrations in advance.\n• The power of the purse — refusing funds, attaching conditions, or declining to fund government at all. Analysis: the most concrete lever because it needs no executive cooperation; used to cut off funding for Indochina operations and in the Boland Amendments.\n• Senate confirmation hearings — scrutiny of nominees and of the policies they will implement.\n• Impeachment — Clinton and Trump (twice) impeached by the House; none convicted.\n\nAnalysis expected: the decisive point is that oversight vigour tracks PARTISAN ADVANTAGE, not constitutional duty. Under unified government committees have little incentive to investigate their own administration.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three reasons why congressional incumbents are usually re-elected.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Structural advantages of office — name recognition, franked mail, staff resources, and a record of casework and federal funding secured for the district.\n• Fundraising — donors prefer likely winners and want continued access, so challengers are typically heavily outspent.\n• Safe districts — drawn deliberately through gerrymandering or produced by voters sorting geographically, so the general election is effectively decided in advance.\n\nAnalysis expected: House re-election rates routinely exceed 90%. The crucial consequence is that where the general election is a formality, the ONLY real threat is a primary challenge from the ideological flank — so members are rewarded for purity and punished for compromise, which is the principal institutional driver of polarisation. Senate incumbents are less secure, since statewide electorates cannot be gerrymandered.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which the filibuster affects the work of the Senate.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• It creates a de facto supermajority requirement — cloture normally needs 60 of 100 senators, so most legislation requires bipartisan support that polarisation has made unavailable.\n• It pushes legislating into budget reconciliation, which allows budget-related measures by simple majority but sharply restricts what can be included — the ACA's 2010 amendments, the 2017 tax cuts, the 2021 American Rescue Plan and the 2022 Inflation Reduction Act all used this route, although the ACA itself needed 60 votes for cloture.\n• It empowers individual senators and small blocs, since a single member can force delay, giving leverage no representative possesses.\n• Its removal for nominations (lower courts 2013, Supreme Court 2017) means appointments now pass on party lines, which transformed confirmation politics.\n\nAnalysis expected: defenders argue it forces consensus and protects minority interests; critics that it is an extra-constitutional veto allowing senators representing a small share of the population to block majority-supported legislation.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three factors that affect the power of Congress relative to the President.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Party control — the dominant variable. Under divided government Congress investigates aggressively, blocks appointments and refuses legislation; under unified government the same powers go largely unused because members will not damage their own administration.\n• The policy area — congressional checks operate far more effectively over domestic policy than foreign affairs and national security, where presidents have conducted sustained military action without declarations of war and Congress has been politically unwilling to cut funding for deployed troops.\n• Internal fragmentation — Congress is 535 individuals with separate electoral bases, rewarded for constituency service rather than for defending congressional prerogatives. It has repeatedly delegated authority over tariffs, emergencies and regulation because delegation lets members avoid responsibility.\n• Presidential approval ratings and the electoral cycle — honeymoon, midterm losses, lame duck.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires causal analysis.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: '\'Congress checks the President far more effectively than Parliament checks the Prime Minister.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `AGREE:\n• CONSTITUTIONAL POWERS: Congress holds the power of the purse, can override vetoes by two-thirds, can impeach (Trump was impeached twice, in 2019 and 2021), and the Senate confirms appointments and ratifies treaties by two-thirds. Nominees are sometimes forced to withdraw, e.g. Matt Gaetz as Attorney General in 2024. Parliament has no fixed constitutional powers; the government controls most of the Commons timetable and the Parliament Acts limit the Lords to delay.\n• SEPARATE MANDATES AND WEAKER DISCIPLINE: members of Congress are elected separately from the President and owe their careers to local voters and primaries, so they can defy a President of their own party. MPs depend on the party whips and many aspire to ministerial office, the "payroll vote".\n• DIVIDED GOVERNMENT: when the opposition controls a chamber, oversight is intense — e.g. House Democrats' investigations and impeachment of Trump in 2019, or House Republicans' use of the debt ceiling against Biden in 2023. A UK PM with a majority never faces a hostile chamber.\n• RESOURCES: congressional committees have large staffs and subpoena powers; UK select committees, despite the 2010 Wright reforms (elected chairs), have smaller resources and limited power to compel.\nDISAGREE:\n• PARTISANSHIP UNDER UNIFIED GOVERNMENT: since 2025 a Republican Congress has largely deferred to Trump — e.g. passing his 2025 rescissions package and rejecting a war powers resolution after the June 2025 strikes on Iranian nuclear sites. Neither impeachment led to conviction, because the Senate requires two-thirds. Checks depend on who controls Congress.\n• PRESIDENTS BYPASS CONGRESS: executive orders, emergency powers and executive agreements allow unilateral action; Congress rarely legislates to reverse them because a veto would need a two-thirds override.\n• PARLIAMENT CAN REMOVE THE PM: a PM depends on keeping the confidence of the Commons and of their party — Callaghan lost a confidence vote in 1979; Johnson was forced out in 2022 after mass ministerial resignations. A President has a fixed term and cannot be removed for political failure.\n• PARLIAMENT CAN DEFEAT GOVERNMENTS: May's Brexit deal was defeated by 230 votes in 2019; Cameron lost the 2013 Syria vote; Starmer, despite a majority over 170, conceded major changes to welfare reform in 2025. PMQs subjects the PM to weekly personal questioning; the President faces nothing comparable.\n• SYNOPTIC LINKS: Paper 1 Parliament and the PM; party systems and party discipline; theory — rational (legislators' incentives: re-election v promotion), structural (separation v fusion of powers).\nJudgement: a strong answer may conclude that Congress has far stronger formal powers but that its effectiveness now depends heavily on partisan control, while Parliament is formally weak but retains the ultimate sanction of removing a PM. Any supported conclusion is valid.\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. The statement is comparative, so credit depends on sustained, explicit comparison of the US and the UK within each point: similarities and differences, and WHY they exist (structural, cultural or rational explanations). Answers that deal with only one country should not go above Level 2; answers that describe the two countries in separate, unlinked sections are unlikely to go above Level 3. Synoptic links are required — e.g. the UK Parliament and PM (Paper 1), party discipline and party systems, the separation v fusion of powers, and rational or structural theory. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant points should be credited; it does not matter which view they reach, provided the conclusion is supported by the preceding argument.` },

      { q: '\'The US Senate is a far more powerful second chamber than the House of Lords.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `AGREE:\n• LEGITIMACY: senators have been directly elected since the Seventeenth Amendment (1913), so the Senate has a democratic mandate equal to the House. The Lords is appointed (plus hereditary peers and bishops) and, under the Salisbury convention, does not block manifesto commitments at second reading.\n• LEGISLATIVE POWER: the Senate is co-equal with the House and can block any bill outright. The Lords can only delay most bills for about a year (Parliament Acts 1911 and 1949) and money bills for one month.\n• EXCLUSIVE POWERS: the Senate confirms appointments (including Supreme Court justices), ratifies treaties by two-thirds and tries impeachments. The Lords has no comparable powers over the executive.\n• MINORITY POWER: the filibuster lets 41 senators block most legislation; there is nothing comparable in the Lords.\nDISAGREE:\n• THE LORDS STILL INFLUENCES LAW: it defeats the government frequently, its revising amendments are often accepted, and ministers make concessions to avoid defeats; the Parliament Acts have been used only rarely (most recently for the Hunting Act 2004). Crossbench expertise gives it authority on technical legislation. During passage of the Safety of Rwanda Bill in 2024 the Lords repeatedly sent amendments back to the Commons, though it ultimately gave way.\n• COMPARE LIKE WITH LIKE: the Senate is a partner in a separation-of-powers system, whereas the Lords is deliberately subordinate within a fused system; its weakness is by design and arguably appropriate for an unelected chamber.\n• SENATE POWER VARIES WITH PARTISANSHIP: under unified government the Senate confirms a President's nominees on party lines; the "nuclear option" (2013, 2017) removed the filibuster for all nominations, reducing minority influence.\n• REFORM DEBATES: Labour's 2024 manifesto pledged to remove the remaining hereditary peers, while calls to abolish or reform the filibuster continue in the US — both chambers' powers are contested.\n• SYNOPTIC LINKS: Paper 1 Parliament and the Lords; bicameralism; representation and democratic legitimacy; theory — structural (constitutional powers) and cultural (deference to an appointed chamber in the UK).\nJudgement: a strong answer is likely to agree — the Senate is arguably the most powerful upper chamber in the world, and the Lords is legally subordinate — but may note that the Lords' influence is greater than its formal powers suggest and that the Senate's power depends on partisan control. Any supported conclusion is valid.\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. The statement is comparative, so credit depends on sustained, explicit comparison of the US and the UK within each point: similarities and differences, and WHY they exist (structural, cultural or rational explanations). Answers that deal with only one country should not go above Level 2; answers that describe the two countries in separate, unlinked sections are unlikely to go above Level 3. Synoptic links are required — e.g. the House of Lords and Parliament (Paper 1), bicameralism, democratic legitimacy and representation, and structural or cultural theory. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant points should be credited; it does not matter which view they reach, provided the conclusion is supported by the preceding argument.` },

      { q: 'EXTRACTS (written for practice):\n\nExtract 1 — written for this practice question in the style of an opinion piece by a campaigner for Senate reform\n"The filibuster is not in the Constitution, and for most of American history it was rarely used. Today it means that almost every significant bill needs sixty votes in a hundred-member Senate, so senators representing a minority of Americans can block laws that a majority wants. The framers knew the dangers of supermajority rules; they had lived under the Articles of Confederation. The result is not careful deliberation but paralysis: Congress stalls, and power drains to presidents who govern by executive order and to judges who fill the gaps. Senators have already abolished the filibuster for every nomination, including to the Supreme Court. The majority should finish the job and let voters judge parties on what they actually pass."\n\nExtract 2 — written for this practice question in the style of a newspaper column by a former Senate staff member who defends the chamber\'s traditions\n"Every party that controls the Senate is tempted to scrap the filibuster, and every party is glad it did not once it is back in the minority. The sixty-vote rule forces the majority to win over at least some of the other side, which is why lasting laws, such as the infrastructure law of 2021, tend to be bipartisan. It protects the interests of smaller states and of the minority party, in keeping with the Senate\'s role as the cooler, more deliberative chamber. Without it, national policy would lurch back and forth after every election. Nor does it block everything: budget measures can pass by a simple majority through reconciliation, as Republicans showed with their major tax and spending law in 2025."\n\nAnalyse, evaluate and compare the arguments in the above extracts regarding whether the Senate filibuster should be abolished.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `EXTRACT 1 — ABOLISH THE FILIBUSTER:\n• MINORITY VETO: 60 votes are needed for cloture, so 41 senators — who may represent far less than half the population, given equal state representation — can block legislation. Evaluation: this is accurate, and own knowledge shows its effects, e.g. Democrats' voting-rights legislation failed in January 2022 when an attempt to change the rules was defeated because two Democratic senators, Manchin and Sinema, opposed it.\n• NOT PART OF THE FRAMERS' DESIGN: the Constitution specifies supermajorities only for particular purposes (treaties, veto overrides, impeachment convictions, amendments). Evaluation: a strong point against Extract 2's appeal to tradition, though the Senate was always intended to slow legislation.\n• PARALYSIS SHIFTS POWER: students may link this to the growth of executive orders and judicial policy-making; the Oct–Nov 2025 shutdown, the longest in US history, during which President Trump urged Republicans to end the filibuster and Senate Republican leaders declined, illustrates both the pressure and the resistance.\n• PRECEDENT OF THE NUCLEAR OPTION: in 2013 Democrats removed the filibuster for executive and lower-court nominations; in 2017 Republicans extended this to Supreme Court nominations. Evaluation: this shows abolition is possible, but also that each party now fears being the minority.\nEXTRACT 2 — KEEP THE FILIBUSTER:\n• FORCES BIPARTISANSHIP AND DURABILITY: the Infrastructure Investment and Jobs Act 2021 passed the Senate with support from both parties. Evaluation: much bipartisanship is now rare because of polarisation, so the filibuster more often produces deadlock than compromise; historically it was used by southern senators to obstruct civil rights legislation, which weakens the claim that it protects minorities.\n• STABILITY: without it, policy could swing with each change of Senate control. Evaluation: supporters of Extract 1 would reply that voters should be able to hold majorities accountable for what they do.\n• RECONCILIATION AS A SAFETY VALVE: accurate — Republicans passed the One Big Beautiful Bill Act by reconciliation in 2025, as Democrats had passed the American Rescue Plan in 2021. Evaluation: reconciliation is restricted to budgetary matters, so it cannot deliver on issues such as immigration, voting rights or gun control, which partly supports Extract 1.\nCOMPARISON AND STATUS:\n• Both are persuasive pieces. Extract 1 is by a reform campaigner and frames the issue as majority rule; Extract 2 is by a former Senate insider and may reflect institutional loyalty. Both accept that the filibuster shapes what Congress can do; they disagree on whether obstruction produces deliberation or paralysis. Students may make synoptic links to UK bicameralism, where the unelected Lords cannot permanently block the elected Commons.\nJudgement: a strong answer weighs majoritarian democracy against minority protection and may conclude that Extract 1 is more convincing about current effects, while Extract 2 correctly identifies why neither party has abolished it. Any supported conclusion is valid.\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and comparing the arguments in the extracts and weighing which are more convincing; evaluate their status (author, type of publication, purpose, date) where relevant. Own knowledge should be used to test the claims the extracts make, not to write a free-standing essay. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant points should be credited; it does not matter which view they reach, provided it is supported. Answers that do not focus on the arguments in the extracts cannot go above Level 2.` },

      { q: '\'Members of Congress represent their constituents more effectively than MPs do.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `AGREE:\n• WEAKER PARTY DISCIPLINE: members of Congress frequently vote against their party on local interests, because there is no confidence vote and no power of patronage over them. MPs who rebel face sanctions — e.g. Labour suspended the whip from seven MPs in July 2024 after they voted against the government on the two-child benefit cap.\n• PRIMARIES AND SHORT TERMS: House members face election every two years and must win a primary, so they are highly responsive to local voters; UK candidate selection is controlled by local and national party organisations.\n• RESOURCES FOR CASEWORK AND LOCAL BENEFITS: large staffs, district offices and earmarks (restored as "community project funding" in 2021) let members deliver for their districts; MPs' offices and influence over spending are much smaller.\n• SEPARATE MANDATE: a member of Congress is not expected to sustain a government in office, so constituency interests can take priority; an MP is elected largely as a representative of a party's manifesto.\nDISAGREE:\n• GERRYMANDERING AND SAFE SEATS: partisan redistricting leaves few competitive House seats, e.g. Texas redrew its congressional map mid-decade in 2025 to favour Republicans. Primaries in safe seats reward activists rather than the typical voter. UK boundaries are drawn by independent Boundary Commissions.\n• MONEY AND ORGANISED INTERESTS: after Citizens United (2010), super PACs and lobbying mean members may respond to donors more than constituents, and much of their time is spent fundraising. UK campaign spending is capped and paid political advertising on TV is banned.\n• DESCRIPTIVE REPRESENTATION: around 40% of MPs elected in 2024 were women, compared with just over a quarter of members of Congress.\n• DIFFERENT MODELS OF REPRESENTATION: MPs are Burkean trustees and party representatives; the party-mandate model gives UK voters a clear choice of programme. MPs also hold constituency surgeries and act as local advocates, and free votes on conscience issues (e.g. assisted dying, 2024–25) let MPs follow their own judgement or constituents' views.\n• SYNOPTIC LINKS: Paper 1 representation, Parliament, elections and electoral systems; pressure groups and campaign finance; theories of representation (delegate, trustee, mandate); rational theory (legislators' incentives).\nJudgement: a strong answer will distinguish types of representation — members of Congress are better local delegates, but gerrymandering, money and polarisation weaken their responsiveness, while MPs represent through party programmes and are more socially representative. Any supported conclusion is valid.\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. The statement is comparative, so credit depends on sustained, explicit comparison of the US and the UK within each point: similarities and differences, and WHY they exist (structural, cultural or rational explanations). Answers that deal with only one country should not go above Level 2; answers that describe the two countries in separate, unlinked sections are unlikely to go above Level 3. Synoptic links are required — e.g. representation and Parliament (Paper 1), elections and electoral systems, campaign finance and pressure groups, and theories of representation or rational theory. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant points should be credited; it does not matter which view they reach, provided the conclusion is supported by the preceding argument.` },
    ]
  },

  '2.3': {
    name: 'The Presidency',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three formal powers of the US President.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• The veto — refusing assent to legislation, overridable only by a two-thirds vote in both chambers. Analysis: rarely overridden, making it formidable — but it is a NEGATIVE power, allowing a President to prevent outcomes rather than produce them.\n• Commander-in-Chief — directing the armed forces, though only Congress may declare war. Analysis: presidents have conducted sustained military action without declarations since 1942.\n• Appointments — nominating cabinet secretaries, ambassadors and federal judges including Supreme Court justices, subject to Senate confirmation. Analysis: judicial appointments extend presidential influence decades beyond the term.\n• Treaties — negotiating, subject to two-thirds Senate ratification.\n• The pardon power for federal offences.\n\nGuidance: Neustadt's point that the formal powers are largely negative is what separates L3 from L2.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three informal powers of the US President.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Executive orders — directives to the federal executive branch with the force of law, requiring no congressional approval. Analysis: attractive under gridlock, but reversible by a successor and strikeable by the courts, so they produce oscillation rather than settled policy.\n• Executive agreements — international agreements concluded without Senate ratification, avoiding the two-thirds threshold. Analysis: the cost is impermanence, as the abandonment of the Iran nuclear agreement and the Paris accord showed.\n• The bully pulpit — using the visibility of the office to shape public opinion and pressure Congress. Analysis: Neustadt regarded persuasion as the essence of presidential power, but polarisation has reduced the number of persuadable legislators, so appeals now mobilise the base and harden the opposition.\n• Signing statements and direction of agency enforcement priorities.\n• Party leadership and the electoral mandate.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires the trade-off for each.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three limits on the power of the US President.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Congress — cannot legislate, cannot appropriate money, and requires Senate confirmation and treaty ratification. Analysis: has defeated major initiatives of almost every modern presidency, but operates inconsistently, powerfully under divided government and weakly under unified.\n• The courts — can strike down executive orders and enforcement policies as exceeding authority. Youngstown (1952) invalidated Truman's steel seizure; Biden v Nebraska (2023) struck down student loan forgiveness and DHS v Regents (2020) blocked the rescission of DACA — though the Supreme Court upheld the final travel ban in Trump v Hawaii (2018). Analysis: significance has grown precisely because presidents shifted from legislation to executive action, which is more legally vulnerable.\n• Federalism and the bureaucracy — many powers rest with states, which can decline to cooperate, and the federal bureaucracy is large, permanent and resistant to direction.\n• The two-term limit and the electoral cycle; impeachment.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires analysis of when each bites.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three reasons why presidents increasingly rely on executive orders.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Congressional gridlock — polarisation, the filibuster and frequent divided government mean major legislation often cannot pass, so executive action is the only route to delivering campaign commitments. Obama's use of DACA after the DREAM Act failed is the standard example.\n• Speed and unilateral control — an order takes effect immediately, requires no negotiation or concession, and can be drafted entirely within the executive branch.\n• Broad statutory delegations — Congress has granted extensive discretion in immigration, environmental and trade policy, which presidents exploit.\n• Political demonstration — presidents issue orders at the start of a term to show delivery and to reverse a predecessor's.\n\nAnalysis expected: the weakness is impermanence. Because orders rest on presidential authority rather than statute, a successor can revoke them immediately and the courts can strike them down — producing policy that oscillates rather than settles.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three factors that affect the power of a US President.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Party control of Congress — the dominant variable; unified government enables legislation, divided government largely prevents it.\n• The electoral cycle — the honeymoon period brings maximum authority, so major achievements are heavily front-loaded; the President's party almost always loses seats at the midterms, usually ending the legislative programme; lame duck status drains authority further.\n• Approval ratings — legislators support a popular President whose coattails help them and distance themselves from an unpopular one.\n• Events — crisis can enhance authority (9/11) or destroy it.\n• Personal skill in persuasion and bargaining — Neustadt's central argument.\n\nAnalysis expected: the FORMAL powers are constant throughout; what varies is political capital. That is why the same office produces very different presidencies.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which the President conducts foreign policy.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• As Commander-in-Chief — directing military deployments. Analysis: no declaration of war since 1942, and the War Powers Resolution 1973 has proved largely ineffective because presidents dispute its constitutionality and Congress will not cut funding for deployed troops.\n• Treaty negotiation — subject to two-thirds Senate ratification, a threshold that has defeated major agreements.\n• Executive agreements — concluded without Senate ratification, now the dominant instrument. Analysis: quicker and easier, but a successor can withdraw unilaterally.\n• Recognition of foreign governments, appointment of ambassadors, and direction of the State Department and NSC.\n• The National Security Council within EXOP centralises foreign policy in the White House rather than the State Department.\n\nAnalysis expected: presidential dominance is greatest here, because secrecy, speed and congressional deference all favour the executive.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: '\'The US President is more constrained in foreign policy and war-making than the UK Prime Minister.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `AGREE:\n• CONSTITUTIONAL CHECKS: Congress declares war, controls funding and regulates the armed forces; the Senate ratifies treaties by two-thirds and confirms ambassadors. The Senate has rejected major treaties (the Treaty of Versailles in 1919, the Comprehensive Nuclear-Test-Ban Treaty in 1999). The War Powers Resolution 1973 requires notification and limits deployments without authorisation.\n• CONGRESS CAN LEGISLATE AGAINST THE PRESIDENT: e.g. the Countering America's Adversaries Through Sanctions Act 2017 restricted President Trump's ability to lift sanctions on Russia.\n• THE PM USES THE ROYAL PREROGATIVE: deploying armed forces and making treaties are prerogative powers exercised by the PM and ministers. The Constitutional Reform and Governance Act 2010 lets the Commons delay, but not veto, treaty ratification. The convention of a Commons vote before military action (2003, 2013) is not binding — May ordered strikes on Syria in 2018 without a prior vote.\nDISAGREE:\n• PRESIDENTIAL UNILATERALISM: Congress last formally declared war in 1942; presidents act as Commander in Chief without authorisation — Obama in Libya (2011), Trump's killing of Soleimani (2020), and the June 2025 strikes on Iranian nuclear sites, after which the Senate rejected a war powers resolution. The 2001 AUMF has been used for over two decades against groups not involved in 9/11.\n• EXECUTIVE AGREEMENTS BYPASS THE SENATE: the 2015 Iran nuclear deal and the Paris climate agreement were joined without Senate ratification, and Trump withdrew from both without Congress.\n• POLITICAL CONSTRAINTS ON THE PM: Cameron lost the 2013 Commons vote on Syria and did not take military action; Blair sought a Commons vote before the 2003 Iraq invasion. The PM is also constrained by cabinet, collective responsibility and the need to keep party support.\n• RELATIVE POWER: the President commands the world's largest military and a large national security apparatus (NSC, State Department); UK action often depends on alliances, notably with the US, so the PM is constrained by capability more than by Parliament.\n• SYNOPTIC LINKS: Paper 1 the PM, royal prerogative and conventions; the US Constitution and checks and balances; theory — structural (formal constitutional powers) and rational (Congress's incentive to avoid responsibility for military action).\nJudgement: a strong answer is likely to conclude that the President is more constrained ON PAPER, but that in practice both leaders dominate foreign policy, with the PM more constrained by political convention and capability and the President by Congress only when it chooses to act. Any supported conclusion is valid.\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. The statement is comparative, so credit depends on sustained, explicit comparison of the US and the UK within each point: similarities and differences, and WHY they exist (structural, cultural or rational explanations). Answers that deal with only one country should not go above Level 2; answers that describe the two countries in separate, unlinked sections are unlikely to go above Level 3. Synoptic links are required — e.g. the PM, royal prerogative and conventions (Paper 1), the US Constitution and checks and balances, and structural or rational theory. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant points should be credited; it does not matter which view they reach, provided the conclusion is supported by the preceding argument.` },

      { q: '\'The power of both the US President and the UK Prime Minister depends more on political circumstances than on constitutional powers.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `AGREE:\n• THE POWER TO PERSUADE: Neustadt argued presidential power is the power to persuade; it rises and falls with approval ratings, party control of Congress and the electoral cycle (lame-duck periods). Trump with a unified Republican Congress in 2025 was far stronger than Trump facing a Democratic House in 2019–21.\n• THE PM AND MAJORITIES: a PM's power depends on the size and loyalty of their majority — Blair (majority of 179 in 1997) dominated; May lost her majority in 2017 and was repeatedly defeated over Brexit; Johnson, with a majority of 80, was still forced out in 2022 when ministers resigned; Truss lasted 49 days.\n• EVENTS AND CRISES: crises strengthen leaders in both countries — Bush after 9/11, and emergency powers used by both governments during COVID.\n• PERSONAL STANDING: popularity and party support give leaders authority to push through controversial policy; unpopular leaders are ignored by their parties.\nDISAGREE:\n• FIXED US CONSTITUTIONAL POWERS: whatever the circumstances, the President keeps the veto (rarely overridden), the pardon power (used extensively by both Biden in 2024 and Trump in 2025), the role of Commander in Chief and appointment powers; Trump v United States (2024) gave presidents broad immunity for official acts.\n• FIXED CONSTITUTIONAL LIMITS: the President cannot dissolve Congress, is limited to two terms by the Twenty-second Amendment and cannot control the Senate's confirmation votes or the courts — structural limits no majority can remove.\n• THE PM'S POWER IS ITSELF CONSTITUTIONAL: fusion of powers, the royal prerogative and parliamentary sovereignty mean that a PM with a majority can legislate on almost anything — the constitution is what converts a majority into power. The PM's dependence on circumstances is a product of an uncodified, convention-based constitution.\n• KEY COMPARISON: circumstances arguably matter MORE for the PM, whose position rests largely on convention and party support, and LESS for the President, whose fixed term and codified powers guarantee a base of power however weak he becomes politically.\n• SYNOPTIC LINKS: Paper 1 the PM and cabinet, the UK constitution and conventions; Neustadt and the presidentialisation debate; party systems; theory — structural v rational explanations of executive power.\nJudgement: a strong answer may conclude that circumstances determine how much of their potential power either leader can use, but that the constitution determines the scale of that potential and the floor beneath it — and that this floor is higher for the President. Any supported conclusion is valid.\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. The statement is comparative, so credit depends on sustained, explicit comparison of the US and the UK within each point: similarities and differences, and WHY they exist (structural, cultural or rational explanations). Answers that deal with only one country should not go above Level 2; answers that describe the two countries in separate, unlinked sections are unlikely to go above Level 3. Synoptic links are required — e.g. the PM, cabinet and conventions (Paper 1), the UK and US constitutions, party systems and majorities, and structural or rational theory. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant points should be credited; it does not matter which view they reach, provided the conclusion is supported by the preceding argument.` },

      { q: 'EXTRACTS (written for practice):\n\nExtract 1 — written for this practice question in the style of an opinion piece by a constitutional scholar who is critical of presidential power\n"Article I gives Congress the power to make laws, yet much of American policy is now made by presidential decree. Presidents of both parties have learned that when Congress will not act, a signature on an executive order will do instead. In the first hundred days of his second term in 2025, Donald Trump signed more executive orders than any president since Franklin Roosevelt, reshaping immigration enforcement, the federal workforce and trade policy without a new law. He even ordered an end to automatic birthright citizenship, a question most scholars believe the Fourteenth Amendment settled long ago. When one person can set national policy with a pen, and Congress declines to push back, the separation of powers exists only on paper."\n\nExtract 2 — written for this practice question in the style of a magazine column by a political scientist who argues that presidential power is overstated\n"The executive order is weaker than it looks. It cannot create a new law or spend money Congress has not provided; it can only direct how the executive branch uses powers it already has. What one president signs, the next can revoke on his first day, as new presidents routinely do to their predecessors\' orders. The courts are a constant check: Joe Biden\'s plan to cancel hundreds of billions of dollars of student debt was struck down by the Supreme Court in 2023, and Donald Trump\'s order ending birthright citizenship was quickly blocked by federal judges. Presidents rely on executive orders not because they are all-powerful but because they are weak: an order is a sign that a president could not persuade Congress to pass the law he really wanted."\n\nAnalyse, evaluate and compare the arguments in the above extracts regarding the extent to which executive orders have made the US President too powerful.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `EXTRACT 1 — EXECUTIVE ORDERS HAVE MADE THE PRESIDENT TOO POWERFUL:\n• POLICY BY DECREE: Extract 1 argues presidents substitute orders for legislation. Own knowledge: Obama's "pen and phone" strategy and the DACA programme (2012); Biden's early orders reversing Trump policies; Trump's 2025 orders on immigration, federal employment and tariffs. Evaluation: the scale in 2025 was exceptional, which supports the extract.\n• CONSTITUTIONALLY DUBIOUS ORDERS: the birthright citizenship order challenged the long-standing reading of the Fourteenth Amendment. Evaluation: it was blocked by lower courts, which supports Extract 2 — but in Trump v CASA (2025) the Supreme Court limited federal judges' use of universal injunctions, reducing one check the second extract relies on.\n• CONGRESSIONAL ACQUIESCENCE: the extract's claim that Congress "declines to push back" reflects unified Republican control after 2025; Congress rarely uses its power to legislate against orders because a veto would need a two-thirds override. Evaluation: this is a point about partisanship as much as about presidential power.\nEXTRACT 2 — PRESIDENTIAL POWER IS OVERSTATED:\n• LEGAL LIMITS: orders must rest on existing constitutional or statutory authority; Youngstown Sheet & Tube v Sawyer (1952) struck down Truman's seizure of the steel mills. Evaluation: accurate, but the breadth of statutory delegations (e.g. emergency and trade statutes) gives presidents wide scope in practice.\n• REVERSIBILITY: new presidents revoke predecessors' orders on their first day — Biden in 2021 and Trump in 2025 both did so. Evaluation: a strong point that orders lack the permanence of legislation, although reversal itself shows how much policy now swings with the presidency.\n• JUDICIAL CHECKS: Biden v Nebraska (2023) struck down student-debt cancellation; courts blocked the birthright order. Evaluation: judicial checks are real but slow, and may be weakened by limits on nationwide injunctions and by emergency-docket rulings allowing policies to take effect during litigation.\n• WEAKNESS NOT STRENGTH (Neustadt): orders reflect a failure to persuade Congress. Evaluation: persuasive as theory, but the practical effect on citizens can be large whatever the motive.\nCOMPARISON AND STATUS:\n• Both are opinion pieces with a clear purpose. Extract 1 is by a critic of presidential power and focuses on 2025; Extract 2 is by an academic sceptic and draws on cases across two administrations, making it arguably more balanced, though it underplays how far recent Supreme Court rulings have limited lower-court checks. Both agree that Congress is failing to legislate; they disagree about whether that makes the President strong or weak. Synoptic links may be made to the UK, where a PM with a majority can change policy through legislation rather than executive order.\nJudgement: a strong answer may conclude that executive orders are legally limited and reversible, as Extract 2 argues, but that congressional inaction and a narrowing of judicial remedies have made them a more powerful tool than Extract 2 allows. Any supported conclusion is valid.\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and comparing the arguments in the extracts and weighing which are more convincing; evaluate their status (author, type of publication, purpose, date) where relevant. Own knowledge should be used to test the claims the extracts make, not to write a free-standing essay. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant points should be credited; it does not matter which view they reach, provided it is supported. Answers that do not focus on the arguments in the extracts cannot go above Level 2.` },

      { q: '\'In both the US and the UK, advisers now matter more than cabinet ministers.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `AGREE:\n• THE US EXECUTIVE OFFICE: the Executive Office of the President (created 1939) — the White House Chief of Staff, the National Security Adviser and the NSC, the Office of Management and Budget — works in close proximity to the President, while cabinet secretaries run departments far away. Cabinet meetings are infrequent and largely symbolic; there is no collective responsibility. In 2025 Elon Musk, an unelected adviser and "special government employee", drove major cuts to federal agencies through DOGE, bypassing cabinet secretaries.\n• THE UK CENTRE: No 10 and the Cabinet Office have grown in size and influence, and special advisers wield great power — Alastair Campbell under Blair and Dominic Cummings under Johnson. The Butler Review (2004) criticised "sofa government" in the run-up to the Iraq war, when decisions were taken in small informal groups rather than full cabinet.\n• PRESIDENTIALISATION IN BOTH: PMs increasingly govern through bilateral meetings, cabinet committees and a personal office, as presidents do; media strategy is centralised in both.\nDISAGREE:\n• UK CABINET MINISTERS ARE POLITICAL HEAVYWEIGHTS: they are MPs or peers with their own political standing, bound by collective responsibility, and can bring down a PM — Thatcher (1990) and Johnson (2022, after the resignations of Sunak and Javid). Advisers depend wholly on the PM's favour; Cummings left No 10 in 2020. Chancellors can rival PMs (Brown under Blair).\n• US DEPARTMENTS STILL MATTER: secretaries of State, Defense and the Treasury head huge bureaucracies and are important in foreign and economic policy. Musk's influence over DOGE lasted only a few months in 2025, suggesting adviser power is personal and fragile.\n• THE KEY DIFFERENCE IS STRUCTURAL: the statement is truer of the US, where the separation of powers keeps secretaries out of Congress and makes them appointees serving at the President's pleasure; in the UK, fusion of powers makes cabinet ministers MPs with a parliamentary base, so the cabinet retains a collective political role even when it is sidelined.\n• SYNOPTIC LINKS: Paper 1 the PM and cabinet, collective and individual ministerial responsibility, the civil service; the presidentialisation thesis (Foley); theory — structural (separation v fusion), rational (leaders centralise to control policy and the message).\nJudgement: a strong answer is likely to conclude that advisers matter more than most cabinet secretaries in the US, but that UK cabinet ministers retain political power advisers lack, above all the collective ability to remove a PM. Any supported conclusion is valid.\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. The statement is comparative, so credit depends on sustained, explicit comparison of the US and the UK within each point: similarities and differences, and WHY they exist (structural, cultural or rational explanations). Answers that deal with only one country should not go above Level 2; answers that describe the two countries in separate, unlinked sections are unlikely to go above Level 3. Synoptic links are required — e.g. the PM, cabinet and ministerial responsibility (Paper 1), the presidentialisation debate, the separation v fusion of powers, and structural or rational theory. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant points should be credited; it does not matter which view they reach, provided the conclusion is supported by the preceding argument.` },
    ]
  },

  '2.4': {
    name: 'The Supreme Court and the Judicial Branch',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three features of the US Supreme Court.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Nine justices appointed for life during good behaviour. Analysis: insulates them from political pressure, but means one appointment can shape constitutional interpretation for thirty years or more, long outlasting the appointing President.\n• Judicial review — the power to declare Acts of Congress, executive actions and state laws unconstitutional and void. Analysis: NOT granted by the Constitution; the Court asserted it in Marbury v Madison (1803).\n• Control of its own docket through the writ of certiorari, granting review in only a small fraction of petitions. Analysis: significant agenda-setting power.\n• Appointment by the President with Senate confirmation by simple majority; since 2017 a filibuster can no longer block a Supreme Court nomination, because cloture on nominations now also needs only a simple majority.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires analysis of consequences, not description.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three reasons why Supreme Court appointments are politically contentious.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Life tenure and stakes — one appointment can determine policy on abortion, guns, campaign finance and voting for decades, and vacancies are rare and unpredictable.\n• The Court's policy significance — because formal amendment is near-impossible, the Court effectively decides the most contested questions, so appointments are the most reliable route to lasting policy change. Interest groups therefore mobilise heavily.\n• Eroded procedural norms — the Senate refused to consider the Garland nomination in 2016 on election-year grounds, then rapidly confirmed Barrett weeks before the 2020 election. Removal of the filibuster for Supreme Court nominations in 2017 eliminated any need for cross-party support.\n• Consequences — Dobbs reversed a fifty-year precedent shortly after three appointments, reinforcing the perception that constitutional meaning tracks appointments rather than law.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires the rational-incentive analysis, not just description of the process.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three limits on the power of the US Supreme Court.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• No enforcement power — it depends on the executive and the states to implement rulings. The decade of resistance to desegregation after Brown required federal troops at Little Rock.\n• Reactive — it cannot initiate cases and can only decide disputes litigants bring to it.\n• Political checks — justices are nominated by the President and confirmed by the Senate, so composition is determined by elected officials; Congress controls appellate jurisdiction and the number of justices.\n• Reversal — statutory rulings can be reversed by ordinary legislation; constitutional rulings by amendment, as the Sixteenth Amendment did.\n• Legitimacy — its authority rests entirely on public acceptance, imposing a real practical limit on departing from settled opinion.\n\nAnalysis expected: several of these are largely theoretical, since amendment is near-impossible and court-packing politically explosive, so the EFFECTIVE constraints are narrower than the formal list.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three landmark US Supreme Court cases and their significance.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Marbury v Madison (1803) — established judicial review, which the Constitution does not grant. Significance: made the Court the authoritative interpreter of the Constitution.\n• Brown v Board of Education (1954) — segregated schooling unconstitutional, overturning Plessy. Significance: a court delivering nationwide change no legislature of the period would; but implementation required a decade of further litigation.\n• Citizens United v FEC (2010) — independent political spending is protected First Amendment speech; enabled Super PACs. Significance: the Court reshaping the rules of political competition itself.\n• Shelby County v Holder (2013) — struck down the VRA pre-clearance coverage formula.\n• Dobbs v Jackson (2022) — overturned Roe, returning abortion to the states.\n\nGuidance: analysis must address CONSTITUTIONAL significance. Narrative recounting caps at L2.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three differences between originalism and the living Constitution approach.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Source of meaning — originalism holds that the Constitution means what its text meant when adopted; living constitutionalism that it should be applied in light of contemporary conditions and values.\n• Engine of change — originalism leaves social change to legislatures and the amendment process; living constitutionalism allows constitutional protection to develop judicially, which matters because the amendment thresholds make formal change practically unattainable.\n• Judicial role — originalism claims to constrain judges by an objective historical standard; living constitutionalism accepts a more creative interpretive role.\n• Associated rulings — Dobbs and Second Amendment cases (originalist); Brown, Roe and Obergefell (living).\n\nAnalysis expected: NEITHER removes discretion as fully as it claims. Originalism requires contested judgments about historical meaning and about the level of generality intended; living constitutionalism requires judgments about which contemporary values count. Justices' conclusions correlate strikingly with their political outlook whatever method they profess.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which the Supreme Court influences US politics.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Determining the scope of rights — extending them (Brown, Obergefell, Bostock) and withdrawing them (Dobbs). Analysis: a constitutional ruling applies nationwide, needs no legislative majority, and is reversible only by the Court or an unattainable amendment.\n• Policing federal–state boundaries — through the commerce clause, the Tenth Amendment and the supremacy clause, the Court effectively determines how much the federal government may do. Analysis: the balance therefore tracks the Court's composition.\n• Shaping the rules of political competition — Citizens United on campaign finance, Shelby County on voting, Rucho on districting. Analysis: this is influence of a distinctive kind, because it affects who wins future elections and therefore who makes future appointments, creating a feedback loop.\n• Constraining the executive — United States v Nixon; Youngstown.\n\nGuidance: the feedback-loop point separates L3 from L2.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: '\'Judges in both the US and the UK have become the main check on executive power.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `AGREE:\n• UK RULINGS AGAINST GOVERNMENT: Miller (2017) held that the government could not trigger Article 50 without an Act of Parliament; Miller/Cherry (2019) unanimously held Johnson's prorogation unlawful; R (AAA) (2023) unanimously held the Rwanda policy unlawful. Judicial review has grown into a routine check on ministers, reinforced by the Human Rights Act 1998.\n• US RULINGS AGAINST PRESIDENTS: United States v Nixon (1974); Youngstown (1952); Biden v Nebraska (2023) struck down student-debt cancellation; in 2025 federal courts blocked many of Trump's executive actions, including the order on birthright citizenship. Loper Bright (2024) ended Chevron deference, increasing judicial scrutiny of agency action.\n• WEAKNESS OF POLITICAL CHECKS: in the UK a government with a large Commons majority faces limited parliamentary constraint; in the US Congress under unified government often defers to the President. Courts fill the gap in both.\nDISAGREE:\n• UK COURTS CAN BE OVERRIDDEN: under parliamentary sovereignty, government can legislate to reverse rulings — the Safety of Rwanda (Asylum and Immigration) Act 2024 declared Rwanda safe despite the Supreme Court's ruling; declarations of incompatibility do not bind. US rulings based on the Constitution can only be reversed by amendment or a later Court.\n• THE US COURT HAS ALSO EXPANDED EXECUTIVE POWER: Trump v Hawaii (2018) upheld the travel ban; Trump v United States (2024) granted presidents broad immunity for official acts; Trump v CASA (2025) limited universal injunctions; and emergency-docket orders in 2025 often allowed administration policies to take effect during litigation.\n• COURTS ARE REACTIVE AND SLOW: they can only rule on cases brought before them and often long after the policy has taken effect; they do not decide on the wisdom of policy.\n• OTHER CHECKS REMAIN IMPORTANT: in the US — Congress's power of the purse and confirmation, federalism (state attorneys general suing the federal government), midterm elections. In the UK — backbench rebellions (Starmer's 2025 welfare concessions), the Lords, the governing party itself (Johnson forced out in 2022) and the media.\n• SYNOPTIC LINKS: Paper 1 the UK Supreme Court, judicial review, parliamentary sovereignty and the HRA; Congress and the presidency; federalism; theory — structural (entrenchment and judicial review) and rational (litigants use courts when political routes fail).\nJudgement: a strong answer may conclude that courts have become a MORE important check in both countries, especially when legislatures defer, but that UK courts can be overridden by Parliament and the US Court has sometimes enlarged executive power — so they are a significant, not the main, check. Any supported conclusion is valid.\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. The statement is comparative, so credit depends on sustained, explicit comparison of the US and the UK within each point: similarities and differences, and WHY they exist (structural, cultural or rational explanations). Answers that deal with only one country should not go above Level 2; answers that describe the two countries in separate, unlinked sections are unlikely to go above Level 3. Synoptic links are required — e.g. the UK Supreme Court, judicial review and parliamentary sovereignty (Paper 1), Congress and the presidency, federalism, and structural or rational theory. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant points should be credited; it does not matter which view they reach, provided the conclusion is supported by the preceding argument.` },

      { q: 'EXTRACTS (written for practice):\n\nExtract 1 — written for this practice question in the style of an opinion piece by a campaigner for Supreme Court reform\n"Nothing in the Constitution says there must be nine justices or that they should serve for decades, yet the chance timing of deaths and retirements now decides the direction of American law. In 2016 the Senate refused even to hold a hearing for Merrick Garland, nominated eight months before the presidential election; in 2020 it confirmed Amy Coney Barrett within about a month, with voting in the presidential election already under way. Confirmation votes now split almost entirely along party lines, and justices time their retirements to suit the party they favour. Three appointments in a single presidential term produced the majority that overturned Roe v Wade. Fixed eighteen-year terms, giving every president two appointments per term, would lower the stakes and restore public trust."\n\nExtract 2 — written for this practice question in the style of a newspaper column by a conservative lawyer who defends the current process\n"The appointment process is noisy because the stakes are high, and that is as it should be. The President, elected by the nation, nominates; the Senate, elected by the states, must consent. That gives voters an indirect say over the Court, as in 2016, when a Supreme Court vacancy became a major campaign issue. Nominees are scrutinised more thoroughly than almost any other public officials, and every justice appointed since 2010 had already served as a federal judge. Once appointed, life tenure frees them to rule against the president who chose them, as Justice Gorsuch did when he wrote the Court\'s 2020 opinion protecting gay and transgender employees from workplace discrimination. Term limits would probably need a constitutional amendment, and would turn every presidential election into a contest for control of the Court."\n\nAnalyse, evaluate and compare the arguments in the above extracts regarding whether the process of appointing US Supreme Court justices should be reformed.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `EXTRACT 1 — THE PROCESS SHOULD BE REFORMED:\n• POLITICISED CONFIRMATIONS: Garland (2016) received no hearing; Barrett (2020) was confirmed 52–48 days before the election. Own knowledge: Kavanaugh was confirmed 50–48 (2018) and Jackson 53–47 (2022), compared with near-unanimous votes for Scalia (98–0, 1986) and Ginsburg (96–3, 1993). The 2017 "nuclear option" removed the filibuster for Supreme Court nominations. Evaluation: strong evidence that confirmation is now partisan.\n• ARBITRARY TIMING AND STRATEGIC RETIREMENT: Trump made three appointments in one term, while Obama made two in eight years; justices time departures (Kennedy 2018, Breyer 2022). Evaluation: the Dobbs majority (2022) illustrates the extract's point that timing shapes law.\n• TERM LIMITS: 18-year staggered terms have been widely proposed; President Biden endorsed them in July 2024, though his 2021 commission on the Court made no recommendation. Evaluation: reform would regularise appointments, but Extract 2 raises the constitutional obstacle.\nEXTRACT 2 — THE PROCESS WORKS:\n• DEMOCRATIC ACCOUNTABILITY: nomination by an elected President and consent by an elected Senate link the Court to voters. Evaluation: in 2016 the Senate majority used that link to block a nominee altogether, and then acted differently in 2020 — which supports Extract 1's charge of inconsistency.\n• QUALITY AND SCRUTINY: accurate — Gorsuch, Kavanaugh, Barrett and Jackson were all federal judges. Evaluation: qualification is not the issue critics raise; the concern is ideological selection, e.g. Trump's 2016 list of potential nominees, drawn up with advice from conservative legal groups.\n• JUDICIAL INDEPENDENCE: Bostock v Clayton County (2020) shows justices can surprise their appointing president; Roberts's vote upholding the ACA (2012) is another example. Evaluation: such cases are exceptions; on the most divisive issues votes often follow ideological lines (6–3).\n• TERM LIMITS NEED AN AMENDMENT: many scholars think Article III's "good behaviour" clause requires one, which is currently unachievable. Evaluation: this is a practical rather than principled objection.\nCOMPARISON AND STATUS:\n• Both are persuasive opinion pieces. Extract 1 is by a reform campaigner writing in the light of Dobbs; Extract 2 by a conservative lawyer who may welcome the current majority. Both agree that appointments matter enormously; they disagree on whether that is healthy democratic accountability or dangerous politicisation. Students may make synoptic comparison with the UK's selection-commission process under the Constitutional Reform Act 2005.\nJudgement: a strong answer may conclude that Extract 1 offers stronger evidence of politicisation, while Extract 2 is right that the Senate's role gives democratic legitimacy and that reform faces real constitutional obstacles. Any supported conclusion is valid.\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and comparing the arguments in the extracts and weighing which are more convincing; evaluate their status (author, type of publication, purpose, date) where relevant. Own knowledge should be used to test the claims the extracts make, not to write a free-standing essay. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant points should be credited; it does not matter which view they reach, provided it is supported. Answers that do not focus on the arguments in the extracts cannot go above Level 2.` },

      { q: '\'The UK Supreme Court is more independent than the US Supreme Court.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `AGREE:\n• APPOINTMENTS: UK justices are chosen by an independent selection commission under the Constitutional Reform Act 2005; the Lord Chancellor has only a limited power to reject or ask for reconsideration, and there are no legislative hearings. US justices are chosen by the President, often from ideologically vetted lists, and confirmed on near party-line votes.\n• IDEOLOGICAL ALIGNMENT: US justices are routinely identified by the party of the President who appointed them, and the current 6–3 conservative majority splits on ideological lines in major cases (Dobbs 2022, SFFA v Harvard 2023). UK judgments show no comparable party-political blocs, and major constitutional rulings such as Miller/Cherry (2019) and AAA (2023) were unanimous.\n• RETIREMENT AND ETHICS: UK justices must retire at 75 (raised from 70 in 2022), preventing strategic timing; US justices often time retirements politically. US justices have faced controversies over undisclosed gifts, and the code of conduct adopted in 2023 has no enforcement mechanism.\nDISAGREE:\n• INDEPENDENCE FROM THE LEGISLATURE: US justices' life tenure and salary protection are entrenched in Article III, and their constitutional rulings cannot be overturned by Congress. The UK Court is a creation of statute and can be overridden or restricted by Parliament — e.g. the Safety of Rwanda Act 2024 and the ouster of certain judicial reviews by the Judicial Review and Courts Act 2022.\n• POLITICAL ATTACKS IN BOTH: after the 2016 High Court ruling in Miller, a newspaper headline branded judges "enemies of the people"; ministers have attacked "activist" lawyers and judges. US presidents also attack judges publicly, but judges' tenure makes them harder to pressure.\n• US JUSTICES DO RULE AGAINST THEIR APPOINTERS: Trump v Vance (2020), Bostock (2020) and the rejection of challenges to the 2020 election result show independence from the appointing President, even if not from ideology.\n• TRANSPARENCY: US confirmation hearings are public, while UK selection is less visible and has been criticised for lack of diversity.\n• SYNOPTIC LINKS: Paper 1 the UK Supreme Court, the Constitutional Reform Act 2005 and the rule of law; parliamentary sovereignty v entrenchment; US appointments and judicial philosophy; theory — structural (codification and tenure) and cultural (a more politicised legal culture in the US).\nJudgement: a strong answer will distinguish independence from party politics (where the UK Court is more independent) from independence from the other branches (where the entrenched US Court is more secure), and reach a supported judgement on which matters more. Any supported conclusion is valid.\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. The statement is comparative, so credit depends on sustained, explicit comparison of the US and the UK within each point: similarities and differences, and WHY they exist (structural, cultural or rational explanations). Answers that deal with only one country should not go above Level 2; answers that describe the two countries in separate, unlinked sections are unlikely to go above Level 3. Synoptic links are required — e.g. the UK Supreme Court, the Constitutional Reform Act 2005 and the rule of law (Paper 1), parliamentary sovereignty v entrenchment, US appointments, and structural or cultural theory. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant points should be credited; it does not matter which view they reach, provided the conclusion is supported by the preceding argument.` },

      { q: '\'Judicial activism poses a greater threat to democracy in the US than in the UK.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `AGREE:\n• FINAL AUTHORITY: the US Supreme Court can strike down federal and state laws on constitutional grounds, and only an amendment or a later Court can reverse it. Its rulings have reshaped policy on abortion (Roe 1973, Dobbs 2022), guns (Heller 2008, Bruen 2022), campaign finance (Citizens United 2010), voting rights (Shelby County 2013) and affirmative action (SFFA 2023).\n• COUNTER-MAJORITARIAN DIFFICULTY: nine unelected justices with life tenure and ideological appointment make decisions that elected branches cannot easily reverse; public confidence in the Court has fallen sharply in recent years.\n• UK COURTS CANNOT STRIKE DOWN ACTS: under parliamentary sovereignty, section 4 HRA declarations leave the decision to Parliament, and Parliament can reverse rulings (the Safety of Rwanda Act 2024). Democratic institutions keep the final word.\nDISAGREE:\n• "ACTIVISM" IS CONTESTED: Dobbs, often called activist, returned abortion policy to elected state legislatures; the Court has also protected democratic processes (Baker v Carr 1962, Reynolds v Sims 1964) and minorities who lack majority power (Brown 1954).\n• US DEMOCRATIC CHECKS ON THE COURT: justices are appointed and confirmed by elected officials, and amendments have reversed decisions (the Sixteenth Amendment after Pollock, 1895; the Twenty-sixth after Oregon v Mitchell, 1970).\n• CONCERN ABOUT UK JUDICIAL POWER: critics called Miller/Cherry (2019) and the Rwanda ruling (2023) political interventions; common-law constitutionalism (UNISON 2017; Privacy International 2019, limiting ouster clauses) has expanded judicial power; debates about leaving the ECHR reflect concern about unelected judges, domestic and European, overriding elected government.\n• JUDICIAL POWER CAN PROTECT DEMOCRACY: the UK rulings in Miller protected Parliament's role against the executive, arguably strengthening rather than threatening democracy.\n• SYNOPTIC LINKS: Paper 1 the UK Supreme Court, the HRA, parliamentary sovereignty and the rule of law; the US Constitution and rights; theory — structural (entrenchment gives US judges finality) and cultural (the US tradition of rights litigation).\nJudgement: a strong answer is likely to agree that the STRUCTURAL potential for judicial activism to override elected institutions is far greater in the US, while questioning whether activism is a threat to democracy rather than a protection of it, and noting growing debate about judicial power in the UK. Any supported conclusion is valid.\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. The statement is comparative, so credit depends on sustained, explicit comparison of the US and the UK within each point: similarities and differences, and WHY they exist (structural, cultural or rational explanations). Answers that deal with only one country should not go above Level 2; answers that describe the two countries in separate, unlinked sections are unlikely to go above Level 3. Synoptic links are required — e.g. the UK Supreme Court, the HRA and parliamentary sovereignty (Paper 1), rights and the US Constitution, the rule of law, and structural or cultural theory. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant points should be credited; it does not matter which view they reach, provided the conclusion is supported by the preceding argument.` },
    ]
  },

  '2.5': {
    name: 'Elections and Direct Democracy',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three features of the Electoral College.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Allocation — each state receives electors equal to its House members plus two senators, with DC receiving three, giving 538 in total and 270 needed to win. Analysis: the two-senator element over-represents small states, so a vote in Wyoming carries several times the weight of one in California.\n• Winner-take-all — almost all states award all their electors to the statewide winner; Maine and Nebraska allocate partly by district. Analysis: this is what allows a candidate to win the presidency while losing the popular vote, as in 2000 and 2016.\n• Contingent election — if no candidate reaches 270 the House chooses, with each state delegation casting one vote.\n• Faithless electors — theoretically possible, never decisive, and many states now bind their electors.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires consequences, particularly the swing-state distortion.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three criticisms of the Electoral College.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• It can elect a President who lost the popular vote — five times in total, twice since 2000 (2000 and 2016). Analysis: violates the principle that each vote should carry equal weight.\n• Over-representation of small states — every state receives two electors regardless of population, so a Wyoming vote carries several times the weight of a Californian one.\n• Distorted campaigning — because most states are reliably partisan under winner-take-all, candidates concentrate almost all spending, visits and policy attention on a handful of swing states, while voters elsewhere are effectively ignored, depressing turnout.\n• The contingent election procedure gives each state delegation one vote regardless of population.\n\nGuidance: one-sided by design; require analysis of MECHANISM.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three features of the US presidential nomination process.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Primaries — state-run secret-ballot elections in which registered voters select the party's candidate, which may be closed, open or semi-closed. Analysis: transferred selection from party elites to voters, so parties cannot control who runs under their label.\n• Caucuses — party-run meetings where supporters gather and publicly declare preference. Analysis: demand far more time and commitment, so turnout is low and participants are ideologically committed activists.\n• The invisible primary — the pre-voting period of fundraising, endorsements and name recognition, which effectively screens out those who cannot raise money. Analysis: advantages establishment candidates, though Trump in 2016 showed media attention can substitute.\n• Iowa and New Hampshire's calendar position generating momentum disproportionate to their delegate numbers (they still open the Republican calendar; in 2024 the Democrats made South Carolina their first official primary).\n• The National Convention formally confirming the nominee and adopting the platform.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires the polarisation consequence.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three features of campaign finance in the USA.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• PACs — raise money and donate directly to candidates, subject to strict statutory limits on both receipts and donations.\n• Super PACs — may raise and spend UNLIMITED sums on independent advocacy but may not donate to or coordinate with a campaign. Created after Citizens United (2010) and the SpeechNow ruling.\n• Dark money — 501(c)(4) "social welfare" organisations may spend on political advocacy without disclosing donors, making it impossible to trace who funds political messaging.\n• The constitutional framework — Buckley v Valeo (1976) held spending limits restrict protected speech; Citizens United extended this to corporations, unions and associations.\n\nAnalysis expected: the crucial point is that reform is CONSTITUTIONALLY FORECLOSED — Congress cannot simply legislate limits, unlike the UK which caps campaign spending by ordinary statute.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three forms of direct democracy used in US states.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Initiative — citizens propose a law or state constitutional amendment by collecting signatures, which is then put to a popular vote. Analysis: genuine citizen agenda-setting power the federal system entirely lacks; has delivered change on cannabis, minimum wage and healthcare expansion where legislatures were blocked.\n• Referendum — a measure passed by the legislature referred to voters for approval or rejection.\n• Recall — voters petition to remove an elected official before their term ends, then vote on it; California recalls in 2003 and 2021.\n\nAnalysis expected: criticisms include that qualifying an initiative requires expensive professional signature-gathering, so the mechanism can amplify organised money rather than popular will; and that complex issues are reduced to binary choices without scrutiny or amendment — California's Proposition 13 constrained property tax revenue for decades while other initiatives mandated spending.\n\nNote: there is NO national referendum in the USA.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three reasons why the President\'s party usually loses seats at midterm elections.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Lower turnout favours the more motivated opposition — voters energised against an administration turn out more reliably than those satisfied with it.\n• Midterms function as a referendum on an incumbent administration whose popularity has usually declined from its inauguration peak.\n• Loss of coattails — congressional candidates who benefited from a popular presidential candidate two years earlier no longer have that boost.\n• Regression from an unusually strong presidential-year result in marginal districts.\n\nAnalysis expected: the consequence is structurally important — losing a chamber usually ends the legislative programme and exposes the President to hostile oversight, which is why major legislative achievements are heavily front-loaded into the first two years and why presidents increasingly turn to executive orders thereafter.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'EXTRACTS (written for practice):\n\nExtract 1 — written for this practice question in the style of an opinion column by a campaigner for a national popular vote\n"Twice this century, in 2000 and 2016, the candidate who won the most votes nationwide lost the presidency. That alone should end the argument. Because almost every state awards all of its electors to whoever tops the poll there, a Republican vote in California or a Democratic vote in Wyoming counts for nothing. Campaigns know it: in 2024 almost all of their visits and advertising went to seven battleground states, while voters in the other forty-three were treated as spectators. Two electors per state regardless of population give a voter in a small state several times the weight of one in a large state. A modern democracy should elect its head of government the simplest way possible: one person, one vote, and the most votes wins."\n\nExtract 2 — written for this practice question in the style of a newspaper editorial by a constitutional conservative\n"Critics treat the Electoral College as a glitch. It is a design. The United States is a union of fifty states, and the presidency is won by assembling support across them, which forces candidates to build broad coalitions rather than piling up votes in a few dense cities. The system also contains disputes: a close result means a recount in one state, as in Florida in 2000, not in every precinct in the country. It usually produces a clear winner, and misfires are rarer than critics imply; in 2024 the winner took both the popular vote and the Electoral College. Abolition would need a constitutional amendment ratified by three-quarters of the states, many of which the present system protects. Reformers should spend their energy elsewhere."\n\nAnalyse, evaluate and compare the arguments in the above extracts regarding the case for replacing the Electoral College with a national popular vote.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n\n• EXTRACT 1 — DEMOCRATIC EQUALITY: argues from the principle of one person, one vote. Its evidence is strong: 2000 (Bush/Gore) and 2016 (Trump/Clinton) are accurate, and the swing-state concentration of campaigning is well documented. Students can develop the winner-take-all point (Maine and Nebraska are the only partial exceptions) and link it to depressed turnout and neglect of safe states.\n• EXTRACT 1 — SMALL-STATE BIAS: the two-senator element over-weights small states. Students may add that this compounds Senate malapportionment, so the same bias runs through two institutions.\n• EXTRACT 2 — FEDERALISM: defends the College as a deliberate federal design requiring geographically broad coalitions. Students should test this: the swing-state focus arguably means campaigns seek a narrow band of competitive states rather than a broad coalition, which Extract 1 directly contradicts.\n• EXTRACT 2 — STABILITY AND CONTAINMENT: a close result is fought in one state (Florida 2000, Bush v Gore) rather than nationally, and the College usually produces a clear outcome. Its 2024 example is accurate, but the claim that misfires are "rare" sits awkwardly with two in six elections this century.\n• FEASIBILITY: Extract 2's strongest point is practical — an amendment needs two-thirds of both houses and three-quarters of the states, including the small and swing states that benefit. Extract 1 ignores feasibility entirely. Students may note the National Popular Vote Interstate Compact as an attempted workaround, still short of 270 electoral votes and legally contested.\n• COMPARISON AND STATUS: the extracts disagree about what the presidential election is FOR — choosing a national leader (Extract 1) or expressing the federal union (Extract 2). Both are written to persuade: a reform campaigner and a constitutional conservative writing an editorial each select evidence. Neither mentions faithless electors or the contingent election in the House.\n\nJudgement: the strongest answers decide which extract is more convincing on principle and which on practice — Extract 1 has the stronger democratic case, Extract 2 the stronger account of why change will not happen — and explain why that gap persists.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and comparing the arguments in the extracts and weighing which are more convincing; evaluate their status (author, type of publication, purpose, date) where relevant. Own knowledge should be used to test the extracts' claims, not as a free-standing essay. Answers that do not focus on the arguments in the extracts cannot go above Level 2.` },

      { q: '\'Elections in the UK are conducted more fairly than elections in the US.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n\n• CAMPAIGN FINANCE: UK campaign spending by parties and candidates is capped by statute (PPERA 2000), paid political advertising on TV and radio is banned, and the Electoral Commission regulates donations. In the US, Buckley v Valeo (1976) and Citizens United (2010) mean spending cannot be capped, and Super PACs and dark money are routine. Counterpoint: UK limits were raised substantially in 2023, large donors and honours controversies recur, and online campaigning is lightly regulated — so the UK difference is one of degree.\n• BOUNDARIES: UK constituency boundaries are drawn by independent Boundary Commissions; the review implemented at the 2024 election equalised electorates. Most US congressional districts are drawn by state legislatures, and Rucho v Common Cause (2019) made partisan gerrymandering non-justiciable in federal courts; the 2025 mid-decade redistricting fight begun by Texas shows how partisan the process is. Some US states use independent commissions, so the contrast is not total.\n• ELECTORAL SYSTEMS AND OUTCOMES: both use first-past-the-post, so both distort. In 2024 Labour won about 63% of Commons seats on about 34% of the vote while Reform UK won five seats on 14%. The US Electoral College has twice this century elected a President who lost the popular vote. Students may argue the UK distortion is greater in seats but the US distortion is greater in the choice of head of government.\n• VOTER ACCESS: the US has a long history of disenfranchisement, and after Shelby County (2013) several states tightened voter ID, early voting and roll purges; felony disenfranchisement varies by state. But the UK's Elections Act 2022 introduced photo voter ID for the first time, and some voters were turned away. The UK now resembles the US on this issue more than it did.\n• ADMINISTRATION: many US elections are run by partisan elected officials (e.g. secretaries of state), and 2020 saw pressure on officials to overturn results. UK returning officers are non-partisan local officials; however, the Elections Act 2022 also gave the government power to issue a strategy and policy statement to the Electoral Commission, criticised as a threat to its independence.\n• CANDIDATE COMPETITION: US incumbents are re-elected at rates above 90%, helped by money and districting; the UK has more competitive seats and larger swings (2024), though many UK seats are also safe.\n\nJudgement: the strongest answers distinguish fairness in the RULES (the UK is generally fairer on finance and boundaries) from fairness in OUTCOMES (both FPTP systems distort) and explain the difference structurally: the UK can reform elections by ordinary statute, whereas US reform is constrained by the Constitution, the courts and federalism.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. This is a Section C comparative essay: answers must compare the US and the UK explicitly and in a sustained way, point by point, and explain WHY the two differ or resemble each other (for example through structural, rational or cultural approaches). An answer that treats the two countries in separate halves with little direct comparison should not go above Level 3; an answer that deals with only one country cannot go above Level 2. Synoptic links may be made to electoral systems (Paper 1), democracy and participation, the Electoral Commission, the Supreme Court and the constitution, federalism, and pressure groups/money in politics. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant points should be credited. It does not matter what conclusion is reached, provided it is supported by the argument and examples.` },

      { q: '\'Candidates are chosen far more democratically in the US than in the UK.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n\n• WHO SELECTS: in the US, primaries and caucuses transfer selection to voters — in open primaries any registered voter can take part. In the UK, parliamentary candidates are selected by local party members under central vetting, and party leaders are chosen by MPs and members. On participation, the US is clearly more open: millions vote in primaries, while UK party membership is a small fraction of the electorate.\n• OUTSIDERS AND CHOICE: US primaries let voters defeat party establishments — Trump in 2016, Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez unseating a senior House Democrat in 2018, Zohran Mamdani winning New York City's Democratic mayoral primary in 2025. In the UK, central parties can block or impose candidates; Labour's NEC selected many candidates directly in the 2024 snap election, and leaders can remove the whip.\n• QUALITY OF THE ELECTORATE: US primary turnout is low and skewed towards committed activists, and in safe districts the primary is effectively the election, so a small ideological electorate chooses the representative. The UK leadership electorate is also tiny and unrepresentative — Liz Truss was chosen by Conservative members in 2022 and Rishi Sunak by MPs alone weeks later, both becoming Prime Minister without a general election.\n• THE BIDEN–HARRIS EXAMPLE: in 2024 Joe Biden won the primaries, then withdrew in July; Kamala Harris became the Democratic nominee through a delegate vote without winning a single primary. This shows US parties can still override primary voters, narrowing the contrast with the UK.\n• MONEY AND THE INVISIBLE PRIMARY: US candidates must raise large sums before any vote is cast, filtering the field by wealth and donor networks. UK selection costs little, so it is more open to candidates without money — a point against the statement.\n• CONSEQUENCES: primaries make US parties weaker and legislators less disciplined, and reward ideological purity, feeding polarisation. UK party control of selection supports party discipline, cohesive manifestos and collective responsibility. The UK briefly experimented with open primaries (the Conservatives in Totnes, 2009) but did not continue.\n\nJudgement: the US is more democratic in FORM (more participants, more voter choice), but both systems hand power to small, unrepresentative selectorates, and US openness has costs for party government. Strong answers explain the difference structurally (separation of powers means US parties do not need to supply a governing team) and rationally (UK leaders protect discipline by controlling selection).\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. This is a Section C comparative essay: answers must compare the US and the UK explicitly and in a sustained way, point by point, and explain WHY the two differ or resemble each other (for example through structural, rational or cultural approaches). An answer that treats the two countries in separate halves with little direct comparison should not go above Level 3; an answer that deals with only one country cannot go above Level 2. Synoptic links may be made to party structures and leadership selection (Paper 1), democracy and participation, polarisation, party discipline in Congress and Parliament, and the fusion and separation of powers. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant points should be credited. It does not matter what conclusion is reached, provided it is supported by the argument and examples.` },

      { q: '\'Direct democracy plays a far more significant role in the US than in the UK.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n\n• LEVEL OF USE: there is no national referendum in the US — the Constitution makes no provision for one — whereas the UK has held national referendums on EC membership (1975), AV (2011) and EU membership (2016). At national level the statement is simply wrong.\n• STATE AND SUB-NATIONAL USE: around half of US states allow citizen-initiated ballot measures, and hundreds of measures appear on state ballots in a typical election cycle. The UK has no citizens' initiative; referendums are called by government when it chooses — devolution (1997), Welsh powers (2011), Scottish independence (2014), the Good Friday Agreement (1998) and local mayoral votes.\n• AGENDA-SETTING POWER: US initiatives let citizens bypass legislatures — cannabis legalisation, minimum wage increases, Medicaid expansion, and after Dobbs abortion measures (Kansas rejected removing abortion rights in 2022; Ohio enshrined them in 2023). UK referendums are controlled by the executive, which chooses the question and timing, often for party-management reasons (Wilson in 1975, Cameron in 2016).\n• IMPACT: yet the UK's rare referendums have had enormous consequences — the 2016 vote took the UK out of the EU and ended a Prime Minister's tenure. No US state measure has comparable national impact. Students can argue significance is about consequence, not frequency.\n• RECALL: several US states allow recall of state officials (California governor, 2003 and 2021). The UK's Recall of MPs Act 2015 is narrower — triggered only by specified misconduct — but has removed MPs (e.g. Fiona Onasanya, 2019; Margaret Ferrier, 2023).\n• CRITICISMS SHARED AND DIFFERENT: US initiatives are shaped by money for signature-gathering and campaigning, and can lock in fiscal constraints (California's Proposition 13). UK referendums raise questions of parliamentary sovereignty (legally advisory, politically binding) and of campaign conduct (2016 spending breaches by Vote Leave found by the Electoral Commission).\n\nJudgement: the US uses direct democracy far more OFTEN and gives citizens real initiating power, so the statement is largely correct at state level; but the UK's executive-controlled national referendums have had greater constitutional SIGNIFICANCE. Strong answers explain the difference structurally — US federalism allows state-level experiment, while UK parliamentary sovereignty keeps referendums in the gift of government.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. This is a Section C comparative essay: answers must compare the US and the UK explicitly and in a sustained way, point by point, and explain WHY the two differ or resemble each other (for example through structural, rational or cultural approaches). An answer that treats the two countries in separate halves with little direct comparison should not go above Level 3; an answer that deals with only one country cannot go above Level 2. Synoptic links may be made to democracy and participation (Paper 1), referendums and parliamentary sovereignty, devolution, federalism, the Supreme Court (Dobbs) and pressure groups. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant points should be credited. It does not matter what conclusion is reached, provided it is supported by the argument and examples.` },
    ]
  },

  '2.6': {
    name: 'US Political Parties',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three differences between the Democratic and Republican parties.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• The role of the federal government in the economy — Democrats support progressive taxation, regulation and social provision (the Affordable Care Act); Republicans favour lower taxes, deregulation and devolution to states and markets. Analysis: the oldest dividing line, tracing to the New Deal realignment, but less stable than it appears given Republican deficits and the recent turn to protectionism.\n• Social and cultural questions — abortion, LGBT rights, gun regulation, immigration and race. Analysis: these have displaced economics as the main predictor of vote choice, and are LESS amenable to compromise than distributive questions, since a budget can be split and a constitutional right cannot.\n• Electoral coalitions — Democrats strong among urban, minority, younger and college-educated voters; Republicans among rural, evangelical, non-college white and older voters.\n• Federalism — but positions here are tactical, tracking where each party currently holds power.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires analysis, not a list of policies.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three reasons why the USA has a two-party system.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Winner-take-all elections — single-member plurality districts and the winner-take-all Electoral College mean dispersed support wins nothing. Duverger's law. Ross Perot won ~19% of the popular vote in 1992 and ZERO electoral votes.\n• Strategic incentives — voters, donors and ambitious candidates converge on the two viable parties, because supporting a third risks helping the least preferred outcome (the spoiler effect, as with Nader in Florida in 2000).\n• Institutional barriers — state-by-state ballot access rules with substantial signature thresholds; presidential debate polling thresholds; campaign finance and primary structures built around the two parties.\n• Absorption — when a third party shows an issue has traction, a major party adopts its position, capturing the support. Populist and Progressive causes were absorbed this way, as more recently were Tea Party concerns.\n• Primaries allow dissenters to contest control of an existing party rather than founding a new one.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires the mechanism, not just the label.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three causes of polarisation in US politics.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Ideological sorting — until the 1960s both parties contained conservative southern Democrats and liberal northern Republicans, making cross-party coalitions routine. Democratic support for civil rights triggered the South's realignment, and each party became internally coherent. Analysis: polarisation is therefore partly the result of parties becoming CONSISTENT, not of anyone becoming more extreme.\n• Safe districts and primary dominance — where the general election is a formality, the only threat is a primary challenge from the flank, so legislators are rewarded for purity and punished for compromise.\n• Media fragmentation — partisan cable news and social media allow citizens to consume information confirming existing views.\n• Ideological donors and groups funding primary challenges; the nationalisation of politics eroding local factors.\n\nGuidance: the sorting point is what separates L3 from L2.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three consequences of polarisation for US government.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Legislative gridlock — the filibuster effectively requires 60 votes, and bipartisan coalitions have become rare, so major legislation frequently fails even under unified government. Analysis: this compounds a constitutional design already built to require broad consensus, raising the effective threshold above what the framers intended.\n• Displacement to the executive and the courts — where Congress cannot legislate, presidents govern by executive order and contested questions are settled by litigation. Analysis: transfers authority from the most representative branch to the two least accountable, and produces oscillation since orders are reversed by successors.\n• Erosion of institutional norms — the treatment of judicial nominations, debt-ceiling brinkmanship and shutdowns reflect willingness to use procedural powers maximally. Analysis: the American system depends on forbearance because its formal rules permit far more obstruction than was historically practised.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires causal analysis.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three arguments that US parties have been renewed rather than have declined.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Party-line voting in Congress is at historic highs, and leadership controls the agenda far more tightly than in the 1970s; the cross-party coalitions that once characterised Congress have largely disappeared.\n• Voter behaviour — partisan identification predicts vote choice more reliably than at any point in decades, split-ticket voting has collapsed, and voters increasingly align positions across issues with their party.\n• Fundraising and infrastructure — national committees raise enormous sums and provide data, analytics and coordinated campaign strategy on a scale not previously possible.\n\nCounter-argument worth crediting: parties remain weak in the respect that matters most — they CANNOT control who runs under their label, since primaries allow voters to nominate candidates the leadership opposes, and candidates fund and organise independently.\n\nAnalysis expected: parties have become stronger as IDEOLOGICAL IDENTITIES and weaker as ORGANISATIONS, so decline and renewal describe different dimensions.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three reasons why third parties struggle in US national elections.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Winner-take-all rules — single-member plurality districts and the Electoral College mean substantial but dispersed support produces nothing. Perot won ~19% of the popular vote in 1992 and no electoral votes at all.\n• The wasted vote and spoiler perception — supporting a third party risks helping the least preferred major candidate, as Nader's candidacy in Florida in 2000 is widely argued to have done, making third-party voting appear counterproductive rather than merely futile.\n• Institutional barriers — differing state ballot access requirements imposing significant signature and legal costs; debate polling thresholds excluding third-party candidates from the largest audiences of the campaign.\n• Absorption of issues — when a third party demonstrates traction, a major party adopts a version of its position, capturing the support and removing the rationale for the insurgent party.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires mechanism and example.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: '\'Political parties in the US are far weaker than political parties in the UK.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n\n• CONTROL OF CANDIDATES: UK parties select their candidates and can withdraw the whip — Starmer suspended seven Labour MPs in July 2024 for voting against the two-child benefit cap. US parties cannot stop primary voters nominating candidates the leadership opposes, and cannot deselect. On this dimension US parties are clearly weaker.\n• DISCIPLINE IN THE LEGISLATURE: fusion of powers means UK government survival depends on party cohesion, so whips, patronage and collective responsibility are strong. US legislators have separate mandates and answer to primary electorates. Yet party-line voting in Congress is now at historic highs — the One Big Beautiful Bill Act (2025) passed both chambers almost entirely on party lines — while UK governments with large majorities still face revolts, as Labour did over welfare cuts in 2025, forcing major concessions.\n• LEADERSHIP INSTABILITY: US factions can topple their own leaders — Kevin McCarthy was removed as Speaker in 2023 on a motion backed by eight Republicans. But UK parties also remove leaders: the Conservatives changed Prime Minister three times between 2019 and 2022.\n• ORGANISATION AND FINANCE: UK parties are national membership organisations with central offices and national manifestos. US parties are decentralised, organised under 50 state laws, without formal membership; candidates build their own campaign organisations and Super PACs can bypass party committees. Counterpoint: the DNC and RNC raise and coordinate very large sums and data operations.\n• PARTY IN THE ELECTORATE: US partisan identity and straight-ticket voting are very strong, and affective polarisation makes party the best predictor of vote choice. UK partisan dealignment is advanced: in 2024 Labour and the Conservatives together won only about 57% of the vote, and volatility and the rise of Reform UK show weakening attachment. On this dimension UK parties may be the weaker.\n• PRESIDENT AND PARTY: Trump's dominance of the Republican Party — endorsements that decide primaries, the defeat of critics such as Liz Cheney in 2022 — shows a personalised form of party strength with no exact UK parallel.\n\nJudgement: US parties are weaker as ORGANISATIONS (selection, discipline, structure), but stronger as IDENTITIES in the electorate and in Congress than UK parties now are. Strong answers explain this through structural (separation vs fusion of powers, primaries) and cultural (polarised partisan identities vs dealignment) approaches.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. This is a Section C comparative essay: answers must compare the US and the UK explicitly and in a sustained way, point by point, and explain WHY the two differ or resemble each other (for example through structural, rational or cultural approaches). An answer that treats the two countries in separate halves with little direct comparison should not go above Level 3; an answer that deals with only one country cannot go above Level 2. Synoptic links may be made to political parties and voting behaviour (Paper 1), Parliament and the executive, the separation and fusion of powers, Congress, the presidency and primaries. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant points should be credited. It does not matter what conclusion is reached, provided it is supported by the argument and examples.` },

      { q: 'EXTRACTS (written for practice):\n\nExtract 1 — written for this practice question in the style of a comment piece by a former congressional staff member\n"Polarisation is no longer a feature of American politics; it is the system. Most Republicans and Democrats in Congress now vote with their party almost all the time, and the ideological overlap between the parties that once allowed deals has all but disappeared. The result is government by brinkmanship: repeated shutdowns, standoffs over the debt ceiling, and judicial confirmations fought as total war. Because most House districts are safe, members fear a primary challenge from their own flank far more than defeat by the other party, so compromise is punished. And when voters come to see the other party as an enemy rather than a rival, they stop accepting defeat, as the attack on the Capitol on 6 January 2021 showed."\n\nExtract 2 — written for this practice question in the style of an academic blog post by a political scientist\n"The alarm about polarisation is overstated. Sharper differences between the parties give voters a real choice and make it clear whom to hold responsible — something critics of the old, muddled parties once demanded. Turnout in 2020 was the highest in over a century, and turnout in 2024 was also high: polarised elections engage citizens. Divided politics has not stopped legislating. The Infrastructure Investment and Jobs Act (2021), the CHIPS and Science Act (2022) and the Respect for Marriage Act (2022) all passed with votes from both parties. Polarisation among politicians is also greater than among voters, many of whom hold mixed views. The United States is contentious, but its institutions have absorbed polarisation before."\n\nAnalyse, evaluate and compare the arguments in the above extracts regarding the consequences of partisan polarisation for US politics.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n\n• EXTRACT 1 — GRIDLOCK AND BRINKMANSHIP: argues polarisation paralyses government through shutdowns, debt-ceiling standoffs and confirmation battles. Students can support this with own knowledge: the 2018–19 and 2025 shutdowns, the Garland (2016) and Barrett (2020) confirmations, and increasing reliance on executive orders when Congress stalls.\n• EXTRACT 1 — CAUSES: identifies safe districts and primaries as the mechanism that punishes compromise. This is a strong structural argument that students can link to gerrymandering (Rucho, 2019) and to the Freedom Caucus's leverage in a narrow House majority.\n• EXTRACT 1 — LEGITIMACY: the claim that polarisation undermines acceptance of results is supported by 6 January 2021 and continuing disputes over the 2020 result. Students should weigh whether this is the gravest consequence of all.\n• EXTRACT 2 — CHOICE AND ACCOUNTABILITY: argues clear party differences are democratic, echoing the responsible-party-government model. Its turnout claim is accurate (2020 was the highest since 1900), though turnout may rise from fear of the other side rather than healthy engagement.\n• EXTRACT 2 — LEGISLATION STILL PASSES: the three bipartisan Acts are accurate, but students should note they were exceptions, several needed Senate deals to avoid the filibuster, and major measures such as the One Big Beautiful Bill Act (2025) passed on party lines through budget reconciliation.\n• EXTRACT 2 — ELITE VS MASS: the point that elites are more polarised than voters is supported by research, but affective polarisation (dislike of the other party) among voters has risen sharply, which weakens the extract's reassurance.\n• COMPARISON AND STATUS: the extracts agree that parties are more distinct but disagree on whether that is a pathology or a clarification. Extract 1 is written by an insider focused on how Congress works; Extract 2 by an academic taking a longer view — each emphasises evidence suited to its perspective.\n\nJudgement: the most convincing answers conclude that Extract 1 better explains how polarisation interacts with US institutions (separation of powers and supermajority rules turn party conflict into gridlock), while Extract 2 is right that polarisation has some democratic benefits — which would be greater in a system, like the UK's, where the majority party can govern.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and comparing the arguments in the extracts and weighing which are more convincing; evaluate their status (author, type of publication, purpose, date) where relevant. Own knowledge should be used to test the extracts' claims, not as a free-standing essay. Answers that do not focus on the arguments in the extracts cannot go above Level 2.` },

      { q: '\'Third parties have far more influence in the UK than in the US.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n\n• REPRESENTATION: in the UK, third and minor parties hold a significant block of Commons seats — the Liberal Democrats won 72 in 2024, and the SNP, Reform UK, the Greens, Plaid Cymru and the Northern Ireland parties are also represented. In Congress the only non-major-party members are a few independents (e.g. Bernie Sanders and Angus King), who caucus with the Democrats.\n• GOVERNMENT: UK third parties can enter or sustain government — the Conservative–Liberal Democrat coalition (2010–15) and the DUP's confidence-and-supply agreement (2017–19). The separation of powers and fixed terms mean no US third party has any comparable role in forming an administration.\n• DEVOLVED AND LOCAL POWER: the SNP has governed Scotland since 2007 and Plaid Cymru has shared power in Wales; Reform UK won control of several English councils in May 2025. In the US, third parties rarely win at state level, although federalism offers some openings.\n• AGENDA-SETTING: in both countries third parties shape policy by forcing major parties to respond. UKIP's electoral pressure contributed to Cameron's promise of the 2016 referendum; Reform UK's poll lead has pushed both main parties to harden immigration policy. In the US, Perot's 1992 campaign (about 19% of the vote, no electoral votes) pushed the deficit up the agenda, and historic Populist and Progressive demands were absorbed by the major parties.\n• SPOILER EFFECT: US third parties matter mainly by splitting votes — Nader in Florida in 2000 — and in 2024 Robert F. Kennedy Jr. ran as an independent before endorsing Trump. This is influence of a negative kind; the UK equivalent is vote-splitting in FPTP seats.\n• WHY THEY DIFFER: both countries use FPTP, so Duverger's law applies to both, but UK third parties benefit from regional concentration (SNP, Plaid, DUP), PR in devolved elections, parliamentary government and weaker partisan alignment. US third parties face the Electoral College, ballot-access rules, debate thresholds, and big-tent parties that absorb dissent through primaries.\n\nJudgement: the statement is largely correct — UK third parties win seats, govern in the devolved nations and have held the balance of power at Westminster, while US third parties are confined to agenda-setting and spoiling. Strong answers explain this with all three approaches and note that the gap may be widening as UK dealignment continues.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. This is a Section C comparative essay: answers must compare the US and the UK explicitly and in a sustained way, point by point, and explain WHY the two differ or resemble each other (for example through structural, rational or cultural approaches). An answer that treats the two countries in separate halves with little direct comparison should not go above Level 3; an answer that deals with only one country cannot go above Level 2. Synoptic links may be made to electoral systems and voting behaviour (Paper 1), devolution, coalition government and parliamentary sovereignty, federalism, the Electoral College and primaries. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant points should be credited. It does not matter what conclusion is reached, provided it is supported by the argument and examples.` },

      { q: '\'The UK party system serves democracy better than the US party system.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n\n• CHOICE AND REPRESENTATION: the UK has a multi-party system in votes, and increasingly in seats — voters can back the Liberal Democrats, Reform UK, the Greens or nationalist parties and see some elected. In the US, two parties hold virtually every seat in Congress and state legislatures, so millions of independents and dissenters have no party of their own. On range of choice the UK serves democracy better.\n• FAIRNESS OF TRANSLATION: but FPTP at Westminster translates this choice poorly — in 2024 Reform UK won about 14% of the vote and five seats, the Greens about 7% and four seats. US voters face a narrower choice, but its outcomes are less distorted between the two parties that are chosen. Students may argue a wide choice that is not reflected in seats is only partly an improvement.\n• ACCOUNTABILITY AND GOVERNMENT: UK parties offer manifestos they can deliver through a disciplined Commons majority, so voters know whom to hold responsible — the responsible-party model. In the US, separation of powers and divided government blur responsibility. However, a UK government can be elected on about a third of the vote, which raises questions of legitimacy.\n• INTERNAL DEMOCRACY: US primaries give ordinary voters a say in candidate selection; UK selection is controlled by members and party machines. Here the US arguably serves participation better.\n• POLARISATION AND CONSENSUS: US polarisation has produced gridlock, shutdowns and disputes over electoral legitimacy (6 January 2021). The UK has seen fragmentation and volatility rather than two-bloc hostility, though Brexit showed divisions can cut across parties.\n• FINANCE AND ACCESS: UK parties campaign under statutory spending limits with free broadcasts; US parties and candidates depend on unlimited outside money after Citizens United, advantaging well-funded interests.\n\nJudgement: the UK party system arguably serves democracy better on CHOICE and ACCOUNTABILITY, the US better on PARTICIPATION IN SELECTION, and both are distorted by FPTP. Strong answers explain why structurally — parliamentary government rewards cohesive programmatic parties; separation of powers and federalism produce loose, candidate-centred ones — and reach a clear view on which democratic value matters most.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. This is a Section C comparative essay: answers must compare the US and the UK explicitly and in a sustained way, point by point, and explain WHY the two differ or resemble each other (for example through structural, rational or cultural approaches). An answer that treats the two countries in separate halves with little direct comparison should not go above Level 3; an answer that deals with only one country cannot go above Level 2. Synoptic links may be made to political parties, electoral systems and democracy and participation (Paper 1), Parliament and the executive, Congress, primaries and campaign finance. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant points should be credited. It does not matter what conclusion is reached, provided it is supported by the argument and examples.` },
    ]
  },

  '2.7': {
    name: 'US Pressure Groups',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three methods used by US pressure groups to influence policy.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Lobbying — members of Congress, congressional staff and federal agencies, often supplying draft legislative language. Analysis: the most consistently effective method, because it operates at the drafting stage and because Congress's own research capacity has declined relative to what it regulates — a dependence that is itself the concern.\n• Electioneering — PAC donations, unlimited Super PAC expenditure, scorecards mobilising single-issue voters, and primary funding. Analysis: distinctively powerful because primaries determine outcomes in most safe districts, so a group able to fund a challenger holds leverage over a legislator's career.\n• Litigation — test cases and amicus curiae briefs. Analysis: can deliver permanent nationwide change beyond legislative reversal, as the NAACP's strategy culminating in Brown showed.\n• Grassroots mobilisation and direct action, including astroturfing.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires evaluation of when each works.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three reasons why pressure groups are powerful in the USA.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Multiple access points — separation of powers means a bill must pass the House, pass the Senate, survive the filibuster, be signed and survive judicial review; federalism adds fifty state governments. A group needs to succeed at only ONE veto point to block.\n• Weak party discipline — legislators are nominated in primaries and fund their own campaigns, so they vote independently. Persuading or funding one member can genuinely change an outcome.\n• Candidate-centred, expensive elections make legislators structurally dependent on continuous fundraising.\n• Entrenched, judicially enforceable rights make litigation a route to permanent change.\n• First Amendment protection of speech, assembly and petition gives group activity explicit constitutional standing.\n\nAnalysis expected: the crucial qualification is that many access points are also many VETO points, so US groups are better at blocking than achieving.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which pressure groups influence Congress.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Direct lobbying of members and staff — supplying research, technical expertise and frequently draft legislative language, at the stage before positions become public and entrenched.\n• Campaign funding through PACs and independent expenditure through Super PACs, creating dependence given the cost of competitive campaigns.\n• Scorecards and primary threats — rating legislators' voting records to mobilise single-issue voters. The NRA's effectiveness rested less on total spending than on a membership that would vote on that issue alone in primaries, making opposition dangerous.\n• Iron triangles — stable relationships between a committee, an agency and a group, insulating a policy area from wider scrutiny.\n• Grassroots mobilisation generating constituent contact.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires the primaries mechanism, which is what makes US group leverage distinctive.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three reasons why US pressure groups use litigation.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• It can deliver permanent nationwide change — because the Constitution is entrenched and judicially enforceable, a ruling that a law is unconstitutional applies across the country and can realistically be reversed only by the Court itself or by an unattainable amendment. No legislative victory offers comparable security.\n• It bypasses hostile majorities — litigation requires only a plaintiff with standing and a strong legal argument rather than electoral or congressional majorities. The NAACP's strategy culminating in Brown achieved what a Congress dependent on southern Democratic votes would not deliver.\n• Methods — carefully selected test cases with sympathetic facts, funding litigation, and amicus curiae briefs in cases brought by others.\n\nLimitations worth crediting: rulings depend on the Court's composition and can be reversed (Dobbs); judgments require executive and state cooperation, as the slow desegregation after Brown showed; litigation is expensive, favouring well-resourced groups.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three criticisms of the influence of US pressure groups.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Influence tracks resources rather than numbers — post-Citizens United unlimited independent expenditure means wealthy individuals, corporations and unions dominate political communication, inverting the democratic principle that a diffuse majority should prevail over a well-funded minority.\n• Dark money — 501(c)(4) organisations conceal donors, preventing voters from discounting messages by their source.\n• Iron triangles and the revolving door — stable committee–agency–group relationships, reinforced by officials moving into industry roles, place whole policy areas beyond effective public scrutiny.\n• Groups are unelected, need not be internally democratic, and bear no responsibility for consequences.\n\nAnalysis expected: the most sophisticated criticism is that the FRAGMENTATION of the American system makes it impossible for voters to identify who is responsible for an outcome — so the accountability failure lies in institutional design.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which iron triangles operate in the USA.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — the three participants and the exchange between them:\n• The interest group supplies campaign funding, technical expertise and political support.\n• The congressional committee or subcommittee supplies favourable legislation, appropriations and sympathetic oversight.\n• The federal agency supplies implementation favourable to the group's interests.\n\nAnalysis expected:\n• The arrangement is mutually beneficial and therefore STABLE — all three participants benefit from the status quo, which makes change difficult.\n• It insulates a policy area from wider political scrutiny, since no participant has an incentive to expose it. Defence procurement and agriculture are the standard examples.\n• The accountability problem: decisions are made by participants who all benefit and none of whom faces the public directly for the outcome.\n• Qualification worth crediting: the model fits some areas far better than others — ISSUE NETWORKS in contested fields such as healthcare and the environment are far more open and competitive.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires the qualification as well as the model.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: '\'Pressure groups do more to enhance democracy in the US than in the UK.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n\n• ACCESS POINTS AND PLURALISM: the US separation of powers and federalism give groups many access points — Congress, committees, agencies, courts and 50 state governments — so defeat in one arena is not final. The UK's fusion of powers concentrates decisions in the executive, so influence depends on insider access to ministers and civil servants, which fewer groups enjoy. The US is therefore more open to a wide range of interests.\n• LITIGATION AND MINORITY RIGHTS: the entrenched US Constitution lets groups win rights through the courts — the NAACP in Brown (1954), the ACLU. UK groups can use judicial review and the Human Rights Act, but parliamentary sovereignty limits what courts can deliver. US groups can protect minorities against majorities more effectively.\n• MONEY AND POLITICAL EQUALITY: after Citizens United (2010) US groups can spend unlimited sums through Super PACs; AIPAC-linked spending helped defeat two incumbent House Democrats in 2024 primaries. The UK's spending caps, broadcast advertising ban and regulation of non-party campaigning limit this. On political equality the UK comes out better.\n• PARTICIPATION AND OUTSIDERS: UK outsider campaigns can succeed — the Justice for Sub-postmasters Alliance, helped by public pressure after the 2024 ITV drama, secured legislation quashing convictions in 2024. But recent UK public order law (Police, Crime, Sentencing and Courts Act 2022; Public Order Act 2023) and the proscription of Palestine Action under terrorism law in 2025 restrict direct action. First Amendment protection of assembly and petition is much stronger in the US.\n• ACCOUNTABILITY AND TRANSPARENCY: both have revolving-door and lobbying concerns — iron triangles in the US; the Greensill affair (2021) in the UK. US lobbying disclosure is more extensive; UK lobbying regulation is narrower.\n• REPRESENTATION: in both countries group membership exceeds party membership, so groups are a major channel of participation — AARP and the National Trust each have millions of members.\n\nJudgement: US groups do more to enhance PLURALISM and minority rights (more access points, entrenched rights, stronger protest protections), while UK groups do less damage to POLITICAL EQUALITY because money is regulated. Strong answers use the pluralist/elitist framework and explain the difference structurally, reaching a view on which democratic value matters more.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. This is a Section C comparative essay: answers must compare the US and the UK explicitly and in a sustained way, point by point, and explain WHY the two differ or resemble each other (for example through structural, rational or cultural approaches). An answer that treats the two countries in separate halves with little direct comparison should not go above Level 3; an answer that deals with only one country cannot go above Level 2. Synoptic links may be made to pressure groups and democracy and participation (Paper 1), the judiciary and rights, the constitution (entrenchment vs sovereignty), campaign finance, and the separation and fusion of powers. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant points should be credited. It does not matter what conclusion is reached, provided it is supported by the argument and examples.` },

      { q: '\'In both the US and the UK, pressure groups have replaced political parties as the most important way of representing citizens.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n\n• MEMBERSHIP AND PARTICIPATION: in the UK the National Trust alone has several times the combined membership of the main parties, and party membership is a small fraction of the electorate. The US has no formal party membership but huge groups — AARP has tens of millions of members. In both countries citizens are more likely to join a group or sign a petition than to join a party.\n• SINGLE-ISSUE REPRESENTATION: groups represent intense views that big-tent parties blur — the NRA, Planned Parenthood, environmental groups. In the UK, groups such as the BMA or the NFU represent occupational interests; the farmers' campaign against inheritance tax changes (2024–25) shows groups voicing concerns neither main party channelled.\n• PARTIES STILL GOVERN: only parties contest elections, write manifestos and form governments. In the UK the fusion of powers makes the governing party the decisive actor, and groups often work through it (unions and Labour). In the US, party-line voting in Congress is at historic highs, so party is the best predictor of how representatives vote.\n• DIFFERENT BALANCE IN EACH COUNTRY: weak US party organisation, primaries and many access points make groups relatively more important in the US; groups can threaten a legislator through primary funding in a way no UK group can. In the UK, strong executive government and party discipline leave groups more dependent on insider access.\n• GROUPS AND PARTIES MERGING: in the US, polarisation has made most major groups reliably partisan (the NRA with Republicans, most unions with Democrats); in the UK, affiliated unions are part of Labour's structure. Groups increasingly represent citizens THROUGH parties rather than instead of them.\n• QUALITY OF REPRESENTATION: groups are unelected, may be run by professional staff (chequebook membership), and represent the well-organised and well-funded; parties aggregate interests and are accountable at elections. Whether groups are "most important" depends on which kind of representation is meant.\n\nJudgement: the statement is stronger for PARTICIPATION (citizens join groups, not parties) than for REPRESENTATION IN GOVERNMENT (parties still decide). Strong answers compare the two countries explicitly — groups have gone further towards displacing parties in the US because of its structure — and explain why using structural, rational and cultural approaches.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. This is a Section C comparative essay: answers must compare the US and the UK explicitly and in a sustained way, point by point, and explain WHY the two differ or resemble each other (for example through structural, rational or cultural approaches). An answer that treats the two countries in separate halves with little direct comparison should not go above Level 3; an answer that deals with only one country cannot go above Level 2. Synoptic links may be made to political parties, pressure groups and democracy and participation (Paper 1), Parliament and the executive, Congress and primaries, and polarisation. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant points should be credited. It does not matter what conclusion is reached, provided it is supported by the argument and examples.` },

      { q: 'EXTRACTS (written for practice):\n\nExtract 1 — written for this practice question in the style of a report summary by a campaign-finance reform organisation\n"In Citizens United v FEC (2010) a five-to-four Supreme Court held that corporations, unions and associations have a First Amendment right to spend unlimited sums on independent political advocacy. A lower-court ruling soon after, SpeechNow.org v FEC, opened the way for Super PACs that can raise unlimited sums. The result has been a flood of outside money, much of it routed through non-profit groups that need not disclose their donors. A handful of billionaires now supply a large share of the money in federal elections; in 2024 one donor alone spent more than $200 million supporting a presidential candidate. Politicians spend their time courting such donors, and ordinary citizens reasonably conclude that the system listens to money first."\n\nExtract 2 — written for this practice question in the style of an article by a civil-liberties lawyer\n"Citizens United protected political speech, which is exactly what the First Amendment is for. The ruling applies equally to unions, advocacy groups and non-profits of every political colour, not just to large companies. Nor does money buy elections. In 2020 a billionaire who spent heavily on his own primary campaign won only American Samoa. In 2024 the Harris campaign raised over a billion dollars in less than four months and lost. Voters decide on partisanship, the economy and the candidates, not on the number of advertisements. Independent spending is, by law, not coordinated with campaigns, so it carries less risk of corruption than direct donations. The answer to speech you dislike is more speech, not government limits on who may speak."\n\nAnalyse, evaluate and compare the arguments in the above extracts regarding the impact of the Citizens United ruling on US democracy.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n\n• EXTRACT 1 — LEGAL CHAIN: accurately explains Citizens United (5–4, 2010) and SpeechNow.org v FEC as the origin of Super PACs. Students can add that Buckley v Valeo (1976) had already treated spending as speech, so Citizens United extended an existing logic.\n• EXTRACT 1 — CONCENTRATION AND DARK MONEY: argues that outside spending and undisclosed 501(c)(4) money concentrate influence in a few donors. Its 2024 example (a single donor spending over $200 million) is accurate. Students should weigh whether this shows influence over OUTCOMES or over ACCESS and agenda.\n• EXTRACT 2 — FREE SPEECH PRINCIPLE: the ruling's reasoning — restricting spending restricts speech — is a serious constitutional argument, and it does apply to unions and advocacy groups. Students can link this to the entrenched First Amendment and the Court's power of judicial review.\n• EXTRACT 2 — MONEY DOES NOT DETERMINE OUTCOMES: both examples are accurate (Bloomberg's 2020 primary run won only American Samoa; Harris raised over $1 billion in 2024 and lost). This effectively rebuts a crude "money buys elections" claim, but not Extract 1's subtler claim about access and dependence on donors.\n• EXTRACT 2 — NO COORDINATION: the formal ban on coordination is real, but critics argue it is weakly enforced and easy to work around, and the FEC is often deadlocked along party lines.\n• COMPARISON AND STATUS: the extracts measure "damage" differently — Extract 1 by political equality and public trust, Extract 2 by liberty and electoral outcomes. Both come from organisations with a clear agenda: a reform group and a civil-liberties lawyer. Students may note that reform is constitutionally foreclosed unless the Court reverses itself or an amendment passes, a point neither extract makes.\n\nJudgement: the most convincing answers conclude that Extract 2 wins the argument about OUTCOMES, but Extract 1 wins the argument about ACCESS and EQUALITY, which is the more important democratic concern — and that the underlying issue is the constitutional status of spending as speech.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and comparing the arguments in the extracts and weighing which are more convincing; evaluate their status (author, type of publication, purpose, date) where relevant. Own knowledge should be used to test the extracts' claims, not as a free-standing essay. Answers that do not focus on the arguments in the extracts cannot go above Level 2.` },

      { q: '\'Lobbying is more effectively regulated in the US than in the UK.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n\n• DISCLOSURE: the US Lobbying Disclosure Act (1995), tightened by the Honest Leadership and Open Government Act (2007), requires lobbyists — in-house as well as consultants — to register and report their clients, issues and spending every quarter, so federal lobbying spending (over $4 billion a year) is publicly known. The UK Transparency of Lobbying Act 2014 created a Register of Consultant Lobbyists covering only third-party consultants who contact ministers or permanent secretaries; in-house lobbyists are excluded. On transparency the US is clearly ahead.\n• THE REVOLVING DOOR: US law imposes cooling-off periods (two years for former senators lobbying Congress, one year for House members) with legal penalties. The UK relies on the Advisory Committee on Business Appointments, whose rulings are advisory and unenforceable. The Greensill affair (2021), in which a former Prime Minister lobbied ministers by text message without registering because he was an employee, exposed the gap.\n• STANDARDS FOR LEGISLATORS: the UK has a Parliamentary Commissioner for Standards and a ban on paid advocacy; the Owen Paterson case (2021) showed the system could find breaches, though the government's attempt to overturn the finding revealed its fragility. US ethics committees are often seen as partisan and slow.\n• SCALE OF THE PROBLEM: regulation may be more extensive in the US because the problem is larger — many access points, weak parties, and unlimited outside spending after Citizens United mean lobbying is more consequential. Disclosure does not limit influence; it records it. The UK's statutory spending caps and broadcast advertising ban limit money in politics in ways US law cannot.\n• DEVOLUTION: the Lobbying (Scotland) Act 2016 covers in-house lobbyists and is more comprehensive than the Westminster regime, showing UK regulation can vary within the country, as US regulation does across states.\n• ENFORCEMENT AND CULTURE: US rules are enforced through the courts and Justice Department, while UK regulation relies heavily on codes and conventions (the Ministerial Code, enforced at the Prime Minister's discretion) — a reflection of the UK's uncodified constitution.\n\nJudgement: the US regulates lobbying more EXTENSIVELY and with legal teeth, but regulation is not the same as effectiveness — the UK limits the underlying influence of money more successfully. Strong answers explain the difference structurally and culturally (codified law vs conventions) and link it to the pluralist/elitist debate.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. This is a Section C comparative essay: answers must compare the US and the UK explicitly and in a sustained way, point by point, and explain WHY the two differ or resemble each other (for example through structural, rational or cultural approaches). An answer that treats the two countries in separate halves with little direct comparison should not go above Level 3; an answer that deals with only one country cannot go above Level 2. Synoptic links may be made to pressure groups and democracy and participation (Paper 1), the uncodified constitution and conventions, the Ministerial Code and the executive, Parliament, devolution, and campaign finance. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant points should be credited. It does not matter what conclusion is reached, provided it is supported by the argument and examples.` },
    ]
  },

  '2.8': {
    name: 'Civil Rights',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three constitutional protections of civil rights in the USA.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• The Fourteenth Amendment (1868) — citizenship by birth, due process and equal protection of the laws, binding on the STATES. Analysis: the basis of most modern civil rights litigation, including Brown and Obergefell.\n• The Thirteenth Amendment (1865) — abolished slavery and involuntary servitude.\n• The Fifteenth Amendment (1870) — no denial of the vote on grounds of race, colour or previous servitude.\n• Later amendments — the Nineteenth (1920, women's suffrage), Twenty-Fourth (1964, poll taxes) and Twenty-Sixth (1971, voting at 18).\n• The Bill of Rights, incorporated against the states through the Fourteenth Amendment.\n\nAnalysis expected: the Reconstruction amendments lay SUBSTANTIALLY DORMANT for nearly a century — Plessy (1896) upheld segregation under the very amendment Brown (1954) later used to strike it down. Text alone achieves nothing without institutional will.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which civil rights have been advanced in the USA.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Constitutional amendment — the Thirteenth, Fourteenth and Fifteenth. Analysis: the most secure route, beyond legislative reversal and judicial reinterpretation, but practically unattainable today.\n• Judicial interpretation — Brown (1954), Loving, Obergefell (2015), Bostock (2020). Analysis: the most productive route, requiring no legislative majority and delivering immediate nationwide effect — invaluable when elected branches were hostile. But Dobbs showed what one Court grants another can withdraw.\n• Legislation and mass mobilisation — the Civil Rights Act 1964 and Voting Rights Act 1965, produced by sustained non-violent direct action. Analysis: reaches PRIVATE discrimination in employment, housing and public accommodation, which constitutional rulings cannot, and supplies enforcement machinery.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires analysis of the DIFFERENT SECURITY PROFILE of each route.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three methods used by the US civil rights movement.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Litigation — the NAACP Legal Defense Fund pursued carefully chosen test cases culminating in Brown (1954). Analysis: highly effective at establishing legal principle, delivering a nationwide ruling no legislature of the period would have produced given southern Democratic power in Congress; but it could not compel implementation.\n• Non-violent direct action — the Montgomery bus boycott, sit-ins, freedom rides, the 1963 March on Washington. Analysis: generated the political pressure litigation could not; televised images of peaceful protesters met with violence shifted northern public opinion decisively.\n• Legislative lobbying — securing the Civil Rights Act 1964 and Voting Rights Act 1965, which supplied enforcement machinery and reached private discrimination.\n\nAnalysis expected: NEITHER method would have succeeded alone. Each addressed a different obstacle, and the movement's effectiveness lay in combining them.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which voting rights have been restricted in the USA.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Historically — literacy tests, poll taxes, grandfather clauses and violence used to suppress black voting under Jim Crow, despite the Fifteenth Amendment.\n• Shelby County v Holder (2013) — struck down the VRA coverage formula, rendering pre-clearance inoperative. Analysis: several formerly covered states subsequently introduced stricter voter ID, reduced early voting, closed polling places and purged rolls; these now take effect immediately and can only be challenged afterwards, placing cost and burden on the challenger.\n• Partisan gerrymandering — Rucho v Common Cause (2019) held such claims non-justiciable in federal courts, leaving remedy to state constitutions and ballot initiatives.\n• Felon disenfranchisement, which disproportionately affects minority communities.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires the Shelby County mechanism, not just the case name.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three arguments against affirmative action.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• The constitutional objection — treating individuals differently by race is itself racial discrimination and sits uneasily with the equal protection clause's guarantee to every person. Analysis: this reasoning prevailed in Students for Fair Admissions v Harvard (2023), ending race-conscious university admissions.\n• Stigma — preferences may lead others, and beneficiaries themselves, to doubt that success was earned on merit. Analysis: the policy can undermine the very equality of esteem it aims to create.\n• Harm to other groups — it can disadvantage applicants from other minority groups, such as the Asian American applicants whose case was central in SFFA. Analysis: race-conscious policy creates new losers rather than simply correcting past wrongs.\n• The wrong target — race is a poor proxy for disadvantage, so policies based on income or class would reach the disadvantaged more accurately. Analysis: many critics favour class-based alternatives rather than no remedy at all.\n\nGuidance: credit accurate case knowledge (Bakke 1978, Grutter 2003, SFFA 2023). The balanced debate belongs in 25-mark answers; here three developed arguments against are required.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which federalism affects civil rights in the USA.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Division of authority — the Tenth Amendment reserves elections, education, policing, criminal justice and (since Dobbs) abortion regulation to the states, so many decisions affecting rights are taken at state level.\n• A historical shield for discrimination — states' rights arguments were used to resist federal intervention throughout the Jim Crow era; it required the Fourteenth Amendment, Supreme Court rulings and eventually federal legislation and troops to overcome state resistance.\n• Contemporary variation — after Dobbs, abortion access differs radically across state lines; voting procedures, LGBT protections and policing standards likewise differ, so a citizen's practical entitlements depend substantially on residence.\n• Federalism can also ADVANCE rights — several states recognised same-sex marriage before Obergefell, and states have legislated protections exceeding the federal floor.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires the point that federalism cuts both ways.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: '\'In both the US and the UK, civil liberties have been sacrificed in the name of national security.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n\n• US AFTER 9/11: the USA PATRIOT Act (2001) expanded surveillance and detention powers; detainees were held without trial at Guantanamo Bay, which remains open; the Snowden revelations (2013) exposed NSA bulk collection of phone records. Surveillance under FISA section 702 was reauthorised in 2024. This supports the statement.\n• UK AFTER 9/11 AND 7/7: indefinite detention of foreign terror suspects (2001), control orders (2005) and TPIMs (2011), extended pre-charge detention, and the Investigatory Powers Act 2016 with its broad bulk-data powers. The proscription of Palestine Action in 2025 led to hundreds of arrests of people showing support at protests. This also supports the statement — arguably more so, since Parliament can legislate without constitutional limit.\n• JUDICIAL PUSHBACK — US: the Court insisted on due process — Hamdi v Rumsfeld (2004) and Boumediene v Bush (2008) gave Guantanamo detainees access to habeas corpus. In 2025 the Court required that people targeted for removal under the Alien Enemies Act receive notice and a chance to challenge it. But it also upheld the travel ban (Trump v Hawaii, 2018), showing deference on security.\n• JUDICIAL PUSHBACK — UK: the Belmarsh case (A v Secretary of State for the Home Department, 2004) produced a declaration of incompatibility, and Parliament replaced indefinite detention with control orders. But under parliamentary sovereignty declarations do not strike down law, so UK courts can only prompt Parliament, whereas US courts can invalidate.\n• LEGISLATIVE CORRECTION: Congress passed the USA FREEDOM Act (2015), ending the bulk phone-records programme; Parliament repealed control orders in favour of TPIMs and cut the maximum pre-charge detention from 28 to 14 days (2012). Both legislatures have corrected excesses, but only partially.\n• PROTEST AND SPEECH: First Amendment protection of speech and assembly is far stronger than UK protection. The UK's Public Order Act 2023 and terrorism law have been used against protest in ways a US court would struggle to uphold, though US federal responses to protest in 2025 also raised civil liberties concerns.\n\nJudgement: both countries have traded liberty for security, but the US Constitution sets a floor that UK law does not — US courts can strike down, UK courts can only declare. Strong answers weigh this against the UK's better record of legislatures responding to court rulings, and explain the difference structurally (entrenched vs sovereign) and culturally (US rights culture vs UK trust in Parliament).\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. This is a Section C comparative essay: answers must compare the US and the UK explicitly and in a sustained way, point by point, and explain WHY the two differ or resemble each other (for example through structural, rational or cultural approaches). An answer that treats the two countries in separate halves with little direct comparison should not go above Level 3; an answer that deals with only one country cannot go above Level 2. Synoptic links may be made to the Human Rights Act and the UK judiciary (Paper 1), parliamentary sovereignty, the executive, the US Supreme Court and constitution, and pressure groups. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant points should be credited. It does not matter what conclusion is reached, provided it is supported by the argument and examples.` },

      { q: '\'In both the US and the UK, legislatures have done more than courts to advance civil rights.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n\n• UK — LEGISLATION LEADS: most UK civil rights come from Acts of Parliament — the Race Relations Acts (1965, 1968, 1976), the Equal Pay Act 1970, the Sex Discrimination Act 1975, the Human Rights Act 1998, the Civil Partnership Act 2004, the Equality Act 2010 and the Marriage (Same Sex Couples) Act 2013. Under parliamentary sovereignty courts cannot create rights that override statute, so the statement is strongly supported for the UK.\n• UK — COURTS STILL MATTER: courts use section 3 HRA to read legislation compatibly (Ghaidan v Godin-Mendoza, 2004) and issue declarations of incompatibility that prompt change (Bellinger v Bellinger led to the Gender Recognition Act 2004). The Supreme Court's ruling in For Women Scotland (2025) that "sex" in the Equality Act 2010 means biological sex shows courts shaping how legislated rights apply.\n• US — COURTS LEAD ON PRINCIPLE: the Supreme Court has delivered change the elected branches would not — Brown (1954), Loving (1967), Obergefell (2015), Bostock (2020) — because the entrenched Bill of Rights and Fourteenth Amendment allow it to strike down laws. This is the key contrast with the UK.\n• US — LEGISLATION DELIVERS ENFORCEMENT: the Civil Rights Act 1964 and Voting Rights Act 1965 did what Brown could not — they reached private discrimination and gave the federal government enforcement powers. The Respect for Marriage Act (2022) gave statutory backing to same-sex marriage.\n• COURTS HAVE ALSO RESTRICTED RIGHTS: in the US, Shelby County (2013), Dobbs (2022), SFFA (2023) and United States v Skrmetti (2025) narrowed or removed protections. UK courts have less power to roll rights back, but legislatures can: the Illegal Migration Act 2023 and Safety of Rwanda Act 2024 were passed despite rights concerns.\n• WHY THEY DIFFER: in the US, entrenchment and judicial review make courts central, and polarised legislatures struggle to legislate; in the UK, sovereignty and a disciplined Commons majority make Parliament the main route.\n\nJudgement: the statement is broadly true of the UK and only partly true of the US, where courts established principles but legislation made them effective. Strong answers argue that courts and legislatures are interdependent in both countries, and explain the difference in balance structurally.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. This is a Section C comparative essay: answers must compare the US and the UK explicitly and in a sustained way, point by point, and explain WHY the two differ or resemble each other (for example through structural, rational or cultural approaches). An answer that treats the two countries in separate halves with little direct comparison should not go above Level 3; an answer that deals with only one country cannot go above Level 2. Synoptic links may be made to the Human Rights Act, the UK Supreme Court and parliamentary sovereignty (Paper 1), Parliament and legislation, the US Supreme Court, Congress, and pressure groups. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant points should be credited. It does not matter what conclusion is reached, provided it is supported by the argument and examples.` },

      { q: 'EXTRACTS (written for practice):\n\nExtract 1 — written for this practice question in the style of a statement by a voting-rights campaign group\n"For almost fifty years, states with a history of discrimination had to get federal approval before changing their voting rules. In Shelby County v Holder (2013) the Supreme Court struck down the formula that decided which states were covered, and pre-clearance stopped. Within hours, some states moved to enforce stricter voter ID laws. Since then states have cut polling places, purged voter rolls and tightened rules on postal and early voting; Georgia\'s 2021 election law even made it a crime for campaigners to hand out food or water to people waiting in line. Congress has failed to restore the protections: voting-rights bills were blocked in the Senate in 2022. The burden now falls on voters, who must sue after the damage is done."\n\nExtract 2 — written for this practice question in the style of a column by a conservative commentator\n"Claims that American democracy is being strangled by voting restrictions do not match the evidence. Turnout in 2020 was the highest for more than a century, and the 2024 turnout was also among the highest in decades. After Georgia\'s supposedly repressive 2021 law, the state saw record early voting in 2022. Most Americans, of every background, support showing identification to vote, as voters must in many other democracies. The courts still protect minority voters where it matters: in Allen v Milligan (2023) the Supreme Court upheld a ruling that Alabama\'s congressional map diluted Black voting power. Meanwhile many states have made voting easier, with more mail voting and automatic registration. Voting rights are being argued about, not abolished."\n\nAnalyse, evaluate and compare the arguments in the above extracts regarding whether voting rights are under threat in the US.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n\n• EXTRACT 1 — LEGAL WEAKENING: accurately describes Shelby County v Holder (2013), which struck down the section 4(b) coverage formula and so disabled section 5 pre-clearance. The shift from prior approval to after-the-fact litigation is its strongest argument, since restrictions can operate through at least one election before a court rules.\n• EXTRACT 1 — NEW RESTRICTIONS AND CONGRESSIONAL FAILURE: its examples (voter ID enforced immediately after Shelby, polling-place closures, roll purges, Georgia's SB 202 ban on handing out food or water) are accurate. The Senate filibuster blocked the Freedom to Vote Act and John Lewis Voting Rights Advancement Act in 2022. Students can link this to the separation of powers and supermajority rules.\n• EXTRACT 2 — TURNOUT EVIDENCE: 2020 turnout was the highest since 1900 and 2024 was also high; Georgia did see record early voting in 2022. But turnout data cannot show who was deterred, and turnout can rise for other reasons (polarisation, pandemic-era postal voting), so this does not refute Extract 1.\n• EXTRACT 2 — COURTS AND VOTER ID: Allen v Milligan (2023) is accurate, but it concerned section 2, which requires after-the-fact litigation — exactly the burden Extract 1 describes. Broad public support for voter ID is real, but popularity is not the same as rights protection; students may compare UK voter ID introduced by the Elections Act 2022.\n• EXTRACT 2 — EXPANSION IN SOME STATES: accurate that some states expanded access. This supports a federalism point that neither extract fully develops — voting rights now depend increasingly on which state a citizen lives in.\n• OTHER THREATS NEITHER EXTRACT STRESSES: partisan gerrymandering (Rucho v Common Cause, 2019, made it non-justiciable in federal courts), felony disenfranchisement, and pressure on election administration after 2020.\n• COMPARISON AND STATUS: Extract 1 focuses on LEGAL PROTECTION, Extract 2 on PARTICIPATION OUTCOMES — they partly talk past each other. Both are advocacy pieces (a campaign group and a partisan commentator) and select evidence accordingly.\n\nJudgement: the most convincing answers conclude that Extract 1 is stronger on the legal weakening of voting rights, which is clear, while Extract 2 is stronger on the empirical effect on participation, which is contested — and that the key question is whether rights should depend on litigation after the event.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and comparing the arguments in the extracts and weighing which are more convincing; evaluate their status (author, type of publication, purpose, date) where relevant. Own knowledge should be used to test the extracts' claims, not as a free-standing essay. Answers that do not focus on the arguments in the extracts cannot go above Level 2.` },

      { q: '\'Affirmative action has been far more controversial in the US than positive action has been in the UK.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content\n\n• SCALE AND FORM: US affirmative action went much further — race-conscious admissions at universities and federal-contractor obligations under Executive Order 11246 (1965). UK law allows only positive action: under the Equality Act 2010 employers may encourage under-represented groups and, where candidates are equally qualified, use a protected characteristic as a tie-breaker, but positive discrimination is generally unlawful. The US policy was more ambitious, so it was more contested.\n• COURTS: in the US, affirmative action has been fought through the Supreme Court for decades — Bakke (1978) outlawed quotas, Grutter (2003) allowed race as one factor, and SFFA v Harvard/UNC (2023) ended race-conscious admissions. The UK has had few comparable legal battles, though an industrial tribunal ruled Labour's all-women shortlists unlawful in 1996 (Jepson), and an RAF inquiry in 2023 found some recruitment practices amounted to unlawful positive discrimination.\n• BALLOT BOX AND FEDERALISM: several US states banned affirmative action by referendum (California's Proposition 209, 1996; Michigan's 2006 measure, upheld in Schuette, 2014). The UK has had no public votes on the issue, and its uniform equality law applies across Great Britain.\n• PARTY POLITICS: in the US, affirmative action and DEI are central to polarised party conflict — in January 2025 Trump revoked EO 11246 and ordered federal DEI programmes ended, and his administration pressured universities including Harvard. UK parties have largely accepted positive action; Parliament legislated to permit all-women shortlists (2002) and extended the permission to 2030. Criticism of DEI has grown, notably from Reform UK and some Conservatives, but less intensely.\n• WHY THEY DIFFER: US race relations were shaped by slavery and segregation, and the equal protection clause gives opponents a constitutional argument (equal treatment of individuals). UK measures were legislated by Parliament, focused on gender as much as race, and have no entrenched constitution for opponents to invoke.\n• BUT NOT UNCONTESTED IN THE UK: all-women shortlists, positive action in the police and armed forces, and university access targets have all been criticised, and the "equality of treatment vs equality of outcome" debate is the same in both countries.\n\nJudgement: the statement is largely correct — the US policy was more far-reaching, was fought over in the courts and at the ballot box, and became a partisan dividing line. Strong answers explain the difference structurally (entrenched equal protection and judicial review vs parliamentary sovereignty) and culturally (the legacy of segregation and a litigious rights culture).\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. This is a Section C comparative essay: answers must compare the US and the UK explicitly and in a sustained way, point by point, and explain WHY the two differ or resemble each other (for example through structural, rational or cultural approaches). An answer that treats the two countries in separate halves with little direct comparison should not go above Level 3; an answer that deals with only one country cannot go above Level 2. Synoptic links may be made to the Equality Act 2010, Parliament and parliamentary sovereignty (Paper 1), rights in the UK, the US Supreme Court and the Fourteenth Amendment, the presidency and executive orders, federalism and direct democracy. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant points should be credited. It does not matter what conclusion is reached, provided it is supported by the argument and examples.` },
    ]
  },

  '2.9': {
    name: 'Comparative — Constitutional Arrangements',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways that structural theory could be used to study the nature of the constitutions in the US and UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in their explanations and analysis, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• STRUCTURAL THEORY: the structural approach focuses on institutions and the rules and processes within them — constitutions, the relationships between branches of government, the distribution of power between levels of government. It treats these structures as the framework that shapes and constrains what individuals and groups can do, so that similar actors behave differently because they operate within different institutional arrangements.\n• CODIFICATION AND HIGHER LAW: the US constitution is a single codified document (1787) that stands above ordinary law; the UK constitution is uncodified, drawn from statute, common law, conventions and works of authority. Structurally, a codified higher law creates a standard against which other laws can be judged, so the US Supreme Court can strike down Acts of Congress (Marbury v Madison, 1803), whereas UK courts can only interpret statute or issue a declaration of incompatibility under the Human Rights Act 1998.\n• ENTRENCHMENT AND SOVEREIGNTY: Article V requires a two-thirds vote in both houses of Congress and ratification by three-quarters of the states, so there have been only 27 amendments since 1789. The UK constitution rests on parliamentary sovereignty, so constitutional law can be changed by an ordinary Act — the Fixed-term Parliaments Act 2011 was repealed by the Dissolution and Calling of Parliament Act 2022. The structure of the rules, not the preferences of politicians, explains the difference in flexibility.\n• SEPARATION VERSUS FUSION OF POWERS: the US constitution deliberately separates the executive, legislature and judiciary, with checks and balances such as the presidential veto and Senate confirmation of appointments. The UK has a fusion of powers: the government is drawn from and sits in Parliament and normally commands a Commons majority. Structural theory explains why a UK government with a majority can pass its programme while a US President faces a separately elected Congress.\n• TERRITORIAL STRUCTURE: US federalism is entrenched — the Tenth Amendment reserves powers to the states, which cannot be removed by ordinary federal legislation, and the states are represented as equals in the Senate. UK devolution was created by ordinary statute (Scotland Act 1998, Government of Wales Act 1998, Northern Ireland Act 1998) and Westminster remains sovereign. Structural theory highlights that UK devolved powers are delegated rather than constitutionally guaranteed, even though the Scotland Act 2016 declared the Scottish Parliament permanent.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three ways that structural theory could be used to study the nature of the constitutions in the US and UK. Relevant points not listed above should be credited. Each point should explain the theory's relevance and analyse it with examples from BOTH the US and the UK; a description of one country only, or of the institutions with no link to the theory, cannot reach the top of the range. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways that cultural theory could be used to study the principles underpinning the constitutions in the US and UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in their explanations and analysis, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• CULTURAL THEORY: the cultural approach focuses on the shared ideas, values, beliefs and historical experiences of a society or of groups within it, and argues that these shape how individuals act and what they regard as legitimate. It links present-day behaviour to each country's history — the American revolutionary, republican tradition and distrust of concentrated power, against the UK's gradual, evolutionary development, monarchy and parliamentary tradition.\n• A SHARED LIBERAL TRADITION: both constitutions rest on liberal ideas of limited government, the rule of law and individual liberty, reflecting a shared political inheritance — the English common-law tradition, the Bill of Rights 1689 and Lockean ideas of consent all influenced the American founders. Cultural theory explains why the two systems, despite different structures, share an independent judiciary, free elections and accountable government.\n• POPULAR VERSUS PARLIAMENTARY SOVEREIGNTY: the US constitution begins "We the People", reflecting a revolutionary belief that authority comes from the people and that government must be limited by a written, fundamental law. The UK's evolutionary history, in which Parliament gradually took power from the Crown, produced a culture in which the elected Parliament is the supreme authority. Cultural theory explains why codification and judicial strike-down powers seem natural in the US and are widely seen as undemocratic in the UK.\n• DISTRUST OF CONCENTRATED POWER: the American experience of rule by a distant monarch and Parliament produced a deep suspicion of government, reflected in the separation of powers, federalism and the Bill of Rights. The British tradition places greater trust in strong, accountable single-party government, restrained by convention and political culture rather than by legal rules. Constitutional conventions such as collective ministerial responsibility or the Salisbury Convention only work in a culture where politicians accept unwritten rules as binding.\n• REVERENCE FOR THE CONSTITUTION: in the US the constitution is a national symbol, taught in schools and invoked by politicians of all parties, and arguments about policy are frequently framed as arguments about constitutional meaning (for example originalism versus a "living constitution"). In the UK there is no comparable document to revere; debate is about what Parliament should do rather than what the constitution permits. Cultural theory helps explain why constitutional argument is more central to US political life.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three ways that cultural theory could be used to study the principles underpinning the constitutions in the US and UK. Relevant points not listed above should be credited. Each point should explain the theory's relevance and analyse it with examples from BOTH the US and the UK; a description of one country only, or of the institutions with no link to the theory, cannot reach the top of the range. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways that structural theory could be used to study the process of constitutional change in the US and UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in their explanations and analysis, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• STRUCTURAL THEORY: the structural approach focuses on institutions and the rules and processes within them — constitutions, the relationships between branches of government, the distribution of power between levels of government. It treats these structures as the framework that shapes and constrains what individuals and groups can do, so that similar actors behave differently because they operate within different institutional arrangements.\n• FORMAL AMENDMENT: the US amendment process under Article V creates multiple veto points — two-thirds of both houses and three-quarters of the states — so a minority of states can block change. The last amendment (the 27th) was ratified in 1992, and proposals such as the Equal Rights Amendment failed to be ratified within the deadline set by Congress. In the UK a government with a Commons majority can change the constitution by simple majority, with the Lords able only to delay under the Parliament Acts 1911 and 1949.\n• SCALE AND SPEED OF CHANGE: because the UK has so few veto points, major constitutional reforms were delivered by ordinary statute within single parliaments — devolution (1998), the Human Rights Act 1998, the House of Lords Act 1999 and the Constitutional Reform Act 2005 creating the Supreme Court. Structural theory explains why the UK constitution has changed more in recent decades than the US constitution has in over fifty years.\n• CHANGE THROUGH JUDICIAL INTERPRETATION: because formal amendment is so difficult, US constitutional change occurs mainly through Supreme Court interpretation — Brown v Board of Education (1954), Obergefell v Hodges (2015) and Dobbs v Jackson (2022) all changed the practical meaning of the constitution without any amendment. In the UK, judicial rulings on the constitution (Miller I, 2017; Miller II, 2019) can be overridden by statute, so Parliament remains the main source of change.\n• CHANGE THROUGH CONVENTION AND PRACTICE: the uncodified UK constitution also changes through the development of conventions, such as the practice since the 2003 Iraq vote of seeking Commons approval before military action. In the US, informal practices change too — the Senate abolished the filibuster for Supreme Court nominations in 2017 by a simple-majority "nuclear option" — but they cannot alter the text. Structural theory shows how the form of each constitution channels change into different routes.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three ways that structural theory could be used to study the process of constitutional change in the US and UK. Relevant points not listed above should be credited. Each point should explain the theory's relevance and analyse it with examples from BOTH the US and the UK; a description of one country only, or of the institutions with no link to the theory, cannot reach the top of the range. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways that cultural theory could be used to study the origins and development of the constitutions in the US and UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in their explanations and analysis, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• CULTURAL THEORY: the cultural approach focuses on the shared ideas, values, beliefs and historical experiences of a society or of groups within it, and argues that these shape how individuals act and what they regard as legitimate. It links present-day behaviour to each country's history — the American revolutionary, republican tradition and distrust of concentrated power, against the UK's gradual, evolutionary development, monarchy and parliamentary tradition.\n• REVOLUTIONARY FOUNDING VERSUS GRADUAL EVOLUTION: the US constitution was written at a single founding moment (Philadelphia, 1787) by a new nation that had rejected monarchy through revolution, so a written document was needed to establish legitimacy and set out the rules. The UK constitution evolved over centuries through negotiated settlements — Magna Carta (1215), the Bill of Rights 1689, the Reform Acts — with no break in continuity that required a new written settlement. Cultural theory explains codification as a product of historical experience.\n• PRAGMATISM VERSUS PRINCIPLE: British constitutional culture values pragmatic, incremental change, reflected in Burke's argument for reform that preserves inherited institutions and in the piecemeal nature of reforms since 1997. American constitutional culture appeals to founding principles, with judges and politicians debating the original meaning of the text and the intentions of the Founders. This explains why UK reform is rarely justified by reference to a grand principle, while US debates are framed in terms of the Founders' design.\n• MONARCHY VERSUS REPUBLICANISM: the UK retains a hereditary monarch as head of state, with many executive powers exercised by ministers under the royal prerogative. The US republican tradition rejected inherited authority, so the head of state is elected and all executive power is vested in the President by Article II. Cultural theory highlights how attitudes to hereditary authority, deference and tradition shaped very different constitutional arrangements for the executive.\n• RIGHTS CULTURE: US political culture is built on the idea of rights held against the state, entrenched in the Bill of Rights (1791), so citizens and groups naturally frame political demands as constitutional claims. The UK tradition was one of residual liberties — people may do anything not prohibited by law — protected by Parliament and the common law rather than a higher law, until the Human Rights Act 1998 incorporated the ECHR. Cultural theory explains why the UK was slower to adopt a codified rights framework and why the HRA remains contested.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three ways that cultural theory could be used to study the origins and development of the constitutions in the US and UK. Relevant points not listed above should be credited. Each point should explain the theory's relevance and analyse it with examples from BOTH the US and the UK; a description of one country only, or of the institutions with no link to the theory, cannot reach the top of the range. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways that rational theory could be used to study attempts to reform the constitutions in the US and UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in their explanations and analysis, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• RATIONAL THEORY: the rational (rational choice) approach focuses on the individual. It assumes political actors are self-interested and make logical, calculated choices to maximise the benefits and minimise the costs to themselves or their cause — for example re-election, promotion, policy success or party advantage. Differences between the two countries are explained by the different incentives that actors face.\n• GOVERNMENTS RESIST LIMITS ON THEIR OWN POWER: rational theory predicts that a party in office will not willingly give up the flexibility that an uncodified, unentrenched constitution provides. This helps explain why UK governments of all parties have not introduced a codified constitution, and why parties often promise more constitutional reform in opposition than they deliver in government. In the US, a President has no power to amend the constitution alone, so the rational strategy is to expand power through executive action instead.\n• SELF-INTEREST BLOCKS US AMENDMENTS: reform of the Electoral College or Senate apportionment would require smaller states to vote to reduce their own influence, which is irrational from their point of view. Rational theory therefore explains why provisions that many Americans criticise survive, even after elections such as 2000 and 2016 in which the popular vote winner lost the presidency.\n• SELECTIVE REFORM IN THE UK: reforms since 1997 were those that suited the governing party — for example Labour delivered devolution and removed most hereditary peers, but left first-past-the-post for Westminster, which benefits the two main parties. The 2011 AV referendum was held because it was part of the price of the Conservative–Liberal Democrat coalition agreement, a rational bargain rather than a principled commitment. In the US, parties similarly support rule changes (such as limits on the filibuster) when in the majority and oppose them in the minority.\n• REFORMS THAT BENEFIT THE GOVERNMENT: the Dissolution and Calling of Parliament Act 2022 restored the Prime Minister's power to choose the date of a general election, returning a clear advantage to the governing party. In the US, the Senate abolished the filibuster for executive and lower-court nominations in 2013 (under a Democratic majority) and for Supreme Court nominations in 2017 (under a Republican majority), each time to secure the majority party's immediate goals.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three ways that rational theory could be used to study attempts to reform the constitutions in the US and UK. Relevant points not listed above should be credited. Each point should explain the theory's relevance and analyse it with examples from BOTH the US and the UK; a description of one country only, or of the institutions with no link to the theory, cannot reach the top of the range. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways that rational theory could be used to study the territorial distribution of power in the US and UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in their explanations and analysis, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• RATIONAL THEORY: the rational (rational choice) approach focuses on the individual. It assumes political actors are self-interested and make logical, calculated choices to maximise the benefits and minimise the costs to themselves or their cause — for example re-election, promotion, policy success or party advantage. Differences between the two countries are explained by the different incentives that actors face.\n• DEVOLUTION AS A RATIONAL POLITICAL STRATEGY: rational theory suggests that Labour introduced devolution in 1997–99 partly to meet demands in Scotland and Wales that threatened its electoral base, and to undercut support for nationalist parties and independence. In the US, the federal structure was itself a rational bargain at the 1787 Convention — the Connecticut Compromise gave small states equal representation in the Senate as the price of their agreement.\n• SUB-NATIONAL GOVERNMENTS PURSUE THEIR OWN ADVANTAGE: US states act to protect and extend their powers, and state attorneys general regularly sue the federal government when it is controlled by the other party — Republican states challenged Biden's student debt relief (Biden v Nebraska, 2023) and Democratic states have challenged many of Trump's policies. In the UK, the SNP has used disputes with Westminster to strengthen the case for independence, and the Scottish Government sought to hold a second referendum, which the Supreme Court ruled in 2022 was outside the Scottish Parliament's competence.\n• THE CENTRE KEEPS CONTROL WHERE IT CAN: because devolution rests on ordinary statute and the Sewel Convention is not legally enforceable (Miller I, 2017), UK governments can legislate on devolved matters without consent when it suits them — the European Union (Withdrawal) Act 2018 and the United Kingdom Internal Market Act 2020 were passed without the consent of the Scottish Parliament. The US federal government cannot remove state powers so easily, so it uses rational incentives instead, such as attaching conditions to federal grants.\n• VOTERS AND PARTIES RESPOND TO INCENTIVES: federalism and devolution give parties a second arena in which to win power, so parties that cannot win nationally invest in sub-national office — the SNP has governed in Scotland since 2007, and US parties that lose the White House concentrate on governorships and state legislatures, which also control redistricting. Rational theory explains why both countries have seen the territorial level become a strategic battleground.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three ways that rational theory could be used to study the territorial distribution of power in the US and UK. Relevant points not listed above should be credited. Each point should explain the theory's relevance and analyse it with examples from BOTH the US and the UK; a description of one country only, or of the institutions with no link to the theory, cannot reach the top of the range. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: '"The US constitution is superior to the UK constitution." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `US SUPERIOR:\n• Entrenchment protects rights and institutions from temporary majorities; UK rights rest on repealable statute.\n• Clarity — citizens can read the rules; disputes turn on a text rather than contested convention, as the 2019 prorogation dispute showed.\n• Genuine limits on the executive — the "elective dictatorship" problem does not arise where the legislature is separately elected.\n• The federal bargain is constitutionally protected; devolved nations were overridden by the UK Internal Market Act 2020.\n• Judicial review provides a real remedy against unconstitutional legislation.\n\nUK SUPERIOR:\n• Flexibility — adapts rapidly without constitutional crisis; devolution, the HRA and the Supreme Court delivered within single parliamentary terms, whereas the US has 27 amendments in 240 years.\n• Democratic authorship — elected politicians rather than appointed judges make constitutional choices.\n• Avoids gridlock — a government with a majority can actually govern.\n• No obsolete provisions survive against majority wishes, unlike the Electoral College.\n• Avoids the politicisation of judicial appointments that Dobbs and the Garland/Barrett episode illustrate.\n\nGuidance for L5: must refuse the framing — flexibility and insecurity are the SAME property; entrenchment and rigidity are the SAME property. "Which is superior" therefore reduces to which risk one would rather run: a government that can do too much, or one that can do too little. That reframing is what reaches L5.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },

      { q: '"Structural factors best explain the differences between the UK and US constitutions." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `STRUCTURAL BEST:\n• Entrenchment alone explains most observable differences — judicial strike-down power, the finality of rulings, and the rate of constitutional change.\n• Veto point counting explains why the US changes so rarely and the UK so readily.\n• Fusion vs separation explains executive–legislative relations, gridlock and party discipline.\n• The causal chain is direct, observable and testable, unlike cultural claims.\n• Where the UK uses proportional systems at devolved level, multi-party systems emerge from the same electorate — strong evidence that institutions, not culture, drive outcomes.\n\nOTHER APPROACHES NEEDED:\n• Structure treats institutions as GIVEN and cannot explain why each country adopted the institutions it has — the cultural approach fills exactly that gap, tracing US entrenchment to a revolutionary founding and UK flexibility to evolutionary development.\n• The rational approach explains variation over TIME and between individuals, which structure (being static) handles poorly — why UK governments resist codification, why US amendments fail, why appointments are politicised.\n• Cultural factors explain why American rights politics is more absolute and less amenable to compromise.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest conclusion is that the three are COMPLEMENTARY rather than competing — culture explains why the institutions exist, structure explains the constraints they impose, rational choice explains how actors behave within them. An answer defending one approach exclusively caps at L4.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },

      { q: '"The UK is converging on the US constitutional model." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `CONVERGING:\n• Devolution disperses power territorially, resembling federalism in practice; the Scotland Act 2016 declared the Scottish Parliament permanent.\n• The CRA 2005 created a separate Supreme Court, removed the Law Lords from Parliament and established independent judicial appointments — sharpening the separation of powers.\n• The HRA created a rights framework performing some functions of a bill of rights, and made courts significant rights actors.\n• Greater use of referendums introduced popular authority alongside parliamentary sovereignty.\n• UK pressure groups increasingly litigate, as US groups do.\n\nNOT CONVERGING:\n• Parliamentary sovereignty survives ALL of these — courts still cannot strike down statute.\n• Rights are not entrenched; the HRA is repealable by simple majority and repeatedly threatened.\n• Devolved powers can legally be reclaimed — Miller I held Sewel unenforceable and the UK Internal Market Act 2020 overrode withheld consent.\n• The UK retains fusion of powers; the executive is drawn from and sits in the legislature.\n• Parliament reversed judicial expansion in 2022, demonstrating the hierarchy is unchanged.\n• The UK has moved AWAY from the US model in retaining FPTP and an appointed second chamber.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest conclusion is that the changes are significant but NOT transformative, because the UK has adopted US-style institutions without the feature that defines the US model — entrenchment. Naming that single variable reaches L5.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },

      { q: '"Entrenchment is the most significant difference between the UK and US constitutions." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `ENTRENCHMENT MOST SIGNIFICANT:\n• It determines judicial power — a higher law gives US courts something to measure statute against and makes their rulings effectively final; without it UK courts can only interpret or declare incompatibility.\n• It determines the rate and mechanism of constitutional change — the US changes through judicial interpretation because amendment is unattainable; the UK changes through ordinary legislation.\n• It determines the security of rights — US rights are beyond legislative repeal but vulnerable to reinterpretation; UK rights are the reverse.\n• It determines the politics of appointments — the return on capturing a US appointment is decades of policy influence, which is why norms have collapsed there and not in the UK.\n• Most other differences can be derived from it.\n\nOTHER DIFFERENCES AS SIGNIFICANT:\n• Fusion vs separation of powers explains executive dominance in the UK and gridlock in the USA — a difference entrenchment does not explain.\n• Federalism vs devolution determines whether regional powers can be overridden.\n• Codification itself affects clarity and public understanding independently of entrenchment.\n• The electoral systems and party discipline differences flow from separation, not entrenchment.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest answers distinguish differences entrenchment EXPLAINS (judicial power, rights security, appointment politics) from those it does NOT (executive–legislative relations, party discipline), and conclude accordingly.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },
    ]
  },

  '2.10': {
    name: 'Comparative — The Executives',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways that structural theory could be used to study the powers of the chief executives in the US and UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in their explanations and analysis, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• STRUCTURAL THEORY: the structural approach focuses on institutions and the rules and processes within them — constitutions, the relationships between branches of government, the distribution of power between levels of government. It treats these structures as the framework that shapes and constrains what individuals and groups can do, so that similar actors behave differently because they operate within different institutional arrangements.\n• SEPARATION VERSUS FUSION OF POWERS: the President is separately elected and does not sit in Congress, so has no guaranteed legislative majority and must persuade a separate branch; the Prime Minister is the leader of the party with a Commons majority and sits in Parliament, so normally controls the legislature. Structural theory explains why a PM with a large majority, such as Blair in 1997 or Starmer in 2024, can usually pass the government's programme, while Presidents regularly face divided government.\n• SOURCES OF POWER: the President's powers are set out in Article II of a codified constitution — Commander-in-Chief, the veto, nominations and treaty-making subject to Senate approval. The PM's powers are largely derived from convention and the royal prerogative, exercised on behalf of the monarch, and are therefore flexible and dependent on political circumstances. The structure of each constitution explains why presidential power is more clearly defined but also more clearly limited.\n• TENURE AND REMOVAL: the President serves a fixed four-year term, is limited to two terms by the 22nd Amendment and can be removed only by impeachment and conviction, which has never happened. The PM has no fixed term and can be removed by their own party at any time — Thatcher (1990), May (2019), Johnson (2022) and Truss (2022) all left office without losing a general election. Structural theory shows how the rules of tenure make a President secure in office but a PM dependent on continued party support.\n• HEAD OF STATE AND HEAD OF GOVERNMENT: the President combines both roles, so has ceremonial authority as well as political power. In the UK the monarch is head of state, so the PM is only head of government and exercises many powers formally in the Crown's name. This structural difference shapes the status and public role of each leader.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three ways that structural theory could be used to study the powers of the chief executives in the US and UK. Relevant points not listed above should be credited. Each point should explain the theory's relevance and analyse it with examples from BOTH the US and the UK; a description of one country only, or of the institutions with no link to the theory, cannot reach the top of the range. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways that cultural theory could be used to study the leadership role of the chief executives in the US and UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in their explanations and analysis, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• CULTURAL THEORY: the cultural approach focuses on the shared ideas, values, beliefs and historical experiences of a society or of groups within it, and argues that these shape how individuals act and what they regard as legitimate. It links present-day behaviour to each country's history — the American revolutionary, republican tradition and distrust of concentrated power, against the UK's gradual, evolutionary development, monarchy and parliamentary tradition.\n• THE PRESIDENT AS A NATIONAL SYMBOL: American political culture treats the President as the embodiment of the nation, combining the roles of head of state and head of government, and the office is surrounded by ritual such as the inauguration and the State of the Union address. In the UK the monarch fulfils the symbolic role, so the PM is seen primarily as a party politician. Cultural theory explains why public expectations of the President are greater and more personal.\n• PERSONALISATION OF LEADERSHIP IN BOTH COUNTRIES: in both countries, media coverage focuses heavily on the individual leader, and elections are increasingly fought around leaders' personalities. Commentators such as Michael Foley have argued that the UK has developed a "British presidency", with leaders such as Thatcher, Blair and Johnson adopting a personal, public-facing style. Cultural theory suggests a shared culture of celebrity and mass media explains this convergence, even though the constitutional structures remain very different.\n• DISTRUST OF EXECUTIVE POWER VERSUS STRONG GOVERNMENT: the US founders' fear of a monarch-like executive produced an office hemmed in by checks and balances, and suspicion of an "imperial presidency" remains a recurring theme of American debate. The Westminster tradition values strong, decisive single-party government, accountable to Parliament and voters. This helps explain why UK voters tend to accept a powerful PM with a large majority, while US voters frequently choose divided government.\n• THE "RALLY ROUND THE FLAG" EFFECT: in both countries, leaders gain support during national crises and conflicts, reflecting shared patriotic values — for example the surge in support for President George W Bush after 9/11 and for Thatcher after the Falklands War in 1982. Cultural theory explains how national identity and patriotism strengthen executive leadership in times of crisis in both systems.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three ways that cultural theory could be used to study the leadership role of the chief executives in the US and UK. Relevant points not listed above should be credited. Each point should explain the theory's relevance and analyse it with examples from BOTH the US and the UK; a description of one country only, or of the institutions with no link to the theory, cannot reach the top of the range. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways that structural theory could be used to study the relationship between the executive and the legislature in the US and UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in their explanations and analysis, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• STRUCTURAL THEORY: the structural approach focuses on institutions and the rules and processes within them — constitutions, the relationships between branches of government, the distribution of power between levels of government. It treats these structures as the framework that shapes and constrains what individuals and groups can do, so that similar actors behave differently because they operate within different institutional arrangements.\n• CONTROL OF THE LEGISLATIVE AGENDA: in the UK the government controls most of the Commons timetable and almost all significant legislation is government legislation, so Parliament is a "legitimating" rather than an initiating legislature. In the US the President cannot introduce bills; legislation must be introduced by members of Congress, and the President relies on persuasion, the State of the Union address and the veto. Structural theory explains why the President is said to have only the "power to persuade" (Neustadt).\n• MULTIPLE VETO POINTS IN THE US: a presidential proposal must pass the House, pass the Senate — where the filibuster means 60 votes are normally needed — survive a possible legal challenge and be signed. In the UK the House of Lords can delay most bills for only about a year under the Parliament Acts, and the Salisbury Convention means it does not block manifesto commitments. Structural theory explains why major US legislation, such as the Affordable Care Act (2010), requires unified government and still often fails.\n• DIFFERENT TOOLS FOR ACTING ALONE: when Congress will not act, Presidents rely on executive orders — Trump issued a large number early in his second term in 2025 — but these can be overturned by the courts or reversed by a successor. UK governments usually do not need such tools because they can legislate, though the royal prerogative allows action without Parliament in areas such as foreign affairs. The structure of each system therefore shapes the methods each executive uses.\n• ACCOUNTABILITY TO THE LEGISLATURE: fusion of powers means UK ministers answer questions in Parliament, including weekly Prime Minister's Questions, and a government can be removed by a vote of no confidence (as in 1979). The President never answers questions in Congress; scrutiny comes through committee hearings, investigations and ultimately impeachment. Structural theory shows how the institutional relationship determines how each executive is held to account.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three ways that structural theory could be used to study the relationship between the executive and the legislature in the US and UK. Relevant points not listed above should be credited. Each point should explain the theory's relevance and analyse it with examples from BOTH the US and the UK; a description of one country only, or of the institutions with no link to the theory, cannot reach the top of the range. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways that rational theory could be used to study the relationship between chief executives and legislators of their own party in the US and UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in their explanations and analysis, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• RATIONAL THEORY: the rational (rational choice) approach focuses on the individual. It assumes political actors are self-interested and make logical, calculated choices to maximise the benefits and minimise the costs to themselves or their cause — for example re-election, promotion, policy success or party advantage. Differences between the two countries are explained by the different incentives that actors face.\n• WHY US LEGISLATORS MAY DEFY A PRESIDENT OF THEIR OWN PARTY: members of Congress are chosen in primaries and raise much of their own campaign money, so they cannot be removed by the President or national party. The rational calculation is to follow the preferences of their district or state, so members of the President's party sometimes vote against his priorities — for example Senator Manchin's opposition to Biden's Build Back Better bill in 2021. However, the threat of a primary challenge can also push members towards loyalty, as Liz Cheney's primary defeat in 2022 after voting to impeach Trump showed.\n• WHY UK MPs USUALLY SUPPORT THE PRIME MINISTER: UK MPs depend on the party for selection, for the party label that delivers most of their votes, and for promotion to ministerial office. Rebellion risks losing the whip — Johnson removed the whip from 21 Conservative MPs in 2019 and Starmer suspended seven Labour MPs in July 2024 after they voted against the government over the two-child benefit cap. Rational theory explains why party discipline is far stronger in the UK.\n• THE PRIME MINISTER'S DEPENDENCE ON PARTY SUPPORT: because a PM can be removed by their own party, MPs rationally withdraw support when a leader becomes an electoral liability — the mass ministerial resignations in July 2022 forced Johnson to announce his resignation. A President cannot be removed by their party, so members of Congress have less incentive to act against an unpopular President and more incentive simply to distance themselves from him.\n• BARGAINING AND PATRONAGE: a PM can use the power of appointment to reward loyalty and bind rivals into collective responsibility, since more than a hundred MPs hold government posts on the "payroll vote". A President has no comparable patronage over members of Congress, so must bargain for votes through policy concessions, support for projects in a member's district, or campaign support. Rational theory explains why the President's relationship with Congress is one of negotiation rather than command.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three ways that rational theory could be used to study the relationship between chief executives and legislators of their own party in the US and UK. Relevant points not listed above should be credited. Each point should explain the theory's relevance and analyse it with examples from BOTH the US and the UK; a description of one country only, or of the institutions with no link to the theory, cannot reach the top of the range. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways that cultural theory could be used to study the conduct of foreign and security policy in the US and UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in their explanations and analysis, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• CULTURAL THEORY: the cultural approach focuses on the shared ideas, values, beliefs and historical experiences of a society or of groups within it, and argues that these shape how individuals act and what they regard as legitimate. It links present-day behaviour to each country's history — the American revolutionary, republican tradition and distrust of concentrated power, against the UK's gradual, evolutionary development, monarchy and parliamentary tradition.\n• AMERICAN EXCEPTIONALISM AND GLOBAL LEADERSHIP: US political culture includes a belief that the United States has a special role in promoting freedom and democracy, which has supported a powerful presidency in foreign affairs since 1945. Britain's post-imperial culture emphasises its role as a bridge between the US and Europe and its "special relationship" with the US. Cultural theory helps explain why both executives have been willing to commit to military action abroad — for example jointly in Iraq in 2003.\n• DEVELOPING NORMS OF LEGISLATIVE CONSENT: in the UK, deploying the armed forces is a prerogative power, but since the Commons vote on Iraq in 2003 a convention has developed that the Commons should normally be consulted, and in 2013 the Commons voted against military action in Syria. However, the convention is not absolute — May authorised strikes on Syria in 2018 without a prior vote. In the US, Congress has not formally declared war since 1942 and Presidents have routinely acted without authorisation despite the War Powers Resolution 1973. Cultural theory explains how expectations of accountability, rather than law, shape each executive's behaviour.\n• ATTITUDES TO INTERNATIONAL COMMITMENTS: a strand of American political culture is suspicious of binding international commitments, reflected in the Senate's refusal to ratify the Treaty of Versailles (1919–20) and in Trump's "America First" approach, including withdrawal from the Paris climate agreement. The UK has a stronger tradition of working through multilateral institutions such as NATO and the UN Security Council. Cultural theory helps explain differences in the style of foreign policy as well as its substance.\n• PATRIOTISM AND THE RALLY EFFECT: in both countries, shared patriotic values mean leaders often gain public support during international crises, and opposition politicians are wary of appearing unpatriotic. This reinforces executive dominance of foreign policy in both systems, even though the constitutional frameworks differ.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three ways that cultural theory could be used to study the conduct of foreign and security policy in the US and UK. Relevant points not listed above should be credited. Each point should explain the theory's relevance and analyse it with examples from BOTH the US and the UK; a description of one country only, or of the institutions with no link to the theory, cannot reach the top of the range. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways that rational theory could be used to study the role of cabinets in the US and UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in their explanations and analysis, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• RATIONAL THEORY: the rational (rational choice) approach focuses on the individual. It assumes political actors are self-interested and make logical, calculated choices to maximise the benefits and minimise the costs to themselves or their cause — for example re-election, promotion, policy success or party advantage. Differences between the two countries are explained by the different incentives that actors face.\n• WHY PRIME MINISTERS APPOINT RIVALS: a PM depends on the continued support of the parliamentary party, so it is rational to include senior figures and representatives of different factions in the cabinet, binding them by collective responsibility — May balanced Leave and Remain supporters in her cabinets after 2016. A President does not depend on cabinet support to stay in office, so can rationally appoint loyalists, technical experts or allies with little independent political base.\n• WHY UK MINISTERS SOMETIMES RESIGN: collective responsibility requires ministers to support government policy or resign. Ministers rationally weigh the costs and benefits — Robin Cook resigned in 2003 over Iraq, and a wave of resignations in July 2022 helped force Johnson out when ministers judged that staying would damage their own careers and their party. US cabinet secretaries do not have a political base in Congress and serve at the President's pleasure, so resignation is rarely a political weapon.\n• WHY PRESIDENTS RELY ON THE EXECUTIVE OFFICE RATHER THAN THE CABINET: cabinet secretaries head large departments and may come to represent their department's interests rather than the President's ("going native"). Presidents therefore rationally rely on White House staff and the Executive Office of the President, who owe their position entirely to the President. Trump dismissed several senior cabinet members during his first term, including Secretary of State Tillerson in 2018, showing that US cabinet members have little power to resist the President.\n• THE CABINET AS A CHECK ON THE CHIEF EXECUTIVE: because UK cabinet ministers are senior MPs with their own supporters, their collective withdrawal of support can end a premiership (Thatcher 1990, Johnson 2022). No US cabinet has ever used the 25th Amendment to remove a President. Rational theory explains why UK cabinet ministers have both the incentive and the means to act against the PM when he or she becomes an electoral liability, while US cabinet members do not.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three ways that rational theory could be used to study the role of cabinets in the US and UK. Relevant points not listed above should be credited. Each point should explain the theory's relevance and analyse it with examples from BOTH the US and the UK; a description of one country only, or of the institutions with no link to the theory, cannot reach the top of the range. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: '"The UK Prime Minister is more powerful than the US President." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `PM MORE POWERFUL:\n• Fusion of powers — a PM with a secure majority controls the executive AND the legislative outcome, faces no veto, a second chamber able only to delay one year, and no court able to strike down the resulting statute.\n• No entrenched constitution, no federal division of powers, no entrenched bill of rights.\n• Patronage over ministers, control of the cabinet agenda and of cabinet committees.\n• Prerogative powers over deployment, treaties, appointments and (since 2022) election timing.\n• A President cannot legislate at all and routinely fails to pass central priorities.\n\nPRESIDENT MORE POWERFUL:\n• Security of tenure — a fixed four-year term, removable only by impeachment and conviction, never achieved. A PM can be removed by their own party within days; three of the last five — May (2019), Johnson (2022) and Truss (2022) — were forced out by their own party.\n• A separate personal national mandate independent of the legislature.\n• The veto, executive orders, executive agreements, the pardon power, command of the world's largest military.\n• Judicial appointments extending influence for decades.\n• Head of state as well as head of government.\n\nGuidance for L5: each is more powerful in DIFFERENT DIMENSIONS, and the dimensions run in opposite directions — the PM over legislation when commanding a majority, the President in tenure security and unilateral action. The strongest answers also note the PM's power is entirely CONDITIONAL, so a small-majority PM may be weaker than any president.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },

      { q: '"Both the UK Prime Minister and the US President have become too powerful." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `BOTH TOO POWERFUL:\n• Both have expanded unilateral action — executive orders and the royal prerogative; both have acted militarily without prior legislative authorisation.\n• Both have grown central staff capacity — EXOP and the PM's Office — insulating decision-making within circles of loyalists.\n• Both dominate media attention, personalising politics around a single leader.\n• The PM controls a sovereign Parliament through the whips (Hailsham's "elective dictatorship"); the President governs by executive order where Congress is gridlocked.\n• Both face their weakest checks in foreign and security policy.\n\nNOT TOO POWERFUL:\n• The PM holds office only while their party permits — three of the last five (May, Johnson and Truss) were forced out by their own party without losing an election. No comparable constraint exists on a President, but it is a severe one.\n• Presidents routinely fail to pass central priorities, lose midterms, see orders reversed by successors and struck down by courts, and have been impeached.\n• Miller I and II constrained prerogative power; Youngstown and United States v Nixon constrained presidential claims.\n• A small-majority PM (May after 2017) may be weaker than almost any president.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest analysis notes the constraints differ IN KIND — the President is constrained by OTHER INSTITUTIONS, the PM by THEIR OWN PARTY — and that both appear imperial chiefly in the domain where legislative checks are weakest in each system.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },

      { q: '"The UK cabinet is more significant than the US cabinet." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `UK CABINET MORE SIGNIFICANT:\n• It is a COLLECTIVE body whose consent legitimises policy and whose withdrawal ends a premiership — Thatcher 1990, Johnson 2022. No US cabinet has ever removed a President.\n• Members sit in Parliament with their own political bases and followings; some have independent power (Blair–Brown).\n• Collective responsibility binds the whole government to decisions, giving ministers a legitimate claim to be consulted.\n• Cabinet committees do substantive policy work under ministerial direction.\n\nUS CABINET MORE SIGNIFICANT THAN ASSUMED / UK LESS:\n• US secretaries run vast departments with enormous budgets and Senate-confirmed independent standing.\n• UK cabinet significance has declined with "sofa government" — the Butler Review criticised informal decision-making before Iraq; meetings are shorter and less deliberative.\n• In both systems power has shifted to central staff — EXOP and the PM's Office/special advisers.\n• A UK PM with a large majority can dominate cabinet almost completely, so its constraint is conditional.\n\nGuidance for L5: the structural point is decisive — the UK cabinet is COLLECTIVE and can remove the chief executive; the US cabinet is a set of INDIVIDUAL advisers to a President in whom all executive power is vested. That difference is constitutional, not cultural, and explains everything else.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },

      { q: '"Structural factors best explain differences between the UK and US executives." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `STRUCTURAL BEST:\n• Fusion vs separation of powers explains legislative success — a PM commands a majority by definition, a President may face divided government.\n• Fixed vs conditional tenure explains why PMs are removed abruptly and failing presidencies limp on.\n• The absence of a UK veto is explained structurally — a PM who opposes a bill simply prevents its introduction, so no veto is needed.\n• Entrenchment explains why US executive action is more vulnerable to judicial invalidation.\n• The causal chains are direct and observable.\n\nOTHER APPROACHES NEEDED:\n• Rational choice explains VARIATION that structure cannot — why US legislators defy their own President (primaries, own funding, no deselection) and UK MPs rarely rebel (party controls nomination, label and promotion). Structure is static; these explain behaviour within it.\n• Rational choice also explains why PMs appoint rivals, and why presidents govern by executive order.\n• Cultural factors explain the personalisation of the presidency — combining head of state and head of government gives it a symbolic national role drawing on patriotic tradition that no PM commands.\n• Culture explains American individualism and British party-government traditions.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest conclusion is complementarity — structure sets the constraints, rational choice explains behaviour within them, culture explains why those institutions were adopted. Defending one approach exclusively caps at L4.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },
    ]
  },

  '2.11': {
    name: 'Comparative — The Judiciaries',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways that structural theory could be used to study the power of the Supreme Courts in the US and UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in their explanations and analysis, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• STRUCTURAL THEORY: the structural approach focuses on institutions and the rules and processes within them — constitutions, the relationships between branches of government, the distribution of power between levels of government. It treats these structures as the framework that shapes and constrains what individuals and groups can do, so that similar actors behave differently because they operate within different institutional arrangements.\n• ENTRENCHED CONSTITUTION VERSUS PARLIAMENTARY SOVEREIGNTY: the US Supreme Court interprets a codified, entrenched constitution and, since Marbury v Madison (1803), can declare Acts of Congress and state laws unconstitutional and void. The UK Supreme Court (created by the Constitutional Reform Act 2005, sitting from 2009) operates under parliamentary sovereignty and cannot strike down an Act of Parliament; under the Human Rights Act 1998 it can only interpret legislation compatibly with Convention rights (s.3) or issue a declaration of incompatibility (s.4) that leaves the law in force. Structural theory explains the difference in power as a consequence of the constitutional framework.\n• FINALITY OF RULINGS: because the US constitution is so hard to amend, a constitutional ruling can usually be reversed only by the Court itself, as Dobbs v Jackson (2022) did to Roe v Wade. In the UK, Parliament can reverse any ruling by ordinary legislation — the Judicial Review and Courts Act 2022 reversed the effect of the Cart judgment, and the Safety of Rwanda (Asylum and Immigration) Act 2024 was passed in response to the Supreme Court's 2023 ruling that the Rwanda policy was unlawful.\n• FEDERALISM AND DEVOLUTION: the US Court acts as umpire between the federal government and the states, giving it a major role in determining the balance of power, for example over abortion after Dobbs. The UK Supreme Court also rules on the limits of devolved powers — in 2022 it held that the Scottish Parliament could not legislate for an independence referendum — but because devolution rests on statute, Westminster can change the settlement.\n• SEPARATION OF POWERS: both courts are formally separate from the other branches. The creation of the UK Supreme Court removed the Law Lords from the House of Lords, strengthening the separation of powers along US lines. Structural theory can be used to show that the courts are now similar in independence but still very different in power.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three ways that structural theory could be used to study the power of the Supreme Courts in the US and UK. Relevant points not listed above should be credited. Each point should explain the theory's relevance and analyse it with examples from BOTH the US and the UK; a description of one country only, or of the institutions with no link to the theory, cannot reach the top of the range. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways that cultural theory could be used to study judicial independence in the US and UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in their explanations and analysis, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• CULTURAL THEORY: the cultural approach focuses on the shared ideas, values, beliefs and historical experiences of a society or of groups within it, and argues that these shape how individuals act and what they regard as legitimate. It links present-day behaviour to each country's history — the American revolutionary, republican tradition and distrust of concentrated power, against the UK's gradual, evolutionary development, monarchy and parliamentary tradition.\n• A SHARED COMMON-LAW AND RULE-OF-LAW TRADITION: both countries share a legal culture derived from the English common law, with binding precedent, an adversarial system and a strong belief that judges must be independent of government. Both protect independence through security of tenure and salaries that cannot be reduced for political reasons. Cultural theory explains why judicial independence is accepted as legitimate in both countries.\n• A MORE POLITICISED US LEGAL CULTURE: in the US, judges are widely identified with the President who appointed them and with judicial philosophies such as originalism, and many state judges are elected. Supreme Court confirmations have become partisan battles — Kavanaugh was confirmed 50–48 in 2018 — and public approval of the Court has become sharply divided along party lines, particularly after Dobbs (2022). Cultural theory explains why the idea of a neutral judiciary is more contested in the US.\n• A PROFESSIONAL, LOW-PROFILE UK JUDICIARY: the UK tradition emphasises a non-partisan, professional judiciary selected on merit by an independent commission, with judges who rarely comment publicly on politics. When the High Court ruled against the government in Miller I (2016), some newspapers attacked the judges as "Enemies of the People", but this was widely criticised as a breach of the convention that judicial independence should be respected. Cultural theory highlights the different expectations of judges in each country.\n• ATTITUDES TO JUDGES MAKING POLITICAL DECISIONS: American political culture, with its tradition of rights claims against the state, accepts that the Court will decide major moral and political questions. The British tradition of parliamentary sovereignty expects elected politicians to decide such questions, so UK judges tend to show deference to Parliament. This explains why UK judges are seen as more independent of party politics but also as less powerful.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three ways that cultural theory could be used to study judicial independence in the US and UK. Relevant points not listed above should be credited. Each point should explain the theory's relevance and analyse it with examples from BOTH the US and the UK; a description of one country only, or of the institutions with no link to the theory, cannot reach the top of the range. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways that structural theory could be used to study the appointment and tenure of senior judges in the US and UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in their explanations and analysis, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• STRUCTURAL THEORY: the structural approach focuses on institutions and the rules and processes within them — constitutions, the relationships between branches of government, the distribution of power between levels of government. It treats these structures as the framework that shapes and constrains what individuals and groups can do, so that similar actors behave differently because they operate within different institutional arrangements.\n• APPOINTMENT PROCESS: in the US, the Constitution gives the President the power to nominate Supreme Court justices and requires Senate confirmation, so appointments pass through two elected, partisan institutions. In the UK, justices are selected by an independent selection commission set up under the Constitutional Reform Act 2005, with the Lord Chancellor able only to accept, reject or ask for reconsideration of a name. Structural theory explains why US appointments are political contests while UK appointments are largely invisible.\n• TENURE: US federal judges hold office "during good behaviour" — effectively for life — so a single appointment can shape the Court for decades. UK Supreme Court justices must retire at 75 (raised from 70 in 2022). The structure of tenure explains why the timing of vacancies matters so much in the US, and why Presidents tend to nominate relatively young candidates.\n• THE ROLE OF THE SENATE: because the Senate must confirm nominees, the party that controls the Senate can block or accelerate appointments — the Republican majority refused to consider Merrick Garland in 2016 and confirmed Amy Coney Barrett shortly before the 2020 election. The 2017 abolition of the filibuster for Supreme Court nominations means a simple majority now suffices. The UK has no equivalent legislative stage, so Parliament plays no role in appointments.\n• SIZE AND COMPOSITION: the US Court has had nine justices since 1869, although the number is set by statute, and proposals to "pack the Court" have been debated. The UK Court has twelve justices and usually sits in panels. Structural theory shows how the design of the institution affects the stakes of each appointment: in a small court with life tenure, one vacancy can change the majority.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three ways that structural theory could be used to study the appointment and tenure of senior judges in the US and UK. Relevant points not listed above should be credited. Each point should explain the theory's relevance and analyse it with examples from BOTH the US and the UK; a description of one country only, or of the institutions with no link to the theory, cannot reach the top of the range. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways that rational theory could be used to study the appointment of Supreme Court justices in the US and UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in their explanations and analysis, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• RATIONAL THEORY: the rational (rational choice) approach focuses on the individual. It assumes political actors are self-interested and make logical, calculated choices to maximise the benefits and minimise the costs to themselves or their cause — for example re-election, promotion, policy success or party advantage. Differences between the two countries are explained by the different incentives that actors face.\n• THE VALUE OF THE PRIZE: because US justices can strike down legislation and serve for life, one appointment can decide policy for thirty years or more. It is therefore rational for Presidents, senators and interest groups to invest heavily in appointments. In the UK, justices cannot strike down statute and face a retirement age of 75, so the return on politicising appointments is much lower and parties rarely campaign on them.\n• STRATEGIC BEHAVIOUR BY SENATORS AND PRESIDENTS: rational theory predicts that the Senate majority will use its power to benefit its party — as in the refusal to hold hearings for Garland in 2016 and the rapid confirmation of Barrett in 2020. Presidents rationally nominate younger candidates to maximise their influence, and Trump used a list of candidates approved by conservative legal groups to win the support of conservative voters in 2016.\n• STRATEGIC RETIREMENT: US justices often time their retirement so that a President who shares their outlook can appoint a successor — Justice Kennedy retired in 2018 under Trump and Justice Breyer in 2022 under Biden, while Democratic Senate control still allowed confirmation. UK justices retire at a fixed age or for personal reasons, and there is no partisan advantage to be gained from timing.\n• INTEREST GROUPS AND VOTERS: interest groups on both sides spend heavily on campaigns for and against US nominees because appointments can deliver policy goals, such as the overturning of Roe v Wade. In the UK, the independent commission and the lack of a strike-down power give pressure groups little reason to lobby over appointments, so they focus instead on bringing cases.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three ways that rational theory could be used to study the appointment of Supreme Court justices in the US and UK. Relevant points not listed above should be credited. Each point should explain the theory's relevance and analyse it with examples from BOTH the US and the UK; a description of one country only, or of the institutions with no link to the theory, cannot reach the top of the range. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways that cultural theory could be used to study the political role of the Supreme Courts in the US and UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in their explanations and analysis, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• CULTURAL THEORY: the cultural approach focuses on the shared ideas, values, beliefs and historical experiences of a society or of groups within it, and argues that these shape how individuals act and what they regard as legitimate. It links present-day behaviour to each country's history — the American revolutionary, republican tradition and distrust of concentrated power, against the UK's gradual, evolutionary development, monarchy and parliamentary tradition.\n• RIGHTS-BASED POLITICAL CULTURE: in the US, political disputes over abortion, guns, affirmative action and same-sex marriage are routinely framed as constitutional rights questions for the Court — for example New York State Rifle & Pistol Association v Bruen (2022) and Students for Fair Admissions v Harvard (2023). In the UK, such issues have usually been decided by Parliament — same-sex marriage was introduced by the Marriage (Same Sex Couples) Act 2013. Cultural theory explains why the US Court is central to political conflict.\n• VENERATION OF THE CONSTITUTION: because Americans treat the Constitution as a sacred founding document, the institution that interprets it gains authority, and decisions such as Brown v Board of Education (1954) became landmarks of national history. In the UK, the Supreme Court is relatively new (2009) and has no such symbolic document to interpret, though the Human Rights Act 1998 has given UK judges a larger role in rights questions.\n• DEFERENCE TO ELECTED INSTITUTIONS IN THE UK: British political culture values parliamentary sovereignty and elected accountability, so UK judges tend to respect the will of Parliament, and even significant rulings such as Miller II (2019) are framed as upholding parliamentary sovereignty rather than challenging it. American culture of distrust of government makes it more acceptable for judges to limit the elected branches.\n• GROWING RIGHTS CONSCIOUSNESS IN THE UK: since the Human Rights Act 1998, UK citizens and groups have become more willing to use the courts, and judges have ruled on politically sensitive issues such as the meaning of "sex" in the Equality Act 2010 (For Women Scotland, 2025). Cultural theory can explain this as a gradual shift towards a more American-style rights culture, although it remains more limited than in the US.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three ways that cultural theory could be used to study the political role of the Supreme Courts in the US and UK. Relevant points not listed above should be credited. Each point should explain the theory's relevance and analyse it with examples from BOTH the US and the UK; a description of one country only, or of the institutions with no link to the theory, cannot reach the top of the range. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways that rational theory could be used to study how governments and legislators respond to court rulings in the US and UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in their explanations and analysis, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• RATIONAL THEORY: the rational (rational choice) approach focuses on the individual. It assumes political actors are self-interested and make logical, calculated choices to maximise the benefits and minimise the costs to themselves or their cause — for example re-election, promotion, policy success or party advantage. Differences between the two countries are explained by the different incentives that actors face.\n• THE POLITICAL COST OF IGNORING THE COURTS: a declaration of incompatibility does not invalidate a UK statute, but governments have usually amended the law in response, because being seen to violate rights and ignore the courts is politically costly and could lead to a case at the European Court of Human Rights. For example, after the Belmarsh case (A v Secretary of State for the Home Department, 2004) the government replaced indefinite detention with control orders. Rational theory explains why UK judicial influence exceeds the courts' formal powers.\n• OVERRIDING RULINGS WHEN IT PAYS: because Parliament is sovereign, a UK government with a majority can legislate to reverse a ruling when the political benefits outweigh the costs — the Safety of Rwanda (Asylum and Immigration) Act 2024 declared Rwanda safe after the Supreme Court had ruled the policy unlawful. In the US, Congress can reverse statutory interpretations — the Lilly Ledbetter Fair Pay Act 2009 reversed the Court's 2007 Ledbetter ruling — but it cannot reverse constitutional rulings without an amendment.\n• STRATEGIC USE OF RULINGS BY POLITICIANS: politicians in both countries can use court rulings to their advantage, blaming judges for unpopular outcomes or campaigning against the courts to mobilise supporters. After Dobbs (2022), Democrats campaigned on abortion rights, and in the UK some politicians have called for leaving the ECHR after adverse rulings on immigration. Rational theory suggests politicians respond to rulings in ways that maximise electoral benefit.\n• COMPLIANCE BY THE EXECUTIVE: in both countries, the executive usually complies with rulings because open defiance would carry heavy political and legal costs — the UK government accepted the Miller II ruling in 2019 and Parliament reconvened the next day, while US Presidents have generally complied with adverse Supreme Court decisions since Nixon handed over the White House tapes after United States v Nixon (1974).\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three ways that rational theory could be used to study how governments and legislators respond to court rulings in the US and UK. Relevant points not listed above should be credited. Each point should explain the theory's relevance and analyse it with examples from BOTH the US and the UK; a description of one country only, or of the institutions with no link to the theory, cannot reach the top of the range. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: '"The US Supreme Court is more powerful than the UK Supreme Court." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `US COURT MORE POWERFUL:\n• It can strike down federal and state legislation as unconstitutional; the UK Court cannot touch an Act of Parliament.\n• Because amendment is practically unattainable, its constitutional rulings are effectively FINAL; any UK ruling can be reversed by ordinary statute, as the 2022 Act showed.\n• Life tenure extends a justice's influence for decades.\n• Federalism gives it authority over the constitutional balance of the entire system.\n• It determines the rules of political competition — Citizens United, Shelby County, Rucho.\n\nTHE GAP IS NARROWER THAN IT APPEARS:\n• HRA s.3 lets UK courts shape statutory meaning substantially, achieving much of what strike-down would.\n• Declarations of incompatibility are almost always acted upon — political force exceeding legal force (Belmarsh).\n• UK judicial review of executive action is unqualified, and most government activity is executive.\n• Miller I and II show UK courts constraining the executive on the most contested political questions of the era.\n• The US Court has no enforcement power and depends on accepted legitimacy.\n\nGuidance for L5: ENTRENCHMENT is the single structural variable explaining most of the difference, and should be named explicitly. The strongest answers also test the obvious conclusion by examining where UK influence exceeds formal power.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },

      { q: '"Judicial appointments in the USA are more politicised than in the UK." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `MORE POLITICISED IN THE USA:\n• Nominees selected for judicial philosophy; confirmation votes near-party-line.\n• Norms have collapsed — the same election-year argument applied to Garland in 2016 and abandoned for Barrett in 2020; the filibuster removed in 2017.\n• Interest groups campaign for and against nominees, spending heavily.\n• Life tenure makes each appointment a decades-long prize; presidents nominate young candidates and justices time retirements strategically.\n• Dobbs reversed a fifty-year precedent after three appointments, reinforcing the perception that outcomes follow appointments.\n\nUK NOT ENTIRELY APOLITICAL:\n• The Lord Chancellor retains a formal role, and the government sets the framework within which the JAC operates.\n• Concerns about the narrow social and educational background of appointees (Griffith's critique) raise questions about whose assumptions inform interpretation.\n• Ministers and the press have attacked judges after Miller I and II, and governments have proposed curbing judicial power.\n• Politicians have periodically criticised the JAC and proposed changes to it.\n\nGuidance for L5: the RATIONAL explanation is decisive and should be named — where an appointment can determine policy for decades, heavy investment is rational; where a court cannot invalidate statute, the return is too low. The difference is institutional incentive, not national temperament.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },

      { q: '"Entrenchment explains the difference in power between the UK and US judiciaries." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `ENTRENCHMENT EXPLAINS IT:\n• It provides a HIGHER LAW against which statute can be measured — without one, UK courts have nothing to measure against, which is why they cannot strike legislation down.\n• It makes US rulings effectively final, since amendment is unattainable; UK rulings are reversible by ordinary Act.\n• It explains politicised appointments — the return on capturing a US appointment is decades of policy control; the return on a UK appointment is far lower.\n• It explains why US pressure groups litigate so heavily and UK groups lobby ministers instead.\n• Given the same constitution, any court would acquire the same power — the difference is not judicial character.\n\nOTHER FACTORS MATTER:\n• Federalism gives the US Court a whole additional jurisdiction over centre–state disputes that entrenchment alone does not create.\n• Life tenure versus a retirement age independently affects the stakes of each appointment.\n• Cultural factors — a rights-based American political tradition channels moral disputes into constitutional litigation, which is why the Court is the arena for national moral conflict.\n• The UK Court only opened in 2009; institutional confidence may still be developing.\n• HRA s.3 shows UK influence can exceed formal powers, so entrenchment does not determine everything.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest conclusion is that entrenchment explains MOST but not ALL — it is the dominant variable, with federalism, tenure and culture as genuine supplements.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },

      { q: '"Both the UK and US judiciaries have become too political." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `BOTH TOO POLITICAL:\n• Both now rule on the most contested political questions of their era — Miller I and II; Dobbs, Citizens United, Shelby County.\n• Both have expanded rights jurisdiction that requires evaluative judgment — HRA s.3; substantive due process.\n• Both attract accusations of overreach and government proposals to curb judicial power.\n• Pressure groups in both systems now use litigation as a deliberate political strategy.\n• Expanding executive unilateralism draws courts into political disputes in both.\n\nNOT EQUALLY, OR NOT AT ALL:\n• The charge has FAR more force in the USA, where appointments are openly partisan and where precedent has been reversed following changes in composition — nobody claims UK judges were appointed to produce particular outcomes.\n• UK appointments are made by an independent commission on merit.\n• In Miller I and II the UK Court ruled on the LIMITS OF EXECUTIVE POWER under existing principle, not on the merits of Brexit — it constrained the government on Parliament's behalf.\n• UK rulings can be reversed by ordinary statute, so they never remove a question from democratic revision.\n• Neither court chooses the disputes brought to it.\n\nGuidance for L5: the comparative distinction is essential — the UK criticism concerns RULINGS on executive power; the US criticism concerns the PROCESS that produces the judges and the reversibility of precedent. An answer treating the two as equivalent caps at L3.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },
    ]
  },

  '2.12': {
    name: 'Comparative — Electoral and Party Systems',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways that structural theory could be used to study the electoral systems in the US and UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in their explanations and analysis, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• STRUCTURAL THEORY: the structural approach focuses on institutions and the rules and processes within them — constitutions, the relationships between branches of government, the distribution of power between levels of government. It treats these structures as the framework that shapes and constrains what individuals and groups can do, so that similar actors behave differently because they operate within different institutional arrangements.\n• SINGLE-MEMBER PLURALITY: both countries use first-past-the-post in single-member districts for their main legislative elections, which structurally favours large parties with geographically efficient support. In the UK in 2024, Labour won 411 of 650 seats on about 34% of the vote. Duverger's law explains why both countries tend towards two-party dominance, although the effect is much stronger in the US.\n• THE ELECTORAL COLLEGE: US presidents are chosen by the Electoral College, and in almost every state the winner of the popular vote takes all of that state's electoral votes (Maine and Nebraska are exceptions). This structure means candidates focus on swing states and can win the presidency while losing the national popular vote, as in 2000 and 2016. The UK has no directly elected executive; the PM is the leader of the largest party in the Commons.\n• PRIMARIES AND CANDIDATE SELECTION: in the US, candidates are chosen by voters in primaries run under state law, so the structure of the electoral process removes control of candidate selection from party leaders. In the UK, candidates are chosen by local party members under central party rules, and the national party can block candidates. Structural theory explains why US parties are weaker and more decentralised than UK parties.\n• FIXED TERMS AND THE ELECTORAL TIMETABLE: US elections take place on a fixed timetable set by the Constitution and federal law — the House every two years, a third of the Senate every two years and the President every four. Since the Dissolution and Calling of Parliament Act 2022, the timing of a UK general election is again chosen by the Prime Minister within a five-year maximum. The frequent US election cycle means members of the House are almost permanently campaigning.\n• MULTI-LEVEL AND DIVERSE SYSTEMS: in the US, the states run elections, so rules on voter registration, voter ID and postal voting vary widely. In the UK, elections are regulated nationally, although devolved and mayoral elections use different systems (AMS in Scotland and Wales before Wales moved to a closed-list system for 2026; STV in Northern Ireland). Structural theory shows how these arrangements shape participation and party competition.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three ways that structural theory could be used to study the electoral systems in the US and UK. Relevant points not listed above should be credited. Each point should explain the theory's relevance and analyse it with examples from BOTH the US and the UK; a description of one country only, or of the institutions with no link to the theory, cannot reach the top of the range. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways that cultural theory could be used to study the ideology and nature of the main parties in the US and UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in their explanations and analysis, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• CULTURAL THEORY: the cultural approach focuses on the shared ideas, values, beliefs and historical experiences of a society or of groups within it, and argues that these shape how individuals act and what they regard as legitimate. It links present-day behaviour to each country's history — the American revolutionary, republican tradition and distrust of concentrated power, against the UK's gradual, evolutionary development, monarchy and parliamentary tradition.\n• A LIBERAL CONSENSUS IN THE US: both major US parties operate within a shared political culture that values individual liberty, limited government and capitalism, and the US has never had a significant socialist party. Cultural theory explains this by reference to the absence of a feudal past and aristocracy, and to the American belief in individual self-reliance. By contrast, the UK Labour Party grew out of the trade union movement and has socialist roots, as reflected in its original Clause IV.\n• CLASS AND PARTY IN THE UK: UK party politics was traditionally structured by social class, with the working class voting Labour and the middle class voting Conservative. Partisan and class dealignment has weakened this, and the 2016 EU referendum created new identities based on education, age and attitudes to Brexit. Cultural theory explains how shared identities shape party support in both countries.\n• CULTURE WARS AND POLARISATION: US party competition has become increasingly shaped by cultural and religious issues such as abortion, guns, immigration and race, with evangelical Christians forming a core part of the Republican coalition. Religion plays a much smaller role in UK party politics. Cultural theory explains why US parties are more ideologically polarised on social issues, though debates on immigration and identity have become more prominent in the UK with the rise of Reform UK.\n• BROAD-CHURCH PARTIES: in both countries, the main parties are broad coalitions containing different ideological traditions — one-nation conservatives and Thatcherites in the Conservative Party, or progressive and moderate Democrats in the US. Cultural theory can show how each party's traditions and values, rather than just its institutional position, influence its policies and internal divisions.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three ways that cultural theory could be used to study the ideology and nature of the main parties in the US and UK. Relevant points not listed above should be credited. Each point should explain the theory's relevance and analyse it with examples from BOTH the US and the UK; a description of one country only, or of the institutions with no link to the theory, cannot reach the top of the range. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways that rational theory could be used to study the selection of party candidates and leaders in the US and UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in their explanations and analysis, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• RATIONAL THEORY: the rational (rational choice) approach focuses on the individual. It assumes political actors are self-interested and make logical, calculated choices to maximise the benefits and minimise the costs to themselves or their cause — for example re-election, promotion, policy success or party advantage. Differences between the two countries are explained by the different incentives that actors face.\n• CANDIDATES RESPOND TO THEIR SELECTORATE: rational theory predicts that politicians appeal to whoever decides whether they are selected. In the US, candidates must win a primary, where turnout is low and voters are often more ideological, so it is rational to adopt positions that appeal to the party base — Liz Cheney lost the Wyoming Republican primary in 2022 after voting to impeach Trump. In the UK, candidates depend on local party members and central party approval, so loyalty to the party leadership is rational.\n• LEADERS CHOSEN BY PARTY MEMBERS: in the UK, party members now play a decisive role in choosing leaders — Labour members elected Corbyn in 2015, and Conservative members chose Truss over Sunak in 2022 and Badenoch in 2024. Members' choices may reflect their own preferences rather than the median voter's, which may not be electorally rational for the party. Similarly, Trump won the Republican nomination in 2016 despite opposition from much of the party establishment, because primary voters, not party elites, decide.\n• PARTY ELITES ACT TO PROTECT THEIR INTERESTS: rational theory also explains why party elites seek to retain some control. UK parties can refuse to endorse candidates — Labour did not allow Corbyn to stand as its candidate in 2024, and he was elected as an independent. In 2024 Democratic leaders effectively ensured Kamala Harris became the nominee after Biden withdrew, without a new round of primaries. Both show elites acting to protect the party's electoral prospects.\n• CAREER INCENTIVES: UK MPs seeking promotion to ministerial office have a strong incentive to remain loyal to the leadership, whose support they need for advancement. US legislators build careers through seniority, committee positions and service to their districts, and do not need the President's approval to remain in office. Rational theory shows why the selection process shapes how representatives behave once elected.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three ways that rational theory could be used to study the selection of party candidates and leaders in the US and UK. Relevant points not listed above should be credited. Each point should explain the theory's relevance and analyse it with examples from BOTH the US and the UK; a description of one country only, or of the institutions with no link to the theory, cannot reach the top of the range. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways that rational theory could be used to study campaign finance in the US and UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in their explanations and analysis, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• RATIONAL THEORY: the rational (rational choice) approach focuses on the individual. It assumes political actors are self-interested and make logical, calculated choices to maximise the benefits and minimise the costs to themselves or their cause — for example re-election, promotion, policy success or party advantage. Differences between the two countries are explained by the different incentives that actors face.\n• DONORS SEEK A RETURN ON THEIR INVESTMENT: rational theory suggests that individuals, corporations and groups donate to gain access or influence, or to promote policies that benefit them. In the US, where Citizens United v FEC (2010) allowed unlimited independent expenditure and led to Super PACs, wealthy donors can spend vast sums — Elon Musk spent more than $250 million supporting Trump and other Republicans in 2024. In the UK, spending limits reduce the potential return from large donations, although large donors still give to parties.\n• CANDIDATES AS INDIVIDUAL FUNDRAISERS: US candidates must raise their own money to win primaries and general elections, so it is rational for them to spend large amounts of time fundraising and to listen to donors. UK candidates rely on the national party and operate under strict local spending limits, so they have little need for personal fundraising. This helps explain why US legislators are more independent of their parties but more dependent on donors.\n• PARTIES RESPOND TO REGULATION: in the UK, the Political Parties, Elections and Referendums Act 2000 set national spending limits and requires donations to be reported to the Electoral Commission, while paid political advertising on television is banned. Parties rationally focus spending on targeted digital campaigning in marginal seats. In the US, the absence of effective spending limits after Buckley v Valeo (1976) means candidates and groups rationally compete in an "arms race" of television and digital advertising.\n• GROUPS TARGET THE MOST EFFECTIVE POINT OF INFLUENCE: because US primaries are decisive in safe seats, interest groups rationally spend heavily in primaries — for example a Super PAC linked to AIPAC spent heavily to help defeat Jamaal Bowman in 2024. In the UK, groups seeking influence rationally concentrate on donations to parties or lobbying ministers, because individual MPs have little independent power.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three ways that rational theory could be used to study campaign finance in the US and UK. Relevant points not listed above should be credited. Each point should explain the theory's relevance and analyse it with examples from BOTH the US and the UK; a description of one country only, or of the institutions with no link to the theory, cannot reach the top of the range. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways that structural theory could be used to study the performance of third and minor parties in the US and UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in their explanations and analysis, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• STRUCTURAL THEORY: the structural approach focuses on institutions and the rules and processes within them — constitutions, the relationships between branches of government, the distribution of power between levels of government. It treats these structures as the framework that shapes and constrains what individuals and groups can do, so that similar actors behave differently because they operate within different institutional arrangements.\n• THE EFFECT OF FIRST-PAST-THE-POST: structurally, plurality systems penalise parties whose support is spread thinly. In the UK in 2024, Reform UK won about 14% of the vote but only five seats, while the Liberal Democrats won 72 seats on around 12% because their support was concentrated. In the US, Ross Perot won nearly 19% of the popular vote in 1992 but no electoral votes, because he did not come first in any state.\n• TERRITORIAL STRUCTURES: the UK's devolved structure and distinct national identities give nationalist parties a geographical base — the SNP won 56 of Scotland's 59 seats in 2015 and has governed Scotland since 2007. The US has no equivalent territorial party system, and the two major parties compete in every state.\n• PROPORTIONAL SYSTEMS AT SUB-NATIONAL LEVEL: more proportional systems used for devolved elections (such as AMS in Scotland and STV in Northern Ireland) give smaller parties representation and governing experience, strengthening their credibility at Westminster. In the US, most elections at every level use plurality rules, although a few states such as Maine use ranked-choice voting.\n• BALLOT ACCESS AND DEBATE RULES: US third parties face structural barriers such as state ballot-access requirements and the rules of the Commission on Presidential Debates, which requires 15% support in national polls for a place in the debates. In the UK, parties can stand candidates relatively easily, and broadcasting rules require balanced coverage of parties with significant support, which helps minor parties gain publicity.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three ways that structural theory could be used to study the performance of third and minor parties in the US and UK. Relevant points not listed above should be credited. Each point should explain the theory's relevance and analyse it with examples from BOTH the US and the UK; a description of one country only, or of the institutions with no link to the theory, cannot reach the top of the range. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways that cultural theory could be used to study polarisation and voting behaviour in the US and UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in their explanations and analysis, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• CULTURAL THEORY: the cultural approach focuses on the shared ideas, values, beliefs and historical experiences of a society or of groups within it, and argues that these shape how individuals act and what they regard as legitimate. It links present-day behaviour to each country's history — the American revolutionary, republican tradition and distrust of concentrated power, against the UK's gradual, evolutionary development, monarchy and parliamentary tradition.\n• PARTISAN IDENTITY: in the US, party identification is increasingly a social and cultural identity, closely linked to religion, race, urban or rural residence and attitudes to issues such as abortion and guns. Cultural theory explains why "negative partisanship" — dislike of the other party — has grown, making Americans less willing to vote across party lines. In the UK, partisan dealignment means fewer voters have a strong attachment to any party, so volatility is higher than in the US.\n• CULTURE WARS: US politics is shaped by conflicts over cultural and moral issues, with evangelical Christians forming a core part of the Republican coalition. The UK has seen its own values-based divides, especially around Brexit, immigration and national identity, which helped Reform UK win about 14% of the vote in 2024. Cultural theory explains why voting has become more closely tied to values rather than class in both countries.\n• MEDIA CULTURE: US voters increasingly consume partisan media and social media that reinforce their existing views. UK broadcasters are required by law to be impartial, which may moderate polarisation, although the press is openly partisan and social media plays a growing role. Cultural theory suggests that the media environment shapes how voters perceive the other side.\n• CONSENSUS AND CIVILITY TRADITIONS: British political culture has traditionally valued pragmatism and relatively consensual norms, and the deep polarisation seen in the US has been less pronounced. In the US, the civil rights era realignment of the South sorted conservatives into the Republican Party and liberals into the Democratic Party, producing parties with distinct cultural identities. Cultural theory therefore helps explain why polarisation is deeper in the US, though structural factors such as primaries also contribute.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three ways that cultural theory could be used to study polarisation and voting behaviour in the US and UK. Relevant points not listed above should be credited. Each point should explain the theory's relevance and analyse it with examples from BOTH the US and the UK; a description of one country only, or of the institutions with no link to the theory, cannot reach the top of the range. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: '"Electoral systems explain the party systems of the UK and the USA." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `ELECTORAL SYSTEMS EXPLAIN:\n• Both countries use single-member plurality and both have two dominant parties, despite very different political cultures — Duverger's law.\n• Strategic incentives follow directly: voters, donors and candidates converge on the two viable parties to avoid the spoiler effect.\n• Where the UK uses PROPORTIONAL systems at devolved level, genuinely multi-party systems emerge from the SAME electorate — the strongest available natural experiment.\n• Perot's ~19% and zero electoral votes in 1992 shows the mechanism starkly.\n• Institutional barriers (ballot access, debate thresholds) reinforce the effect.\n\nOTHER FACTORS NEEDED:\n• Identical rules produce DIFFERENT outcomes — the UK sustains significant nationalist and third parties at Westminster while the USA does not, so the GEOGRAPHY of support matters independently.\n• Territorial identity and the salience of national cleavages explain the SNP's position; the USA has no comparable cleavage.\n• Cultural factors — American individualism supporting candidate-centred politics; British collective party traditions.\n• Primaries (an institution, not an electoral system) explain much of the discipline and polarisation difference.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest conclusion is that electoral systems explain the NUMBER of major parties and the geography of support explains the survival of minor ones — a distinction that resolves the apparent contradiction.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },

      { q: '"Party discipline differences between the UK and the USA are best explained by rational choice." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `RATIONAL CHOICE BEST:\n• It identifies WHO CONTROLS what a politician needs — nomination, money and promotion — and predicts discipline accordingly. UK parties control all three; US parties control none.\n• The prediction matches observation precisely: strong UK discipline, weak US discipline.\n• It explains VARIATION over time — UK discipline weakened over Brexit because the party's own selectorate was divided, which a static structural account cannot capture.\n• It explains individual behaviour — why a US legislator in a safe district fears a primary challenge more than a general election defeat.\n\nOTHER APPROACHES:\n• STRUCTURAL — fusion of powers means a UK government falls if it loses confidence, so rebellion is a constitutional act with terminal consequences; a US President's survival is independent of congressional votes. This raises the stakes of UK dissent enormously and is not reducible to individual incentives.\n• Separation of powers also explains why US legislators have no ministerial prize to pursue.\n• CULTURAL — American individualism and suspicion of centralised authority; British parliamentary traditions of collective party government. These explain why primaries were adopted in the Progressive era specifically to weaken party machines.\n\nGuidance for L5: rational choice is the strongest SINGLE approach here, but structure supplies the constitutional stakes and culture explains why those institutions were chosen. The best answers rank the approaches and justify the ranking rather than asserting complementarity vaguely.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },

      { q: '"Money is more important in US than UK elections." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `MORE IMPORTANT IN THE USA:\n• Effectively unlimited independent expenditure after Citizens United; UK spending is capped by statute.\n• Paid political TV advertising is the dominant US medium and is PROHIBITED in the UK.\n• Candidate-centred campaigns mean US legislators must raise their own money continuously; UK candidates rely on a centrally run campaign.\n• Primaries plus a general election means US candidates effectively fund two campaigns.\n• Dark money through 501(c)(4) groups has no UK equivalent.\n• The invisible primary screens out candidates who cannot raise money before a vote is cast.\n\nNOT SO DIFFERENT / qualified:\n• UK parties also depend heavily on a small number of very large donors, with recurring honours controversies.\n• Money does not determine US outcomes — Trump was outspent in 2016; heavily outspent candidates win.\n• Partisanship now predicts vote choice more reliably than campaign spending in both countries.\n• UK spending limits are not comprehensive, and non-party campaigning is regulated only loosely.\n• Both have concerns about access purchased by donors.\n\nGuidance for L5: the decisive structural point is that US reform is CONSTITUTIONALLY FORECLOSED while the UK regulates by ordinary statute. The same problem exists in both; only one can legislate a remedy. That reaches L5.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },

      { q: '"The UK and US party systems are converging." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `CONVERGING:\n• Both show partisan DEALIGNMENT and the weakening of class-based or traditional loyalties.\n• Both have seen rising polarisation and insurgent challenges to established parties (UKIP/Reform; the Tea Party/Trump).\n• Both have opened leadership or candidate selection to wider participation, with similar results — Corbyn 2015 and Truss 2022 parallel Trump 2016 in producing leaders the parliamentary party or establishment opposed.\n• Both have personalised, leader-focused and media-driven campaigns.\n• UK party membership has become more ideologically committed, resembling US primary electorates.\n\nNOT CONVERGING:\n• Selection remains fundamentally different — the UK has NO primaries, so parties retain gatekeeping power US parties lack.\n• UK discipline remains strong because the party controls nomination, funding and promotion.\n• UK campaign spending is capped by statute; US independent expenditure is unlimited and constitutionally protected.\n• The UK sustains significant third parties at Westminster; the USA does not.\n• The UK uses proportional systems sub-nationally; the USA does not.\n• UK fusion of powers ties government survival to party discipline in a way separation does not.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest formulation is that the TRENDS converge while the INSTITUTIONS do not — and since institutions shape behaviour, convergence is likely to remain partial. That distinction reaches L5.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },
    ]
  },

  '2.13': {
    name: 'Comparative — Pressure Groups',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways that rational theory could be used to study the methods used by pressure groups in the US and UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in their explanations and analysis, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• RATIONAL THEORY: the rational (rational choice) approach focuses on the individual. It assumes political actors are self-interested and make logical, calculated choices to maximise the benefits and minimise the costs to themselves or their cause — for example re-election, promotion, policy success or party advantage. Differences between the two countries are explained by the different incentives that actors face.\n• GROUPS CHOOSE THE MOST COST-EFFECTIVE METHODS: rational theory suggests groups will focus their resources where they are most likely to gain influence. In the US, where individual members of Congress are independent and depend on campaign funds, groups rationally lobby legislators, give through PACs and spend through Super PACs. In the UK, where the executive dominates policy-making, groups rationally focus on ministers and civil servants rather than individual MPs.\n• ELECTIONEERING AND PRIMARIES: because US primaries decide most seats, groups can threaten a legislator's career by funding a primary challenger or publishing scorecards of how members voted, as the NRA has long done. UK groups cannot fund candidates in the same way and face strict spending limits under electoral law, so electioneering is a less rational use of their resources.\n• INSIDER STATUS: in both countries, groups whose expertise government needs — such as the National Farmers' Union in the UK or major business associations in the US — rationally seek insider status and avoid confrontational tactics that could lose them access. Groups that lack access, such as Just Stop Oil or Extinction Rebellion in the UK, rationally turn to direct action to gain publicity.\n• LOBBYING AND THE REVOLVING DOOR: in both countries, groups hire former ministers, officials and legislators as lobbyists because their contacts and knowledge give privileged access. The US lobbying industry is much larger, reflecting the greater number of access points, but the UK has also seen controversies such as the Greensill affair involving former Prime Minister David Cameron in 2021. Rational theory explains why both groups and former officials benefit from the revolving door.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three ways that rational theory could be used to study the methods used by pressure groups in the US and UK. Relevant points not listed above should be credited. Each point should explain the theory's relevance and analyse it with examples from BOTH the US and the UK; a description of one country only, or of the institutions with no link to the theory, cannot reach the top of the range. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways that cultural theory could be used to study the use of protest and direct action by pressure groups in the US and UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in their explanations and analysis, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• CULTURAL THEORY: the cultural approach focuses on the shared ideas, values, beliefs and historical experiences of a society or of groups within it, and argues that these shape how individuals act and what they regard as legitimate. It links present-day behaviour to each country's history — the American revolutionary, republican tradition and distrust of concentrated power, against the UK's gradual, evolutionary development, monarchy and parliamentary tradition.\n• A SHARED TRADITION OF PROTEST: both countries have long traditions of peaceful protest, from the suffragettes in the UK to the civil rights movement in the US, and protest is widely accepted as a legitimate form of participation. The Black Lives Matter protests in 2020 spread from the US to the UK, showing how shared values and a common language allow movements to cross borders.\n• CONSTITUTIONAL PROTECTION IN THE US: the First Amendment protects freedom of speech, assembly and the right "to petition the Government for a redress of grievances", so protest is seen as a constitutional right. In the UK, protest is protected by Articles 10 and 11 of the ECHR through the Human Rights Act, but Parliament has restricted it — the Police, Crime, Sentencing and Courts Act 2022 and the Public Order Act 2023 gave the police greater powers over disruptive protest. Cultural theory explains why restrictions of this kind would be more controversial in the US.\n• NEW SOCIAL MOVEMENTS: in both countries, younger and more educated citizens are more likely to take part in issue-based movements such as climate campaigns, reflecting a post-materialist shift in values away from traditional party and union membership. Cultural theory explains the growth of groups such as Extinction Rebellion in the UK and the Sunrise Movement in the US.\n• ATTITUDES TO DISRUPTIVE ACTION: public opinion in both countries is often hostile to highly disruptive direct action, and governments have responded with tougher laws. In the UK, the government proscribed Palestine Action as a terrorist organisation in 2025 after members damaged military aircraft, a decision that was strongly contested. Cultural theory helps explain the balance each society strikes between order and the right to protest.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three ways that cultural theory could be used to study the use of protest and direct action by pressure groups in the US and UK. Relevant points not listed above should be credited. Each point should explain the theory's relevance and analyse it with examples from BOTH the US and the UK; a description of one country only, or of the institutions with no link to the theory, cannot reach the top of the range. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways that structural theory could be used to study the influence of pressure groups on policy-making in the US and UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in their explanations and analysis, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• STRUCTURAL THEORY: the structural approach focuses on institutions and the rules and processes within them — constitutions, the relationships between branches of government, the distribution of power between levels of government. It treats these structures as the framework that shapes and constrains what individuals and groups can do, so that similar actors behave differently because they operate within different institutional arrangements.\n• MULTIPLE ACCESS POINTS IN THE US: the separation of powers and federalism create many access points — congressional committees, both houses, the executive branch, the courts and fifty state governments. A group needs to win at only one veto point to block a proposal, which helps explain the long success of the NRA in preventing major federal gun control. In the UK, power is concentrated in the executive, so there are fewer access points, and a government with a majority can pass legislation despite group opposition.\n• INDEPENDENT LEGISLATORS: because members of Congress are not bound by strong party discipline, groups can influence the outcome of votes by persuading or supporting individual members. In the UK, strong party discipline means most MPs vote as their party directs, so lobbying individual backbenchers is less effective, except through select committees or on free votes.\n• DEVOLUTION CREATES NEW ACCESS POINTS IN THE UK: devolution has created additional arenas for groups — for example, health campaigners secured minimum unit pricing for alcohol in Scotland (2018) and Wales (2020), although it has not been introduced in England. This resembles, on a smaller scale, the US pattern of groups pursuing policy at state level when blocked federally.\n• ENTRENCHED RIGHTS: US groups can use the constitution to challenge laws in court and, if successful, have them struck down nationally. UK groups can challenge executive decisions through judicial review but cannot overturn Acts of Parliament. Structural theory explains why US groups can achieve permanent change through the courts that UK groups cannot.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three ways that structural theory could be used to study the influence of pressure groups on policy-making in the US and UK. Relevant points not listed above should be credited. Each point should explain the theory's relevance and analyse it with examples from BOTH the US and the UK; a description of one country only, or of the institutions with no link to the theory, cannot reach the top of the range. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways that rational theory could be used to study the organisation and membership of pressure groups in the US and UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in their explanations and analysis, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• RATIONAL THEORY: the rational (rational choice) approach focuses on the individual. It assumes political actors are self-interested and make logical, calculated choices to maximise the benefits and minimise the costs to themselves or their cause — for example re-election, promotion, policy success or party advantage. Differences between the two countries are explained by the different incentives that actors face.\n• THE COLLECTIVE ACTION PROBLEM: rational theory, especially Mancur Olson's Logic of Collective Action (1965), suggests individuals will not join a group if they can benefit from its success without contributing — the "free-rider" problem. This explains why large groups representing diffuse interests (such as consumers or taxpayers) are often weaker in both countries than small groups with concentrated interests, such as particular industries.\n• SELECTIVE INCENTIVES: to overcome free-riding, groups offer benefits available only to members. AARP in the US offers insurance and discounts to its members, and the National Trust in the UK offers free entry to its properties. Rational theory explains why many of the largest membership groups in both countries rely on these benefits as much as on their political campaigns.\n• CONCENTRATED BUSINESS INTERESTS: businesses have strong incentives and resources to organise because the benefits of influencing policy are large and fall on a small number of firms. In both countries, business lobbying is extensive, although the scale is much greater in the US, where federal lobbying expenditure runs to billions of dollars a year. Rational theory helps explain elitist critiques of pressure group politics in both systems.\n• CHEQUEBOOK MEMBERSHIP: many members of large groups in both countries simply pay a subscription and leave campaigning to professional staff, because this is the least costly way of supporting a cause. This rational behaviour explains the professionalisation of groups such as Greenpeace in the UK or the Sierra Club in the US.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three ways that rational theory could be used to study the organisation and membership of pressure groups in the US and UK. Relevant points not listed above should be credited. Each point should explain the theory's relevance and analyse it with examples from BOTH the US and the UK; a description of one country only, or of the institutions with no link to the theory, cannot reach the top of the range. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways that structural theory could be used to study the use of the courts by pressure groups in the US and UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in their explanations and analysis, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• STRUCTURAL THEORY: the structural approach focuses on institutions and the rules and processes within them — constitutions, the relationships between branches of government, the distribution of power between levels of government. It treats these structures as the framework that shapes and constrains what individuals and groups can do, so that similar actors behave differently because they operate within different institutional arrangements.\n• STRIKING DOWN LEGISLATION: because the US has an entrenched constitution, groups can ask the courts to declare laws unconstitutional — the NAACP's litigation strategy led to Brown v Board of Education (1954), and Students for Fair Admissions won its challenge to race-conscious admissions at Harvard in 2023. In the UK, parliamentary sovereignty means groups cannot have an Act of Parliament struck down; the most they can secure under the Human Rights Act is a declaration of incompatibility.\n• JUDICIAL REVIEW OF EXECUTIVE ACTION: UK groups can challenge the legality of government decisions through judicial review, and have had significant successes — UNISON's case led the Supreme Court in 2017 to quash employment tribunal fees, and environmental groups including Friends of the Earth and ClientEarth won a 2022 ruling that the government's net zero strategy did not meet the requirements of the Climate Change Act 2008. US groups also challenge executive action, including many executive orders in both Trump administrations.\n• DURABILITY OF RULINGS: a US constitutional ruling can only be reversed by a later Court or a constitutional amendment, so a litigation victory can deliver lasting change. In the UK, Parliament can reverse a ruling by ordinary statute — the Judicial Review and Courts Act 2022 reversed the effect of the Cart ruling. Structural theory explains why litigation is a more valuable strategy for US groups.\n• FEDERALISM AND MULTIPLE COURTS: the US federal and state court systems give groups many venues in which to bring cases, and groups often choose the court most likely to be sympathetic. The UK has a single Supreme Court for the whole country and separate legal systems in Scotland and Northern Ireland, but far fewer venues. Structural theory shows how the design of the court system shapes group strategy.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three ways that structural theory could be used to study the use of the courts by pressure groups in the US and UK. Relevant points not listed above should be credited. Each point should explain the theory's relevance and analyse it with examples from BOTH the US and the UK; a description of one country only, or of the institutions with no link to the theory, cannot reach the top of the range. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways that cultural theory could be used to study the relationship between pressure groups and government in the US and UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in their explanations and analysis, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• CULTURAL THEORY: the cultural approach focuses on the shared ideas, values, beliefs and historical experiences of a society or of groups within it, and argues that these shape how individuals act and what they regard as legitimate. It links present-day behaviour to each country's history — the American revolutionary, republican tradition and distrust of concentrated power, against the UK's gradual, evolutionary development, monarchy and parliamentary tradition.\n• AMERICAN ASSOCIATIONAL CULTURE: in the 1830s Alexis de Tocqueville observed that Americans formed voluntary associations for every purpose. Cultural theory argues this tradition, combined with distrust of government, makes pressure group activity a normal and accepted part of American democracy, and pluralist theorists such as Robert Dahl saw competition between groups as the essence of US politics.\n• BRITISH CONSULTATION TRADITION: the UK has a culture of government consulting recognised representative bodies, such as professional associations and trade unions, and during the post-war consensus unions and business groups were closely involved in economic policy. This produced a tradition of insider groups working quietly with civil servants, rather than open public competition between groups.\n• THE LABOUR MOVEMENT: in the UK, trade unions founded the Labour Party and many remain affiliated to it, reflecting a political culture in which class-based organisations have been directly linked to a major party. In the US, unions have supported the Democrats but have never formed their own party, reflecting the weakness of class-based politics in American culture.\n• SUSPICION OF LOBBYING: in both countries there is public suspicion that wealthy interests have too much influence — in the US, campaigns against "special interests" and money in politics; in the UK, scandals such as cash-for-questions in the 1990s and the Greensill affair in 2021. Cultural theory explains why both societies accept groups as legitimate but remain uneasy about the influence of money.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three ways that cultural theory could be used to study the relationship between pressure groups and government in the US and UK. Relevant points not listed above should be credited. Each point should explain the theory's relevance and analyse it with examples from BOTH the US and the UK; a description of one country only, or of the institutions with no link to the theory, cannot reach the top of the range. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: '"Pressure groups are more powerful in the USA than the UK." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `MORE POWERFUL IN THE USA:\n• Many access points — separation of powers, bicameralism, the filibuster, federalism and the courts; a group needs to win at only one to block.\n• Weak party discipline makes individual legislators genuinely persuadable and individually dependent on outside money.\n• Primaries allow groups to threaten a legislator's renomination — a lever no UK group possesses.\n• Unlimited independent expenditure after Citizens United.\n• Litigation can strike down legislation permanently.\n\nQUALIFICATIONS:\n• Many access points are also many VETO points, so US groups are better at BLOCKING than achieving. A UK group that persuades a government with a secure majority sees its aim legislated quickly and in full.\n• UK insider groups enjoy embedded, routine consultation at the drafting stage that is highly effective.\n• Success in BOTH systems depends primarily on alignment with the governing agenda.\n• UK devolution and judicial review have multiplied access points since 1999.\n\nGuidance for L5: "more powerful" depends on whether the group's aim is CHANGE or its PREVENTION. The strongest answers make that distinction explicitly rather than simply accumulating US advantages.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },

      { q: '"Institutional factors rather than culture explain differences in UK and US pressure group activity." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `INSTITUTIONS EXPLAIN:\n• Access points follow directly from separation of powers versus fusion, and from federalism versus devolution.\n• Primaries explain why US groups target individual legislators and UK groups target ministers — the return differs because the lever differs.\n• Entrenchment explains the litigation asymmetry: US courts can strike down statute, UK courts cannot.\n• Campaign finance rules explain the electioneering difference — one system caps spending by statute, the other cannot.\n• DECISIVE EVIDENCE: UK group behaviour has SHIFTED towards the US pattern precisely as devolution and expanded judicial review created new access points — behaviour tracking institutions, not culture.\n\nCULTURE ALSO EXPLAINS:\n• American associational density and litigiousness PREDATE the modern institutional arrangements, which institutions alone cannot explain.\n• A rights-based political tradition makes constitutional litigation a natural form of political action.\n• The British insider-consultation tradition shaped how the executive engages groups.\n• Institutions were themselves CHOSEN because of cultural assumptions, so the two cannot be cleanly separated.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest conclusion is that institutions explain CURRENT BEHAVIOUR most precisely, while culture explains the ORIGIN of the institutions — and that the UK's post-1999 shift is the best available evidence for the primacy of institutions in explaining behaviour.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },

      { q: '"Both UK and US pressure group politics are elitist." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `BOTH ELITIST:\n• In both, influence tracks resources, organisation and access rather than the number of people affected.\n• Business enjoys STRUCTURAL power in both — governments depend on investment and employment decisions, so the anticipated reaction of business constrains policy without any lobbying.\n• The revolving door operates in both; both have lobbying registers criticised as inadequate.\n• In both, well-resourced groups dominate the technical consultation stages where policy is actually shaped.\n• US dark money and UK party dependence on a small number of large donors are different forms of the same concentration.\n\nBOTH ALSO PLURALIST:\n• Group competition is genuine in each, and outcomes frequently reflect compromise.\n• Both offer multiple access points — more in the USA, but devolution and judicial review have increased them in the UK.\n• Poorly resourced groups do win — the 2017 ACA repeal defeat; the Snowdrop campaign; Marcus Rashford.\n• Litigation gives minorities routes to victory without majorities.\n\nGuidance for L5: the defensible conclusion is that both systems are PLURALIST IN FORM and ELITIST IN EFFECT — competition is real but pluralism's assumption of rough parity of access fails in each. They differ in DEGREE (sharper in the USA post-Citizens United) rather than in KIND.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },

      { q: '"UK and US pressure group politics are converging." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `CONVERGING:\n• Devolution created additional access points, replicating multi-level lobbying.\n• The HRA and expanded judicial review made the courts a serious UK arena, with groups bringing test cases as US groups do — and the Judicial Review and Courts Act 2022 shows how significant that route became.\n• Professionalised commercial lobbying and a revolving door have developed in the UK.\n• Greater use of media and celebrity campaigning in both.\n• Both face declining party membership, increasing the relative weight of groups.\n\nNOT CONVERGING:\n• NO PRIMARIES in the UK — so no group can threaten a legislator's renomination, removing the single most powerful US lever.\n• Statutory spending limits cap UK campaign activity; US independent expenditure is unlimited and constitutionally protected.\n• Parliamentary sovereignty means UK courts cannot strike down statute, so litigation can never deliver what it delivers in the USA.\n• UK fusion of powers keeps the executive as the decisive target; US fragmentation keeps individual legislators central.\n• Iron triangles have no real UK equivalent.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest formulation is convergence in BEHAVIOUR, divergence in STRUCTURE — and since structure shapes behaviour, convergence is bounded. Naming the three structural barriers (primaries, spending limits, sovereignty) reaches L5.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },
    ]
  },

  '2.14': {
    name: 'Comparative — Civil Rights',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways that structural theory could be used to study the protection of civil rights in the US and UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in their explanations and analysis, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• STRUCTURAL THEORY: the structural approach focuses on institutions and the rules and processes within them — constitutions, the relationships between branches of government, the distribution of power between levels of government. It treats these structures as the framework that shapes and constrains what individuals and groups can do, so that similar actors behave differently because they operate within different institutional arrangements.\n• ENTRENCHED VERSUS STATUTORY RIGHTS: US rights are set out in the Bill of Rights and later amendments, notably the Fourteenth Amendment's equal protection clause, and are entrenched, so they cannot be removed by ordinary legislation. UK rights rest mainly on ordinary statutes — the Human Rights Act 1998 and the Equality Act 2010 — which Parliament could repeal by simple majority. Structural theory explains why US rights are legally more secure.\n• JUDICIAL REMEDIES: US courts can strike down laws that violate constitutional rights. UK courts can interpret legislation compatibly with Convention rights (s.3 HRA) or issue a declaration of incompatibility (s.4 HRA), but the law remains in force unless Parliament changes it. Structural theory shows how parliamentary sovereignty limits the judiciary's ability to protect rights in the UK.\n• THE ROLE OF THE LEGISLATURE: in the UK, Parliament is the main guardian of rights, with the Joint Committee on Human Rights scrutinising legislation and ministers making statements of compatibility under s.19 HRA. In the US, Congress also passes major rights legislation — the Civil Rights Act 1964 and Voting Rights Act 1965 — but it cannot pass laws that breach the Bill of Rights.\n• SUPRANATIONAL PROTECTION: the UK is a member of the Council of Europe and bound by the European Convention on Human Rights, so individuals can take cases to the European Court of Human Rights in Strasbourg. The US has no equivalent external court that its citizens can use. Structural theory explains how this provides an additional safeguard in the UK.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three ways that structural theory could be used to study the protection of civil rights in the US and UK. Relevant points not listed above should be credited. Each point should explain the theory's relevance and analyse it with examples from BOTH the US and the UK; a description of one country only, or of the institutions with no link to the theory, cannot reach the top of the range. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways that cultural theory could be used to study the development of civil rights movements in the US and UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in their explanations and analysis, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• CULTURAL THEORY: the cultural approach focuses on the shared ideas, values, beliefs and historical experiences of a society or of groups within it, and argues that these shape how individuals act and what they regard as legitimate. It links present-day behaviour to each country's history — the American revolutionary, republican tradition and distrust of concentrated power, against the UK's gradual, evolutionary development, monarchy and parliamentary tradition.\n• A SHARED LIBERAL TRADITION: both countries share liberal ideas of individual rights, equality before the law and tolerance, and civil rights campaigners in both appealed to these values. Cultural theory explains why campaigns for racial equality, women's rights and LGBT rights developed in both countries and influenced each other.\n• TRANSATLANTIC INFLUENCE: the US civil rights movement inspired campaigners in the UK — the Bristol Bus Boycott of 1963 drew on the example of the Montgomery Bus Boycott and helped build support for the Race Relations Act 1965. More recently, Black Lives Matter spread from the US to the UK in 2020. Shared language, media and culture allow movements to cross the Atlantic.\n• THE LEGACY OF SLAVERY AND SEGREGATION: in the US, slavery and legal segregation in the South (the Jim Crow era) made racial equality the central civil rights struggle, and the movement framed its demands as constitutional rights. The UK's experience of race relations developed mainly through post-war migration from the Commonwealth and was addressed chiefly through legislation. Cultural theory explains why race remains more central to US civil rights debates.\n• CHANGING SOCIAL ATTITUDES: in both countries, public attitudes on issues such as same-sex marriage changed rapidly in the early twenty-first century. In the UK this change was expressed through Parliament (the Marriage (Same Sex Couples) Act 2013), while in the US it was expressed through the courts (Obergefell v Hodges, 2015). Cultural theory explains the shared direction of change, while structural differences explain the different routes.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three ways that cultural theory could be used to study the development of civil rights movements in the US and UK. Relevant points not listed above should be credited. Each point should explain the theory's relevance and analyse it with examples from BOTH the US and the UK; a description of one country only, or of the institutions with no link to the theory, cannot reach the top of the range. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways that rational theory could be used to study the use of the courts to advance civil rights in the US and UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in their explanations and analysis, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• RATIONAL THEORY: the rational (rational choice) approach focuses on the individual. It assumes political actors are self-interested and make logical, calculated choices to maximise the benefits and minimise the costs to themselves or their cause — for example re-election, promotion, policy success or party advantage. Differences between the two countries are explained by the different incentives that actors face.\n• LITIGATION AS A RATIONAL STRATEGY: groups rationally choose the route most likely to succeed. When southern Democrats in Congress blocked civil rights legislation for decades, the NAACP turned to the courts, leading to Brown v Board of Education (1954). In the UK, where courts cannot strike down legislation, groups and individuals more often use judicial review and the Human Rights Act to challenge executive decisions, or campaign for legislation.\n• STRATEGIC CHOICE OF CASES: US groups select test cases and plaintiffs carefully to maximise the chance of a favourable ruling, and bring cases in courts likely to be sympathetic. Conservative groups used the same strategy to secure rulings such as Students for Fair Admissions v Harvard (2023) and Dobbs v Jackson (2022). In the UK, groups such as Liberty and For Women Scotland have also brought strategic cases — the Supreme Court's 2025 ruling on the meaning of "sex" in the Equality Act 2010 followed litigation by For Women Scotland.\n• COSTS AND BENEFITS OF A COURT VICTORY: a US constitutional victory binds the whole country and is very hard to reverse, so large investment in litigation is rational. A UK victory can be reversed by Parliament and may produce only a declaration of incompatibility, so groups rationally combine litigation with lobbying and public campaigns.\n• POLITICIANS' INCENTIVES: politicians may prefer courts to decide controversial rights issues so they avoid blame, or may attack the courts to mobilise supporters. After Dobbs, many Republican-controlled states passed restrictions on abortion while Democrats campaigned to protect abortion rights, showing how politicians respond rationally to court rulings.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three ways that rational theory could be used to study the use of the courts to advance civil rights in the US and UK. Relevant points not listed above should be credited. Each point should explain the theory's relevance and analyse it with examples from BOTH the US and the UK; a description of one country only, or of the institutions with no link to the theory, cannot reach the top of the range. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways that structural theory could be used to study the protection of rights at different levels of government in the US and UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in their explanations and analysis, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• STRUCTURAL THEORY: the structural approach focuses on institutions and the rules and processes within them — constitutions, the relationships between branches of government, the distribution of power between levels of government. It treats these structures as the framework that shapes and constrains what individuals and groups can do, so that similar actors behave differently because they operate within different institutional arrangements.\n• FEDERALISM AND VARIATION IN RIGHTS: US federalism reserves many areas — including elections, policing, education and, since Dobbs (2022), the regulation of abortion — to the states, so a citizen's rights in practice depend on where they live. Structural theory explains why abortion access differs radically between states such as Texas and California.\n• A COMMON FLOOR IN THE UK: UK devolution transferred areas such as health and education, but the Human Rights Act applies across the UK and the Equality Act 2010 covers Great Britain (Northern Ireland has its own equality law). The devolved legislatures cannot legislate incompatibly with Convention rights under the devolution Acts. Structural theory explains why rights protection is more uniform in the UK.\n• FEDERALISM AS A SHIELD FOR DISCRIMINATION: historically, states' rights were used to defend segregation in the South, and federal intervention required the Fourteenth Amendment, Supreme Court rulings and federal legislation such as the Civil Rights Act 1964 and the Voting Rights Act 1965. After Shelby County v Holder (2013) ended the requirement for certain states to obtain federal approval before changing voting rules, several states introduced stricter voting laws.\n• DIVERGENCE UNDER DEVOLUTION: devolution also allows some variation in the UK — same-sex marriage was introduced in England and Wales in 2013, in Scotland in 2014, and in Northern Ireland only in 2020 after Westminster legislated. Scotland and Wales lowered the voting age to 16 for devolved elections. Structural theory shows that both systems allow territorial variation, though far less in the UK than in the US.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three ways that structural theory could be used to study the protection of rights at different levels of government in the US and UK. Relevant points not listed above should be credited. Each point should explain the theory's relevance and analyse it with examples from BOTH the US and the UK; a description of one country only, or of the institutions with no link to the theory, cannot reach the top of the range. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways that cultural theory could be used to study debates over civil rights in the US and UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in their explanations and analysis, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• CULTURAL THEORY: the cultural approach focuses on the shared ideas, values, beliefs and historical experiences of a society or of groups within it, and argues that these shape how individuals act and what they regard as legitimate. It links present-day behaviour to each country's history — the American revolutionary, republican tradition and distrust of concentrated power, against the UK's gradual, evolutionary development, monarchy and parliamentary tradition.\n• CULTURE WARS: in the US, rights issues such as abortion, gun ownership, affirmative action and transgender rights are central to deep cultural divisions, reinforced by religion — evangelical Christians form a core part of the Republican coalition. In the UK, religion plays a smaller role in politics, and issues such as abortion have been less politically divisive since the Abortion Act 1967. Cultural theory explains why rights are more fiercely contested in the US.\n• RIGHTS AS CONSTITUTIONAL IDENTITY: many Americans see rights such as the Second Amendment right to bear arms as part of their national identity and heritage, so any restriction is opposed as an attack on the Constitution. The UK has no comparable tradition of gun ownership, and strict gun controls were introduced after the Dunblane massacre in 1996 with broad public support.\n• MORAL ISSUES AND PARLIAMENTARY TRADITION: in the UK, moral questions are often decided by free votes in Parliament — the Commons approved the assisted dying bill (the Terminally Ill Adults (End of Life) Bill) on a free vote in 2025. In the US, such questions often become constitutional disputes decided by the Supreme Court. Cultural theory explains the different expectations about who should decide contested moral questions.\n• CONTESTED RIGHTS IN THE UK: rights debates have grown more prominent in the UK — over the Human Rights Act and the ECHR, especially in relation to immigration, and over the balance between transgender rights and sex-based rights after the For Women Scotland ruling (2025). Cultural theory can explain these debates as reflecting changing values and identities, and suggests the UK is becoming more like the US, though still less polarised.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three ways that cultural theory could be used to study debates over civil rights in the US and UK. Relevant points not listed above should be credited. Each point should explain the theory's relevance and analyse it with examples from BOTH the US and the UK; a description of one country only, or of the institutions with no link to the theory, cannot reach the top of the range. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways that rational theory could be used to study attempts by governments to change rights protections in the US and UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in their explanations and analysis, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• RATIONAL THEORY: the rational (rational choice) approach focuses on the individual. It assumes political actors are self-interested and make logical, calculated choices to maximise the benefits and minimise the costs to themselves or their cause — for example re-election, promotion, policy success or party advantage. Differences between the two countries are explained by the different incentives that actors face.\n• WHY UK GOVERNMENTS THREATEN BUT RARELY REPEAL: rational theory suggests governments weigh the political benefits and costs of change. Conservative governments repeatedly promised to replace the Human Rights Act, and the Bill of Rights Bill was introduced in 2022, but it was dropped in 2023 because the costs — legal complexity, opposition in Parliament and the devolved nations, and the continuing obligation of the ECHR — outweighed the benefits.\n• GOVERNMENTS TEST THE LIMITS OF RIGHTS: UK governments with majorities have been willing to pass legislation that challenges Convention rights when this is electorally popular — the Illegal Migration Act 2023 was introduced with a statement under s.19(1)(b) HRA that the minister could not confirm it was compatible, and the Safety of Rwanda Act 2024 disapplied parts of the HRA. Rational theory explains this as governments seeking electoral advantage on immigration.\n• US STATE LEGISLATURES PURSUE PARTY ADVANTAGE: in the US, legislatures cannot repeal constitutional rights, but they rationally use their powers to shape rights in practice. After Shelby County v Holder (2013), several states introduced stricter voter ID and registration rules, which critics argued were designed to benefit one party. After Dobbs (2022), states controlled by each party moved abortion law in opposite directions to satisfy their voters.\n• PRESIDENTS ACT THROUGH EXECUTIVE POWER: because Presidents cannot change the constitution, they rationally use executive orders and appointments to shape rights policy — Trump's nominations of three justices in his first term produced the majority that decided Dobbs. In the UK, the government can change rights by legislation, so there is less need to act through the courts or executive orders.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three ways that rational theory could be used to study attempts by governments to change rights protections in the US and UK. Relevant points not listed above should be credited. Each point should explain the theory's relevance and analyse it with examples from BOTH the US and the UK; a description of one country only, or of the institutions with no link to the theory, cannot reach the top of the range. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: '"Civil rights are better protected in the USA than the UK." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `USA BETTER:\n• Entrenchment places rights beyond ordinary legislative repeal; no Commons-style majority could remove them.\n• Judicially enforceable against BOTH federal and state government.\n• Courts have delivered change elected branches would not — Brown, Obergefell, Bostock.\n• The Fourteenth Amendment provides a standard against which any law can be measured.\n• Strong First Amendment protection of speech, assembly and religion.\n\nUK BETTER:\n• Rights are broadly UNIFORM — the HRA applies UK-wide and the Equality Act 2010 across Great Britain (Northern Ireland has its own equality law) — whereas US rights vary radically by state, particularly after Dobbs.\n• Continuing ECHR obligations provide an external backstop independent of the HRA.\n• Legislated rights carry democratic legitimacy and reach PRIVATE discrimination in employment, housing and services.\n• UK rights do not depend on the composition of a court; despite theoretical fragility, no government has repealed them.\n• The UK has avoided the politicisation of judicial appointments that Dobbs and the Garland/Barrett episode illustrate.\n\nGuidance for L5: the decisive formulation is that the USA protects rights better against LEGISLATURES and the UK protects them better against COURTS — and which matters more depends on which institution is currently more hostile to the right in question. Dobbs and Shelby County show the US answer changed within a decade. That reaches L5.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },

      { q: '"Entrenchment is the best protection for civil rights." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `ENTRENCHMENT IS BEST:\n• It places rights beyond the reach of ordinary majorities, protecting minorities against hostile legislatures — exactly what rights are for.\n• It makes rights judicially enforceable against both levels of government.\n• It enabled Brown, Obergefell and Bostock, delivering change elected branches would not.\n• UK rights are theoretically repealable by simple majority, and replacement of the HRA has been repeatedly proposed.\n• Without it, rights depend entirely on political restraint.\n\nENTRENCHMENT IS NOT ENOUGH / NOT BEST:\n• Entrenched rights depend on INTERPRETATION, so they can be withdrawn by a changed Court — Dobbs reversed a fifty-year precedent with no change to the text.\n• It transfers the definition of rights from elected legislatures to nine appointments, making the Senate and vacancy timing the real determinants.\n• The Reconstruction amendments lay dormant for nearly a century — text without institutional will achieves nothing.\n• Legislated rights reach PRIVATE discrimination that constitutional rulings cannot, and carry democratic legitimacy.\n• UK rights have proved stable in practice despite theoretical fragility, backed by continuing ECHR obligations.\n• Entrenchment makes rights harder to EXTEND as well as harder to remove.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest conclusion is that entrenchment RELOCATES vulnerability rather than removing it — from electoral majorities to judicial appointments — and that the choice is therefore about which risk is preferable, not about which is safe.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },

      { q: '"Federalism undermines civil rights in the USA." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `UNDERMINES:\n• States control elections, policing, education and — since Dobbs — abortion regulation, so entitlements vary radically by residence, which sits uncomfortably with national citizenship.\n• Federalism historically SHIELDED segregation; states' rights arguments resisted federal intervention throughout the Jim Crow era, requiring federal troops at Little Rock.\n• Shelby County restored state control over voting rules, after which several states introduced restrictions.\n• Poorer states with weaker rights regimes disadvantage their most vulnerable residents.\n• It fragments enforcement and creates a patchwork of protection.\n\nDOES NOT UNDERMINE / can advance rights:\n• Federalism permits ADVANCE as well as retreat — several states recognised same-sex marriage before Obergefell, and many legislate protections exceeding the federal floor.\n• States function as policy laboratories, and successful state reforms spread.\n• Federal constitutional rights and the incorporation doctrine set a floor no state may fall below.\n• The Civil Rights Act 1964 and Voting Rights Act 1965 show federal power overriding state resistance when Congress acts.\n• Local control can be more responsive to genuinely different local circumstances.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest analysis notes that federalism produces BOTH the strongest and the weakest rights regimes in the same country, so the question is whether a floor plus variation is preferable to uniformity — and the UK comparison, where rights law is reserved, provides the counterfactual.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },

      { q: '"The UK should adopt an entrenched bill of rights." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `SHOULD ADOPT:\n• Current rights rest on ordinary statute repealable by a simple Commons majority, with replacement of the HRA repeatedly proposed — protection depends on political restraint rather than legal guarantee.\n• Entrenchment would protect minorities against hostile majorities, which is precisely what rights are for.\n• It would give courts a real remedy rather than a declaration of incompatibility that leaves the offending statute in force.\n• Rights have been curtailed by counter-terrorism legislation and restrictions on protest with no effective legal check.\n• It would bring the UK into line with almost all comparable democracies.\n\nSHOULD NOT ADOPT:\n• Entrenchment is INCOMPATIBLE with parliamentary sovereignty as traditionally understood — no Parliament can bind its successors, so it requires a prior constitutional transformation, not a reform.\n• It transfers the final word on contested questions from elected politicians to appointed judges; the US experience shows this politicises appointments severely.\n• Dobbs shows entrenched rights can still be withdrawn by reinterpretation, so the security gained is less than it appears.\n• UK rights have proved STABLE in practice despite theoretical fragility, and ECHR membership provides an external backstop.\n• Legislated rights reach private discrimination and carry democratic legitimacy.\n\nGuidance for L5: must engage the SOVEREIGNTY OBJECTION directly — this is not merely a policy choice but a constitutional impossibility within the current framework. An answer that ignores it caps at L4.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4.` },
    ]
  },

  /* ══════════════════════ PAPER 3 — POLITICAL IDEAS ══════════════════════ */

  '3.1': {
    name: 'Liberalism',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three core ideas of liberalism.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Individualism — the individual is the central unit of moral and political value, and society exists to serve individuals rather than the reverse. Analysis: distinguish EGOISTICAL individualism (classical, self-reliant) from DEVELOPMENTAL individualism (Mill, Green — potential to be realised).\n• Freedom — the supreme political value, but contested between NEGATIVE freedom (absence of constraint) and POSITIVE freedom (capacity to act and develop). Analysis: this ambiguity is the fault line between classical and modern liberalism.\n• Rationalism — human beings are capable of reason and can identify their own interests better than any authority. Analysis: the premise from which consent, rights, toleration and democracy all follow.\n• Equality — foundational and legal equality, and equality of OPPORTUNITY, but not of outcome.\n• The limited, constitutional state — government by consent, constrained by law and dispersed through checks and balances.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires the internal contestation, not just definitions.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three liberal views on the state.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Necessary but dangerous — Locke argued that in the state of nature rights exist but are insecure for want of an impartial judge, so a state is needed; but because it holds coercive power it is the chief threat to liberty.\n• Government by consent and fiduciary power — Locke held that government holds authority IN TRUST for the people, who may withdraw it if the trust is broken.\n• Constitutionalism — because power corrupts, liberals insist on limited government, the rule of law, separation of powers, checks and balances and entrenched rights.\n• The MINIMAL state (classical) — state action is inherently a restriction of liberty, so the state should be confined to protecting person, property and contract.\n• The ENABLING state (modern) — Green argued that poverty, ignorance and ill health are themselves constraints, so state provision EXPANDS freedom.\n\nAnalysis expected: both strands want the state LIMITED, accountable and constitutionally constrained; they disagree about its SIZE.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three differences between classical and modern liberalism.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Concept of freedom — classical liberals hold to NEGATIVE freedom (absence of interference), so freedom is maximised by minimising state action; modern liberals, following T.H. Green, argue that poverty, ignorance and ill health are themselves constraints, so genuine freedom requires the CAPACITY to act.\n• Role of the state — a minimal "night-watchman" state versus an ENABLING state providing education, healthcare and welfare.\n• The economy — laissez-faire and free markets as expressions of individual choice, versus managed capitalism, Keynesian demand management and regulation on the view that unregulated markets produce insecurity that itself restricts freedom.\n• Equality — formal and legal equality versus a substantive equality of opportunity requiring redistribution, most systematically in Rawls's difference principle.\n\nAnalysis expected: the CONTINUITY matters — both treat individual freedom as the supreme value; they disagree about means, not ends. Mill is the bridge.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three liberal thinkers\' views on freedom.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• LOCKE — freedom consists in the secure enjoyment of natural rights to life, liberty and property, which exist prior to government. Analysis: establishes liberalism's foundational move, placing the individual before the state; but says little about the social conditions in which rights can actually be exercised.\n• MILL — the harm principle: liberty may be restricted only to prevent harm to others, never for a person's own good. Analysis: the most influential liberal formulation, ruling out state paternalism and the legal enforcement of morality; but "harm" is contested, and Mill qualified the principle for children and those he regarded as not yet self-governing.\n• GREEN — positive freedom: freedom is the actual capacity for self-development, so poverty and ignorance are constraints as real as legal prohibition. Analysis: transformed liberalism by making an active state an instrument of freedom; but Berlin warned that positive freedom permits others to define a person's "real" interests and coerce them accordingly.\n• RAWLS — equal basic liberties plus the difference principle.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires the critical assessment of each, not just exposition.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three liberal views on human nature.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• RATIONALITY — human beings are capable of reason and can therefore identify their own interests better than any authority. Analysis: the premise from which consent, rights, toleration, free expression and democracy all follow, and the sharpest contrast with conservative human imperfection. It is also liberalism's most vulnerable claim, given evidence on systematic irrationality.\n• Self-interest and self-reliance (egoistical individualism) — classical liberals treat individuals as motivated primarily by their own interests, which the market channels productively.\n• Developmental potential — Mill regarded human beings as PROGRESSIVE beings capable of growth, and Green built the enabling state on the claim that people require certain conditions to develop. Analysis: this shift within liberalism is what permits an active state.\n• Foundational equality — all individuals are of equal moral worth. Analysis: Wollstonecraft showed the radical potential of this premise, since if rights derive from rationality and women are equally rational, excluding them is inconsistent with liberalism's own foundations.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires analysis of CONSEQUENCES.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three liberal views on the economy.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Classical laissez-faire — the free market is the economic expression of individual liberty, allowing people to dispose of their property and labour as they choose; competition allocates resources efficiently without central direction, and state intervention distorts incentives. Private property is both a natural right and a guarantee of independence from government.\n• Modern managed capitalism — modern liberals accept the market's efficiency but reject laissez-faire, arguing that unregulated markets produce booms, slumps, monopolies and insecurity that themselves restrict freedom. Drawing on Keynes, they support demand management, regulation and welfare funded through progressive taxation.\n• Rawls's difference principle — market inequalities are legitimate only where they work to the greatest benefit of the least advantaged.\n• Neo-liberalism — a twentieth-century revival of classical liberal economics: deregulation, privatisation and a minimal state. Note it belongs to the CLASSICAL lineage.\n\nAnalysis expected: all liberals accept private property and the market; the disagreement is about how far the market can be left to itself.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'EXTRACTS (written for practice):\n\nExtract 1 — a summary of Locke\'s argument in the Second Treatise of Government (1689), written for this question\n"Locke argues that individuals in the state of nature already possess natural rights to life, liberty and property, which they can know through reason and the law of nature. The state of nature is not a war of all against all, but it is inconvenient: there is no settled law, no impartial judge and no power to enforce judgements, so each person must judge their own case. People therefore consent to form a political society and a government, which holds its power only in trust. Its purpose is limited: the chief end of men uniting into commonwealths is "the preservation of their property", by which Locke means their lives, liberties and estates. A government that acts arbitrarily, or takes property without consent, breaks that trust and may rightfully be resisted by the people."\n\nExtract 2 — a summary of Rawls\'s argument in A Theory of Justice (1971), written for this question\n"Rawls asks which principles of justice rational people would choose in an "original position", behind a veil of ignorance that hides their class, talents, sex and beliefs about the good life. He argues they would choose two principles. First, each person should have an equal right to the most extensive basic liberties compatible with the same liberties for all. Second, social and economic inequalities are acceptable only if they are attached to positions open to all under fair equality of opportunity, and work to the greatest benefit of the least advantaged — the difference principle. The first principle takes priority over the second. The basic structure of society, including its constitution, economy and tax system, must therefore be arranged by the state so that it meets these principles of justice."\n\nAnalyse, evaluate and compare the arguments made in the above extracts on the role of the state. In your answer you should refer to the thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in analysing, evaluating and comparing the arguments made in the extracts on the role of the state, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• SOCIAL CONTRACT AND CONSENT: both extracts ground the legitimacy of the state in an agreement between free, equal and rational individuals. Locke presents consent as something people give (expressly or tacitly) on leaving the state of nature; Rawls's contract is openly hypothetical, a thought experiment about what people would choose. Students may evaluate Hume's objection that Lockean tacit consent is a fiction, against the communitarian objection that Rawls's choosers behind the veil are abstract "unencumbered selves".\n• PURPOSE OF THE STATE: for Locke the state exists to protect rights that exist before it, so its role is essentially protective and limited, the root of the classical liberal minimal state. For Rawls the state must shape the whole basic structure so that it is fair, which justifies progressive taxation, welfare and public education — the modern liberal enabling state. Students may link Rawls to Green's positive freedom and Locke to negative freedom.\n• PROPERTY: Locke treats property as a natural right acquired by mixing one's labour with the world, so taxation requires the consent of the people or their representatives. Rawls argues that the distribution of natural talents and social starting points is "arbitrary from a moral point of view", so market rewards are not a pre-political entitlement but something institutions must regulate. Nozick's entitlement theory, built on Lockean premises, is the classic reply to Rawls.\n• LIMITS ON POWER: the extracts agree that the state is necessary but must be constrained. Locke's trust and right of resistance, and Rawls's priority of liberty (basic liberties cannot be traded for economic gain) and his defence of civil disobedience, both reflect liberal constitutionalism, the rule of law and government by consent.\n• EQUALITY: Locke assumes foundational equality — no one is naturally subordinate to another in the state of nature — but draws formal conclusions from it. Rawls extends the same premise to fair equality of opportunity and the difference principle, showing how modern liberalism radicalises a shared starting point.\n• STATUS AND CONTEXT: Locke wrote in the context of the Exclusion Crisis and the Glorious Revolution (1688), to justify limits on royal power; Rawls wrote in the post-war welfare-state and US civil-rights era as an academic philosopher. Both are works of normative theory rather than descriptions of real states.\n• OTHER THINKERS: Mill's harm principle limits state and social power over self-regarding conduct; Friedan and Wollstonecraft show liberals looking to law and state action to widen opportunity; conservatives such as Hobbes and Nozick, and socialists who see the liberal state as protecting class interests, offer useful contrasts.\n\nJudgement: Rawls is more convincing about the state's role in a modern society because formal rights mean little without the resources to use them, but Locke is more convincing as a safeguard against state coercion, and Rawls's own priority of liberty accepts that point. The best answers conclude that the extracts share liberal premises (consent, limited and constitutional government, individual rights) and divide over the SCOPE of the state because they understand freedom and fairness differently.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and comparing the arguments in the extracts and weighing which are more convincing; evaluate their status (author, type of publication, purpose, date) where relevant — the extracts are summaries written for this question, so status means the original work and its context. Answers that do not focus on the arguments in the extracts cannot go above Level 2. Answers that make no reference to the thinkers that the student has studied cannot go above Level 2. Analysis and evaluation of thinkers other than Locke and Rawls may be credited where it is clearly focused on the extracts and the arguments within them. The strongest answers compare the extracts point by point rather than summarising each in turn.` },

      { q: 'EXTRACTS (written for practice):\n\nExtract 1 — a summary of Wollstonecraft\'s argument in A Vindication of the Rights of Woman (1792), written for this question\n"Wollstonecraft argues that reason is the capacity that distinguishes human beings from animals, and that it was given to women as well as to men. Because virtue depends on the exercise of reason, there cannot be one standard of virtue for men and another for women. The weakness, vanity and dependence that writers such as Rousseau treated as natural to women are, she argues, the product of a faulty education and of a society that trains girls to please men rather than to think for themselves. If women were educated properly, alongside men, and allowed to become rational and independent, they would be better citizens, wives and mothers. Her aim is not to reverse the hierarchy between the sexes: "I do not wish them to have power over men; but over themselves.""\n\nExtract 2 — a summary of Mill\'s argument in On Liberty (1859), written for this question\n"Mill argues that human beings are progressive beings whose well-being lies in developing their individuality. The human faculties of perception, judgement, discrimination and moral preference are exercised only in making choices, so a person who simply lets custom choose their plan of life never develops them. Human nature, he writes, is not a machine built to a model, but "a tree, which requires to grow and develop itself on all sides". Mill therefore regards the despotism of custom and the pressure of public opinion as serious threats, because they stunt individuality and produce conformity. Diversity and even eccentricity benefit everyone, since new truths and better ways of living come from experiments in living. This capacity for self-development, not merely self-interest, is why freedom matters."\n\nAnalyse, evaluate and compare the arguments made in the above extracts on human nature. In your answer you should refer to the thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in analysing, evaluating and comparing the arguments made in the extracts on human nature, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• RATIONALITY: both extracts rest on the core liberal claim that human beings are rational and able to direct their own lives. For Wollstonecraft reason is a God-given capacity shared equally by both sexes; for Mill it is exercised and strengthened through choice. Students may compare this optimism with the conservative view of human imperfection (Hobbes, Burke, Oakeshott).\n• NATURE VERSUS CONDITIONING: both argue that what passes for "human nature" is largely shaped by education, custom and social expectation. Wollstonecraft says women's apparent inferiority is manufactured by their upbringing; Mill says conformity to custom stunts people. Mill made the same argument about women in The Subjection of Women (1869), so the extracts are closely aligned.\n• DEVELOPMENTAL INDIVIDUALISM: Mill's image of a growing tree expresses developmental rather than egoistical individualism — people are capable of growth, not just of pursuing fixed interests. Wollstonecraft's goal of independence and self-mastery ("power ... over themselves") is a similar ideal of autonomy. Students may note this is the view of human nature on which modern liberals later built the enabling state.\n• THE OBSTACLE AND THE REMEDY: for Wollstonecraft the main obstacle is an education system and culture designed to keep women dependent, so the remedy is educational reform (she proposed national co-educational day schools). For Mill the obstacle is social pressure, so the remedy is freedom of thought, expression and lifestyle, protected by the harm principle. Wollstonecraft's remedy implies a more active role for public institutions than Mill's extract suggests.\n• LIMITS AND CRITICISMS: both views may be too optimistic about reason. Mill applied his principle only to "civilised" adults, excluding children and societies he regarded as backward; Wollstonecraft's argument is framed around middle-class women and still values women partly as wives and mothers, which later feminists criticised. Conservatives argue both underestimate the need for tradition and authority to restrain human beings.\n• STATUS AND CONTEXT: Wollstonecraft wrote in 1792, in the debates around the French Revolution and in reply to Rousseau's Emile and to proposals that excluded girls from national education; Mill wrote in 1859 against Victorian social conformity, influenced by Harriet Taylor. Both are polemical works written to persuade.\n• OTHER THINKERS: Locke's view of reason and natural rights; Friedan's argument that post-war culture confined women's potential; Rawls's rational choosers in the original position.\n\nJudgement: The extracts largely agree: both see human beings as rational and capable of development, and both blame custom and education for apparent failings. Mill's account is broader and explains why liberty matters for everyone; Wollstonecraft's is more radical in its context, because it applies liberal premises to a group liberals of her day excluded. The best answers weigh whether this shared optimism about reason is convincing against conservative and socialist accounts of human nature.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and comparing the arguments in the extracts and weighing which are more convincing; evaluate their status (author, type of publication, purpose, date) where relevant — the extracts are summaries written for this question, so status means the original work and its context. Answers that do not focus on the arguments in the extracts cannot go above Level 2. Answers that make no reference to the thinkers that the student has studied cannot go above Level 2. Analysis and evaluation of thinkers other than Wollstonecraft and Mill may be credited where it is clearly focused on the extracts and the arguments within them. The strongest answers compare the extracts point by point rather than summarising each in turn.` },

      { q: 'EXTRACTS (written for practice):\n\nExtract 1 — a summary of Friedan\'s argument in The Feminine Mystique (1963), written for this question\n"Friedan argues that post-war America trapped educated, middle-class women in an idealised image of fulfilment through housework, marriage and motherhood, which she calls the feminine mystique. Women\'s magazines, advertisers, educators and popular psychology told women that careers and serious study were unfeminine, and many left college early to marry. The result was a widespread, unspoken dissatisfaction that Friedan called "the problem that has no name". Women were formally equal citizens, yet in practice they were denied the chance to develop their abilities and identities through meaningful work. Friedan\'s remedy is for women to pursue education and careers outside the home, supported by changes in attitudes, education and employment practices, so that they have the same opportunities as men to realise their potential."\n\nExtract 2 — a summary of Rawls\'s argument in A Theory of Justice (1971), written for this question\n"Rawls argues that the natural talents people are born with, and the social circumstances they are born into, are "arbitrary from a moral point of view": no one deserves their starting place. Formal equality, with careers legally open to talents, is therefore not enough. Justice requires fair equality of opportunity: people with similar abilities and willingness to use them should have similar life chances, whatever the social class of their family, which requires measures such as good education available to all. Beyond this, the difference principle permits inequalities of income and wealth only if they benefit the least advantaged, for example where higher rewards encourage work that improves everyone\'s prospects. Rawls does not seek equality of outcome, but a society where life chances are not settled by the accident of birth."\n\nAnalyse, evaluate and compare the arguments made in the above extracts on equality. In your answer you should refer to the thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in analysing, evaluating and comparing the arguments made in the extracts on equality, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• FORMAL VERSUS SUBSTANTIVE EQUALITY: both extracts argue that legal or formal equality is not enough. Friedan shows women who were equal citizens on paper but excluded in practice; Rawls argues that careers open to talents still leave life chances determined by birth. Both reflect the modern liberal shift towards a substantive equality of opportunity.\n• SOURCE OF INEQUALITY: Friedan locates inequality in culture, social expectations and the private sphere of the family; Rawls locates it in the "basic structure" of society and the distribution of social and natural advantages. Students may note the feminist criticism (e.g. Susan Okin) that Rawls's early theory said little about justice within the family — exactly the sphere Friedan exposes.\n• REMEDIES: Friedan's remedy is liberal feminist — changing attitudes, opening education and employment, and legal reform against sex discrimination (she co-founded the National Organization for Women in 1966). Rawls's remedy is institutional: public education, redistribution through taxation and welfare, and the difference principle. Rawls therefore justifies a more extensive state role.\n• OPPORTUNITY, NOT OUTCOME: both remain liberal in rejecting equality of outcome. Friedan wants women to compete on equal terms, not a collective transformation of society; Rawls accepts inequalities that benefit the least advantaged. Socialists would argue both leave the structures of capitalism, and radical feminists the structures of patriarchy, largely intact.\n• CRITICISMS: bell hooks criticised Friedan for generalising from the experience of white, middle-class, college-educated women, ignoring women who already worked out of economic necessity. Nozick argued that Rawls's "patterned" principle violates property rights and self-ownership; classical liberals and the New Right say it justifies too much state intervention.\n• STATUS AND CONTEXT: The Feminine Mystique (1963) was a best-selling work of social criticism drawing on interviews and surveys, and helped spark second-wave feminism; A Theory of Justice (1971) is an academic work of political philosophy, written against the background of the post-war welfare state and the US civil-rights movement.\n• OTHER THINKERS: Wollstonecraft's earlier argument that women's inferiority is a product of education; Locke's foundational equality; Mill's The Subjection of Women.\n\nJudgement: The extracts agree that liberal equality must mean real opportunity, not just equal legal status. Rawls offers the more complete theory, because he explains why unequal starting points are unjust and what the state must do about them; Friedan is more convincing in showing that culture and the family, not just law and markets, block equality — a gap in Rawls's own account. The best answers use each extract to expose the other's limits.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and comparing the arguments in the extracts and weighing which are more convincing; evaluate their status (author, type of publication, purpose, date) where relevant — the extracts are summaries written for this question, so status means the original work and its context. Answers that do not focus on the arguments in the extracts cannot go above Level 2. Answers that make no reference to the thinkers that the student has studied cannot go above Level 2. Analysis and evaluation of thinkers other than Friedan and Rawls may be credited where it is clearly focused on the extracts and the arguments within them. The strongest answers compare the extracts point by point rather than summarising each in turn.` },

      { q: 'EXTRACTS (written for practice):\n\nExtract 1 — a summary of Locke\'s argument in the Second Treatise of Government (1689), written for this question\n"Locke argues that people are naturally in a state of perfect freedom to order their actions and dispose of their possessions as they think fit, within the bounds of the law of nature. That law, which can be known through reason, forbids anyone to harm another in their life, health, liberty or possessions. Freedom is therefore not licence to do whatever one likes: it is freedom under law. Locke insists that "where there is no law, there is no freedom", because law, properly understood, preserves and enlarges freedom by protecting people from the arbitrary will of others. When people enter civil society they give up only the power to judge and punish breaches of natural law for themselves. They keep their natural rights, and a government that invades them loses its authority."\n\nExtract 2 — a summary of Mill\'s argument in On Liberty (1859), written for this question\n"Mill argues that the only purpose for which power can rightfully be exercised over any member of a civilised community, against their will, is to prevent harm to others. A person\'s own good, physical or moral, is not a sufficient reason. In conduct that concerns only themselves, the individual\'s independence is absolute: "Over himself, over his own body and mind, the individual is sovereign." Mill is as worried about the tyranny of the majority, exercised through social opinion and custom, as about the tyranny of governments. He defends almost complete liberty of thought and discussion, since a silenced opinion may be true, partly true, or keep the truth from becoming dead dogma. Mill grounds freedom not on abstract natural rights but on utility, understood in terms of the permanent interests of people as progressive beings."\n\nAnalyse, evaluate and compare the arguments made in the above extracts on freedom. In your answer you should refer to the thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in analysing, evaluating and comparing the arguments made in the extracts on freedom, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• FOUNDATIONS OF FREEDOM: Locke grounds freedom in natural rights under a God-given law of nature; Mill explicitly declines to rely on abstract right and grounds liberty in utility — the long-term interests of progressive beings. Students may evaluate which foundation is stronger: natural rights are hard to prove, while a utilitarian case could in principle justify restricting liberty if that maximised welfare.\n• NEGATIVE FREEDOM: both treat freedom primarily as the absence of interference by others, the classical liberal conception, rather than Green's positive freedom as the capacity to act. Both allow restriction only to protect others — Locke's law of nature forbids harming others' life, health, liberty or possessions; Mill's harm principle limits interference to preventing harm to others.\n• THE MAIN THREAT: for Locke the main threat is arbitrary government, so the remedy is consent, the rule of law and a right of resistance. For Mill the threat also comes from society — the tyranny of the majority through opinion and custom — so constitutional government alone is not enough. This is a significant development of the liberal view of freedom.\n• LAW AND FREEDOM: Locke sees law as enlarging freedom, since it protects people from others' arbitrary will; Mill sees law as coercion that must be justified by harm to others. Students may note that both accept law is necessary, but Mill sets a stricter test for when it is legitimate, ruling out legal paternalism and the enforcement of morality.\n• LIMITS AND CRITICISMS: "harm" is contested and self-regarding actions often affect others; Mill limited his principle to "civilised" adults. Locke's natural rights included property, and critics (including socialists) argue this protects existing inequalities. Conservatives such as Burke and Oakeshott say freedom depends on inherited institutions and tradition, not abstract principles.\n• STATUS AND CONTEXT: Locke wrote around the Glorious Revolution to justify limited monarchy; Mill wrote in 1859, when the main danger he saw was Victorian social conformity and emerging mass democracy. Both are works of political philosophy written to persuade.\n• OTHER THINKERS: Rawls's priority of equal basic liberties; Friedan's argument that social expectations restrict women's freedom (supporting Mill's concern about social tyranny); Hobbes's contrasting view that liberty lies in "the silence of the law"; Nozick's use of Lockean rights.\n\nJudgement: The extracts share a negative conception of freedom and a principle that people may be restrained only to protect others. Mill is more convincing for modern societies because he recognises that social pressure, not just the state, can crush freedom, and he does not depend on contested natural-law premises; Locke is more convincing in giving freedom a firm basis that cannot be traded away for the general good. The best answers weigh the strengths of rights-based and utilitarian defences of liberty.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and comparing the arguments in the extracts and weighing which are more convincing; evaluate their status (author, type of publication, purpose, date) where relevant — the extracts are summaries written for this question, so status means the original work and its context. Answers that do not focus on the arguments in the extracts cannot go above Level 2. Answers that make no reference to the thinkers that the student has studied cannot go above Level 2. Analysis and evaluation of thinkers other than Locke and Mill may be credited where it is clearly focused on the extracts and the arguments within them. The strongest answers compare the extracts point by point rather than summarising each in turn.` },
    ]
  },

  '3.2': {
    name: 'Conservatism',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three core ideas of conservatism.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Human imperfection — people are psychologically limited (needing security and order), morally flawed (capable of selfishness and cruelty) and intellectually limited (reason cannot grasp a world too complex to redesign). Analysis: this last limb generates conservative caution about radical reform.\n• Tradition — established customs and institutions embody ACCUMULATED PRACTICAL WISDOM tested by generations, more reliable than the reasoning of any single generation; Burke's partnership between the living, the dead and those yet to be born.\n• Organic society — a living whole that has evolved, whose parts are interdependent, naturally hierarchical but with hierarchy carrying OBLIGATION. Burke's "little platoons".\n• Pragmatism — judging policies by what works rather than by conformity to doctrine.\n• Change to conserve — cautious reform is the CONDITION of preservation, not its opposite.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires analysis of how the ideas connect.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three conservative views on human nature.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• PSYCHOLOGICAL imperfection — human beings are needy, restless and fearful of isolation and instability, so they require security and order. Hobbes's state of nature is the extreme statement: without a common power, life would be one of continual insecurity.\n• MORAL imperfection — people are capable of selfishness, cruelty and violence, which is why law, authority and punishment are necessary rather than optional.\n• INTELLECTUAL imperfection — reason is limited and the social world too complex to be fully understood, let alone redesigned. Analysis: this is the philosophical basis of conservative scepticism about grand schemes, and of Oakeshott's anti-rationalism.\n• The New Right exception — Rand's rational, self-creating egoist is close to the opposite view, which is why her conservative credentials are contested.\n\nAnalysis expected: human imperfection is the SHARPEST contrast with liberalism's rational individual and socialism's malleable, cooperative human nature.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three conservative views on society.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• ORGANIC rather than mechanical — society is a living whole that has evolved naturally, not a machine that can be dismantled and redesigned. Its parts are interdependent, so change to one institution has unpredictable effects on others. Analysis: the principal argument against radical reform.\n• HIERARCHICAL — inequality of position, wealth and authority is natural and inevitable, arising from differences in ability and function; attempts to abolish hierarchy are futile and destructive of order.\n• Bound by OBLIGATION — hierarchy carries duty. Those with wealth and position owe an obligation to those below them, which is the basis of paternalism and of Disraeli's One Nation, and which distinguishes conservative hierarchy from mere privilege.\n• Rooted in SMALL INSTITUTIONS — Burke's "little platoons" (family, parish, locality, voluntary association) are where loyalty and moral habit are acquired.\n\nAnalysis expected: contrast with liberal society as a collection of individuals and socialist society as structured by class conflict.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three differences between One Nation conservatism and the New Right.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• The state and welfare — One Nation accepts intervention and the welfare state out of paternalistic obligation and to prevent social division becoming dangerous (Disraeli's "two nations"); the New Right's neo-liberal strand regards welfare dependency as undermining self-reliance and would roll the state back economically.\n• The economy — mixed economy and managed capitalism versus free markets, privatisation, deregulation and lower direct taxation.\n• Philosophical basis — One Nation rests on organic society, obligation and Burkean pragmatism; the neo-liberal strand rests on abstract rational individualism (Rand, Nozick), which Burke and Oakeshott would identify as precisely the rationalism they warned against.\n• Attitude to change — One Nation is cautious and evolutionary; Thatcherism was radical and disruptive of established institutions and communities.\n\nAnalysis expected: the New Right's conservative credentials are genuinely contested, and the internal tension between its neo-liberal and neo-conservative strands is examinable.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three conservative thinkers\' views on the state.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• HOBBES — because human beings are needy, restless and in competition, individuals rationally contract to submit to an ABSOLUTE SOVEREIGN whose authority must be undivided; divided sovereignty invites civil war. Analysis: establishes that order precedes liberty, but reasons from an abstract social contract — the very method Burke and Oakeshott reject.\n• BURKE — authority rests on inherited tradition and an intergenerational partnership that individuals do not choose; the state should reform cautiously in order to conserve. Analysis: a wholly different foundation from Hobbes, reaching similar conclusions about the need for authority.\n• NOZICK — a MINIMAL or night-watchman state confined to protection against force, theft and fraud and the enforcement of contracts; redistributive taxation violates self-ownership by treating a person's labour as a resource for others. Analysis: the most radical position, and the hardest to reconcile with organic society and paternalism.\n• One Nation paternalism — the state should relieve distress to maintain cohesion.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires the tensions between them.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three conservative views on tradition and change.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Tradition as ACCUMULATED WISDOM — institutions that have survived have been tested by generations of experience, which is more reliable than the abstract reasoning of any single generation. Burke described society as a partnership between the living, the dead and those yet to be born, so the present generation are trustees rather than owners.\n• CHANGE TO CONSERVE — Burke's principle that a state without the means of some change is without the means of its conservation. Reform is the CONDITION of preservation, not its opposite, but should grow out of existing practice rather than from a blueprint.\n• OAKESHOTT'S ANTI-RATIONALISM — abstract theory cannot capture the complexity of social practice; rationalists recognise only technical knowledge and discard the practical knowledge acquired through experience that makes governing possible. Politics is navigating a boundless sea with no appointed destination.\n• The American/French contrast — Burke supported the American colonists because they defended established rights, and opposed the French because they destroyed an inheritance to build from theory.\n\nGuidance: the Burke test — whether change GROWS FROM existing practice — is the essential analytical point.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'EXTRACTS (written for practice):\n\nExtract 1 — a summary of Hobbes\'s argument in Leviathan (1651), written for this question\n"Hobbes argues that human beings are roughly equal in their strength and cunning and are driven by a restless desire for power, security and reputation. Without a common power to keep them in awe, they would live in a war of every man against every man, in which there would be no industry, no culture and no security, and the life of man would be "solitary, poor, nasty, brutish, and short". Rational people therefore agree to hand over their right of governing themselves to a sovereign — one person or an assembly — whose authority must be absolute and undivided, since divided power leads back to civil war. Subjects keep only the right to preserve their own lives. Whatever its faults, even a harsh government is preferable to the anarchy of the state of nature."\n\nExtract 2 — a summary of Nozick\'s argument in Anarchy, State, and Utopia (1974), written for this question\n"Nozick begins from the claim that individuals have rights, and there are things no person or group, including the state, may do to them without violating those rights. He argues that a minimal state, limited to protecting people against force, theft and fraud and to enforcing contracts, is justified, and could arise from a state of nature without violating anyone\'s rights. But any more extensive state is unjustified. A distribution of property is just if it arose from just acquisition and voluntary transfer, whatever pattern results, so the state has no right to redistribute. "Taxation of earnings from labor is on a par with forced labor", because it takes part of the time a person has worked. The minimal state treats individuals as ends, not means, and leaves them free to join voluntary communities of their choosing."\n\nAnalyse, evaluate and compare the arguments made in the above extracts on the role of the state. In your answer you should refer to the thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in analysing, evaluating and comparing the arguments made in the extracts on the role of the state, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• STARTING POINT: both extracts justify the state by imagining a state of nature. For Hobbes it is a war of all against all, so almost any state is better; for Nozick, following Locke, the state of nature is tolerable and a minimal state emerges from voluntary protective associations. The different starting points explain their opposite conclusions.\n• EXTENT OF AUTHORITY: Hobbes's sovereign is absolute and undivided; Nozick's minimal state is confined to protection and contract enforcement. Hobbes sees order as the precondition of every other good; Nozick sees an extensive state as itself a threat to rights. Students may present this as the tension between traditional conservatism's authority and order and the New Right's neo-liberal commitment to a rolled-back state.\n• RIGHTS: for Hobbes, subjects surrender their natural right to govern themselves, keeping only the right of self-preservation; for Nozick, rights are "side constraints" that the state may never cross, based on self-ownership. Hobbes subordinates rights to security; Nozick subordinates the state to rights.\n• PROPERTY AND REDISTRIBUTION: Hobbes held that property exists only under the sovereign, who distributes and protects it, so the state could in principle regulate it; Nozick's entitlement theory treats redistributive taxation as a violation of property rights. Students may link Nozick to Thatcher-era tax cuts and privatisation, and to his critique of Rawls.\n• HUMAN NATURE: Hobbes's pessimistic view of human beings as competitive and fearful demands strong authority; Nozick assumes rational individuals able to cooperate voluntarily. Students may evaluate which is more realistic — Hobbes's view is echoed by conservatives who stress law and order.\n• STATUS AND CONTEXT: Leviathan was written during and after the English Civil War, when the collapse of authority was a lived experience; Anarchy, State, and Utopia was a libertarian reply to Rawls's A Theory of Justice (1971), written in the era of the expanding welfare state.\n• OTHER THINKERS: Burke and Oakeshott on limited but authoritative government, pragmatism and the state as umpire; Rand's argument that the state's only proper role is to protect individual rights; Locke's influence on Nozick.\n\nJudgement: Hobbes is more convincing about why a state is needed at all, and his emphasis on order explains traditional conservative support for strong law-and-order government; Nozick is more convincing about the dangers of an unlimited state, but his minimal state struggles to explain public goods and the loyalty that holds a society together. The best answers conclude that the extracts reveal a genuine divide within conservatism between authority and liberty.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and comparing the arguments in the extracts and weighing which are more convincing; evaluate their status (author, type of publication, purpose, date) where relevant — the extracts are summaries written for this question, so status means the original work and its context. Answers that do not focus on the arguments in the extracts cannot go above Level 2. Answers that make no reference to the thinkers that the student has studied cannot go above Level 2. Analysis and evaluation of thinkers other than Hobbes and Nozick may be credited where it is clearly focused on the extracts and the arguments within them. The strongest answers compare the extracts point by point rather than summarising each in turn.` },

      { q: 'EXTRACTS (written for practice):\n\nExtract 1 — a summary of Burke\'s argument in Reflections on the Revolution in France (1790), written for this question\n"Burke argues that society is indeed a contract, but not a trade agreement for temporary interests that the parties can dissolve at will. It is a partnership in all science, art and virtue, and since its ends cannot be reached in one generation it becomes "a partnership not only between those who are living, but between those who are living, those who are dead, and those who are to be born". Individuals are formed by, and attached to, society through inherited institutions, customs and prejudices, and through the little platoon they belong to in society, which is the first link in the chain of public affection. Rights and liberties are an inheritance passed down, not the product of abstract reasoning. The French revolutionaries\' attempt to rebuild society from first principles would therefore end in disorder."\n\nExtract 2 — a summary of Rand\'s argument in The Virtue of Selfishness (1964), written for this question\n"Rand argues that only individuals exist, think and act; society is simply a number of individual people, not an entity with interests or rights of its own. Reason is each person\'s basic means of survival, so each must be free to act on their own judgement and keep what they produce. The moral purpose of each person\'s life is their own rational happiness: no one should sacrifice themselves to others or sacrifice others to themselves. A proper society is therefore one in which people deal with each other as traders, exchanging value for value by voluntary agreement to mutual benefit, and in which physical force is banned from social relationships. Altruism and collectivism, which subordinate the individual to the group, are the source of tyranny and the loss of freedom."\n\nAnalyse, evaluate and compare the arguments made in the above extracts on society. In your answer you should refer to the thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in analysing, evaluating and comparing the arguments made in the extracts on society, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• ORGANIC VERSUS ATOMISTIC SOCIETY: Burke presents society as an organic, intergenerational partnership that forms individuals; Rand treats society as nothing more than the individuals who compose it. This is the sharpest division between traditional conservatism and the New Right's neo-liberal strand, famously echoed in Thatcher's 1987 remark that there is "no such thing as society".\n• CONTRACT AND OBLIGATION: both use the language of contract, but Burke explicitly rejects a merely commercial contract and emphasises inherited duties to past and future generations; Rand's society is a network of voluntary trades in which obligations exist only by agreement. Students may evaluate whether Burke's inherited obligations are convincing, since no one chose them.\n• REASON AND PREJUDICE: Burke distrusts abstract reason and defends prejudice and custom as stored wisdom; Rand's Objectivism makes individual reason the only guide to action and rejects faith and tradition. Rand therefore challenges the conservative claim that inherited institutions deserve respect because they have lasted.\n• HIERARCHY AND ALTRUISM: Burke accepts a natural hierarchy and the duties of those at the top (the root of paternalism and One Nation conservatism); Rand rejects altruism and celebrates productive individuals who owe nothing to the less successful. Students may contrast noblesse oblige with rational egoism.\n• EVALUATION: Burke explains attachment, loyalty and social stability better, and his warnings about revolutionary rebuilding were borne out by the Terror; Rand explains the moral case for markets and individual choice but struggles to account for family, community and duties people never chose. Critics note that Burke's organic society can justify existing privilege.\n• STATUS AND CONTEXT: Reflections (1790) was a polemic written in response to the French Revolution and to English radicals who welcomed it; The Virtue of Selfishness (1964) is a collection of essays setting out Rand's Objectivist philosophy. Rand rejected the label "conservative", which is itself relevant to comparing the extracts.\n• OTHER THINKERS: Oakeshott's defence of the familiar and practical knowledge; Hobbes's view that society depends on a sovereign; Nozick's voluntary communities under a minimal state.\n\nJudgement: The extracts represent opposite poles of conservative thought on society. Burke is more convincing as an account of why people are attached to their society and why rapid change is dangerous; Rand is more convincing in protecting the individual against claims made in the name of the collective. The best answers conclude that the tension is real and unresolved, which is why the New Right combined neo-liberal economics with neo-conservative appeals to order, family and nation.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and comparing the arguments in the extracts and weighing which are more convincing; evaluate their status (author, type of publication, purpose, date) where relevant — the extracts are summaries written for this question, so status means the original work and its context. Answers that do not focus on the arguments in the extracts cannot go above Level 2. Answers that make no reference to the thinkers that the student has studied cannot go above Level 2. Analysis and evaluation of thinkers other than Burke and Rand may be credited where it is clearly focused on the extracts and the arguments within them. The strongest answers compare the extracts point by point rather than summarising each in turn.` },

      { q: 'EXTRACTS (written for practice):\n\nExtract 1 — a summary of Hobbes\'s argument in Leviathan (1651), written for this question\n"Hobbes argues that human beings are driven by their appetites and aversions, and share "a perpetual and restless desire of power after power, that ceaseth only in death". This is not because people are wicked, but because no one can be secure in what they already have without acquiring more power. Because people are roughly equal in their ability to kill one another, three causes of conflict arise: competition for gain, diffidence (fear) for safety, and the pursuit of glory or reputation. Reason does not govern the passions but serves them; it can, however, show people that peace is in their interest, and the fear of violent death inclines them to accept a common power. Human nature therefore makes strong, unified authority necessary if people are to live together at all."\n\nExtract 2 — a summary of Oakeshott\'s argument in the essays \'Rationalism in Politics\' (1947) and \'On Being Conservative\' (1956), collected in Rationalism in Politics and Other Essays (1962), written for this question\n"Oakeshott argues that human beings are limited, above all, in what they can know. He distinguishes technical knowledge, which can be written down as rules, from practical knowledge, which exists only in use and is gained through experience and tradition. The Rationalist in politics believes that society can be understood and redesigned by applying abstract reason and blueprints, but this ignores the practical knowledge contained in existing practices and institutions. The conservative disposition, by contrast, is a realistic response to human limits: it prefers the tried to the untried and the familiar to the unknown. Government should not try to impose a single vision of the good life; its role is modest, to keep the peace between people pursuing many different activities, rather like an umpire."\n\nAnalyse, evaluate and compare the arguments made in the above extracts on human nature. In your answer you should refer to the thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in analysing, evaluating and comparing the arguments made in the extracts on human nature, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• PSYCHOLOGICAL VERSUS INTELLECTUAL IMPERFECTION: Hobbes stresses psychological imperfection — people are driven by fear, competition and the desire for power; Oakeshott stresses intellectual imperfection — people cannot grasp society well enough to redesign it. These are two of the main forms of the conservative belief in human imperfection (with Burke's moral imperfection as a third).\n• ROLE OF REASON: for Hobbes, reason is the servant of the passions but can still work out that peace requires a sovereign; for Oakeshott, abstract reason is dangerous in politics and practical knowledge matters more. Students may note the irony that Hobbes's own method — deducing the state from first principles — is close to the rationalism Oakeshott criticised, though Oakeshott admired and edited Leviathan.\n• CONSEQUENCES FOR THE STATE: Hobbes concludes that only absolute, undivided authority can contain human nature; Oakeshott concludes that government should be limited and pragmatic, keeping the peace rather than pursuing grand projects. They agree that politics should secure order rather than perfect human beings, but differ sharply over how much power this needs.\n• IS CONSERVATISM BASED ON HUMAN NATURE? Hobbes builds his whole theory on an account of human nature; Oakeshott argued that the conservative disposition in government need not rest on any particular belief about human nature, but on a preference for the familiar. Students may evaluate whether conservatism is therefore a theory of human nature at all.\n• NEW RIGHT CONTRAST: Rand and Nozick see individuals as rational and self-reliant, so less authority is needed — a more optimistic view that conflicts with both extracts. Students may evaluate whether this makes the New Right less recognisably conservative.\n• EVALUATION: Hobbes's view is supported by the collapse of order in civil wars and failed states, but may be too bleak, ignoring cooperation and altruism; Oakeshott's view is supported by the failures of ambitious planning, but critics say it can justify inaction in the face of injustice.\n• STATUS AND CONTEXT: Leviathan was written against the background of the English Civil War; Oakeshott's essays were written in post-war Britain, when he opposed the rationalist planning of the Attlee era and ideological politics generally.\n\nJudgement: Both extracts treat human imperfection as the basis for conservative politics, but draw different conclusions: Hobbes's pessimism about motives leads to authority, Oakeshott's scepticism about knowledge leads to modest, limited government. Oakeshott is arguably more convincing for a stable modern society, since he explains the failures of large-scale planning without justifying absolute power; Hobbes is more convincing about why order is the first requirement of politics. The best answers use the New Right to test both.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and comparing the arguments in the extracts and weighing which are more convincing; evaluate their status (author, type of publication, purpose, date) where relevant — the extracts are summaries written for this question, so status means the original work and its context. Answers that do not focus on the arguments in the extracts cannot go above Level 2. Answers that make no reference to the thinkers that the student has studied cannot go above Level 2. Analysis and evaluation of thinkers other than Hobbes and Oakeshott may be credited where it is clearly focused on the extracts and the arguments within them. The strongest answers compare the extracts point by point rather than summarising each in turn.` },

      { q: 'EXTRACTS (written for practice):\n\nExtract 1 — a summary of Oakeshott\'s argument in the essays \'Political Education\' (1951) and \'On Being Conservative\' (1956), collected in Rationalism in Politics and Other Essays (1962), written for this question\n"Oakeshott argues that being conservative is a disposition rather than a doctrine: a tendency to use and enjoy what is available rather than to wish for something else. Change is unavoidable, but it always involves loss as well as possible gain, and the gains are uncertain, so the burden of proof lies with the person proposing the change. Innovation should be small and gradual, respond to a specific defect rather than a general vision of perfection, and preserve continuity. Politics is best understood as pursuing the intimations already present in a society\'s traditions, not as carrying out an abstract plan. The conservative, he says, prefers the familiar to the unknown and "present laughter to utopian bliss"."\n\nExtract 2 — a summary of Rand\'s argument in the essay \'Conservatism: An Obituary\' (1960), collected in Capitalism: The Unknown Ideal (1966), written for this question\n"Rand argues that American conservatives were losing the battle for capitalism because they defended it on the wrong grounds. Some appealed to faith, some to tradition, and some to the depravity of human nature — and each of these concedes the moral argument to collectivists. Tradition cannot justify anything: an idea or institution is not right because it is old, and defending the status quo meant defending the mixed economy and the growing state controls that already existed. Capitalism, she argues, has never been fully practised and must be defended as a moral ideal based on reason, individual rights and rational self-interest. What is needed is not conservatives who cling to the past but radicals for capitalism, prepared to challenge prevailing ideas and fight for a fully free society."\n\nAnalyse, evaluate and compare the arguments made in the above extracts on change and tradition. In your answer you should refer to the thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in analysing, evaluating and comparing the arguments made in the extracts on change and tradition, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• NATURE OF CONSERVATISM: Oakeshott sees conservatism as a disposition, not an ideology or a programme; Rand rejects conservatism as a position because it lacks a principled, rational foundation. The extracts therefore disagree about whether politics should be guided by principles at all.\n• ATTITUDE TO TRADITION: Oakeshott treats tradition as a store of practical knowledge and the proper source of political direction; Rand treats the appeal to tradition as intellectually empty, since age is no proof of value. Students may link Oakeshott to Burke's view of tradition as accumulated wisdom and "change in order to conserve".\n• RATIONALISM: Rand is precisely the kind of thinker Oakeshott called a Rationalist — she wants politics remade according to a principled ideal. Oakeshott also criticised Hayek on this ground, arguing that a plan to resist all planning belongs to the same style of politics as planning itself.\n• RADICAL CHANGE: Oakeshott wants change to be small, slow and remedial; Rand calls for radical change towards an ideal of laissez-faire capitalism. Students may evaluate the New Right in practice — privatisation, trade-union reform and deregulation under Thatcher — as radical change carried out in the name of conservatism.\n• HUMAN NATURE: Rand rejects the appeal to human depravity and bases her case on rational individuals; Oakeshott stresses the limits of human knowledge. Hobbes's pessimism offers a further contrast.\n• EVALUATION: Oakeshott is convincing that large-scale redesign destroys knowledge and has unpredictable consequences, but his approach can entrench injustice and gives no guidance when traditions conflict; Rand is convincing that the status quo is not self-justifying, but her ideal has never been implemented and may be the kind of utopian project conservatives distrust.\n• STATUS AND CONTEXT: Oakeshott's essays were written in post-war Britain, partly against the planning of the Attlee governments; Rand's essay began as a 1960 lecture during the Cold War, addressed to American conservatives, and was published in a collection defending capitalism. Nozick's minimal state and Burke's pragmatic reform offer useful comparisons.\n\nJudgement: The extracts expose a fundamental tension in conservatism between pragmatic traditionalism and principled New Right radicalism. Oakeshott is more convincingly conservative, because he explains why change should be cautious without committing to a utopian blueprint; Rand is more convincing in showing that tradition alone cannot tell us which traditions deserve to survive. The best answers conclude on whether Rand's position can be called conservative at all.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and comparing the arguments in the extracts and weighing which are more convincing; evaluate their status (author, type of publication, purpose, date) where relevant — the extracts are summaries written for this question, so status means the original work and its context. Answers that do not focus on the arguments in the extracts cannot go above Level 2. Answers that make no reference to the thinkers that the student has studied cannot go above Level 2. Analysis and evaluation of thinkers other than Oakeshott and Rand may be credited where it is clearly focused on the extracts and the arguments within them. The strongest answers compare the extracts point by point rather than summarising each in turn.` },
    ]
  },

  '3.3': {
    name: 'Socialism',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three core ideas of socialism.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Collectivism — human beings are social beings who achieve more through collective action than individual effort, and cannot be understood as the isolated pre-social individuals of liberal theory.\n• Common humanity and fraternity — people are bound by a shared humanity, so solidarity and cooperation are more authentic expressions of human nature than competition.\n• Equality — the defining socialist value. Socialists regard equality of OPPORTUNITY as inadequate, since a fair race is meaningless when competitors start from radically different positions; they seek far greater equality of outcome on the grounds that needs are broadly similar and inequality is socially produced — though how much varies: Marx and Engels envisaged a classless society, while Crosland sought greater social equality, reducing inequality rather than abolishing it.\n• Social class — a person's relationship to the means of production shapes life chances, consciousness and political interests (Marx and Engels; Luxemburg).\n• Common ownership and workers' control — private ownership is the source of exploitation; revisionists (Crosland) and the Third Way (Giddens) later detached socialism from ownership, while Webb favoured gradual, state-led collectivisation.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires analysis of how the ideas connect and where socialists divide, supported by specified thinkers (Marx and Engels, Luxemburg, Webb, Crosland, Giddens).\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three socialist views on human nature.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• MALLEABILITY — human nature is shaped by social and economic conditions rather than fixed. Competitive, selfish and acquisitive behaviour is a PRODUCT of capitalist social relations, not an inherent characteristic. Analysis: this is the foundation of socialist optimism, since changing structures can change behaviour.\n• NATURAL COOPERATION — human beings have a capacity for fraternity and solidarity that different institutions would allow to flourish.\n• SOCIAL BEINGS — individuals are formed by the communities and classes they belong to, so the liberal atomised individual is an abstraction.\n• Marx's ALIENATION — under capitalism workers are estranged from the product of their labour, from the act of production, from their own human potential and from one another, so capitalism prevents the expression of authentic human nature.\n\nAnalysis expected: the sharpest contrast with conservative human imperfection and with liberal rational self-interest. The standard objection — that socialism underestimates self-interest — is met by the reply that observed behaviour is produced by existing institutions.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three socialist views on equality.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Rejection of equality of OPPORTUNITY as sufficient — a fair race is meaningless when competitors start from radically different positions, and formal equality of opportunity within a grossly unequal society merely LEGITIMISES the resulting inequality.\n• Equality of OUTCOME — sought on three grounds: needs are broadly similar; inequality is produced by unequal social structures rather than differences in merit; and material equality underpins genuine social solidarity.\n• Revolutionary socialists hold that outcome equality requires ABOLISHING private ownership of the means of production, since exploitation is structural.\n• Social democrats (Crosland) sought not absolute equality but greater social equality — a substantial narrowing of outcome inequality — through progressive taxation, welfare and comprehensive education while retaining a market economy.\n• Third Way socialists (Giddens) moved BACK towards equality of opportunity, emphasising social investment and employability.\n\nAnalysis expected: the Giddens shift is why the Third Way's socialist credentials are contested — Crosland kept the goal of reducing outcome inequality and changed the instrument; Giddens narrowed the goal to equality of opportunity.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three differences between revolutionary socialism and social democracy.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• View of capitalism — revolutionary socialists hold it cannot be reformed because exploitation is intrinsic to the ownership of the means of production; social democrats hold it can be humanised through redistribution, welfare and regulation.\n• View of the STATE — this is the fundamental disagreement. Marx and Luxemburg regarded the state as an instrument of class rule whose structure serves capital and will resist fundamental change; Webb regarded it as broadly neutral, capturable through elections and directable towards socialist ends.\n• Method — revolution and mass action versus incremental parliamentary reform ("the inevitability of gradualness").\n• Ownership — common ownership versus a mixed economy with selective nationalisation, later abandoned altogether.\n• Agency — the organised working class versus persuasion of elites and permeation of institutions.\n\nAnalysis expected: the disagreement turns on an EMPIRICAL question about the nature of the state, and the evidence is mixed — social democrats did win power and build welfare states, but did not abolish capitalism.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three socialist thinkers\' views on achieving socialism.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• MARX and ENGELS — capitalism's internal contradictions produce recurring crises and immiseration; the proletariat develops class consciousness and overthrows the system, passing through a dictatorship of the proletariat to a classless society in which the state withers away. The parliamentary road is rejected because the state is not neutral.\n• LUXEMBURG — revolution through the MASS STRIKE and the spontaneous action of workers, whose consciousness develops through the struggle itself. She attacked Bernstein's revisionism (reforms cannot abolish exploitation) AND Lenin's vanguardism (a party substituting itself for the class would end in the dictatorship of a few).\n• WEBB — Fabian gradualism and "the inevitability of gradualness" (Sidney Webb's 1923 phrase); socialism arrives through incremental reform as the electorate recognises its merits, with great faith in expert administration and permeation of institutions.\n• CROSLAND — revisionism; managerial capitalism separated ownership from control, so socialist ends could be achieved by taxing growth rather than nationalising.\n\nGuidance: credit the tensions between them.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three socialist views on the economy.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Common ownership (Marx, Luxemburg) — the means of production must pass into collective ownership, because private ownership is itself the mechanism of exploitation through the appropriation of SURPLUS VALUE. Analysis: identifies a structural cause rather than a distributional symptom, which is why reform is held insufficient.\n• Social democratic managed capitalism (Crosland) — managerial capitalism had separated ownership from control, so nationalisation was unnecessary; socialist ends could be achieved by taxing the proceeds of GROWTH and spending them on welfare, public services and comprehensive education.\n• Third Way market socialism (Giddens) — accept the market as the most effective generator of wealth while directing it to social ends through a social investment state emphasising skills and employability.\n• Webb's co-operative movement and the national minimum.\n\nAnalysis expected: Crosland's strategy depends entirely on sustained growth, which is why stagflation and later globalisation left it without a mechanism.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 6, AO2 3. Only three points are needed — credit the best three. One relevant point only: maximum 3 marks. Two relevant points only: maximum 6 marks.` },

      { q: 'EXTRACTS (written for practice):\n\nExtract 1 — a summary of Marx and Engels\' argument in The Communist Manifesto (1848), written for this question\n"Marx and Engels argue that capitalism is a stage in history based on the exploitation of the proletariat, who own nothing but their ability to work, by the bourgeoisie, who own the means of production. The bourgeoisie has created productive forces greater than all previous generations together, but it has reduced human relations to naked self-interest and cash payment, and made the worker an appendage of the machine. Capitalism is also unstable: it produces recurring commercial crises of over-production, drives small producers down into the proletariat and concentrates wealth. By creating an ever larger and more organised working class, it produces "its own grave-diggers". Communists therefore aim to abolish bourgeois private property and to centralise the means of production in the hands of the state, controlled by the working class."\n\nExtract 2 — a summary of Crosland\'s argument in The Future of Socialism (1956), written for this question\n"Crosland argues that the capitalism Marx analysed had been transformed. Ownership of industry had become separated from its control, which had passed to professional managers; governments could now manage demand using Keynesian techniques, so mass unemployment and the old cycle of slumps could be avoided; and trade unions and the welfare state had shifted power towards working people. Nationalisation was therefore not the essence of socialism but only one possible means, to be judged by its results. Socialism should be defined by its values, above all social equality, not by a particular form of ownership. A mixed economy with a large private sector, steady growth and progressive taxation could pay for public services and reduce class divisions without wholesale common ownership."\n\nAnalyse, evaluate and compare the arguments made in the above extracts on the economy. In your answer you should refer to the thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in analysing, evaluating and comparing the arguments made in the extracts on the economy, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• NATURE OF CAPITALISM: Marx and Engels see capitalism as inherently exploitative, because profit comes from the labour of workers who do not own the means of production; Crosland argues that post-war capitalism had been reformed so much that the old analysis no longer applied. Students may frame this as revolutionary socialism versus revisionist social democracy.\n• OWNERSHIP: the Manifesto calls for the abolition of bourgeois private property and common ownership; Crosland separates ownership from control, arguing that who owns industry matters less than how it is managed and how its rewards are shared. Students may refer to the debate over Labour's Clause IV, which committed the party to common ownership in 1918 and was rewritten under Blair in 1995.\n• STABILITY AND CRISIS: the Manifesto predicts recurring crises and ever-deepening class polarisation; Crosland claims Keynesian demand management had tamed the business cycle. Students may evaluate this using later evidence — the stagflation of the 1970s undermined Crosland's confidence, while the 2008 financial crisis revived interest in Marxist accounts of capitalist instability.\n• MEANS AND ENDS: for Marx and Engels, common ownership is central to ending exploitation; for Crosland it is a means that can be replaced by others (taxation, welfare, education) if they achieve equality better. Students may evaluate whether Crosland's revisionism abandons socialism's core or clarifies its values.\n• CLASS: Marx and Engels predict polarisation into two hostile classes and revolution; Crosland argues class divisions persist mainly in status, education and life chances, which the state can reduce through reform (as Education Secretary he issued Circular 10/65 promoting comprehensive schools).\n• STATUS AND CONTEXT: the Manifesto (1848) was a short political pamphlet written for the Communist League in a year of European revolutions, meant to rally workers; The Future of Socialism (1956) was a detailed work of revisionist theory by a Labour politician, written during the post-war boom.\n• OTHER THINKERS: Luxemburg's argument that reforms cannot abolish exploitation; Webb's gradualist Fabianism; Giddens's acceptance of the market in the Third Way.\n\nJudgement: Crosland is more convincing as an account of mid-twentieth-century capitalism, which delivered growth, full employment and rising living standards, but his assumption that capitalism had been permanently tamed was challenged by the 1970s and by the later growth of inequality. Marx and Engels are less convincing in their predictions of revolution in advanced economies, but their account of instability and concentration of wealth still has force. The best answers judge the extracts against later economic evidence.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and comparing the arguments in the extracts and weighing which are more convincing; evaluate their status (author, type of publication, purpose, date) where relevant — the extracts are summaries written for this question, so status means the original work and its context. Answers that do not focus on the arguments in the extracts cannot go above Level 2. Answers that make no reference to the thinkers that the student has studied cannot go above Level 2. Analysis and evaluation of thinkers other than Marx and Engels and Crosland may be credited where it is clearly focused on the extracts and the arguments within them. The strongest answers compare the extracts point by point rather than summarising each in turn.` },

      { q: 'EXTRACTS (written for practice):\n\nExtract 1 — a summary of the Fabian argument of Beatrice Webb, especially in the Minority Report of the Royal Commission on the Poor Laws (1909), written with Sidney Webb, written for this question\n"Beatrice Webb argues that poverty is caused not mainly by individual moral failure but by social and economic conditions — unemployment, sickness, old age, low wages and bad housing — which individuals cannot control. The Poor Law, which treated the destitute as paupers and deterred them with the workhouse, should therefore be broken up. In its place the state should prevent destitution by guaranteeing a national minimum standard of life, through specialised public services for health, education, the elderly and the unemployed, and a national system of labour exchanges. Such collective provision should be planned by trained experts and introduced gradually through democratic institutions — local government and Parliament — extending and improving the existing state rather than overthrowing it."\n\nExtract 2 — a summary of Luxemburg\'s argument in Reform or Revolution (1899; revised 1908), written for this question\n"Luxemburg argues, against Eduard Bernstein, that socialism cannot be reached by gradually accumulating reforms. Trade unions and social legislation can improve the conditions under which workers are exploited, but they cannot abolish exploitation itself, because wage labour and capitalist relations of production remain. The existing state is not a neutral body standing above society: it is a class state, and parliamentary democracy under capitalism expresses the interests of the bourgeoisie. Those who choose legislative reform instead of the conquest of political power are not choosing a slower road to the same goal, but a different goal — the reform of capitalism rather than its abolition. Reforms still matter because they educate and organise the working class, but only a revolution by the working class can transform society."\n\nAnalyse, evaluate and compare the arguments made in the above extracts on the state. In your answer you should refer to the thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in analysing, evaluating and comparing the arguments made in the extracts on the state, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• NATURE OF THE STATE: Webb treats the state as a neutral instrument that can be captured through elections and used to promote the common good; Luxemburg sees it as a class state that serves the bourgeoisie, so it cannot be the vehicle for socialism. This is the core divide between evolutionary and revolutionary socialism.\n• REFORM VERSUS REVOLUTION: Webb's Fabian gradualism holds that each reform moves society towards socialism; Luxemburg argues that reform and revolution lead to different destinations, not the same destination at different speeds. Students may evaluate this through the later history of social democracy, which reformed capitalism without replacing it.\n• WHO LEADS CHANGE: Webb looks to expert administrators, planning and a professional civil service — a top-down, sometimes elitist approach; Luxemburg looks to the self-activity of the working class and mass action, and later (in The Russian Revolution, 1918) criticised the Bolsheviks for suppressing democracy. Both reject a small elite seizing power for itself, but for different reasons.\n• ROLE OF REFORMS: the extracts agree that reforms improve workers' lives. For Webb they are the substance of socialism; for Luxemburg they are useful mainly because they build working-class organisation and consciousness.\n• EVALUATION: the Webbs' ideas influenced the Beveridge Report (1942) and the post-war welfare state, and Sidney Webb drafted Labour's 1918 Clause IV — strong evidence that the state could be used for socialist ends. Luxemburg would reply that reforms can be reversed (as privatisation and welfare cuts later showed) and that capitalism survived intact. Webb's reliance on experts also faces the criticism that it is paternalist.\n• STATUS AND CONTEXT: the Minority Report was an official dissenting report to a Royal Commission, a practical policy document; Reform or Revolution began as newspaper articles in a debate within the German SPD, written as a polemic. Luxemburg was killed in 1919 after the Spartacist uprising.\n• OTHER THINKERS: Marx and Engels on the state as a committee of the bourgeoisie and the dictatorship of the proletariat; Crosland and Giddens on using the state to reform capitalism.\n\nJudgement: Webb is more convincing in terms of what socialists actually achieved in liberal democracies, since the welfare state was built through the existing state; Luxemburg is more convincing in explaining why those achievements stopped short of socialism and proved reversible. The best answers conclude on whether the difference is about MEANS (gradual or revolutionary) or, as Luxemburg claims, about ENDS.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and comparing the arguments in the extracts and weighing which are more convincing; evaluate their status (author, type of publication, purpose, date) where relevant — the extracts are summaries written for this question, so status means the original work and its context. Answers that do not focus on the arguments in the extracts cannot go above Level 2. Answers that make no reference to the thinkers that the student has studied cannot go above Level 2. Analysis and evaluation of thinkers other than Webb and Luxemburg may be credited where it is clearly focused on the extracts and the arguments within them. The strongest answers compare the extracts point by point rather than summarising each in turn.` },

      { q: 'EXTRACTS (written for practice):\n\nExtract 1 — a summary of Crosland\'s argument in The Future of Socialism (1956), written for this question\n"Crosland argues that, now that capitalism has been reformed, the central aim of socialism is greater social equality. This means more than equality of opportunity, which on its own could produce a harsh meritocracy in which a new elite justifies its privileges by talent. Socialists should seek a more equal distribution of income and wealth and, above all, a reduction of class divisions in status, education and life chances. Britain\'s divided school system, with selective state schools and private schools, was a major source of class inequality and should be reformed. Economic growth in a managed mixed economy would pay for better public services and redistribution through progressive taxation, allowing the living standards of the poor to rise without making the better-off absolutely worse off."\n\nExtract 2 — a summary of Giddens\'s argument in The Third Way (1998), written for this question\n"Giddens argues that globalisation, the decline of traditional class politics and the collapse of state socialism mean that social democracy must be renewed. Equality should be understood as inclusion: every citizen should have the civil and political rights, and the practical opportunities, above all in work and education, to take part fully in society. Exclusion is the problem — at the bottom through poverty, and at the top through the rich withdrawing from public institutions. Rather than relying mainly on redistributing income after the event, the state should become a social investment state, investing in people\'s skills through education and training. Welfare should help and encourage people into work, on the principle of "no rights without responsibilities". Some inequality is the price of a dynamic market economy."\n\nAnalyse, evaluate and compare the arguments made in the above extracts on equality. In your answer you should refer to the thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in analysing, evaluating and comparing the arguments made in the extracts on equality, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• MEANING OF EQUALITY: Crosland seeks social equality that goes beyond equality of opportunity, reducing differences of income, wealth and class status; Giddens redefines equality as inclusion and equality of opportunity. Students may argue that Giddens's view moves socialism closer to modern liberalism.\n• REDISTRIBUTION VERSUS INVESTMENT: Crosland relies on progressive taxation and public spending funded by growth; Giddens prefers investment in human capital to redistribution after the event. Students may evaluate whether social investment tackles the causes of inequality or simply accepts inequality produced by the market.\n• ROLE OF THE MARKET: both accept a mixed economy and reject wholesale nationalisation, so the extracts share revisionist ground; but Giddens accepts globalised markets and some inequality as necessary, whereas Crosland saw a managed economy as a tool for equality.\n• EDUCATION: both see education as central — Crosland to break down class divisions (comprehensive schools), Giddens to equip individuals for a knowledge economy (reflected in New Labour's emphasis on education). The difference is between education as a route to social equality and education as a route to employability.\n• RESPONSIBILITY AND CONDITIONALITY: Giddens links rights to responsibilities, so welfare becomes conditional on seeking work; Crosland's approach is closer to universal provision as a right of citizenship. Critics argue the Third Way shifts responsibility for inequality onto individuals.\n• EVALUATION: Crosland's approach assumed steady growth, which the 1970s undermined; the Third Way was criticised from the left for accepting high inequality at the top, and Marxists would argue both leave capitalism's class structure intact. Whether Giddens's approach is still socialist is itself a key evaluative question.\n• STATUS AND CONTEXT: The Future of Socialism was written in the post-war boom by a Labour revisionist; The Third Way (1998) was written by an academic sociologist whose ideas were associated with Blair's New Labour and Clinton's Democrats. Marx and Engels's goal of a classless society, and Webb's national minimum, offer useful comparisons.\n\nJudgement: Both extracts reject equality of outcome and accept the market, but Crosland keeps a clearly socialist commitment to reducing class inequality, while Giddens narrows equality to inclusion and opportunity. Crosland is more convincing as a socialist account of equality; Giddens is more convincing about the constraints that globalisation places on national governments. The best answers judge whether the Third Way's view of equality marks a development of socialism or its abandonment.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and comparing the arguments in the extracts and weighing which are more convincing; evaluate their status (author, type of publication, purpose, date) where relevant — the extracts are summaries written for this question, so status means the original work and its context. Answers that do not focus on the arguments in the extracts cannot go above Level 2. Answers that make no reference to the thinkers that the student has studied cannot go above Level 2. Analysis and evaluation of thinkers other than Crosland and Giddens may be credited where it is clearly focused on the extracts and the arguments within them. The strongest answers compare the extracts point by point rather than summarising each in turn.` },

      { q: 'EXTRACTS (written for practice):\n\nExtract 1 — a summary of Marx and Engels\' argument in The Communist Manifesto (1848), written for this question\n"Marx and Engels argue that "The history of all hitherto existing society is the history of class struggles." Every form of society is defined by who owns the means of production, and its law, politics and dominant ideas reflect the interests of that ruling class. Modern society is splitting more and more into two great hostile classes, the bourgeoisie and the proletariat. Institutions that seem natural, such as the family, religion and the nation, are shaped by class relations, and the modern state is in effect a committee for managing the common affairs of the bourgeoisie. Because people are shaped by the material conditions they live in, a society without private ownership of the means of production would be an association in which the free development of each is the condition for the free development of all."\n\nExtract 2 — a summary of Giddens\'s argument in The Third Way (1998), written for this question\n"Giddens argues that society has changed in ways that make class-based socialism outdated. Globalisation, the knowledge economy and new technology have transformed work, and the old industrial working class has shrunk. People now live in a more individualised society, in which identities are chosen rather than inherited from class, and in which they must shape their own lives amid greater risk and uncertainty. Socialists should therefore stop seeing society as a conflict between classes. Instead, they should aim to renew civil society and community, balancing individual freedom with responsibility, and to rebuild shared citizenship through partnership between the state, the market and voluntary organisations. The old division between left and right, he argues, is increasingly unable to explain modern politics."\n\nAnalyse, evaluate and compare the arguments made in the above extracts on society. In your answer you should refer to the thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — in analysing, evaluating and comparing the arguments made in the extracts on society, students may be expected to cover areas such as the following:\n• CLASS: Marx and Engels see society as fundamentally divided by class, with history driven by class struggle; Giddens argues class has declined as the basis of identity and politics. Students may use evidence of class dealignment in voting and deindustrialisation to support Giddens, and persistent class differences in wealth, health and education to support the Manifesto.\n• CONFLICT VERSUS COMMUNITY: the Manifesto sees conflict between classes as inevitable until capitalism is overthrown; Giddens seeks social cohesion through community, citizenship and partnership. Students may argue Giddens draws on communitarian ideas rather than on class analysis.\n• STRUCTURE AND INDIVIDUAL: both extracts agree that people are shaped by social conditions, but Marx and Engels stress material and economic structures, while Giddens stresses individualisation and choice in shaping identity. This affects their view of human nature — malleable and collective for Marx, reflexive and individual for Giddens.\n• THE STATE AND SOCIETY: for Marx and Engels the state serves the ruling class; for Giddens it is a partner of the market and civil society. Students may compare this with the social democratic view (Crosland, Webb) of the state as a neutral tool for reform.\n• THE GOAL: the Manifesto's goal is a classless society in which "the free development of each" depends on the free development of all; Giddens's goal is an inclusive society in which individuals have opportunities and accept responsibilities within a market economy. Students may evaluate whether Giddens has kept socialism's collectivist view of society or replaced it with a liberal one.\n• EVALUATION: the Manifesto's prediction that society would polarise and advanced capitalist countries would experience proletarian revolution was not borne out; Giddens has been criticised for underestimating persistent inequality and for accepting the market's effects on community. Luxemburg offers a further revolutionary contrast.\n• STATUS AND CONTEXT: the Manifesto was a revolutionary pamphlet from 1848, written for the Communist League; The Third Way was a 1998 work of political sociology that shaped New Labour, written after the collapse of the Soviet bloc.\n\nJudgement: Giddens offers the more convincing description of how class identity and work have changed in advanced economies, but Marx and Engels offer a more powerful explanation of why inequalities of wealth and power persist. The best answers conclude on whether Giddens's account of society is still socialist, given that it abandons class conflict — socialism's traditional view of what society is.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and comparing the arguments in the extracts and weighing which are more convincing; evaluate their status (author, type of publication, purpose, date) where relevant — the extracts are summaries written for this question, so status means the original work and its context. Answers that do not focus on the arguments in the extracts cannot go above Level 2. Answers that make no reference to the thinkers that the student has studied cannot go above Level 2. Analysis and evaluation of thinkers other than Marx and Engels and Giddens may be credited where it is clearly focused on the extracts and the arguments within them. The strongest answers compare the extracts point by point rather than summarising each in turn.` },
    ]
  },

  '3.4': {
    name: 'Nationalism',
    questions: [
      { q: '\'Self-determination is the core principle that unites all nationalists.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the nationalist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — students may analyse and evaluate:\n\nSELF-DETERMINATION IS THE UNIFYING CORE:\n• ROUSSEAU: sovereignty belongs to the people as a whole and is expressed through the general will, so no people can legitimately be ruled by an authority it has not authorised. This supplied the democratic foundation for the claim that a people should govern itself, and his Considerations on the Government of Poland (written 1771–72) applied it to a nation threatened with partition.\n• MAZZINI: every nation has an equal right to govern itself. Young Italy (1831) sought a unified, independent Italian republic, and his vision of a Europe of free nations makes self-determination the organising principle of liberal nationalism.\n• GARVEY: 'Africa for the Africans' — the demand that a dispersed people should have a homeland free of colonial rule. Anti-colonial nationalism across Asia and Africa made the same demand, which suggests self-determination is what nationalists of very different kinds share.\n\nSELF-DETERMINATION IS NOT THE UNIFYING CORE:\n• HERDER: cultural nationalism does not require statehood. The nation's value lies in its language, customs and Volksgeist, and Herder did not make political independence the central demand — which suggests the deeper core idea is the nation itself rather than its right to a state.\n• MAURRAS: France was already a sovereign state, and integral nationalism was concerned with national unity, order and the restoration of the monarchy rather than with popular self-government; he rejected popular sovereignty outright. Because it asserts French superiority, it also denies that other nations share an equal right.\n• SELECTIVE APPLICATION: nationalists typically demand self-determination for their own nation while denying it to minorities within their territory; the post-1918 settlement left substantial stranded minorities and irredentist claims.\n\nSynoptic links: the Scottish independence referendum of 2014 (55% No) and the SNP's continuing demand for another vote; Brexit framed as restoring national sovereignty; devolution and the UK as a multinational state; liberalism (Locke's government by consent).\n\nJudgement: Self-determination is the most widely shared nationalist demand and the one that connects Rousseau, Mazzini and Garvey. But it is better seen as a consequence of the deeper core idea — that the nation is the primary object of political loyalty — since Herder can be a nationalist without demanding a state, and Maurras shows the principle need not be universal. The strongest answers distinguish self-determination as a universal principle (Mazzini) from self-determination for 'us' alone.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and evaluating the statement through accurate use of the specified nationalist thinkers (Rousseau, Herder, Mazzini, Maurras, Garvey) and weighing the competing arguments to a substantiated conclusion; it does not matter which view is reached, provided it is supported. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant, accurate points should be credited.` },

      { q: '\'Membership of a nation is determined by birth rather than by choice.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the nationalist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — students may analyse and evaluate:\n\nMEMBERSHIP IS DETERMINED BY BIRTH:\n• HERDER: nations are organic communities formed by language, custom and shared history. People are born into a Volksgeist and are shaped by it; a national culture is not something one simply chooses to join.\n• MAURRAS: integral nationalism defined the true France by descent, soil and Catholic tradition. Jews, Protestants, Freemasons and resident foreigners (métèques) were cast as internal enemies who could never fully belong, however long they had lived in France.\n• GARVEY: black nationalism rested on shared race and African descent across the diaspora. Garvey promoted racial pride and racial separateness and opposed intermarriage, so membership was defined by ancestry.\n\nMEMBERSHIP IS DETERMINED BY CHOICE:\n• ROUSSEAU: the people is constituted by the social contract — an act of association in which citizens commit themselves to the general will. Membership is political and willed, which is the basis of civic nationalism.\n• MAZZINI: the nation is a moral community bound by shared duties and a common mission. Although he stressed common language and territory, the nation is realised through citizens' active commitment to a democratic republic, not through blood.\n• CIVIC NATIONALISM IN PRACTICE: the French revolutionary tradition and the United States define the nation by shared political values; naturalisation shows that nations do admit members by choice.\n\nSynoptic links: UK naturalisation and the Life in the UK test; the Windrush scandal (2018) as a dispute about who belongs; immigration debates in party politics (Conservatives, Labour, Reform UK); liberalism's emphasis on consent and voluntary association.\n\nJudgement: Birth dominates ethnic and cultural nationalism (Herder, Maurras, Garvey) and choice dominates civic nationalism (Rousseau, Mazzini). Most real nations combine the two, granting membership by birth while allowing naturalisation, so the statement describes one strand rather than nationalism as a whole. The distinction matters because it largely decides whether a nationalism is inclusive or exclusive.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and evaluating the statement through accurate use of the specified nationalist thinkers (Rousseau, Herder, Mazzini, Maurras, Garvey) and weighing the competing arguments to a substantiated conclusion; it does not matter which view is reached, provided it is supported. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant, accurate points should be credited.` },

      { q: '\'Nationalism is more often a conservative force than a progressive one.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the nationalist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — students may analyse and evaluate:\n\nMAINLY CONSERVATIVE:\n• MAURRAS: integral nationalism was reactionary — monarchist, Catholic, anti-Dreyfusard, anti-democratic and antisemitic. Action Française supported the Vichy regime, and Maurras was sentenced to life imprisonment in 1945.\n• CONSERVATIVE NATIONALISM: the nation is used to bind society together, defend inherited institutions and displace class conflict. Once a nation-state exists, nationalism usually becomes a force for preserving it.\n• HERDER: the emphasis on inherited language, folklore and custom can be read as backward-looking — a defence of tradition against the universal reason of the Enlightenment — and romantic ideas of the organic nation were later taken up by exclusive, right-wing nationalisms.\n\nMAINLY PROGRESSIVE:\n• ROUSSEAU: the sovereign people replaced the dynastic monarch as the source of authority. This idea, taken up in the French Revolution, was radically progressive in challenging aristocratic and royal power.\n• MAZZINI: liberal nationalism was tied to republicanism, democracy and the moral and social improvement of working people; it treated all nations as equals and was compatible with internationalism.\n• GARVEY and ANTI-COLONIAL NATIONALISM: national self-assertion was a means of liberation from imperial rule and racial oppression, extending self-government to peoples denied it.\n\nSynoptic links: the Conservative Party's use of national identity and one-nation rhetoric; Brexit and Reform UK; the SNP as a centre-left civic nationalist party; socialism's internationalism; conservatism's organic view of society.\n\nJudgement: Nationalism has no fixed political direction. It tends to be progressive when it challenges dynastic or imperial power (Rousseau, Mazzini, Garvey) and conservative once the nation-state exists and needs defending (Maurras). The best answers note the historical shift of nationalism from the left in the nineteenth century to the right in the twentieth, and conclude that its character depends on the ideology it combines with.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and evaluating the statement through accurate use of the specified nationalist thinkers (Rousseau, Herder, Mazzini, Maurras, Garvey) and weighing the competing arguments to a substantiated conclusion; it does not matter which view is reached, provided it is supported. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant, accurate points should be credited.` },

      { q: '\'The nation is best understood as a political community rather than a cultural one.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the nationalist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — students may analyse and evaluate:\n\nA POLITICAL COMMUNITY:\n• ROUSSEAU: the people is defined by shared participation in the general will; he even proposed a civil religion to bind citizens together. The nation is a community of citizens rather than of shared ancestry.\n• MAZZINI: the nation was to be realised politically, as a unified democratic republic; nationhood was fulfilled through self-government rather than through culture alone.\n• CIVIC NATIONS: the United States and republican France show that people of diverse origins can form a nation around shared institutions and values.\n\nA CULTURAL COMMUNITY:\n• HERDER: the nation is pre-political, defined by language and the Volksgeist, and exists whether or not it has a state — in his time German speakers were divided among many states but were still, for Herder, one people.\n• MAURRAS: the nation is defined by heritage, religion and descent, and the state exists to serve and protect that inherited culture rather than to create it.\n• GARVEY: a diaspora nation without a state, united by race and a shared history of slavery and colonialism — a nation in cultural and racial terms before it could be a political one.\n\nSynoptic links: the UK as a political union of several cultural nations; devolution to Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland; Welsh language legislation; debates over 'British values'; liberalism's contractual view of the state.\n\nJudgement: Most nations are both: culture supplies the sense of belonging, and political institutions supply the demand for self-government. The key evaluative point is that the choice matters — a political definition (Rousseau, Mazzini) allows an inclusive, civic nation, whereas a cultural or racial definition (Maurras, and to a lesser extent Herder and Garvey) makes membership harder to acquire. A strong conclusion may argue that cultural nationhood becomes politically significant only when it generates a demand for a state.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and evaluating the statement through accurate use of the specified nationalist thinkers (Rousseau, Herder, Mazzini, Maurras, Garvey) and weighing the competing arguments to a substantiated conclusion; it does not matter which view is reached, provided it is supported. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant, accurate points should be credited.` },

      { q: '\'Nationalism is fundamentally a democratic ideology.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the nationalist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — students may analyse and evaluate:\n\nFUNDAMENTALLY DEMOCRATIC:\n• ROUSSEAU: popular sovereignty — legitimate authority comes only from the people as a whole. By making the nation the sovereign people, he linked nationhood and democracy from the start.\n• MAZZINI: his motto 'God and the People' expressed a republican, democratic nationalism opposed to monarchy and foreign rule; he wanted a republic and the political education of working people for citizenship.\n• SELF-GOVERNMENT: anti-colonial nationalism, and Garvey's demand that black people govern themselves, extended self-rule to peoples previously governed by others.\n\nNOT FUNDAMENTALLY DEMOCRATIC:\n• MAURRAS: integral nationalism was explicitly anti-democratic; he regarded democracy and parliamentary government as a source of decline and wanted a restored hereditary monarchy, subordinating the individual to the nation.\n• GARVEY: the UNIA was organised around Garvey's own leadership, with titles, uniforms and parades, and its 1920 convention named him Provisional President of Africa. National movements can be mobilised around a leader who claims to embody the people rather than through democratic deliberation.\n• HERDER: cultural nationalism is indifferent to the form of government; a nation's Volksgeist can exist under any regime. Expansionist nationalisms of the twentieth century show nationalism combined with dictatorship.\n\nSynoptic links: referendums as expressions of national self-determination (Scotland 2014, the EU referendum 2016); populism and the claim to speak for 'the people'; parliamentary sovereignty; liberal democracy; conservatism and authority.\n\nJudgement: Nationalism is democratic in origin — Rousseau and Mazzini tied the nation to popular sovereignty — but not in essence. The claim that the people should rule does not settle how the people's will is identified, and where the nation is defined organically a monarch, leader or movement can claim to embody it without elections. Whether nationalism is democratic therefore depends on whether it is combined with liberalism (Mazzini) or with authoritarian conservatism (Maurras).\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and evaluating the statement through accurate use of the specified nationalist thinkers (Rousseau, Herder, Mazzini, Maurras, Garvey) and weighing the competing arguments to a substantiated conclusion; it does not matter which view is reached, provided it is supported. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant, accurate points should be credited.` },

      { q: '\'Nationalism serves the interests of elites rather than the people.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the nationalist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — students may analyse and evaluate:\n\nSERVES ELITES:\n• MAURRAS: integral nationalism aimed to restore the monarchy, the authority of the Church and a hierarchical social order; the nation was invoked to defend traditional elites against democracy and socialism.\n• MARXIST CRITIQUE: Marx and Engels held that 'the working men have no country' (Communist Manifesto, 1848). Nationalism divides workers of different nations and displaces class conflict, which serves the property-owning class; conservative nationalism deliberately used national unity to bind classes together.\n• CONSTRUCTED NATIONS: if states and educated elites standardised languages and national histories to create loyalty, nationalism is in part an instrument of rule from above.\n\nSERVES THE PEOPLE:\n• ROUSSEAU: the general will is the will of the whole people directed at the common good, set against privileged factions and particular interests.\n• MAZZINI: The Duties of Man (1860) was addressed to Italian working men, linking national unity with education, association and the improvement of their conditions.\n• GARVEY: the UNIA was a mass movement of ordinary black people in the Americas and beyond, promoting racial pride and economic self-reliance (for example the Black Star Line) among the dispossessed.\n• HERDER: celebrated the culture of ordinary people — folk songs and vernacular language — against a courtly elite that imitated French culture.\n\nSynoptic links: populism and claims to represent 'left-behind' voters; Brexit and Reform UK; the relationship between class and voting behaviour; socialism (Marx and Engels) and conservatism (one-nation paternalism).\n\nJudgement: Nationalism can serve either. Movements that arose from below (Herder's cultural revival, Mazzini's appeal to workers, Garvey's mass movement) served people denied recognition or self-government, but once nations have states, elites can readily use national loyalty to deflect class grievances, as Maurras illustrates. The best answers conclude that the answer depends on who defines the nation and against whom.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and evaluating the statement through accurate use of the specified nationalist thinkers (Rousseau, Herder, Mazzini, Maurras, Garvey) and weighing the competing arguments to a substantiated conclusion; it does not matter which view is reached, provided it is supported. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant, accurate points should be credited.` },

      { q: '"Nationalism is inherently dangerous." Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the nationalist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `INHERENTLY DANGEROUS:\n• Defining a nation NECESSARILY defines outsiders, so exclusion is structural rather than incidental.\n• Where the nation is conceived ethnically and organically, this has justified expulsion and genocide — Maurras's integral nationalism; the ethnic cleansings during the break-up of Yugoslavia.\n• Expansionist nationalism asserts superiority and justifies conquest, and cannot be universalised without contradiction.\n• It can displace class and economic questions, serving established interests.\n• The nation-state ideal is practically unworkable, leaving stranded minorities and irredentist grievance, as after 1918.\n\nNOT INHERENTLY DANGEROUS:\n• CIVIC nationalism defines the nation by shared political values rather than descent, so membership is open and compatible with diversity.\n• Mazzini's liberal nationalism universalises its own claim — every nation has an EQUAL right to self-determination — and holds that free nations would cooperate peacefully.\n• Anti-colonial nationalism delivered self-government to peoples under imperial rule; Garvey's black nationalism asserted dignity against racial oppression.\n• Herder celebrated the distinctiveness of ALL cultures, implying equal worth.\n• National identity can underpin social solidarity and the willingness to fund collective provision.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest conclusion is that nationalism is CROSS-CUTTING — the premise that peoples should govern themselves does not by itself determine WHO COUNTS as the people, and that question is answered by whichever ideology nationalism fuses with. The danger therefore lies in particular variants, not in the core premise — but the civic/ethnic boundary is easier to cross than liberal nationalists assume.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2.` },

      { q: '"Nationalists disagree more than they agree." Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the nationalist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `DISAGREE:\n• Are nations EQUAL? Mazzini treats them as moral equals each entitled to freedom; Maurras asserts particular superiority. This is not a difference of degree but of LOGICAL FORM — one principle can be granted to everyone, the other cannot.\n• Cultural or political definition? Herder locates the nation in a Volksgeist existing prior to any state; Rousseau and Mazzini in a political community exercising sovereignty.\n• Inclusive or exclusive membership? Civic nationalism by shared values; ethnic by inherited descent.\n• Relationship to the individual — liberal nationalism holds the nation serves its members' freedom; integral nationalism subordinates the individual entirely.\n• Compatibility with internationalism — Mazzini says yes; expansionist nationalism says no.\n\nAGREE:\n• All hold humanity is naturally divided into NATIONS and that the nation is the primary object of political loyalty.\n• All value SELF-DETERMINATION for their own nation, at minimum.\n• All regard national identity and culture as politically significant.\n• All reject a purely universal or cosmopolitan basis for political organisation.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest conclusion is that nationalism is best understood as a CROSS-CUTTING ideology attaching to others rather than standing alone — which is why Mazzini and Maurras share only the vocabulary of the nation. That claim reaches L5.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2.` },

      { q: '"Nationalism and internationalism are compatible." Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the nationalist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `COMPATIBLE:\n• Mazzini's liberal nationalism holds that every nation has an EQUAL right to self-determination, so a world of free self-governing nations would cooperate peacefully — the principle is universalisable.\n• Herder celebrated the distinctiveness of all cultures, implying equal worth rather than hierarchy, and therefore cultural pluralism.\n• Self-determination was the founding principle of the post-1918 settlement and of the United Nations system.\n• Anti-colonial nationalists frequently cooperated internationally — pan-Africanism explicitly transcended individual national boundaries.\n• Nation-states are the building blocks of international institutions; there can be no international order without them.\n\nINCOMPATIBLE:\n• Nationalism prioritises the national interest, which by definition may conflict with international obligations.\n• EXPANSIONIST and integral nationalism assert national SUPERIORITY, which cannot be reconciled with the equality international cooperation presupposes.\n• Defining a nation creates outsiders, generating suspicion of foreigners and of supranational bodies.\n• Nationalism has repeatedly been mobilised AGAINST international institutions — Brexit and the EU being a contemporary case.\n• The post-1918 application of self-determination produced stranded minorities, irredentism and eventually war.\n\nGuidance for L5: the answer depends entirely on WHICH nationalism — the compatibility question cannot be answered for nationalism as a whole, which is itself evidence that it is not a single ideology. That move reaches L5.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2.` },

      { q: '"Nations are constructed rather than natural." Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the nationalist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `CONSTRUCTED (modernism):\n• Nations were produced by print capitalism, mass education, industrialisation and state-building, all of which are modern developments.\n• They are "imagined communities" — members never meet the overwhelming majority of their compatriots yet feel a shared bond, which must therefore be culturally produced.\n• National languages, histories and traditions were frequently STANDARDISED or invented by states in the nineteenth century.\n• National boundaries are historically contingent, and many nations postdate the states that created them.\n• If nations are recent constructions, the claim that they represent a NATURAL basis for political organisation is undermined.\n\nNATURAL (primordialism — what nationalists themselves assume):\n• Herder's Volksgeist treats nations as organic and pre-political, rooted in language and culture that long predate modern states.\n• Some national identities demonstrably predate print capitalism and industrialisation.\n• Shared language, history and territory are real, not invented.\n• The persistence and emotional force of national identity across centuries and through attempts at suppression suggests something deeper than construction.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest reply to modernism is that CONSTRUCTED IDENTITIES ARE ENTIRELY REAL IN THEIR EFFECTS — people demonstrably feel national loyalty and act on it, whatever its historical origin, and a political theory must deal with identities as they are rather than as their genealogy suggests they ought to be. That reaches L5.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2.` },
    ]
  },

  '3.5': {
    name: 'Feminism',
    questions: [
      { q: '\'Equality with men is the fundamental goal of all feminists.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the feminist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — students may analyse and evaluate:\n\nEQUALITY WITH MEN IS THE FUNDAMENTAL GOAL:\n• GILMAN: Women and Economics (1898) argued that women's economic dependence on men — the 'sexuo-economic relation' — had exaggerated sex differences; the goal was economic independence so that women could take part in productive life as men's equals.\n• DE BEAUVOIR: woman has been made 'the Other', defined in relation to man. Liberation means becoming an autonomous subject on the same footing as men, above all through paid work and economic independence; she was sceptical of celebrating a distinct feminine nature.\n• LIBERAL FEMINISM: equal rights, equal pay and equality of opportunity — the goal pursued by Wollstonecraft and Friedan (synoptic, liberalism) and embodied in UK equality legislation.\n\nEQUALITY WITH MEN IS NOT THE FUNDAMENTAL GOAL:\n• MILLETT and RADICAL FEMINISM: the aim is to abolish patriarchy and transform sexual politics in personal life and culture, not to win equality inside a male-defined order; 'equality with men' takes men as the standard.\n• hooks: asked which men women want to be equal to, since poor and black men are themselves oppressed. For hooks, feminism's goal is to end sexist oppression and the interlocking systems of domination of race, class and gender.\n• ROWBOTHAM: liberation requires transforming capitalism and the way work, the family and childcare are organised, not equality within existing structures.\n• DIFFERENCE FEMINISM: some feminists aim to value women's distinctive experiences and qualities rather than to make women the same as men.\n\nSynoptic links: the Equal Pay Act 1970, Sex Discrimination Act 1975 and Equality Act 2010; gender pay gap reporting for large employers since 2017; a record 263 women MPs (about 40%) elected in 2024; liberalism and socialism.\n\nJudgement: All feminists want an end to women's subordination, so some form of equality is shared. But 'equality with men' is the liberal formulation; radical feminists, socialist feminists and hooks aim at liberation from systems of domination, which may require changing what men's lives look like as well. The stronger conclusion is that liberation, not equality with men as they are, is the goal that unites feminism.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and evaluating the statement through accurate use of the specified feminist thinkers (Gilman, de Beauvoir, Millett, Rowbotham, hooks) and weighing the competing arguments to a substantiated conclusion; it does not matter which view is reached, provided it is supported. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant, accurate points should be credited.` },

      { q: '\'Liberal feminism has achieved more for women than radical feminism.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the feminist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — students may analyse and evaluate:\n\nLIBERAL FEMINISM HAS ACHIEVED MORE:\n• LEGAL GAINS: the vote, access to education and the professions, the Equal Pay Act 1970, the Sex Discrimination Act 1975 and the Equality Act 2010 all reflect the liberal strategy of reform through law and within existing institutions. Friedan co-founded the National Organization for Women in 1966 (synoptic).\n• GILMAN: her case for women's economic independence through paid work has been substantially realised through mass female employment, the central liberal aim of equal access to public life.\n• BROAD APPEAL: working within liberal-democratic institutions built wide support, whereas radical proposals for transforming the family and sexuality remained marginal and provoked backlash.\n\nRADICAL FEMINISM HAS ACHIEVED MORE:\n• MILLETT and 'THE PERSONAL IS POLITICAL': radical feminism made domestic violence, marital rape, sexual harassment and reproductive control into public political questions. Marital rape was recognised as a crime in England and Wales in R v R (1991); coercive control became an offence in 2015 and the Domestic Abuse Act followed in 2021.\n• ROWBOTHAM and socialist feminists exposed unpaid domestic labour and the lack of childcare — inequalities that equal-rights law left untouched — and recovered women's history (Hidden from History, 1973).\n• hooks: the gains of liberal feminism flowed mainly to white, middle-class women; Friedan's Feminine Mystique ignored women who already did low-paid work.\n• LIMITS OF LIBERAL SUCCESS: the persistent gender pay gap and unequal share of domestic work despite formal equality suggest liberal reforms have not reached the roots of inequality.\n\nSynoptic links: the Equality Act 2010; the Domestic Abuse Act 2021; #MeToo (2017) and pressure-group campaigning; representation of women in Parliament; liberalism and the public/private divide.\n\nJudgement: Liberal feminism secured the most concrete legal victories, but many later reforms came from issues radical feminists first politicised, so the two strands worked in combination. The strongest answers argue that liberal feminism achieved formal equality, while radical feminism explains why formal equality has not produced substantive equality and widened what counts as political.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and evaluating the statement through accurate use of the specified feminist thinkers (Gilman, de Beauvoir, Millett, Rowbotham, hooks) and weighing the competing arguments to a substantiated conclusion; it does not matter which view is reached, provided it is supported. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant, accurate points should be credited.` },

      { q: '\'The family is the main source of women\'s oppression.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the feminist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — students may analyse and evaluate:\n\nTHE FAMILY IS THE MAIN SOURCE:\n• MILLETT: Sexual Politics (1970) presented the family as patriarchy's chief institution, socialising children into the roles, temperament and status of their sex and linking private life to patriarchy in wider society.\n• GILMAN: women's subordination was rooted in economic dependence on husbands and confinement to domestic work; she proposed kitchenless homes, communal kitchens and professional childcare to free women from it.\n• DE BEAUVOIR: marriage and motherhood presented as woman's natural destiny confine her to repetitive domestic life and prevent her from becoming a free, self-defining subject.\n• ROWBOTHAM: unpaid domestic labour within the family reproduces the workforce at no cost to employers, so the family is where patriarchy and capitalism meet.\n\nTHE FAMILY IS NOT THE MAIN SOURCE:\n• SOCIALIST FEMINISM: for Rowbotham the deeper source is capitalism, which shapes the family to its needs; the family is a mechanism of oppression rather than its root.\n• hooks: for many black women the home was a place of resistance to racism rather than the primary site of oppression, so treating the family as the main source generalises from white, middle-class experience.\n• PATRIARCHY IS EVERYWHERE: Millett herself traced patriarchy through literature, religion, education and the economy, and liberal feminists such as Friedan concentrated on discrimination in education and employment.\n\nSynoptic links: shared parental leave (introduced 2015) and childcare funding; the Domestic Abuse Act 2021; conservatism's defence of the traditional family; socialism's analysis of capitalism.\n\nJudgement: The family is a central site of women's oppression for radical feminists, and every specified thinker treats it as important. Whether it is the main source depends on the strand: socialist feminists see it as serving capitalism, and hooks shows its meaning varies with race and class. The strongest conclusion is that the family is a key mechanism through which oppression is reproduced rather than its single source.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and evaluating the statement through accurate use of the specified feminist thinkers (Gilman, de Beauvoir, Millett, Rowbotham, hooks) and weighing the competing arguments to a substantiated conclusion; it does not matter which view is reached, provided it is supported. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant, accurate points should be credited.` },

      { q: '\'Patriarchy is a universal feature of human societies.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the feminist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — students may analyse and evaluate:\n\nPATRIARCHY IS UNIVERSAL:\n• MILLETT: patriarchy is found in every historical civilisation and survives across different economic and political systems; it is the most pervasive ideology of our culture and its most fundamental concept of power.\n• DE BEAUVOIR: throughout history woman has been constituted as the Other, defined in relation to man as the norm; no society she examined treated women as full subjects on equal terms with men.\n• PERSISTENCE ACROSS SYSTEMS: male dominance survived in state socialist as well as capitalist societies, which radical feminists take as evidence that patriarchy is not reducible to any economic system.\n\nPATRIARCHY IS NOT UNIVERSAL (OR NOT UNIFORM):\n• ROWBOTHAM: in 'The Trouble with "Patriarchy"' (1979) she warned that the term implies a single, fixed, universal structure and obscures how relations between men and women vary historically and are bound up with class.\n• GILMAN: treated women's dependence as a product of social and economic evolution — the sexuo-economic relation — that could be outgrown, rather than a permanent feature of all societies.\n• hooks: patriarchy is not experienced in the same way by all women; it interlocks with race and class, and some men are themselves subordinated. Claims of a universal patriarchy often generalise from white women's experience.\n• UNIVERSAL IS NOT NATURAL: all feminists reject biological determinism, so even if male dominance has been widespread it is socially produced and can be changed.\n\nSynoptic links: variation in women's legal status between states (for example restrictions on women in Afghanistan since 2021 compared with Nordic states); UK equality legislation; conservatism's view of natural hierarchy.\n\nJudgement: Male dominance has been extremely widespread, which gives Millett and de Beauvoir strong support, but its forms vary greatly between societies, classes and races. The strongest conclusion is that patriarchy is near-universal but historically variable. Answers may note the danger in the universal claim: if patriarchy is everywhere and always, it risks looking natural, which would undermine the sex/gender distinction on which feminism rests.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and evaluating the statement through accurate use of the specified feminist thinkers (Gilman, de Beauvoir, Millett, Rowbotham, hooks) and weighing the competing arguments to a substantiated conclusion; it does not matter which view is reached, provided it is supported. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant, accurate points should be credited.` },

      { q: '\'Feminism has been too focused on the experiences of white, middle-class women.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the feminist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — students may analyse and evaluate:\n\nTOO FOCUSED ON WHITE, MIDDLE-CLASS WOMEN:\n• hooks: Ain't I a Woman? (1981) and Feminist Theory: From Margin to Center (1984) argued that mainstream feminism presented white, middle-class concerns as universal. Friedan's 'problem that has no name' ignored women who already worked, often in low-paid jobs.\n• GILMAN: her writing contains racist and nativist views, and her programme assumed the position of educated, economically comfortable women.\n• LIBERAL GAINS: access to the professions, boardrooms and public office has disproportionately benefited better-off women, while low-paid, part-time and care work — disproportionately done by working-class and ethnic-minority women — has changed far less.\n\nNOT TOO FOCUSED ON WHITE, MIDDLE-CLASS WOMEN:\n• ROWBOTHAM: socialist feminism centred working-class women, their paid and unpaid labour and their history (Hidden from History, 1973).\n• MILLETT: radical feminism analysed patriarchy as an oppression common to all women, providing the basis for sisterhood across class and race — though hooks would reply that this assumed a shared experience that did not exist.\n• DE BEAUVOIR: The Second Sex compared women's situation with other oppressed groups and sought a general account of women's condition rather than one confined to a class.\n• INTERSECTIONALITY is now central to feminism: hooks's critique has been absorbed into the ideology rather than standing outside it.\n\nSynoptic links: the Equality Act 2010 (its dual-discrimination provision, section 14, was never brought into force); representation of women from ethnic minorities in Parliament; pressure groups; liberalism and socialism.\n\nJudgement: The charge is strongest against liberal second-wave feminism, which hooks showed universalised a narrow experience. Socialist feminism (Rowbotham) and intersectional feminism (hooks) corrected it, so the statement is more true of feminism's past than its present. A strong conclusion might argue that feminism's capacity to absorb hooks's critique shows the ideology can be self-correcting, even if solidarity now rests on a shared political project rather than a single shared experience.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and evaluating the statement through accurate use of the specified feminist thinkers (Gilman, de Beauvoir, Millett, Rowbotham, hooks) and weighing the competing arguments to a substantiated conclusion; it does not matter which view is reached, provided it is supported. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant, accurate points should be credited.` },

      { q: '\'Women\'s liberation cannot be achieved within capitalism.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the feminist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — students may analyse and evaluate:\n\nLIBERATION CANNOT BE ACHIEVED WITHIN CAPITALISM:\n• ROWBOTHAM: capitalism depends on women's unpaid domestic labour, which reproduces the workforce, and on women as a flexible, low-paid reserve of labour. Women's liberation therefore requires socialist transformation, though socialism must address women's specific oppression.\n• GILMAN: women's subordination was economic in origin, and her solution — socialising cooking, cleaning and childcare — drew on the socialist thought of her day rather than on market provision.\n• hooks: capitalism is one of the interlocking systems of domination; a feminism that seeks equality for some women within capitalism leaves poor women, and especially poor black women, oppressed.\n\nLIBERATION CAN BE ACHIEVED WITHIN CAPITALISM:\n• MILLETT and RADICAL FEMINISM: patriarchy predates capitalism and survived in state socialist societies, so abolishing capitalism would not by itself end women's oppression — the problem is patriarchy, not the economic system.\n• LIBERAL FEMINISM: huge gains have been made within capitalist liberal democracies — employment, equal pay law, reproductive rights — and market economies provide the economic independence Gilman and de Beauvoir saw as the basis of freedom.\n• DE BEAUVOIR: stressed economic independence through paid work as the condition of freedom; that independence has been achieved by many women under capitalism, although she herself looked to socialism.\n• STATE SOCIALISM: the Soviet Union brought women into paid work but left them with a 'double burden' of domestic labour, suggesting the economic system is not the decisive variable.\n\nSynoptic links: the gender pay gap; the expansion of funded childcare in England from 2024; the Equality Act 2010; socialism (Marx and Engels, Crosland) and liberalism.\n\nJudgement: Capitalism has allowed significant advances in women's legal and economic position, which weakens the strong socialist claim. But socialist feminists and hooks show that those advances are unevenly distributed and that unpaid care still falls mainly on women. The strongest conclusion is that ending capitalism is neither sufficient (the radical feminist point) nor clearly necessary, but that full liberation for all women requires changes to how work and care are organised that go beyond what market economies have so far delivered.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and evaluating the statement through accurate use of the specified feminist thinkers (Gilman, de Beauvoir, Millett, Rowbotham, hooks) and weighing the competing arguments to a substantiated conclusion; it does not matter which view is reached, provided it is supported. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant, accurate points should be credited.` },

      { q: '"Feminists disagree more than they agree." Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the feminist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `DISAGREE:\n• Incompatible FOUNDATIONS — liberal feminism rests on liberal individualism, socialist feminism on a Marxist analysis of capitalism, radical feminism on the primacy of patriarchy. These are not variations but different theoretical systems.\n• Different DIAGNOSES — legal discrimination, capitalism, or patriarchy as the root cause — and therefore different remedies.\n• The PUBLIC/PRIVATE divide — liberal feminism accepts it, radical feminism rejects it as the mechanism of concealment.\n• EQUALITY versus DIFFERENCE — whether to minimise or value sex difference.\n• Whether "WOMAN" is even a stable category — postmodern feminists and intersectional theorists question the shared subject on which the movement depends.\n\nAGREE:\n• All hold women are SYSTEMATICALLY DISADVANTAGED because of their sex.\n• All hold this disadvantage is SOCIALLY PRODUCED rather than natural — all accept the sex/gender distinction and reject biological determinism.\n• All hold it SHOULD BE ENDED.\n• All reject the idea that existing arrangements are just or inevitable.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest conclusion is that solidarity can rest on a shared POLITICAL PROJECT rather than an assumed identity of experience — arguably a firmer foundation than the false universalism hooks exposed. An answer that treats the disagreements as fatal, without addressing what the strands share, caps at L3.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2.` },

      { q: '"The personal is political." Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the feminist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `THE CLAIM IS CORRECT:\n• Private-sphere relationships are structured by POWER — who does the housework, how childcare is distributed, the dynamics of marriage, sexuality and reproduction are instances of a wider system of male dominance.\n• Confining political concern to the public realm EXEMPTED from scrutiny the sphere in which most women's lives were lived and most of their subordination occurred.\n• It made domestic violence, marital rape, unpaid domestic labour, reproductive rights and sexual harassment into political questions requiring public remedy — a transformation in what counts as political.\n• Consciousness-raising rested on the insight that individual experiences shared across many women revealed a STRUCTURAL pattern.\n• It explains why legal equality in the public sphere has not produced substantive equality.\n\nOBJECTIONS:\n• LIBERALS value the private sphere as a zone of freedom protected from state intrusion, and worry that politicising it licenses surveillance of personal life.\n• The distinction between public and private protects individual autonomy, including women's.\n• Taken to its limit, no aspect of life remains free of political scrutiny.\n• Liberal feminists such as Friedan achieved substantial gains focusing on the PUBLIC sphere.\n• It risks treating all personal relationships as sites of oppression.\n\nGuidance for L5: the feminist reply to the liberal objection is decisive and should appear — the private sphere was never genuinely free FOR WOMEN, and a neutrality that leaves existing domestic power untouched is not neutrality but endorsement of the status quo.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2.` },

      { q: '"Feminism is a coherent ideology." Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the feminist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `COHERENT:\n• All strands share three core claims — women are systematically disadvantaged because of their sex; this is socially produced rather than natural; and it should be ended.\n• All accept the SEX/GENDER distinction and reject biological determinism, which is a substantive shared theoretical commitment.\n• All identify structures rather than individual prejudice as the cause.\n• Shared concepts — patriarchy, gender, the critique of the public/private divide — provide a common analytical vocabulary.\n• Ideologies are generally internally contested; liberalism and socialism contain comparable divisions.\n\nNOT COHERENT:\n• The strands rest on INCOMPATIBLE FOUNDATIONS — liberal individualism, Marxist class analysis, and the primacy of patriarchy cannot all be true.\n• They diagnose different causes and prescribe incompatible remedies — reform within structures versus their abolition.\n• Intersectional and postmodern feminism question whether "WOMAN" is a stable category at all, which threatens the collective subject on whose behalf feminism claims to act.\n• Difference and equality feminism give opposite answers to what equal treatment requires.\n• Some strands regard men as structural opponents; others seek integration.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest resolution is that solidarity rests on a shared POLITICAL PROJECT rather than a shared theory or a common experience — which is a weaker but more defensible basis for coherence than the false universalism hooks exposed.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2.` },

      { q: '"Sex and gender are separate concepts." Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the feminist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `SEPARATE:\n• Sex refers to BIOLOGICAL difference; gender to the SOCIAL roles, behaviours and expectations attached to those categories.\n• Gender roles vary enormously across societies and over time, which biological difference cannot explain.\n• De Beauvoir: one is not born but BECOMES a woman — femininity is imposed through culture, not a consequence of biology.\n• The distinction is politically essential: it undermines the argument that women's subordinate position is natural and therefore inevitable. If roles are constructed, they can be changed.\n• It is the analytical foundation of most feminist thought.\n\nNOT ENTIRELY SEPARATE / contested:\n• Some DIFFERENCE feminists argue genuine differences exist — particularly around reproduction and caring — that should be recognised and valued rather than erased.\n• Biology does impose some real constraints, notably pregnancy and childbirth, which policy must accommodate (maternity provision treats women differently precisely to enable equal participation).\n• POSTMODERN feminists question whether "sex" is itself an unmediated biological category rather than one already shaped by social interpretation, which collapses the distinction from the other direction.\n• Essentialist accounts hold that gender differences have some biological basis.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest analysis notes that the distinction is doing POLITICAL work as well as analytical work — it was developed precisely to defeat naturalistic justifications of inequality — and that both difference feminism and postmodern feminism destabilise it, though from opposite directions.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2.` },
    ]
  },

  '3.6': {
    name: 'Multiculturalism',
    questions: [
      { q: '\'Recognition of cultural identity is essential to individual well-being.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the multiculturalist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — students may analyse and evaluate:\n\nRECOGNITION IS ESSENTIAL:\n• TAYLOR: 'The Politics of Recognition' (1992) argues that identity is formed in dialogue with others, so misrecognition or non-recognition can inflict real harm, confining people to a reduced and demeaning view of themselves. Due recognition is a vital human need.\n• KYMLICKA: culture provides the 'context of choice' — the framework within which options have meaning — so secure membership of one's culture is a condition of meaningful individual autonomy.\n• PAREKH: humans are culturally embedded beings whose self-understanding is shaped by their culture; denying or belittling that culture damages self-respect.\n• MODOOD: equal citizenship requires public recognition of group identities, including religious identities such as being Muslim, not merely private toleration.\n\nRECOGNITION IS NOT ESSENTIAL:\n• BERLIN: freedom to choose between plural and competing goods is what matters. In 'Two Concepts of Liberty' (1958) he warned that the search for status and recognition can lead people to prefer rule by members of their own group over individual freedom.\n• LIBERAL CRITICS: identity is individual, fluid and partly chosen; emphasising group recognition may trap individuals in ascribed identities — a danger Kymlicka acknowledges in rejecting internal restrictions.\n• TOLERATION AND EQUAL RIGHTS may be enough for well-being: Locke's toleration and Mill's harm principle (synoptic, liberalism) protect cultural practice without public recognition.\n\nSynoptic links: the Equality Act 2010 (religion and race as protected characteristics); state-funded faith schools; identity politics and voting behaviour; liberalism.\n\nJudgement: Taylor, Kymlicka, Parekh and Modood give strong reasons to think that cultural belonging and respect matter for well-being, but they disagree about whether recognition must be public and group-based. Berlin and liberal critics show the risk that recognition subordinates individual freedom to group identity. The strongest conclusion is that recognition is important but not sufficient: it supports well-being only when combined with the individual's freedom to question or leave their culture, which is Kymlicka's position.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and evaluating the statement through accurate use of the specified multiculturalist thinkers (Berlin, Taylor, Kymlicka, Modood, Parekh) and weighing the competing arguments to a substantiated conclusion; it does not matter which view is reached, provided it is supported. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant, accurate points should be credited.` },

      { q: '\'Pluralist multiculturalism offers a more convincing response to diversity than liberal multiculturalism.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the multiculturalist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — students may analyse and evaluate:\n\nPLURALIST MULTICULTURALISM IS MORE CONVINCING:\n• PAREKH: liberalism is itself one cultural tradition, not a neutral standard, so judging all cultures by liberal criteria imposes one culture's values. He favours intercultural dialogue and a 'community of communities' held together by operative public values that can themselves change.\n• BERLIN: value pluralism — there are many genuine goods that cannot be ranked on one scale — gives philosophical support to deep diversity, since no single culture has a monopoly on the good life.\n• TAYLOR: difference-blind liberalism is not neutral but reflects the majority culture; distinct societies such as Quebec need public support to survive.\n\nLIBERAL MULTICULTURALISM IS MORE CONVINCING:\n• KYMLICKA: group-differentiated rights justified by individual autonomy, with external protections for minorities but no internal restrictions on members — protecting vulnerable individuals such as women and dissenters within minority groups.\n• MODOOD: a civic multiculturalism within shared citizenship and a strong, inclusive national identity, with moderate secularism, balances diversity and unity.\n• RELATIVISM: pluralism may leave no ground to criticise illiberal practices; the UK has criminalised forced marriage (2014) and female genital mutilation, reflecting liberal limits to toleration.\n• BERLIN himself remained a liberal and believed there were basic values common to humanity, which suggests value pluralism need not lead beyond liberalism.\n\nSynoptic links: the Human Rights Act 1998 and Equality Act 2010; the Parekh Report (2000); debates about faith schools and religious exemptions; liberalism (Mill, Rawls) and conservatism.\n\nJudgement: Pluralist multiculturalism is more faithful to the depth of cultural difference and exposes the cultural bias of supposedly neutral liberalism. Liberal multiculturalism is more convincing as a basis for policy because it gives a principled way to protect individuals within groups. The strongest conclusion is that Kymlicka resolves most practical dilemmas within liberalism, while Parekh's pluralism is better described as post-liberal — and that the choice depends on whether individual autonomy is treated as a universal value or as one culture's value.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and evaluating the statement through accurate use of the specified multiculturalist thinkers (Berlin, Taylor, Kymlicka, Modood, Parekh) and weighing the competing arguments to a substantiated conclusion; it does not matter which view is reached, provided it is supported. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant, accurate points should be credited.` },

      { q: '\'Multiculturalists agree that the state should actively support minority cultures.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the multiculturalist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — students may analyse and evaluate:\n\nTHEY AGREE:\n• KYMLICKA: Multicultural Citizenship (1995) defends self-government rights for national minorities, polyethnic rights (such as funding and exemptions) for immigrant groups, and special representation rights.\n• TAYLOR: the politics of recognition requires public acknowledgement and, where a culture's survival is at stake, positive support — for example Quebec's measures to protect the French language.\n• PAREKH: the Parekh Report (The Future of Multi-Ethnic Britain, 2000) urged that Britain see itself as a 'community of communities' and that public institutions actively accommodate diversity.\n• MODOOD: equal citizenship requires accommodating religious minorities within existing arrangements, such as state funding for Muslim schools on the same basis as Anglican, Catholic and Jewish schools.\n\nTHEY DISAGREE:\n• BERLIN: a liberal pluralist rather than an advocate of group rights; his negative liberty implies a state that tolerates many ways of life rather than one that promotes particular cultures.\n• KYMLICKA distinguishes between groups: stronger support for national minorities than for immigrant groups, and none for internal restrictions — support is conditional, not general.\n• PAREKH and MODOOD make support conditional on shared civic commitments — Parekh's operative public values, Modood's inclusive national identity — so they differ from Taylor on how far survival of a culture is itself a goal.\n\nSynoptic links: state-funded faith schools (the first state-funded Muslim schools in 1998); Welsh language policy and devolution; religious exemptions (for example for turbans); the Human Rights Act; liberalism and conservatism.\n\nJudgement: Most specified multiculturalists (Taylor, Kymlicka, Parekh, Modood) agree that toleration is not enough and that the state should make some positive accommodation for minorities, while Berlin does not go this far. They disagree about which groups deserve support, how much, and on what conditions. The strongest conclusion is that there is agreement on the principle of accommodation but significant disagreement on its extent and justification.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and evaluating the statement through accurate use of the specified multiculturalist thinkers (Berlin, Taylor, Kymlicka, Modood, Parekh) and weighing the competing arguments to a substantiated conclusion; it does not matter which view is reached, provided it is supported. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant, accurate points should be credited.` },

      { q: '\'Multiculturalism treats cultures as more fixed and uniform than they really are.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the multiculturalist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — students may analyse and evaluate:\n\nIT DOES TREAT CULTURES AS FIXED AND UNIFORM:\n• ESSENTIALISM CRITIQUE: group rights assume bounded, internally uniform cultures, which can freeze traditions and empower conservative 'community leaders' who claim to speak for everyone.\n• TAYLOR: making the survival of a culture a political goal (as in Quebec) implies preserving it over time for future members, which can restrict how individuals revise it.\n• KYMLICKA: sorting people into national minorities and polyethnic groups risks treating cultures as discrete units with clear boundaries, though identities are often mixed and overlapping.\n\nIT DOES NOT:\n• PAREKH: cultures are internally plural, porous and constantly evolving; intercultural dialogue changes both majority and minority cultures, and multiculturalism is a process, not the preservation of fixed blocs.\n• MODOOD: hyphenated identities such as British-Muslim show individuals holding several identities at once and choosing how far to emphasise each.\n• KYMLICKA rejects internal restrictions, so individuals remain free to question, revise or leave their culture.\n• BERLIN: value pluralism recognises conflict between goods within cultures as well as between them, so no culture is a single harmonious whole.\n\nSynoptic links: the Casey Review (2016) on segregation; consultation with faith and community bodies; census ethnic categories; feminist critiques of group rights; liberalism's individualism.\n\nJudgement: The criticism has real force against policies that deal with communities through self-appointed leaders, and some multiculturalist arguments (Taylor on survival) do assume a relatively stable culture. But Parekh and Modood explicitly treat cultures as dynamic and identities as multiple, so the charge applies more to some policies than to multiculturalist theory as a whole. A strong conclusion distinguishes the theory from its implementation.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and evaluating the statement through accurate use of the specified multiculturalist thinkers (Berlin, Taylor, Kymlicka, Modood, Parekh) and weighing the competing arguments to a substantiated conclusion; it does not matter which view is reached, provided it is supported. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant, accurate points should be credited.` },

      { q: '\'Toleration, not recognition, is all that a diverse society requires.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the multiculturalist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — students may analyse and evaluate:\n\nTOLERATION IS ENOUGH:\n• LIBERAL TRADITION: Locke's Letter Concerning Toleration (1689) and Mill's harm principle (synoptic, liberalism) — the state stays neutral, cultural and religious life is private, and individuals have equal rights.\n• BERLIN: negative liberty and value pluralism justify tolerating many ways of life without the state endorsing any; pluralism is protected by limiting state power, not by recognition.\n• RECOGNITION CAN DIVIDE: critics argue group recognition entrenches separate identities and competition between groups, while equal rights and anti-discrimination law (the Equality Act 2010) already protect minorities.\n\nRECOGNITION IS ALSO REQUIRED:\n• TAYLOR: difference-blind toleration is not neutral; misrecognition harms, and minorities need their identities publicly affirmed as of equal worth.\n• MODOOD: toleration treats minorities as outsiders whom the majority puts up with; equal citizenship requires recognising them as full members and accommodating religious identity in public life.\n• PAREKH: toleration assumes the majority's standards as the norm; mutual respect and intercultural dialogue are needed.\n• KYMLICKA: 'benign neglect' is impossible, because the state inevitably favours the majority's language, holidays and symbols; fairness therefore requires group-differentiated rights.\n\nSynoptic links: the Equality Act 2010; religious exemptions and faith schools; the Human Rights Act (freedom of religion); liberalism (Locke, Mill, Rawls) and conservatism.\n\nJudgement: Toleration protects freedom of cultural practice but leaves the majority culture as the unspoken norm, as Kymlicka and Taylor show. Recognition addresses that bias but risks entrenching group identities over individual freedom. The strongest conclusion is that toleration is a necessary foundation but, in societies with established majority cultures, not sufficient for equal citizenship — which is the core claim that separates multiculturalism from classical liberalism.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and evaluating the statement through accurate use of the specified multiculturalist thinkers (Berlin, Taylor, Kymlicka, Modood, Parekh) and weighing the competing arguments to a substantiated conclusion; it does not matter which view is reached, provided it is supported. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant, accurate points should be credited.` },

      { q: '\'Assimilation is a more effective response to cultural diversity than multiculturalism.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the multiculturalist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — students may analyse and evaluate:\n\nASSIMILATION IS MORE EFFECTIVE:\n• SOCIAL COHESION: a shared language, culture and values build trust and underpin support for the welfare state; the French republican model and laïcité (including the 2004 ban on conspicuous religious symbols in state schools) reflect this.\n• POLICY RETREAT: David Cameron's Munich speech (2011) argued that 'state multiculturalism' had encouraged separate lives, and schools in England have been required since 2014 to promote 'fundamental British values'.\n• EVEN MULTICULTURALISTS ACCEPT SHARED FOUNDATIONS: Parekh's operative public values and Modood's call for a strong, inclusive national identity suggest a common framework is essential.\n\nMULTICULTURALISM IS MORE EFFECTIVE:\n• TAYLOR: forced assimilation is a form of misrecognition that damages people's identity and self-respect.\n• PAREKH: assimilation asks minorities to abandon their identity to fit a majority culture that is itself only one tradition; it is unjust and, in practice, provokes alienation.\n• KYMLICKA: assimilation of national minorities and indigenous peoples — for example Canada's residential schools — caused lasting harm; Canada's multiculturalism policy (1971) and Multiculturalism Act (1988) offer an alternative.\n• MODOOD: integration must be two-way; demanding assimilation denies Muslims and other minorities equal citizenship and can deepen division. Berlin's value pluralism adds that no single culture embodies the whole of the good.\n\nSynoptic links: the Cameron speech (2011) and 'British values' in schools; the Casey Review (2016); the Equality Act 2010; conservatism's emphasis on shared tradition; liberalism.\n\nJudgement: Assimilation may promote a shared identity but at the cost of misrecognition and alienation, and it assumes that minorities can and should abandon their cultures. Multiculturalism respects identity but needs common civic foundations to hold society together. The strongest conclusion is that the real choice is not assimilation versus multiculturalism but how to combine a shared civic identity with recognition of difference, which is the position of Parekh and Modood.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and evaluating the statement through accurate use of the specified multiculturalist thinkers (Berlin, Taylor, Kymlicka, Modood, Parekh) and weighing the competing arguments to a substantiated conclusion; it does not matter which view is reached, provided it is supported. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant, accurate points should be credited.` },

      { q: '"Multiculturalism is compatible with liberalism." Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the multiculturalist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `COMPATIBLE:\n• Kymlicka's argument is INTERNAL to liberalism — he begins from individual autonomy, the core liberal value, not from the value of groups. Culture supplies the CONTEXT OF CHOICE, so protecting minority cultures is REQUIRED BY liberalism rather than a concession to it.\n• His EXTERNAL PROTECTIONS / INTERNAL RESTRICTIONS distinction answers the standard objection: liberals can defend a minority against the wider society while refusing to let it coerce its own dissenting members.\n• Both value toleration and diversity; multiculturalism developed largely out of liberal concern for equal treatment.\n• Berlin's value pluralism is itself a liberal position.\n• Recognising that minorities lack the secure cultural context majorities enjoy automatically is an EQUALITY argument.\n\nINCOMPATIBLE:\n• Liberalism locates rights in INDIVIDUALS; conferring them on groups empowers community leaders over dissenting members — the gender critique (Okin) is the sharpest form.\n• Taylor argues liberal NEUTRALITY is illusory and must be abandoned for positive recognition, which challenges a foundational liberal commitment.\n• Parekh's PLURALIST version accords value to non-liberal cultures in their own terms, treating liberalism as one tradition among others — which departs from the liberal framework rather than extending it.\n• Liberalism values the capacity to REVISE inherited values; some practices are premised on transmission rather than examination.\n\nGuidance for L5: the answer depends on WHICH multiculturalism — liberal multiculturalism is compatible, pluralist multiculturalism is post-liberal. Making that distinction explicitly is what reaches L5.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2.` },

      { q: '"Multiculturalism undermines social cohesion." Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the multiculturalist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `UNDERMINES:\n• Conservatives argue a stable society requires shared culture, common values and mutual obligation; endorsing separate identities produces "parallel lives" and communities living alongside one another without meaningful interaction.\n• It may erode the shared identity that sustains willingness to fund a welfare state — a material as well as a symbolic consequence.\n• Essentialism — treating cultures as bounded and homogeneous, and requiring "community representatives", entrenches separateness and privileges conservative voices.\n• Several European states retreated from multicultural policy after the 2000s, citing exactly these concerns.\n• Group rights can create resentment among the majority.\n\nDOES NOT UNDERMINE:\n• The causal claim is contested — ALIENATION results from exclusion and discrimination rather than from recognition, and people whose identity is respected are MORE likely to identify with the wider society.\n• Parekh's DIALOGICAL model addresses the objection directly by constructing shared values through negotiation between communities rather than assuming or imposing them.\n• Modood's multicultural citizenship builds an inclusive national identity continually remade to include minorities.\n• Assimilationist policies have their own record of generating resentment and failure.\n• Cosmopolitan multiculturalism produces hybrid identities that connect rather than separate communities.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest analysis notes the counterfactual problem — cohesion difficulties may be caused by exclusion rather than by multicultural policy, and the evidence cannot easily distinguish the two. Naming that difficulty reaches L5.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2.` },

      { q: '"Group rights are incompatible with individual rights." Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the multiculturalist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `INCOMPATIBLE:\n• Rights properly attach to INDIVIDUALS; conferring them on groups empowers self-appointed community leaders over dissenting members.\n• The GENDER critique (Okin) is the sharpest case — practices subordinating women may be shielded in the name of cultural respect, so protecting the group comes at the expense of its most vulnerable members.\n• Children may be denied education or exit options in the name of cultural transmission.\n• Liberalism values the capacity to REVISE inherited values; group rights can entrench practices premised on transmission rather than examination.\n• Group rights presuppose cultures are homogeneous, which suppresses internal dissent.\n\nCOMPATIBLE:\n• KYMLICKA's argument is decisive here — culture supplies the CONTEXT OF CHOICE within which individuals exercise autonomy, so protecting it SERVES individual rights rather than competing with them.\n• His EXTERNAL PROTECTIONS / INTERNAL RESTRICTIONS distinction directly resolves the objection: shield a minority from the wider society, but refuse to let it coerce its own members.\n• Members of majority cultures enjoy a secure cultural context automatically; group rights correct an INEQUALITY between individuals.\n• Many group rights (language rights, exemptions) impose no constraint on group members at all.\n• Taylor argues recognition is a vital HUMAN need, so denying it harms individuals.\n\nGuidance for L5: Kymlicka's distinction is the strongest available material and should be applied, not merely stated — the objection succeeds against PLURALIST multiculturalism, which declines to judge cultures by liberal standards, far more than against the liberal version.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2.` },

      { q: '"Multiculturalism is a coherent ideology." Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the multiculturalist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `COHERENT:\n• All strands REJECT ASSIMILATION and hold that diversity is valuable rather than merely tolerable.\n• All hold that identity has legitimate PUBLIC standing rather than being a purely private matter.\n• All accept CULTURAL EMBEDDEDNESS — that people are formed within cultures that shape values and meaning.\n• Shared concepts — recognition, minority rights, the critique of difference-blind liberalism — provide a common analytical vocabulary.\n• It offers positions on identity, the state, rights and social organisation, which are the usual tests for an ideology.\n\nNOT COHERENT:\n• The strands pull in INCOMPATIBLE directions — liberal multiculturalism tolerates diversity only within liberal limits, which pluralists call disguised assimilation; pluralist multiculturalism accords value to non-liberal cultures but then struggles to object to practices harming members.\n• COSMOPOLITAN multiculturalism treats cultures as optional resources for individuals, which DISSOLVES the group dimension that the politics of recognition existed to address — so it is arguably not multiculturalism at all.\n• It lacks a distinctive account of the economy, human nature or the state comparable to liberalism or socialism.\n• It may be better understood as a corrective WITHIN other ideologies than as a free-standing one.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest conclusion notes the parallel with NATIONALISM and ECOLOGISM — a distinctive set of premises that must combine with a prior political theory to generate a programme. That comparative move reaches L5.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2.` },
    ]
  },

  '3.7': {
    name: 'Anarchism',
    questions: [
      { q: '\'The state is by its nature oppressive and can never be legitimate.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the anarchist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — students may analyse and evaluate:\n\nTHE STATE IS INHERENTLY OPPRESSIVE:\n• BAKUNIN: every state, including a workers' state, is an instrument of domination by a minority; a Marxist 'dictatorship of the proletariat' would produce a new ruling bureaucracy rather than wither away. He treated church and state as twin pillars of authority.\n• PROUDHON: government is coercive by its nature — to be governed is to be watched, directed and taxed without consent — and the social contract is a fiction that cannot make it legitimate.\n• STIRNER: the state is a 'spook' that demands the ego's submission; its laws express its own will, and the individual owes it no duty of obedience.\n• KROPOTKIN: The State: Its Historic Role (1897) argued the state arose to protect the privileges of landowners, the military and the Church, destroying free associations such as the medieval city.\n\nTHE STATE CAN BE LEGITIMATE:\n• CONSENT AND DEMOCRACY: liberals (Locke) argue legitimacy comes from consent, elections and the rule of law; accountable democratic states are not simply instruments of domination.\n• THE STATE CAN EMANCIPATE: social democratic states provide welfare, healthcare and education that reduce inequality (synoptic: Webb, Crosland), and conservatives from Hobbes onwards argue the state is necessary for order.\n• ANARCHIST COMPROMISES: Proudhon sat in the French Constituent Assembly in 1848; Kropotkin supported the Allied war effort in the First World War; CNT ministers joined the Spanish Republican government in 1936 — suggesting that in practice anarchists sometimes treated some states as less bad than others.\n\nSynoptic links: the UK constitution, elections and the rule of law; the Human Rights Act; state surveillance and policing powers; liberalism (consent), conservatism (Hobbes) and socialism.\n\nJudgement: Anarchists agree the state is coercive by nature and that coercion cannot be made legitimate by consent fictions — this is the one principle that unites them. The strongest counter-argument is that democratic states can be accountable and emancipatory, and anarchists' own compromises suggest a practical distinction between states. A strong answer can conclude that the anarchist case is most convincing as a critique of the state's claim to authority and least convincing as a claim that all states are equally oppressive.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and evaluating the statement through accurate use of the specified anarchist thinkers (Stirner, Proudhon, Bakunin, Kropotkin, Goldman) and weighing the competing arguments to a substantiated conclusion; it does not matter which view is reached, provided it is supported. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant, accurate points should be credited.` },

      { q: '\'Individual liberty matters more to anarchists than economic equality.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the anarchist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — students may analyse and evaluate:\n\nINDIVIDUAL LIBERTY MATTERS MORE:\n• STIRNER: The Ego and Its Own (1844) — only the ego matters; equality, like morality, humanity and the state, is a 'spook' that constrains the individual. Egoists unite only when it serves their own interests.\n• PROUDHON: mutualism defended the possession of land and tools by independent producers and free exchange between them; his priority was independence from both state and exploitative landlords and lenders, not full collective equality.\n• GOLDMAN: stressed the free development of the individual against state, church, marriage and convention; she defined anarchism as a social order resting on liberty rather than on man-made law.\n\nECONOMIC EQUALITY MATTERS AS MUCH OR MORE:\n• BAKUNIN: collectivism — the means of production held in common by workers' associations. He insisted that liberty without socialism is privilege and injustice, and socialism without liberty is slavery; for him freedom is social and requires equality.\n• KROPOTKIN: anarcho-communism — distribution according to need (The Conquest of Bread, 1892). Without economic equality the poor are not free, since they must sell their labour to survive.\n• GOLDMAN was also a communist anarchist: she argued that freedom was impossible under capitalism's economic domination.\n• COLLECTIVIST ANARCHISM treats liberty and equality as inseparable rather than competing values, so the statement sets up a false choice.\n\nSynoptic links: liberalism's emphasis on individual liberty (Mill) and socialism's emphasis on equality (Marx and Engels); anarcho-capitalism; UK debates on inequality and welfare.\n\nJudgement: Individualist anarchists (Stirner, and to a degree Proudhon) prioritise the independence of the individual, while collectivist anarchists (Bakunin, Kropotkin, Goldman) insist that liberty and economic equality stand or fall together. The strongest conclusion is that for most anarchists the statement is a false dichotomy — they reject capitalism precisely because it destroys individual freedom — but that Stirner shows anarchism contains a strand for which liberty is everything.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and evaluating the statement through accurate use of the specified anarchist thinkers (Stirner, Proudhon, Bakunin, Kropotkin, Goldman) and weighing the competing arguments to a substantiated conclusion; it does not matter which view is reached, provided it is supported. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant, accurate points should be credited.` },

      { q: '\'Social order can be maintained without the state.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the anarchist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — students may analyse and evaluate:\n\nORDER IS POSSIBLE WITHOUT THE STATE:\n• KROPOTKIN: Mutual Aid (1902) argued that cooperation within species is a major factor in evolution and that human history is full of voluntary institutions — guilds, village communes, free cities — that maintained order without the state.\n• PROUDHON: 'anarchy is order' — order can rest on freely made contracts between producers, mutual credit and federations of communities rather than on command.\n• BAKUNIN: humans are naturally social; order emerges from free association and solidarity once the artificial authority of state and church is removed.\n• GOLDMAN: crime and disorder are largely produced by poverty and by the state itself, so abolishing the state removes a cause of disorder. The CNT collectives in Spain (1936–39) showed workers organising production and daily life without state direction.\n\nORDER REQUIRES THE STATE:\n• STIRNER: the union of egoists is voluntary and unstable; if each person pursues their own interest, conflict is likely — anarchism's own egoist strand undercuts the optimistic case.\n• HOBBES and CONSERVATISM (synoptic): without a sovereign, life becomes a war of all against all; Burke and Oakeshott see order as resting on inherited institutions and authority.\n• SCALE AND DEFENCE: small communities may cooperate, but complex societies need coordination, law and defence; the Spanish collectives were destroyed in civil war, which raises the question of how a stateless society protects itself.\n\nSynoptic links: the rule of law and policing in the UK; conservatism (Hobbes, Burke); liberalism's social contract; examples of cooperatives and voluntary organisations.\n\nJudgement: Kropotkin and Proudhon make a strong case that much everyday order already rests on cooperation rather than coercion. But their case depends on an optimistic view of human nature that Stirner's egoism and conservative thinkers challenge, and on small-scale communities that may not defend themselves or coordinate at scale. The strongest conclusion is that some order is possible without the state, but anarchists have not shown that order can be maintained in large, complex societies without some coercive authority.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and evaluating the statement through accurate use of the specified anarchist thinkers (Stirner, Proudhon, Bakunin, Kropotkin, Goldman) and weighing the competing arguments to a substantiated conclusion; it does not matter which view is reached, provided it is supported. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant, accurate points should be credited.` },

      { q: '\'Anarchists offer no workable economic alternative to capitalism.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the anarchist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — students may analyse and evaluate:\n\nNO WORKABLE ALTERNATIVE:\n• DIVISION: anarchists propose mutualism (Proudhon), collectivism (Bakunin), communism (Kropotkin) and egoism (Stirner) — incompatible models, so there is no single anarchist economic alternative.\n• KROPOTKIN: distribution according to need assumes abundance and a willingness to work without wages, which critics see as utopian and vulnerable to free-riding.\n• STIRNER offers no economic system at all, only the ego's appropriation of what it can take and hold. More generally, no anarchist economy has operated at national scale for long: the Spanish collectives lasted only a few years and were overtaken by war.\n\nTHERE ARE WORKABLE ALTERNATIVES:\n• PROUDHON: mutualism — mutual credit and interest-free lending (his attempted People's Bank of 1849), producer cooperatives and fair exchange — anticipates credit unions and cooperatives that function today.\n• BAKUNIN: collective ownership by workers' associations with reward according to labour; workers' self-management has been practised in cooperatives.\n• KROPOTKIN: Fields, Factories and Workshops (1899) proposed integrating agriculture and industry in decentralised communities — ideas later echoed by ecological thinkers.\n• GOLDMAN supported the CNT's collectivisation in Spain in 1936, where many workplaces and farms were run collectively for a time.\n\nSynoptic links: the cooperative sector and mutuals in the UK; socialism's alternatives to capitalism (Marx and Engels, Webb); liberalism's defence of markets; ecologism (Bookchin).\n\nJudgement: Anarchists offer several economic alternatives and some elements (cooperatives, mutual credit) are clearly workable on a small scale. The weaknesses are that the alternatives are mutually incompatible and have not been sustained at the scale of a modern economy. The strongest conclusion is that anarchism offers workable fragments rather than a complete alternative, and that its economic proposals depend on its wider claims about human nature and decentralisation.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and evaluating the statement through accurate use of the specified anarchist thinkers (Stirner, Proudhon, Bakunin, Kropotkin, Goldman) and weighing the competing arguments to a substantiated conclusion; it does not matter which view is reached, provided it is supported. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant, accurate points should be credited.` },

      { q: '\'Anarchism must liberate people from social conventions, not just from the state.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the anarchist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — students may analyse and evaluate:\n\nLIBERATION FROM CONVENTION IS ESSENTIAL:\n• GOLDMAN: attacked marriage as an economic arrangement that made women dependent ('Marriage and Love'), puritan morality and religion; she campaigned for birth control (and was arrested for it in 1916) and for free love. Freedom from the state alone would leave people bound by internalised authority.\n• STIRNER: morality, religion, humanity and society are 'spooks' — fixed ideas that enslave the ego as surely as the state; liberation means freeing the self from all of them.\n• BAKUNIN: God and the State argued that religious belief is a foundation of political authority, so liberation from convention and belief is necessary to liberation from the state.\n\nLIBERATION FROM THE STATE (AND CAPITAL) IS WHAT MATTERS:\n• PROUDHON: focused on the state, property and credit, and held conventional, patriarchal views of women and the family — showing that anarchism has not always challenged social convention.\n• KROPOTKIN: mutual aid rests on shared customs and social habits; conventions of cooperation are the basis of anarchist order, not something to be abolished.\n• COERCION IS THE ISSUE: anarchists reject coercive authority; conventions that are freely followed are not coercion, and forcing people to abandon them would itself contradict anarchism.\n• CAUSATION: many anarchists hold that oppressive conventions are sustained by state and capital, so they will change once those institutions are removed.\n\nSynoptic links: feminism (the personal is political; Gilman, de Beauvoir); liberalism (Mill on the tyranny of the majority); social and moral change through law and pressure groups.\n\nJudgement: Goldman and Stirner show that anarchism can be a total critique of authority, including the authority of convention, and Proudhon's patriarchal views illustrate the cost of ignoring it. But anarchism's own commitment to voluntarism means conventions can only be challenged by persuasion and example, and Kropotkin shows that some conventions support free order. The strongest conclusion is that anarchism must challenge coercive and internalised authority, not every convention.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and evaluating the statement through accurate use of the specified anarchist thinkers (Stirner, Proudhon, Bakunin, Kropotkin, Goldman) and weighing the competing arguments to a substantiated conclusion; it does not matter which view is reached, provided it is supported. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant, accurate points should be credited.` },

      { q: '\'Anarchists and Marxists share the same goal and disagree only about the means of achieving it.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the anarchist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — students may analyse and evaluate:\n\nSAME GOAL, DIFFERENT MEANS:\n• SHARED END STATE: Marx and Engels envisaged a classless, stateless communist society in which the state would 'wither away' (Engels); anarcho-communists such as Kropotkin also sought a stateless society with distribution according to need.\n• BAKUNIN and Marx were both in the First International, both opposed capitalism and both advocated revolution by the working class.\n• THE DISPUTE OVER THE TRANSITIONAL STATE: the main quarrel was whether a dictatorship of the proletariat was needed — a question of means.\n\nNOT THE SAME GOAL, OR MEANS SHAPE ENDS:\n• BAKUNIN: means and ends cannot be separated — a workers' state would create a new ruling class (Statism and Anarchy, 1873) and never wither away. Underlying this is a different analysis: Marxists see the state as a product of class division, anarchists as an independent source of domination. The split led to Bakunin's expulsion from the International at the Hague Congress in 1872.\n• GOLDMAN: My Disillusionment in Russia (1923) described how the Bolshevik state crushed independent socialists and the Kronstadt rising (1921); she concluded that authoritarian means had destroyed the revolution's ends.\n• STIRNER: rejected communism as another 'spook' subordinating the individual to society; Marx and Engels attacked him at length in The German Ideology — their goals were opposed, not shared.\n• PROUDHON: mutualism kept individual possession and exchange, which Marx criticised in The Poverty of Philosophy (1847); this is a different economic goal, not just a different route.\n\nSynoptic links: socialism (Marx and Engels, Luxemburg); revolutionary versus democratic change; the history of twentieth-century communist states.\n\nJudgement: The statement fits the relationship between Marxism and anarcho-communism (Kropotkin) reasonably well at the level of the final society. But it fails for individualist anarchists (Stirner) and mutualists (Proudhon), and even collectivists (Bakunin, Goldman) argued that the disagreement over means was really a disagreement about ends, because a state cannot abolish itself. The strongest conclusion is that the history of the Soviet state lends considerable support to Bakunin's view.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and evaluating the statement through accurate use of the specified anarchist thinkers (Stirner, Proudhon, Bakunin, Kropotkin, Goldman) and weighing the competing arguments to a substantiated conclusion; it does not matter which view is reached, provided it is supported. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant, accurate points should be credited.` },

      { q: '"Anarchism is utopian." Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the anarchist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `UTOPIAN:\n• Its view of HUMAN NATURE is unsupported — without enforceable rules, disputes would be settled by whoever is strongest, so abolishing the state risks replacing public authority with private domination rather than harmony.\n• NO MECHANISM — anarchism is vague about dispute resolution, large-scale infrastructure planning, and dealing with a minority that refuses to cooperate. The problems government exists to solve do not disappear when government does.\n• THE RECORD — no anarchist society has been sustained at scale; revolutionary Catalonia and Ukraine were destroyed.\n• Its rejection of hierarchy makes sustained large-scale organisation difficult.\n• The claim that observed selfishness is produced by existing institutions risks being UNFALSIFIABLE, since any evidence of conflict can be attributed to structures not yet abolished.\n\nNOT UTOPIAN:\n• KROPOTKIN'S MUTUAL AID provides genuine empirical evidence that cooperation is widespread in nature and human society.\n• Small-scale cooperative and mutualist organisation demonstrably works.\n• Catalonia and Ukraine were destroyed by STATES, not by internal collapse — which partly proves the anarchist point about state power.\n• Anarchist critiques have been VINDICATED — Bakunin's prediction about the workers' state was borne out by twentieth-century history.\n• Demanding a detailed blueprint imports statist assumptions about how societies must be organised.\n\nGuidance for L5: the most sophisticated version of the charge is STRUCTURAL rather than moral — anarchism's rejection of hierarchy and of capturing state power deprives it of the instruments by which movements consolidate change, so it is systematically disadvantaged against opponents who use both. That is not a refutation of its principles but describes a movement whose means are constrained by its ends.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2.` },

      { q: '"Anarchists disagree more than they agree." Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the anarchist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `DISAGREE:\n• PROPERTY — Kropotkin's common ownership with distribution by need versus Stirner's egoism and anarcho-capitalism's private property and unrestricted markets. These are opposites.\n• HUMAN NATURE — Kropotkin's naturally cooperative human being versus Stirner's self-interested, self-creating ego owing nothing to others.\n• INTELLECTUAL ORIGIN — collectivist anarchism derives from socialism, individualist anarchism from liberalism, so they share no theoretical foundation.\n• EXCHANGE — Proudhon's fair exchange between independent producers versus Kropotkin's abolition of exchange and wages altogether.\n• METHOD — Bakunin's revolution and propaganda of the deed versus Proudhon's gradual construction of alternative institutions.\n\nAGREE:\n• ALL reject the state as coercive, sovereign, corrupting and unnecessary.\n• Collectivist anarchists reject all hierarchy, but anarcho-capitalists accept authority arising from property and contract — so agreement beyond opposition to the state is incomplete.\n• All are ANTI-CLERICAL, seeing organised religion as teaching submission and reinforcing hierarchy.\n• All believe order can emerge from VOLUNTARY cooperation without compulsion.\n• Most anarchists reject electoral participation as legitimising the state, holding that it cannot be used to abolish itself (Proudhon's brief spell in the 1848 Constituent Assembly being a notable exception).\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest conclusion is that anarchism contains TWO IRRECONCILABLE TRADITIONS sharing only opposition to the state — and that this exposes a deeper ambiguity about whether anarchism opposes the STATE specifically or DOMINATION in general. The anarcho-capitalism dispute is the sharpest test of that ambiguity.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2.` },

      { q: '"Anarcho-capitalism is not genuinely anarchist." Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the anarchist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `NOT GENUINELY ANARCHIST:\n• Classical anarchists argue concentrated PRIVATE economic power is itself a form of domination — Proudhon's "property is theft" where it permits living off others' labour.\n• Private protection agencies holding a monopoly of force in a territory would RESEMBLE STATES in all but name, so it abolishes the state in name only.\n• It preserves precisely the hierarchy — of employer over worker, owner over non-owner — that anarchism exists to oppose.\n• It derives from liberalism rather than from the socialist tradition that produced most anarchist thought.\n• Kropotkin, Bakunin and Goldman all regarded capitalism as a system of domination requiring abolition.\n\nGENUINELY ANARCHIST:\n• It opposes the state outright, which IS the defining anarchist commitment.\n• It is internally consistent — voluntary exchange and contract with no compulsory authority.\n• INDIVIDUALIST anarchism has always been part of the tradition; Stirner's egoism is equally distant from Kropotkin's communism, yet Stirner is a named anarchist thinker.\n• All arrangements would be voluntary, which is the anarchist test of legitimacy.\n• Excluding it requires defining anarchism by its ECONOMICS rather than by its anti-statism, which is contestable.\n\nGuidance for L5: this question forces the underlying definitional issue — is anarchism defined by opposition to the STATE or to HIERARCHY AND DOMINATION generally? An answer that identifies and resolves that question reaches L5; one that simply asserts a conclusion caps at L3.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2.` },

      { q: '"Anarchists agree about human nature." Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the anarchist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `AGREE:\n• All are broadly OPTIMISTIC — all reject the conservative view that people are irredeemably flawed and require restraint, since that would make entrusting some with state power the worst possible response.\n• All hold that human beings are capable of ordering their affairs WITHOUT compulsory authority.\n• All hold that observed selfishness and violence are PRODUCED by hierarchical institutions rather than inherent — the state, capitalism, religion and property teach domination and obedience.\n• All believe people can live cooperatively once those institutions are removed.\n\nDISAGREE:\n• KROPOTKIN'S human being is naturally SOCIAL and cooperative, with mutual aid as an evolutionary fact; STIRNER'S is a self-interested, self-creating EGO who recognises no authority and owes nothing to others. These are close to opposite anthropologies.\n• Collectivists treat people as fundamentally communal, deriving fulfilment from association; individualists treat association as instrumental — a "union of egoists" entered for mutual advantage and dissolvable at will.\n• Anarcho-capitalists assume rational self-interested actors of the kind classical economics posits, not Kropotkin's cooperators.\n• Whether human beings need community or merely tolerate it divides the traditions entirely.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest conclusion is that anarchists agree on the NEGATIVE claim (people do not need rulers) and disagree fundamentally on the POSITIVE one (what people are actually like) — and that the shared conclusion is reached from incompatible premises.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2.` },
    ]
  },

  '3.8': {
    name: 'Ecologism',
    questions: [
      { q: '\'Ecologism is based, above all, on a holistic view of the relationship between humans and nature.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the ecologist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — students may analyse and evaluate:\n\nHOLISM IS THE CORE:\n• LEOPOLD: the land ethic makes humans ordinary members and citizens of the land community rather than its conquerors; right action preserves the integrity, stability and beauty of the biotic community as a whole.\n• CARSON: Silent Spring (1962) showed how pesticides such as DDT moved through soils, water and food chains, harming birds and threatening humans — an argument from the interconnection of living systems — and called the 'control of nature' an arrogant idea.\n• MERCHANT: The Death of Nature (1980) argued that the Scientific Revolution replaced an organic, holistic view of nature with a mechanistic one that licensed the domination of both nature and women; ecologism restores the holistic view.\n\nHOLISM IS NOT THE CORE (OR NOT THE WHOLE STORY):\n• BOOKCHIN: social ecology holds that the domination of nature arises from hierarchy and domination among humans; he criticised the mystical holism of deep ecology and saw humanity as nature rendered self-conscious, with a distinctive role.\n• SCHUMACHER: Small Is Beautiful (1973) was subtitled 'a study of economics as if people mattered' — its central concerns were scale, technology and human well-being as much as holism.\n• SHALLOW ECOLOGY and light-green thinking are anthropocentric: they aim at sustainability for human benefit, suggesting that sustainability or limits to growth may be the unifying idea instead.\n\nSynoptic links: the Climate Change Act 2008 and the net-zero target legislated in 2019; the Green Party; conservatism's organic view of society; liberalism's individualism and atomism.\n\nJudgement: Holism is the idea that most clearly distinguishes ecologism from other ideologies, and it is central to Leopold, Carson and Merchant. But Bookchin and Schumacher show that ecologism is also about social hierarchy, scale and human flourishing, and light greens are only weakly holistic. The strongest conclusion is that holism is the foundational philosophical claim of dark-green ecologism, while ecologism as a whole is better united by the view that humans are part of nature and depend on its limits.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and evaluating the statement through accurate use of the specified ecologist thinkers (Leopold, Carson, Schumacher, Merchant, Bookchin) and weighing the competing arguments to a substantiated conclusion; it does not matter which view is reached, provided it is supported. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant, accurate points should be credited.` },

      { q: '\'Shallow ecology is an inadequate response to the environmental crisis.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the ecologist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — students may analyse and evaluate:\n\nSHALLOW ECOLOGY IS INADEQUATE:\n• LEOPOLD: conservation based solely on economic self-interest is hopelessly lopsided, because most members of the land community have no economic value; only a land ethic extending moral concern to the whole community can work.\n• MERCHANT: reforms that leave the mechanistic, patriarchal worldview intact still treat nature as a resource to be managed, so they cannot address the root cause.\n• BOOKCHIN: distinguished 'environmentalism', which manages symptoms within existing hierarchical and capitalist society, from ecology, which requires transforming that society.\n• OUTCOMES: despite decades of environmental policy, global emissions and biodiversity loss have continued, suggesting that management within existing systems is not enough.\n\nSHALLOW ECOLOGY IS ADEQUATE OR NECESSARY:\n• CARSON: her reformist, science-based case led to real change within the existing system — the US Environmental Protection Agency (1970) and the US ban on DDT for agricultural use (1972).\n• SCHUMACHER: intermediate technology and practical changes in how economies use resources show that achievable reforms can make a real difference.\n• POLITICAL REALISM: UK greenhouse gas emissions have roughly halved since 1990 through regulation, carbon targets and the growth of renewables, while deep-green alternatives lack public support.\n\nSynoptic links: the Climate Change Act 2008 and net zero; the Paris Agreement (2015) and the US withdrawal ordered in 2025; party policies on net zero; liberalism and conservatism.\n\nJudgement: Shallow ecology has achieved measurable progress and is politically feasible, which Carson's legacy shows. Dark greens argue it treats the symptoms rather than the causes and cannot deliver the scale of change required. The strongest conclusion is that shallow ecology is necessary but insufficient — or, from Bookchin's and Merchant's perspective, that its successes depend on radical critiques that shift what is thought politically possible.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and evaluating the statement through accurate use of the specified ecologist thinkers (Leopold, Carson, Schumacher, Merchant, Bookchin) and weighing the competing arguments to a substantiated conclusion; it does not matter which view is reached, provided it is supported. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant, accurate points should be credited.` },

      { q: '\'A sustainable society requires small-scale, decentralised economic and political units.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the ecologist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — students may analyse and evaluate:\n\nSMALL-SCALE AND DECENTRALISED UNITS ARE REQUIRED:\n• SCHUMACHER: Small Is Beautiful (1973) argued for human-scale production, intermediate technology suited to local needs and Buddhist economics, criticising 'giantism' that treats natural capital as income.\n• BOOKCHIN: libertarian municipalism (communalism) — directly democratic municipalities in confederation — as the political form of an ecological society free of hierarchy.\n• LEOPOLD: the land ethic grows from a close, personal relationship with a particular place, which large, remote institutions undermine.\n\nTHEY ARE NOT REQUIRED:\n• GLOBAL PROBLEM: climate change is a collective-action problem that needs international agreements (the Paris Agreement, 2015) and national legislation (the Climate Change Act 2008), not only local action.\n• CARSON: the response to Silent Spring came through national regulation — the Environmental Protection Agency and the federal DDT ban — showing the value of central state action.\n• MERCHANT: her emphasis falls on changing the worldview and relationships between humans, gender and nature (a 'partnership ethic'), not on scale as such.\n• COSTS OF LOCALISM: small units may lack resources, be parochial or entrench inequality between areas, and some green technologies (such as offshore wind) depend on economies of scale.\n\nSynoptic links: devolution and localism in the UK; international cooperation and COP summits; anarchism (Kropotkin); the Green Party's policies.\n\nJudgement: Schumacher and Bookchin make a strong case that ecological living requires human-scale institutions and participation. But the global nature of climate change and Carson's example suggest that large-scale state and international action is also required. The strongest conclusion is that decentralisation may be desirable for an ecological society but is not sufficient, and that most realistic green strategies combine local and global levels.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and evaluating the statement through accurate use of the specified ecologist thinkers (Leopold, Carson, Schumacher, Merchant, Bookchin) and weighing the competing arguments to a substantiated conclusion; it does not matter which view is reached, provided it is supported. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant, accurate points should be credited.` },

      { q: '\'Ecologism has more in common with socialism than with any other ideology.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the ecologist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — students may analyse and evaluate:\n\nCLOSEST TO SOCIALISM:\n• BOOKCHIN: social ecology comes from the libertarian socialist and anarchist tradition; capitalism's 'grow or die' imperative and social hierarchy drive ecological destruction — the core eco-socialist claim (synoptic: Marx and Engels).\n• MERCHANT: a socialist ecofeminist — capitalism and patriarchy together treat nature and women as resources to be exploited.\n• SCHUMACHER: criticised profit-maximising economics, worked for the nationalised National Coal Board for two decades and praised the Scott Bader Commonwealth, a firm held in common by its workers.\n\nNOT CLOSEST TO SOCIALISM:\n• SOCIALIST PRODUCTIVISM: state socialist regimes pursued industrial growth with severe environmental damage (for example the shrinking of the Aral Sea in the Soviet Union).\n• CARSON and LEOPOLD belonged to a reformist conservation tradition — science-based regulation and stewardship of land — closer to liberal or conservative thought than to socialism.\n• CONSERVATISM: Burke's idea of society as a partnership between the living, the dead and those yet to be born, together with stewardship and organicism, resembles ecological concern for future generations.\n• ANARCHISM: Bookchin's decentralised communalism is closer to anarchism than to state socialism; and ecocentrism makes ecologism distinct from all human-centred ideologies.\n\nSynoptic links: socialism (Marx and Engels, Crosland), conservatism (Burke, Oakeshott), anarchism (Kropotkin); the Green Party's left-of-centre policy platform.\n\nJudgement: Ecologism shares socialism's critique of capitalism and growth, especially in Bookchin and Merchant, but it rejects the productivism of much socialist practice and draws heavily on conservative and anarchist ideas. The strongest conclusion is that ecologism overlaps most with socialism among dark greens and with liberalism or conservatism among light greens, and that its ecocentrism makes it a distinct ideology rather than a branch of any other.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and evaluating the statement through accurate use of the specified ecologist thinkers (Leopold, Carson, Schumacher, Merchant, Bookchin) and weighing the competing arguments to a substantiated conclusion; it does not matter which view is reached, provided it is supported. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant, accurate points should be credited.` },

      { q: '\'Ecologism is fundamentally hostile to industrialism.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the ecologist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — students may analyse and evaluate:\n\nFUNDAMENTALLY HOSTILE:\n• SCHUMACHER: industrial society treats natural capital — fossil fuels and the environment's capacity to absorb pollution — as income, which is unsustainable; he rejected the 'giantism' of industrial production.\n• MERCHANT: the mechanistic worldview of the Scientific Revolution underpinned the commercial and industrial exploitation of nature.\n• CARSON: exposed the chemical industry's indiscriminate use of synthetic pesticides and the arrogance of attempts to 'control' nature.\n\nNOT FUNDAMENTALLY HOSTILE:\n• BOOKCHIN: Post-Scarcity Anarchism (1971) argued that modern technology could free humanity from toil and underpin an ecological society; the problem is hierarchy, not technology.\n• CARSON: did not call for abolishing chemicals but for their careful, selective use and for biological alternatives — a reform of industrial practice.\n• SCHUMACHER: favoured appropriate or intermediate technology rather than rejecting technology; his target was scale, not industry itself.\n• LIGHT GREENS: green growth and ecological modernisation rely on industries such as renewable energy and electric vehicles.\n\nSynoptic links: net zero and the growth of renewable industries; debates on green growth; socialism, liberalism and conservatism's attitudes to economic growth.\n\nJudgement: Ecologism is hostile to industrialism understood as a 'super-ideology' — the commitment to growth and the domination of nature that greens argue capitalism and socialism share — and Schumacher and Merchant show why. But few ecologists reject technology or industry as such: Bookchin sees liberating potential in technology and Carson sought reform. The strongest conclusion is that ecologism is hostile to industrialism as an ideology of endless growth rather than to industry itself.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and evaluating the statement through accurate use of the specified ecologist thinkers (Leopold, Carson, Schumacher, Merchant, Bookchin) and weighing the competing arguments to a substantiated conclusion; it does not matter which view is reached, provided it is supported. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant, accurate points should be credited.` },

      { q: '\'Nature has value independent of its usefulness to humans.\' Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the ecologist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — students may analyse and evaluate:\n\nNATURE HAS INTRINSIC VALUE:\n• LEOPOLD: the land ethic affirms the right of soils, waters, plants and animals to continued existence; conservation cannot rest on economic value alone.\n• MERCHANT: the mechanistic worldview reduced nature to dead matter to be used; she argues for a partnership ethic in which nature is an active partner rather than a resource.\n• DEEP ECOLOGY: ecocentrism holds that all living things have value in themselves, and that humans have no right to reduce the richness of life except to meet vital needs.\n\nNATURE'S VALUE DEPENDS ON HUMANS:\n• CARSON: much of Silent Spring's argument rests on human health — cancer risk and chemical residues in food and water — showing that anthropocentric arguments can drive environmental protection.\n• SCHUMACHER: 'economics as if people mattered' — his case rests largely on human well-being and on keeping natural capital for future generations, though he also argued for reverence for nature.\n• BOOKCHIN: rejected deep ecology's biocentrism as potentially misanthropic; he saw humans as having a distinctive role as nature rendered self-conscious, so ecological ethics is grounded in human society.\n• LIGHT-GREEN ANTHROPOCENTRISM: sustainability means meeting the needs of the present without compromising future generations (the Brundtland Report, 1987) — nature matters because humans depend on it. Critics also argue that value is something humans attribute.\n\nSynoptic links: biodiversity and rewilding policy; the Environment Act 2021; animal welfare law; liberalism and conservatism's anthropocentrism.\n\nJudgement: Leopold and Merchant make the case for intrinsic value central to dark-green ecologism, and it is what distinguishes ecologism from environmentalism. But Carson, Schumacher and Bookchin show that much ecological thought rests on human interests or on humanity's special role, and anthropocentric arguments are often more politically persuasive. The strongest conclusion is that the claim divides ecologists: it is the defining belief of deep ecology but not a requirement of ecologism as a whole.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Credit comes from analysing and evaluating the statement through accurate use of the specified ecologist thinkers (Leopold, Carson, Schumacher, Merchant, Bookchin) and weighing the competing arguments to a substantiated conclusion; it does not matter which view is reached, provided it is supported. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Students need not cover every point above, and other relevant, accurate points should be credited.` },

      { q: '"Ecologism is a distinct ideology." Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the ecologist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `DISTINCT:\n• It offers a COMPLETE WORLDVIEW — an account of humanity's place in nature, a critique of industrialism, materialism and growth, and a prescription for restructuring economy and society.\n• HOLISM and ECOCENTRISM genuinely BREAK with the anthropocentric individualism shared by liberalism, conservatism and socialism alike. No other ideology questions whether human beings should be the measure of political value.\n• It generates positions no other tradition reaches — rejecting growth as an objective, and treating the interests of future generations and other species as political considerations.\n• Schumacher's reconception of natural resources as capital rather than income reconceives economics rather than adding a constraint to it.\n\nNOT DISTINCT:\n• MOST of ecologism ATTACHES to other ideologies — eco-socialism to a Marxist critique of capitalism, eco-anarchism to hierarchy, ecofeminism to patriarchy. Each combines ecological premises with a PRIOR analysis of domination.\n• Only DEEP ECOLOGY makes a genuinely free-standing claim, and it is the strand with the LEAST developed political programme.\n• Each strand's politics comes from its prior analysis, not from its ecology.\n• It lacks a distinctive account of human nature comparable to the major ideologies.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest conclusion draws the parallel with NATIONALISM — a distinctive set of premises that must combine with a social analysis to generate politics, rather than a free-standing tradition. That comparative move reaches L5.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2.` },

      { q: '"Ecologists disagree about the causes of environmental crisis." Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the ecologist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `DISAGREE:\n• DEEP ECOLOGY blames ANTHROPOCENTRISM itself — the belief that nature exists for human use — and often human population.\n• BOOKCHIN'S SOCIAL ECOLOGY blames HIERARCHY — the domination of nature extends the domination of humans by other humans — and explicitly rejects deep ecology's attribution to humanity in general as politically useless.\n• ECO-SOCIALISM blames CAPITALISM's structural requirement for continuous accumulation, since firms that do not expand are eliminated.\n• ECOFEMINISM (Merchant) blames PATRIARCHAL DUALISMS and the mechanistic worldview that replaced an organic conception of nature.\n• SHALLOW ECOLOGY blames specific policy failures and treats the crisis as manageable.\n\nAGREE:\n• All accept that a crisis EXISTS and is human-caused.\n• All accept HOLISM and ecological interdependence — that intervening in one element has consequences throughout.\n• All reject unlimited consumption and the assumption that more material acquisition means a better life.\n• All accept SUSTAINABILITY as a constraint, and the finitude of the planet.\n• All radical (dark-green) strands identify INDUSTRIALISM and modern economic organisation as central to the problem; shallow ecology treats them as manageable.\n\nGuidance for L5: the disagreement about CAUSES is severe and generates incompatible prescriptions — but it follows from each strand attaching ecological premises to a prior analysis of domination, which is itself evidence about ecologism's status as an ideology. Linking the two questions reaches L5.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2.` },

      { q: '"Economic growth is incompatible with environmental protection." Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the ecologist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `INCOMPATIBLE:\n• Indefinite EXPONENTIAL GROWTH cannot continue on a finite planet with limited resources and limited capacity to absorb waste.\n• Schumacher — conventional economics treats natural resources as INCOME rather than irreplaceable CAPITAL, so depletion registers as profit; GDP counts activity regardless of whether it creates or destroys value.\n• ECO-SOCIALISTS argue growth is not a policy choice but capitalism's structural requirement, so it cannot be abandoned within capitalism.\n• Beyond the point at which basic needs are met, further consumption yields little additional wellbeing (post-materialism), so the growth is not even serving its purpose.\n• Historical evidence associates growth with rising emissions and resource depletion.\n\nCOMPATIBLE:\n• Growth funds the TECHNOLOGY and environmental protection that poorer societies need; environmental quality has improved in many wealthy countries.\n• DECOUPLING — some economies have grown while reducing territorial emissions, suggesting the link is not necessary.\n• The target should be the ENVIRONMENTAL IMPACT of growth rather than growth itself; green growth and circular economy models attempt exactly this.\n• Degrowth would impose severe costs on the poorest, both within and between countries, and has no evident political constituency.\n• Innovation driven by growth may deliver the solutions.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest analysis interrogates DECOUPLING — whether it is absolute or relative, and whether it survives accounting for emissions embedded in imports. That empirical scrutiny reaches L5; asserting either position does not.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2.` },

      { q: '"Ecologism requires a fundamental change in human values." Analyse and evaluate this statement with reference to the ecologist thinkers that you have studied.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `REQUIRES VALUE CHANGE:\n• DEEP ECOLOGY holds the crisis originates in ANTHROPOCENTRISM itself, so nothing less than a new ecocentric ethic will suffice — Leopold's land ethic extends the moral community to soils, waters, plants and animals.\n• The critique of MATERIALISM and consumerism is a critique of what people value, not merely of how they produce.\n• HOLISM challenges the individualism underpinning liberal societies.\n• MERCHANT argues the mechanistic worldview that replaced an organic conception of nature removed the ethical restraints on exploitation, so restoring restraint requires reversing that conceptual shift.\n• Sustainability requires valuing the interests of future generations who cannot vote or bargain.\n\nSTRUCTURAL CHANGE MATTERS MORE:\n• BOOKCHIN argues the crisis originates in HIERARCHY, so changing values without dismantling hierarchical structures achieves nothing — and blaming humanity's values obscures who actually profits from destruction.\n• ECO-SOCIALISTS argue capitalism's structural requirement for accumulation operates regardless of individual values; firms that do not expand are eliminated whatever their owners believe.\n• SHALLOW ECOLOGY shows substantial gains are achievable through regulation and technology without any value transformation.\n• Value change is slow, unreliable and hard to engineer, whereas institutional change can be legislated.\n• Individuals with green values are constrained by the systems they live within.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest conclusion notes this reproduces ecologism's central internal division — whether the problem lies in VALUES or in INSTITUTIONS — and that the answer determines whether ecologism is a free-standing ideology or a set of premises attaching to a prior social analysis.\n\nGuidance: Target AO1 5, AO2 10, AO3 10. Any response without synoptic points cannot go above Level 4. Answers that make no reference to thinkers cannot go above Level 2.` },
    ]
  },

};
